// Single source of truth for FDS feature flags.
//
// Three macros, each with the same signature:
//   FDS_FLAG_BOOL (NAME, ENV, DEFAULT, CATEGORY, HELP)
//   FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(NAME, ENV, DEFAULT, CATEGORY, HELP)
//   FDS_FLAG_INT  (NAME, ENV, DEFAULT, CATEGORY, HELP)
//
// NAME    — C identifier; also the CLI form ("--<name>" / "--no-<name>" / "--<name>=value").
// ENV     — environment variable name (existing FDS_* names preserved).
// DEFAULT — compile-time default. Some defaults reference macros (FDS_*_DEFAULT_ON)
//           so the WASM build can flip them via -DFDS_DEFERRED_DEFAULT_ON=1.
// CATEGORY — short string used to group output of --help.
// HELP    — one-line description shown in --help.
//
// Adding a flag is a single line in this file.

// ─── engine ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(param_scripts,            "FDS_PARAM_SCRIPTS",            1,                              "engine",   "Per-scene scripted parameters: load SCRIPTS/<scene>.params (relative to CWD) at scene start, apply its FeatureFlags values each frame (time-keyed lines lerp over the scene Timer), hot-reload on file change. CLI/env-set flags always win over the script. See docs/PARAM_SCRIPTS.md.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(vanilla,                  "FDS_VANILLA",                  0,                              "engine",   "BARE BASELINE: force EVERY flag back to its COMPILE-TIME default and mark it explicitly-set, so scene-level defaults (FeatureFlags::setDefault — greets' PBR/HDR/shadow block, chase's bolt-light reach, crash's exposure, ...) and SCRIPTS/<scene>.params lines are suppressed too. Without the explicitly-set part 'vanilla' would be a lie: the scenes would re-enable their own look one frame later. PUT IT FIRST — semantics are pure parse order, so tokens named AFTER it still win (`--vanilla --deferred --pbr` = bare + those two) and tokens before it are erased. It also CLEARS ENV-SET VALUES: the eager FDS_* env scan runs before argv, so a --vanilla on the command line wipes it; the env form FDS_VANILLA=1 is applied after the env scan for the same reason, then --flags-file / FDS_BAKED_ARGS / argv tokens are processed on top. Note this is compile-time DEFAULTS, not 'everything off' — flags whose default is ON (param_scripts, tune_server, warn_unknown_flags, deferred_zcull, metal_map, roughness_map, ao_map, ...) stay on, and since `deferred` defaults off a vanilla run renders the FORWARD path. Prints a one-line [FLAGS] note when it fires so a run is self-identifying. Startup-only: the live tune console rejects it (a mid-run mass reset would contradict geometry/bakes already built from the init-time flags).")
FDS_FLAG_INT (scrub_speed,              "FDS_SCRUB_SPEED",              4,                              "engine",   "REVIEW SCRUB — SHIFT MULTIPLIER on the existing F1/F2 scene-clock scrub (DEMO/SceneDriver.cpp tickSceneTimer). Unmodified: F2 runs the scene clock FORWARD at 8x the frame delta while playing, 10 ticks/frame while paused (fine enough to step single animation frames); F1 runs it BACKWARD; P pauses, U unpauses. Hold SHIFT and that step is multiplied by this value, so one knob gives two speeds and the unmodified rate is exactly what it always was. The scene clock is the global Timer and RENDER.CPP derives CurFrame from it linearly, so scrubbing moves camera splines, object animation and scene progress together — a review tool for reaching a pose, not a playback-rate control (music, the SDL frame rate and anything driven off wall-clock are unaffected, so audio desyncs while you hold it; that is expected). Rewind clamps at 0. Set 1 to make Shift inert. One-shot init and bakes keyed on 'first frame' do NOT re-run when you rewind past them. HISTORY: this flag first drove a SEPARATE F3/F4 scrub inside REV.CPP's SDL timer callback; that was REMOVED 2026-08-06 because it never worked — the callback incremented Timer, and tickSceneTimer re-pins Timer from the wall clock every frame (`if (pauseMode || scrubbed) Timer = sceneT`), clobbering the increments. The scene driver owns the clock; scrub from there.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(warn_unknown_flags,       "FDS_WARN_UNKNOWN_FLAGS",       1,                              "engine",   "Warn on stderr ([FLAGS] unknown flag '--foo' (ignored)) when a CLI / flags-file token matches no registered flag — catches typos like --fast-fog-blob (for fast_fog_blobs) that were silently dropped before. Tokens owned by the other argv parsers (--snapshot/--bench/--out/--material-import*/--print-completion) are exempt. Parse-order caveat: --no-warn-unknown-flags silences only tokens AFTER it; use FDS_WARN_UNKNOWN_FLAGS=0 to silence a whole run from scripts.")

FDS_FLAG_BOOL(profiler,                 "FDS_PROFILER",                 0,                              "engine",   "On-screen profiler overlay (per-section ms, FPS, polys/Mpx). Runtime equivalent of rev.cfg's ProfilerEnable (the cfg seeds this flag; CLI/script/tune-console can override either way, per frame).")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(tune_server,              "FDS_TUNE_SERVER",              1,                              "engine",   "Live tuning console: embedded HTTP server (localhost only) serving a knob page over every FeatureFlag. Open http://localhost:<tune_port> while the demo runs; changes apply immediately with CLI precedence (param scripts yield); release buttons hand knobs back; copy buttons export CLI/.params lines.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(tune_port,                 "FDS_TUNE_PORT",                8666,                           "engine",   "Live tuning console port (see tune_server).")

// ─── deferred pipeline ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(deferred,                 "FDS_DEFERRED",                 FDS_DEFERRED_DEFAULT_ON,        "deferred", "Deferred G-buffer pipeline")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(spot_cone_cull,           "FDS_SPOT_CONE_CULL",           1,                              "deferred", "Tile-vs-cone culling for spot lights in the per-tile light lists + volumetric cone pass. Range-sphere culling alone puts a narrow spot (disco beams: 4.5 deg, range 38) in nearly every tile while it lights almost none of it.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(cone_fine_tiles,          "FDS_CONE_FINE_TILES",          1,                              "deferred", "Volumetric cone pass uses the 12x8 lighting grid (96 tiles) instead of the coarse 6x4 (24). Same per-pixel work (byte-identical output — verified on conetest), but the cone-heavy tiles (disco/beam region) spread across 4x more work-stealing tasks -> less idle at the pass barrier (~8% off the greets central-room frame). HDR-safe (separate pass, runs after activation). Default on; set 0 for the legacy coarse 6x4.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(deferred_tile_sphere_cull, "FDS_DEFERRED_TILE_SPHERE_CULL", 1,                        "deferred", "3-D range-sphere-vs-tile-chunk-sphere cull in the per-tile light lists, on top of the separable screen-rect + z-extent tests. BYTE-NULL: it can only drop a (tile x light) pair for which every pixel of the tile is farther from the light than its cull range, i.e. a pair whose every pixel would have failed the per-pixel `len2 > range2` test anyway; it reuses the same range2 the per-pixel test compares against, so a --deferred_max_range clamp cannot desynchronise them. WHY IT EXISTS: a conjunction of two separable projections of a sphere is strictly weaker than the sphere test -- a light off the DIAGONAL corner of a tile's frustum chunk passes the rect test and the z test and reaches no pixel. Measured with --shadow_tap_census at greets t=5743: 9.6 %% of admitted entries had zero in-range pixels before this, 93 %% of them carrying a cube shadow, i.e. paying the per-pixel loop prologue ~0.87 M times a frame for nothing. The test itself is per (tile x light) at list-build time -- ~840 of them a frame against ~9.1 M prologue iterations -- so it cannot cost what it saves. Turn OFF to A/B it or to bisect a suspected light-list bug.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(deferred_zcull,           "FDS_DEFERRED_ZCULL",           1,                              "deferred", "Cull G-buffer ROP writes against existing Z (default on)")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(deferred_vec,             "FDS_DEFERRED_VEC",             FDS_DEFERRED_VEC_DEFAULT,       "deferred", "SIMD (8-omni) lighting tile kernel. Default ON for x86 (native AVX2), OFF for arm64 (neutral-to-slower via simde). Vec spec now respects cube-shadow+cone; still omits 2D spot-shadow maps + mirror-clone shadows.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(deferred_vec_force,       "FDS_DEFERRED_VEC_FORCE",       0,                              "deferred", "[test] Force the vec (SIMD) per-light path even for normal-mapped / AO-mapped pixels that normally divert to scalar. Loses the nmap perturbation (uses geometric N) — COST MEASUREMENT ONLY, lets the vec path run on heavy-light scenes like greets to A/B scalar-vs-vec (esp. with --pbr). Requires FDS_DEFERRED_VEC=1.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(deferred_outer_vec,       "FDS_DEFERRED_OUTER_VEC",       0,                              "deferred", "Outer-vec kernel (8 px x 1 omni); when unset, falls back to Scene::PreferOuterVec")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(deferred_lm_addr_skip,    "FDS_DEFERRED_LM_ADDR_SKIP",    1,                              "deferred", "Resolve the per-pixel static-shadow LIGHTMAP ADDRESS only when the lightmap kernel is actually live. resolveCubeAtten reads the PixelLightmap exclusively behind `useLightmap && pl.lm`, and useLightmap IS lmKernelEnabled -- so whenever the lightmap kernel is off (greets ships --shadow_dynamic on with --shadow_lm_dynamic off, which is exactly that case) the two G-buffer plane loads, the scene-table index and the two pointer chases run per pixel for a value nothing reads, and their four results stay live across the whole pixel body. OFF restores the unconditional resolve. Byte-null by construction: the value is consumed only under the flag that gates it.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(deferred_cube_direct,     "FDS_DEFERRED_CUBE_DIRECT",     1,                              "deferred", "Call CubeShadow_Sample directly from the omni loop when resolveCubeAtten can only forward to it. The wrapper takes 20 arguments and carries the lightmap arm, the two lightmap debug-recompute arms and the Depth-mode slope-bias arm; with the lightmap kernel off and ShadowMode::PolyId (greets' shipping configuration) every one of those is unreachable and the wrapper is pure frame + argument setup around one call. The direct form passes 8 arguments instead of 20 and drops wx/wy/wz/lenInv/nGeo/the PixelLightmap/the CubeAttenFlags reference from the innermost body's live set. Predicate is hoisted per tile (flags) x per pixel (the receiver's shadow id); anything it does not cover still goes through resolveCubeAtten. BIT-EXACT by construction -- same callee, same arguments, in the arm it replaces.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(deferred_cube_prepass,     "FDS_DEFERRED_CUBE_PREPASS",    1,                              "deferred", "Compute the cube tap's PROLOGUE 8-wide over PIXELS for a fixed light, once per tile row, instead of scalar per (pixel x light) inside the omni loop. The tap is 204 instructions and 2.943 M calls/frame at greets t=5743 (0.599 Gi/f, 33 %% of DeferredLighting-call); the FDS_CUBE_ABLATE ladder prices its projection prologue -- face select, the viewToLight 3x3, the near/frustum/bounds rejects, 1/lz and the two projection muls -- at 125 of those 204, and every one is pure SIMD. It cannot go wide in the shipping loop because the loop is PIXEL-major: for a fixed pixel the eight lights carry eight different cubes, faces and matrices, so the matrix would have to be GATHERED and a gather costs what the scalar loads cost. Fixed LIGHT x eight PIXELS makes the matrix a BROADCAST. The tail (uniformity pyramid + 2x2 PCF) is a dependent load and stays scalar and lazy behind the omni loop's own early-outs, so the eager prologue only wastes work on pairs those early-outs kill. Gated on the same predicate as --deferred_cube_direct (PolyId mode, lightmap kernel off, tap not short-circuited) plus `no swizzle` and `no checker env/edge full-shade`, and the light order and every accumulation the omni loop performs are untouched -- only WHERE the prologue runs changes. DEFAULT ON. Bit-exact (0 mismatches in 47 M taps under --deferred_cube_prepass_verify, five acceptance poses and nine pins identical to the parent), and it is a TIME win at every pose measured -- but read docs/OPTIMIZATION_BACKLOG.md 2026-08-16l before quoting it, because at t=3409 it retires 1.6 %% MORE instructions while taking 4.2 %% fewer cycles and 5.5 %% less core time. A round that watches Ginstr/f alone will read that pose as a regression. Blast radius is greets: the gate needs lmKernelEnabled false (--shadow_dynamic without --shadow_lm_dynamic) plus ShadowMode::PolyId, and city / chase / fountain / the four render_gate arms take ZERO taps through it.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(deferred_cube_prepass_verify, "FDS_DEFERRED_CUBE_PREPASS_VERIFY", 0,                        "deferred", "[INSTRUMENT] Run the SCALAR cube-tap prologue behind every vectorised one and count lanes whose verdict, map index, iX, iY or smX/smY BIT PATTERN disagrees. Prints per frame to stderr. The landing bar for --deferred_cube_prepass is 0 mismatches of ~3 M taps -- 2026-08-16e shipped a vectorisation whose bilinear disagreed on 84 %% of taps because simde's fnmadd is not an fma on arm64, and the only reason that was caught was a counter exactly like this one.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(deferred_fill_hdr_skip,   "FDS_DEFERRED_FILL_HDR_SKIP",   1,                              "deferred", "In the checkerboard/quarter FILL (wave 2), skip the LDR sharp-reconstruction accumulators when the frame is HDR. slB/slG/slR are read only by the `haveOwn && nsharp > 0 && !hdrWrite` arm, so under --hdr they are three DIVISIONS per compatible neighbour computed for a value nothing reads (two neighbours per pixel in checkerboard). Also hoists --quarter_tex_sharp out of the per-pixel body. Byte-null by construction.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(deferred_fill_ldr_skip,   "FDS_DEFERRED_FILL_LDR_SKIP",   1,                              "deferred", "In the checkerboard/quarter FILL (wave 2), skip the 8-bit VPage average entirely when the frame is HDR. Render_TonemapToVPage overwrites EVERY VPage pixel from g_hdrBuf unconditionally (it does not consult the coverage lane), so a VPage write issued before it is discarded -- the #1 HDR gotcha in docs/GRAPHICS_PIPELINE.md, applied to the fill. That kills the neighbour colour load, its three byte extracts and three adds per compatible neighbour, and the compose + store per filled pixel. The sibling of --deferred_fill_hdr_skip, which already killed the LDR *sharp* accumulators on the same argument. Gated on ctx.ldrDiscarded, NOT on hdrWrite: renderFrame's tonemap sits inside `if (!skipVolumetric)` and CITY.CPP's water-reflection underlay renders with skipVolumetric=1 into the MAIN VPage at the MAIN resolution, so its hdrBuf is live, no tonemap runs, and its VPage IS the product. Every override/offscreen bake is excluded too. Byte-null by construction under that predicate; the pin battery and the five-pose diff are the gate.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(deferred_fill_oct_pair,   "FDS_DEFERRED_FILL_OCT_PAIR",   1,                              "deferred", "[MEASUREMENT HATCH -- INERT unless built with -DFDS_W2_OCTPAIR_HATCH=ON, the same shape as --omni_census.] In the checkerboard FILL (wave 2), decode the cell's two NEIGHBOUR octahedral normals and its CENTRE in ONE 4-wide meka::oct_decode_u32_x4 instead of three scalar oct_decode_u32 calls. Bit-exact per lane, not a judge call: every operation the scalar compiles to is element-wise on AArch64 -- sxth/asr, scvtf, one scale mul, the ordered az = (1-|ox|)-|oy|, the az<0 fold (a BRANCH in the scalar, a SELECT here because the lanes disagree; both arms are finite for every input word), the fmul + two FUSED fmadd length, and fast_rsqrt which is already vrsqrte + one Newton step on a float32x2_t. The dot is reproduced term for term in the order -ffp-contract=fast chose (fmul ncY*ny, then fmadd ncX*nx, then fmadd ncZ*nz). Verdicts ride a 2-BIT MASK, never a bool[2] -- an indexed array in this pixel body lands on the stack. MEASURED (greets, 1512x848, his acceptance arm, min of 11 order-rotated rounds): lighting-w2 -11.9 to -13.4 %% instructions at six poses, DeferredLighting-call -1.9 to -2.3 %%, renderFrame -0.6 to -0.9 %%, lighting-w1 flat. The SHIPPING form is FLAGLESS (FDS_W2_OCTPAIR_HATCH=0): the ON arm measures IDENTICAL with and without this flag at all six poses, so the hatch buys no speed, the change is bit-exact so it needs no look dial, and the only thing the dial can do is cost +0.7 to +1.1 %% of the fill. Kept so a future round can rebuild the three arms. See docs/OPTIMIZATION_BACKLOG.md 2026-08-16o.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(deferred_shade_ldr_skip,  "FDS_DEFERRED_SHADE_LDR_SKIP",  1,                              "deferred", "In the SHADE wave (wave 1, Render_DeferredLighting_Tile), skip the 8-bit VPage compose -- fdB/fdG/fdR and their metal + diffuse-energy scalings, outB/outG/outR, the isWater gamma blend, the debug-viz stomps, the clamps and the out[i] store. The wave-1 sibling of --deferred_fill_ldr_skip and it needs ONE MORE TERM than the fill does: it is gated on ctx.ldrDiscarded AND on --hdr_linear. ldrDiscarded is renderFrame's promise that this pass ends in Render_TonemapToVPage, which rewrites every VPage pixel from g_hdrBuf unconditionally (hdrWrite alone would be a bug -- see --deferred_fill_ldr_skip for CITY.CPP:3823's underlay, and every override/offscreen bake is excluded there too). --hdr_linear is what makes the chain dead rather than merely overwritten: under it the shipped radiance rlB is built from dlB, the raw light accumulator, and never from fdB, whereas under plain --hdr the B1 gamma radiance IS hB = fdB + sB and the chain is LIVE. Any debug-viz stomp also forces it back on. MEASURED (greets, 1512x848, --deferred --hdr --hdr-linear --texture-filter=2 --ssao --ssao-gtao --greets-displace): the chain is 0.033 Gi/f, 2.1 % of lighting-w1 and 1.8 % of DeferredLighting-call -- NOT the whole 1.570 Gi/f of wave 1. Byte-null by construction under that predicate; the pin battery and the five-pose diff are the gate.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(deferred_checkerboard,    "FDS_DEFERRED_CHECKERBOARD",    0,                              "deferred", "Half-rate lighting on a checkerboard pattern")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(deferred_quarter,         "FDS_DEFERRED_QUARTER",         0,                              "deferred", "Quarter-rate lighting (one of every 2x2 pixels)")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(deferred_no_spec,         "FDS_DEFERRED_NO_SPEC",         0,                              "deferred", "Skip specular term in tile kernel")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(quarter_tex_sharp,        "FDS_QUARTER_TEX_SHARP",        1,                              "deferred", "C: in the quarter/checker fill, reconstruct each neighbour's LIGHTING (final / its own texel) and re-apply THIS pixel's own texel, instead of averaging final colours — keeps far-floor texture detail sharp under --deferred_quarter/--deferred_checkerboard instead of mushing it. LDR + HDR paths, both fills. Neighbours whose texel ratio exceeds 4 are excluded (trust region — the divide-out explodes on high-contrast textures, e.g. LED dots on black).")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(pbr,                      "FDS_PBR",                      0,                              "deferred", "[test] Cook-Torrance microfacet specular (GGX NDF + Smith-Schlick geometry + Schlick Fresnel), SIMD 8-lights-wide, replacing Blinn-Phong in the vec spec path. Cost/quality probe — only affects pixels that take the vec path (no normal map). F0=0.04 dielectric.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(pbr_roughness,           "FDS_PBR_ROUGHNESS",            0.0f,                           "deferred", "[test] Fixed roughness for --pbr (0..1). 0 = derive per-material from Glossiness (roughness=sqrt(2/(gloss+2))).")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(deferred_unified_tbr,     "FDS_DEFERRED_UNIFIED_TBR",     1,                              "deferred", "Unified transparent + particle TBR. Default on — scenes without a TBR (city, greets) fall through to the legacy peel via the SBufferHead/NumTiles check in deferredUnifiedTbrEnabled().")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (xpar_peel_passes,        "FDS_XPAR_PEEL_PASSES",         1,                              "deferred", "Transparent depth-peel passes PER SIDE (front and back each), so total stacked layers = 2x this. Each pass peels one fragment farther than the last via a per-pixel peel floor, then composites it — fixes >2 overlapping transparents (nested fountain glass orbs sharing one mesh, stacked greets panels) that the single front/back peel drops. 1 = legacy 2-deep (byte-identical). Costs one extra transparent raster+composite pass per (mesh/clump, side) per increment; only transparent area pays.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(deferred_gloss_stats,     "FDS_DEFERRED_GLOSS_STATS",     0,                              "deferred", "Dump per-frame glossiness histogram")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(deferred_tile_stats,      "FDS_DEFERRED_TILE_STATS",      0,                              "deferred", "Dump per-tile lighting cost stats")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(deferred_max_range,      "FDS_DEFERRED_MAX_RANGE",       0.0f,                           "deferred", "Optional Range cap used for culling only (0 = disabled)")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(env_brdf_analytic,        "FDS_ENV_BRDF_ANALYTIC",        0,                              "deferred", "Analytic split-sum env-BRDF (Karis 'Mobile' SIGGRAPH-2014 polynomial fit to the pre-integrated GGX LUT) for the deferred env-specular reflection weight in EnvSpecComposeScalar (greets primary; city OuterVec scalar-fallback lanes). OFF (default) keeps the ad-hoc f90=1-rough Schlick weight (ek=fres*envReflGain), byte-identical. ON replaces it with ek=(f0*A+B)*envReflGain so rough reflections carry the roughness-dependent grazing scale+bias the split-sum requires (energy-correct). Disengages the city glass EnvComposeCityVec8 fast path (routes those lanes to the scalar compose, like --env_ssr) so the analytic form is used everywhere without an AVX2 exp2 reimpl. Needs --env_refl.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(sh_ambient,               "FDS_SH_AMBIENT",               0,                              "deferred", "L2 spherical-harmonic irradiance ambient: replaces the flat scalar Sc->Ambient constant in the deferred surface kernel with a 9-coefficient (27-float) RGB SH irradiance evaluated per-pixel along the shading normal (~9 FMAs/channel, NO texture read). Gives directional colored fill — sky tint from above, ground bounce from below — instead of one flat number, attacking the dark/flat look. At first use SHAmbient_EnsureBaked projects a scene-center environment cube (six faces rendered through the same deferred pipeline the env-reflection probes use) into the coefficients, A_l/pi folded in so a uniform environment evaluates back to its own color (magnitude-comparable to the flat ambient it replaces; SH ringing clamped >=0). The ambient still rides the material Diffuse * albedo multiply and any AO term, exactly like the flat path. OFF (default) is byte-identical to the flat-constant behavior. Applies to the OPAQUE kernel (scalar wave-1 + OuterVec vec-fill and scalar-fallback); transparent surfaces keep the flat ambient. One-shot bake per scene is nondeterministic on greets (shared env-bake root cause) so gate md5 pins with the flag OFF.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(diffuse_energy,           "FDS_DIFFUSE_ENERGY",           0,                              "deferred", "(1-F) diffuse energy conservation: scale the deferred DIFFUSE accumulator by (1 - fres) at the combine, where fres is the SAME per-pixel Schlick Fresnel the env-specular reflection already computed (EnvSpecComposeScalar / EnvComposeCityVec8). Light that reflects specularly (the Fresnel term F) cannot also diffuse, so the canonical dielectric BRDF weights diffuse by (1-F); the engine already scales diffuse by (1-metalness) but skipped (1-F), double-counting at grazing angles (full diffuse AND a strong Fresnel reflection). Fix darkens diffuse subtly at glancing edges (less blown-out grazing highlights) on reflective dielectrics. Only pixels with an env-reflection term (Reflection>0 / metal) pay — pixels with no reflection have no F and are unchanged. Applies to the OPAQUE kernel (scalar wave-1 = greets/fountain; OuterVec vec-fill + both scalar-fallback lanes = city glass); transparent surfaces carry no env term so keep full diffuse. OFF (default) is byte-identical. Needs --env_refl for anything to change.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(pbr_multiscatter,         "FDS_PBR_MULTISCATTER",         0,                              "deferred", "Multi-scatter energy compensation (Fdez-Aguera 2019) for the env-specular reflection. The split-sum env-BRDF (--env_brdf_analytic) is SINGLE-scatter only — it drops the energy returned by repeated microfacet bounces, so rough metals read too dark. Adds it back from the A,B split-sum terms --env_brdf_analytic ALREADY computes in EnvSpecComposeScalar (a few extra ALU ops on reflective pixels only, NO new gather): Ess=A+B (single-scatter energy), Favg=F0+(1-F0)/21 (avg Fresnel), Fms=Favg*Ess/(1-Favg*(1-Ess)) (multi-scatter multiplier), then specular *= 1 + Fms*(1-Ess)/Ess. DEPENDS ON --env_brdf_analytic: it needs the A,B terms, so with --env_brdf_analytic OFF this flag is a NO-OP (the ad-hoc Schlick weight computes no A,B). Only the env-specular term is brightened; the (1-F) diffuse Fresnel handed to --diffuse_energy is left at single-scatter. Applies to the OPAQUE kernel scalar env-compose (--env_brdf_analytic already routes city glass off the OuterVec fast path to this scalar compose). OFF (default) is byte-identical. Needs --env_refl + --env_brdf_analytic to change anything.")

// ─── shadows ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(shadows,                  "FDS_SHADOWS",                  FDS_SHADOWS_DEFAULT_ON,         "shadows",  "Shadow mapping (requires deferred)")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(shadow_polyid,            "FDS_SHADOW_POLYID",            FDS_SHADOW_POLYID_DEFAULT_ON,   "shadows",  "Use PolyId equality test instead of depth comparison")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(shadow_tap_census,       "FDS_SHADOW_TAP_CENSUS",        0,                              "shadows",  "[INSTRUMENT, default OFF, changes no pixel] Per (tile x light-slot) census of the deferred SCALAR light loop, printed to stderr every 8 frame-equivalents. It is the CEILING measurement for the two per-tile shadow early-outs parked in docs/OPTIMIZATION_BACKLOG.md, so neither has to be built to be judged. Columns: DEAD entries = tile-light pairs the list builder admitted for which NOT ONE pixel of the tile passed the per-pixel mirror/dot/range gate -- removing those is byte-null by construction (the pixels contributed nothing), and 'dead prologue px' is the loop-prologue work they cost. tile-UNIFORM taps = cube taps belonging to a (tile,light) entry whose taps ALL resolved fully-lit or ALL fully-shadowed; that sum is what a PERFECT tile-uniformity oracle could replace with one answer, i.e. the ceiling of the byte-AFFECTING classification variant, measured without its approximation error. Scalar path only (--deferred_vec / OuterVec pixels are not counted) -- which is all of greets, where the normal maps force scalar.")
FDS_FLAG_INT (shadow_tap_census_block, "FDS_SHADOW_TAP_CENSUS_BLOCK", 0,                             "shadows",  "[INSTRUMENT] Granularity for --shadow_tap_census's uniformity columns, in PIXELS. 0 (default) = the whole 12x8 lighting tile, which at 1920x1080 is 160x135 px. Any B>0 subdivides each tile into BxB blocks and asks the uniformity question per (block x light) instead. It exists because the tile answer alone cannot tell you whether tile-uniform shadow classification is a bad IDEA or merely a bad GRANULARITY -- sweep B and read where, if anywhere, the uniform fraction becomes worth a classifier. Costs a heap-backed per-thread counter block; census runs only, no effect with --shadow_tap_census off.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(omni_census,             "FDS_OMNI_CENSUS",              0,                              "prof",     "[INSTRUMENT, default OFF, changes no pixel, needs -DFDS_OMNI_CENSUS=ON] Per (pixel x light) census of the deferred SCALAR omni loop -- the companion to --shadow_tap_census, which counts per (tile x light) and therefore cannot say how many lights a PIXEL actually accumulates. Prints, per frame-equivalent: shaded pixels, (px x light) pairs entered, the share of pairs killed at each link of the reject chain (mirrorId / N.L<0 / range / bounce-window portal / spot cone / 2-D map shadow / cube tap / relief horizon), the share that reach the accumulate, and the histogram of live lights per shaded pixel. It is the denominator every omni-loop ceiling needs: a lever that removes work per ENTERED pair and one that removes work per ACCUMULATED pair are priced against numbers that differ by ~4x at greets. Scalar path only (--deferred_vec / OuterVec pixels are not counted), which is all of greets and fountain.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(shadow_polyid_no_pcf,     "FDS_SHADOW_POLYID_NO_PCF",     0,                              "shadows",  "Disable 2x2 PCF for the PolyId cube path — use a single nearest-neighbor tap instead. Diagnostic: lets you see how much of the intra-mesh material-boundary darkening comes from PCF blurring the artifact across 4 taps vs being intrinsic to the matID-vs-receiver identity test. With PCF the artifact spreads ~2 px; without PCF it shrinks to a 1 px sharp transition (but doesn't go away).")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(shadow_backface_cull,     "FDS_SHADOW_BACKFACE_CULL",     0,                              "shadows",  "Cull back-facing tris from shadow maps")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(shadow_validate,          "FDS_SHADOW_VALIDATE",          0,                              "shadows",  "Capture clipper outputs outside the input triangle hull")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(shadow_prof,              "FDS_SHADOW_PROF",              0,                              "shadows",  "Per-light shadow timing")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(shadow_bake_time,         "FDS_SHADOW_BAKE_TIME",         0,                              "shadows",  "Per-frame total wall-clock of the DYNAMIC shadow-map bake stage (Render_DeferredShadowMaps in a per-frame mode; the init StaticOnce bake is excluded). One [SHADOW-BAKE] line averaged over FDS_SHADOW_PROF_INTERVAL frames (default 60). Coarser than --shadow-prof (which splits xform/raster per light); use this for the single 'how long is the dynamic bake' number.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(shadow_prof_cache,        "FDS_SHADOW_PROF_CACHE",        0,                              "shadows",  "Per-frame shadow-sample cache-line transition stats")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(shadow_cone_cull,         "FDS_SHADOW_CONE_CULL",         0,                              "shadows",  "Mesh-bsphere-vs-spot-cone cull during shadow-pass Transform_Objects")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(shadow_skip_animated,     "FDS_SHADOW_SKIP_ANIMATED",     0,                              "shadows",  "Static shadow bake: skip meshes whose Pos spline has more than one key. Off by default — many scenes (greets) have *every* mesh animated, so skipping would leave shadow maps empty. Enable when the scene has real static occluders + a separate dynamic pass for moving meshes.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(shadow_dynamic,           "FDS_SHADOW_DYNAMIC",           0,                              "shadows",  "Per-frame dynamic-mesh shadow pass. For each static omni, re-renders only animated meshes into a parallel dynamic shadow buffer; lighting kernel samples min(static, dynamic). Pair with --shadow-skip-animated so the static bake is dynamic-mesh-free. Off by default — costs an extra Transform_Objects + raster per static omni per frame.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(shadow_fzp_mult,         "FDS_SHADOW_FZP_MULT",          3.0f,                           "shadows",  "Shadow camera FZP multiplier over light IRange")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(shadow_gbuffer_overlap,   "FDS_SHADOW_GBUFFER_OVERLAP",   0,                              "shadows",  "Stage A: run the per-frame dynamic shadow bake (Render_DeferredShadowMaps) on its own orchestrator thread concurrently with the gbuffer fill, joining before deferred lighting. Off by default — validate with FDS_THREADS=1 byte-identity + the 12-concurrent-bench STEADY-count (TSan-blind race class) before defaulting on. See docs/THREADING_OVERLAP_PLAN.md.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(vertex_light_parallel,   "FDS_VERTEX_LIGHT_PARALLEL",    1,                              "render",   "Fan the forward per-vertex Lighting() pass across the thread pool, one task per mesh (writes are per-mesh Verts -> disjoint; math untouched -> byte-identical to serial). The pool is parked during the scene tick, so this is free parallelism (~1 ms serial on greets). --no-vertex_light_parallel restores the serial walk.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(bake_tick_overlap,       "FDS_BAKE_TICK_OVERLAP",        0,                              "shadows",  "Dispatch the per-frame dynamic shadow bake BEFORE the serial tick chunk (main Transform + vertex Lighting + Radix) instead of after it, joining before Render — the bake's pool tasks run while the tick thread does its serial work (complementary resources, unlike Stage A's pool-vs-pool overlap which measured net-negative). Skipped while the mirror shatter is active (fresh shard meshes would race their first VertexScratch clone-init copy) and for the first frames (clone warmup). Mutually exclusive with --shadow-gbuffer-overlap. Default off pending the concurrent-bench STEADY validation (TSan-blind race neighborhood).")
#if FDS_DEV
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(dump_shadowmap,           "FDS_DUMP_SHADOWMAP",           0,                              "shadows-dev", "[dev] Dump shadow map to PGM on first frame")
#endif
FDS_FLAG_INT(shadow_bias,               "FDS_SHADOW_BIAS",              512,                            "shadows",  "Constant Z bias for shadow comparison")
FDS_FLAG_INT(shadow_slope_bias,         "FDS_SHADOW_SLOPE_BIAS",        1024,                           "shadows",  "Slope-scale Z bias for shadow comparison")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(shadow_lightmap,          "FDS_SHADOW_LIGHTMAP",          0,                              "shadows",  "Pre-bake per-face shadow factor for static omnis into TriMesh::staticShadowLM at scene init; deferred kernel reads the lightmap instead of doing a per-pixel cube-shadow tap on static meshes. Off by default — bake is opt-in until kernel hookup ships.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(shadow_lightmap_res,       "FDS_SHADOW_LIGHTMAP_RES",      16,                             "shadows",  "Per-face lightmap edge length N (atlas is N×N per face). Defaults to 16 → 256 texels/face/omni. Powers of two recommended.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(shadow_lightmap_viz,       "FDS_SHADOW_LIGHTMAP_VIZ",      0,                              "shadows",  "Debug viz mode: render lightmap data instead of normal lighting. 0 = off; 1 = lightmap meshLMId (greyscale, 0=red); 2 = faceIdx (color hash); 3 = bary s (red→green); 4 = bary t (red→blue); 5 = lightmap factor for omni 0 nearest-neighbor (greyscale); 9 = bary world delta: |interp(F.A/B/C->Pos, runtime bary) - pixel world from depth|. Green ≤0.1, yellow ≤0.5, orange ≤1.0, red >1.0. Tests whether bary points to the pixel's actual 3D position on F's plane.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(shadow_lightmap_recompute_bake, "FDS_SHADOW_LIGHTMAP_RECOMPUTE_BAKE", 0,                    "shadows",  "Debug: in resolveCubeAtten, skip the atlas bilinear lookup and call SampleStaticCubeAtWorld(pixelWorldPos) per pixel — same code the bake uses. Verifies the bake function matches CubeShadow_Sample. (Has been bit-perfect since the depth-compare bake landed.)")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(shadow_lightmap_recompute_at_bary, "FDS_SHADOW_LIGHTMAP_RECOMPUTE_AT_BARY", 0,               "shadows",  "Debug: same as --shadow-lightmap-recompute-bake but sample at the world position the RUNTIME-STORED bary points to (interpolate face A/B/C world from the stamped sB/tB), not at the rendered pixel's world position. Mismatch with recompute-bake means the runtime bary doesn't correspond to the pixel's actual world position.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(shadow_lightmap_nearest,  "FDS_SHADOW_LIGHTMAP_NEAREST",  0,                              "shadows",  "Debug: use sampleNearest instead of sampleBilinear for the per-face atlas lookup. Helps separate bilinear cross-edge blend artifacts (then nearest gives crisp blocky shadow) from atlas-content artifacts (then nearest shows the same shape just sharper).")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(shadow_lightmap_planar,   "FDS_SHADOW_LIGHTMAP_PLANAR",   0,                              "shadows",  "Bake + sample the per-face atlas in a world-axis-aligned planar projection (Quake/idTech trick) instead of the per-triangle (B-A, C-A) bary basis. Eliminates the sheared-lattice zig-zag on shadow edges that hits the bary atlas when the face isn't right-isoceles with axis-aligned legs. Picks the projection plane from the face's dominant cardinal axis (max |N.x/y/z|). Runtime sampler uses the pixel's world position instead of stamped bary.")

// ─── SoA vertex refactor (Phase 1+) ──────────────────────────────────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(soa_verify,               "FDS_SOA_VERIFY",               0,                              "soa",      "Verification: in Transform_Objects, after per-vertex transform, assert that the AoS-written and SoA-written values match bit-for-bit. ~free when off; quadratic-ish cost per vert when on. Default off; turn on during migration to catch drift between AoS and SoA write paths.")

// ─── rasterizer ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(rast_full_store,          "FDS_RAST_FULL_STORE",          1,                              "raster",   "When all 8 lanes of a tile row pass edge+Z, take a vector-store fast path. Mekalele: one wide store per G-buffer plane (normal/tangent/lightmapST/lightmapMF/shadowMatID) instead of an 8-lane scatter. ShadowBarry: direct compressed-Z store + broadcast polyId store instead of blendv RMW + per-lane scatter.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(rast_inside_template,     "FDS_RAST_INSIDE_TEMPLATE",     1,                              "raster",   "Mekalele rasterizer: dispatch apply_exact<true> for tiles the dispatcher proves are fully inside the triangle (conservative min_a/b/c test), eliding the per-row edge mask.")

// ─── sort ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(no_sort,                  "FDS_NO_SORT",                  0,                              "sort",     "Bypass Radix_SortingASM — dispatch FList in transform order (correctness depends on the rasterizer being order-independent)")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(tile_bbox_cull,           "FDS_TILE_BBOX_CULL",           1,                              "sort",     "S2/B5 per-tile face pre-reject: at FList-build time stamp each face's projected screen bbox into its FListEntry; the per-tile rasterizer walk (RenderInnerMekalele/RenderInner) 4-compare-rejects faces whose bbox misses the tile rect BEFORE dereferencing the Face — a rejected face costs only the sequential FListEntry read (skips the 3 scattered Vertex flag loads + the clipper entry). PURE reject (the clipper already clips to the tile → byte-identical output; near-plane-straddling faces keep the cover-all sentinel and are never rejected). Default ON; --no-tile_bbox_cull restores the un-culled walk for A/B + the byte-null baseline. General win at the 400k-poly poses (city/chase/displaced greets); scales with face count.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(needle_cull,              "FDS_NEEDLE_CULL",              1,                              "sort",     "DEGENERATE-FACE PRE-REJECT at FList-build time: a face whose own projected screen determinant already sits at or below the value EVERY rasterizer rejects a fan triangle at (`fabs(det) <= 0.01f` — Mekalele.h, TheOtherBarry.h, ShadowMap.cpp, all three verbatim) is never pushed, so it costs no FList slot, no sort key, no per-tile walk entry and no FrustumClipper::Render. WHAT IT REMOVES: collinear authored needles (city's 1575 zero-normal verts are 525 of them — 102 units long, 1.2e-6 thick; 2026-08-16v) plus edge-on quads and sub-pixel slivers, which together were measured at 818 rasterizer rejects/frame in city t=1961 (1.46 %% of its 55 929 triangle setups) and 2 302 in chase t=100 (6.0 %% of 38 512). BYTE-NULL BY CONSTRUCTION, and the argument is the same one --tile_bbox_cull rests on: the clipper and the mip subdivision only ever partition or SHRINK the projected triangle, so every fan triangle either pass can produce has |det| no larger than the face's own — if the face is at or under the reject threshold, so is every triangle derived from it, in every tile and in every pass. Applied ONLY when all three verts are in FRONT of the pass's near plane (behind-near PX/PY are stale — the projection skips the divide there — so those faces are always kept, exactly as the bbox stamp keeps them) and never to sprite/flare faces (A == B, where C is a float). The test rides on loads the push already does (the bbox stamp reads the same six PX/PY and three z), so its cost is ~6 flops on a face the loop has already accepted. Live at all three FList builders: Transform_Objects (main, shadow and every offscreen/bake pass) and the hand-written mirror transforms in CHASE.CPP / CITY.CPP Reflected_Transform. WHAT IT IS NOT: a load-time collinearity scan. `-DFDS_NEEDLE_CENSUS=1` classifies every walked face in OBJECT space too, and the two populations barely overlap — chase has ZERO collinear faces and city's 182-203 a pass are never PUSHED (a zero-area face has a zero normal, so its backface test is `0 < 0`), so a load-time cull would have removed nothing outside greets' shadow bake (724 pushes, where backface culling is off by design). PRICE (measured, 1512x848, min AND median of 16 interleaved rounds/arm against a floor arm): the row that carries the work moves — chase t=100 gbuffer **-1.60 %% instructions (exact, every round) and -0.30 ms (-3.7 %% median, -4.9 %% min) against a +0.5 %% / -0.3 %% floor**, chase t=800 -0.60 %% instructions; the FRAME does not resolve it (renderFrame -0.13 %% instructions, and its wall delta flips sign between two ladders at the same pose). city -0.07 %% and greets 0.00 %% frame instructions — nothing measurable, nothing lost. Default ON because it is a strict work-remover that is byte-null by construction and loses nowhere; --no-needle_cull restores the un-culled push. See docs/OPTIMIZATION_BACKLOG.md 2026-08-16x.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(face_tile_bin,            "FDS_FACE_TILE_BIN",            1,                              "sort",     "Face->raster-tile binning for the opaque tile pass: instead of every one of the 30 tiles re-walking the whole FList and 4-compare-rejecting (--tile_bbox_cull), renderFrame walks the list TWICE (count, then scatter) and hands each tile a dense, sequential Face* run. Order inside a tile is preserved EXACTLY (chunk-major prefix sum over a contiguous split of FList), so it is a pure restatement of the bbox reject and byte-identical; requires --tile_bbox_cull (with that off the walk rejects nothing and every tile legitimately sees every face). Skipped below 3 000 faces, where the two pool dispatches cost more than they save. Default ON; --no-face_tile_bin restores the per-tile walk for A/B.")

// ─── transparency ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(no_quad_sort,             "FDS_NO_QUAD_SORT",             0,                              "xpar",     "Skip quad reorder during sort")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(xpar_force_twosided,      "FDS_XPAR_FORCE_TWOSIDED",      0,                              "xpar",     "Force transparent fills to be two-sided")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(no_xpar_frontback,        "FDS_NO_XPAR_FRONTBACK",        0,                              "xpar",     "Skip front/back separation for two-sided xpar")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(no_xpar_objgroup,         "FDS_NO_XPAR_OBJGROUP",         0,                              "xpar",     "Skip per-object grouping of xpar faces")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(xpar_light_all,           "FDS_XPAR_LIGHT_ALL",           0,                              "xpar",     "Run every omni on every xpar pixel (vs per-strip)")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(xpar_strip_extent,        "FDS_XPAR_STRIP_EXTENT",        1,                              "xpar",     "Bound each unified-TBR transparent clump's layer CLEAR and its deferred COMPOSITE to the tile-column extent that clump's own raster touched, instead of the full strip width. BYTE-NULL by construction: the clear happens over exactly the columns the previous raster dirtied (every other column is already in its cleared state), and the composite kernel's first test is `mat32 == 0xFFFFFFFF -> continue`, so the skipped columns contributed nothing. WHY IT EXISTS: a clump flushes on every (mesh, side) change AND on every interleaved sprite, so fountain t=1200 issues 3224 flushes/frame; each flush was clearing and re-scanning all 1920x8 px of its strip, x4 peel passes = 198 M px scanned a frame for ~0.5 %% live fragments. Turn OFF to A/B it or to bisect a suspected xpar composite bug.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(xpar_peel_early_out,      "FDS_XPAR_PEEL_EARLY_OUT",      1,                              "xpar",     "Stop a transparent clump's reverse depth peel as soon as a pass leaves its layer Z untouched. BYTE-NULL by construction: pass N's accept mask is `(z_candidate < z_existing) & (z_candidate > peelFloor)` with z_existing pre-cleared to 0xFFFF and peelFloor = pass N-1's layer Z, so if pass N-1 committed nothing its whole extent is still 0xFFFF, the two halves of that conjunction cannot both hold, and no later pass can commit anything either (each inherits an untouched layer in turn). A committed fragment can never store 0xFFFF — pass 0 already requires z_candidate < 0xFFFF — so 'all 0xFFFF' is an exact 'nothing here', not a heuristic. WHY IT EXISTS: --xpar-peel-passes is a per-SCENE constant (fountain sets 4) but a clump is a run of consecutive same-(mesh, side) faces between interleaved sprites, and those almost never stack in depth: at fountain t=1200, peel passes 1-3 are 100.0 %% / 100.0 %% / 100.0 %% empty on the front layer and 99.9 %% / 100.0 %% / 100.0 %% on the back, i.e. 9663 of 12884 raster+composite passes a frame produce nothing. Turn OFF to A/B it.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(xpar_tile_lights,         "FDS_XPAR_TILE_LIGHTS",         1,                              "xpar",     "CORRECTNESS FIX, default ON. Make the TRANSPARENT deferred lighting kernel resolve its per-tile light list from the PIXEL rather than from its dispatch ordinal. THE BUG: the transparent composite is dispatched by RENDER.CPP's runTilePass over renderFrame's 6x5 FRAME tiler (NT = 30 tiles, tileSizeY = (YRes+4)/5 & ~7 = 168 rows at 848), and handed that ordinal straight to Render_DeferredTransparentLighting_Tile, which used it to subscript ctx.tileLights[] — an array built by buildTileLightLists on the DEFERRED 12x8 LIGHTING grid (DEFERRED_NUM_TILES = 96, tileSizeY = 106). Two different grids, one subscript. It is always in bounds (30 < 96) so nothing ever asserted, faulted or looked wrong in a stack trace; the only symptom is that every transparent pixel is lit with the omni/spot list culled for a DIFFERENT REGION OF THE SCREEN. The mapping is 6x5 tile (i,j) -> 12x8 tile ((6j+i)/12, (6j+i)%12), i.e. frame-tile rows 0 and 1 both read lighting row 0, rows 2 and 3 both read lighting row 1, row 4 reads row 2 — constant within a frame-tile row, so the error lands as HORIZONTAL BANDS 168 px tall that are fixed in SCREEN space and move with nothing. Opaque surfaces are unaffected (they are dispatched on the lighting grid itself), so the only thing in the demo that shows it is a large transparent surface: the greets mirror. Lights that should have been culled for the mirror's own tile get applied to it (too bright, a flat additive haze) and lights that should have lit it are missing. OFF restores the old ordinal subscript verbatim for A/B. A 6x5 frame tile is 248x168 px against the 12x8 grid's 128x106, so one dispatch rect straddles several light tiles and the lookup is per pixel — two integer divides on the transparent path only, which the kernel's own comment already scopes as 'transparent coverage is small, scalar is fine'.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(xpar_extent_census,       "FDS_XPAR_EXTENT_CENSUS",       0,                            "xpar",     "Per-frame census of the unified-TBR xpar clump composite: flushes, peel passes, pixels a full-width strip scan would cover, pixels the xpar_strip_extent bound covers, and pixels that actually carry a live transparent fragment. Diagnostic only (it adds its own scan, so do not read frame times from a census run).")
// ── screen-space refraction for transparent glass ──
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(glass_refract,           "FDS_GLASS_REFRACT",            0.0f,                           "xpar",     "Screen-space refraction for transparent glass: warp the opaque background behind a transparent surface by the surface relief. Per glass pixel the transparent kernel Snell-refracts the view ray through the (normal-map-perturbed) surface normal and samples the ALREADY-COMPOSITED opaque scene (VPage, or g_hdrBuf under --hdr — whichever the peel writes) at the refracted ray's screen projection instead of straight through. 0 = OFF (byte-identical, zero cost). Value = offset strength (1 ~= the physical angular bend, >1 exaggerates the warp). Fresnel-weighted (more refraction head-on, less at grazing). Needs a transparent surface (Transparency>0 / XparBlendAlpha); relief comes from the material NormalMap sampled via an on-the-fly tangent (no NormalMap -> flat facets refract uniformly). Water (city) and mirror-mask walls (XparBlendAlpha<0) are skipped.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(glass_refract_ior,       "FDS_GLASS_REFRACT_IOR",        1.5f,                           "xpar",     "glass_refract index of refraction for the Snell bend (air=1.0). 1.5 ~= glass; higher bends the refracted ray more. Also sets the Schlick Fresnel F0 = ((ior-1)/(ior+1))^2 that weights refraction vs grazing reflection (the offset is scaled by 1-Fresnel).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(glass_refract_max,       "FDS_GLASS_REFRACT_MAX",        48.0f,                          "xpar",     "glass_refract maximum background sample offset in PIXELS (per-axis clamp), so strong relief or a high IOR can't sample wildly off the glass. Scale with resolution.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(glass_refract_rough_blur,"FDS_GLASS_REFRACT_ROUGH_BLUR", 0.0f,                           "xpar",     "glass_refract frosted-glass blur: radius in PIXELS of a 5-tap plus-shaped blur of the refracted background fetch, scaled per pixel by the material RoughnessMap (white = rough = blurrier). 0 = sharp refraction (single tap). Needs glass_refract>0.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(glass_test,               "FDS_GLASS_TEST",               0,                              "xpar",     "[test] Configure the fountain's central 'mizraka glass' sphere and the spire orbs ('f_sphere'/'in shpere') as relief glass at scene init (bakes a relief normal map from each material's OWN diffuse via BakeNormalMapFromDiffuse, so it shares the diffuse's dimensions + swizzle) so --glass_refract has a normal-mapped transparent surface with the fountain scene behind it to warp. Default OFF (materials byte-unchanged). Fountain scene only.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(xpar_pbr,                 "FDS_XPAR_PBR",                 0,                              "xpar",     "PBR shading for TRANSPARENT surfaces: the transparent lighting kernel applies the material's full PBR stack like the opaque kernel does, so 'material X at 50%% transparency' keeps X's look instead of degrading to a flat transparent texture. Per pixel (when the material carries the map): NormalMap perturbs the shading normal for ambient/diffuse/specular (Mikkelsen on-the-fly tangent — the xpar G-buffer has no tangent plane; same LOD fade as opaque), AoMap / albedo-alpha AO attenuates the ambient term (x --ao_map_strength x Material::AoStrength), RoughnessMap modulates specular intensity (x --roughness_strength), MetallicMap kills diffuse + tints specular by the albedo (env-reflection for transparent metals is NOT wired — no env term in the xpar composite). Only the LIT inputs change; the alpha/additive blend math is untouched. Respects the same map gates as opaque (--no-nmap/--ao_map/--roughness_map/--metal_map). Water and HDR-mirror panels keep their dedicated paths. Default OFF = byte-identical.")
#if FDS_DEV
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(debug_xpar_ids,           "FDS_DEBUG_XPAR_IDS",           0,                              "xpar-dev", "[dev] Colorize transparent fills by object id")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(debug_xpar_min_area,     "FDS_DEBUG_XPAR_MIN_AREA",      0.0f,                           "xpar-dev", "[dev] Min triangle area for xpar debug coloring")
#endif

// ─── profiling ablation gates ────────────────────────────────────────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(prof_no_tex,              "FDS_PROF_NO_TEX",              0,                              "prof",     "Skip texture sample in deferred kernel")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(prof_no_lights,           "FDS_PROF_NO_LIGHTS",           0,                              "prof",     "Skip omni loop in deferred kernel")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(prof_no_spec,             "FDS_PROF_NO_SPEC",             0,                              "prof",     "Skip specular in deferred kernel")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(prof_no_fog,              "FDS_PROF_NO_FOG",              0,                              "prof",     "Skip fog pass")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(prof_no_vertex_light,     "FDS_PROF_NO_VERTEX_LIGHT",     0,                              "prof",     "MEASUREMENT ONLY, CHANGES PIXELS: make the per-vertex forward Lighting() pass (FDS/RENDER/Lighting.cpp, the PROF_LGHT section) a no-op after the one-shot StaticLighting. Exists because LGHT has never had an ablation: on city under --deferred it is ~10 %% of the frame, and the deferred kernel does not read Vertex::L* at all — only the FORWARD rasterizer (TheOtherBarry: transparents, sprites, water, the forward mirror-pass glass) does. This gate prices the whole pass so the 'which meshes still need it' question has a ceiling before anyone builds the per-mesh predicate.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(prof_no_cube_tap,         "FDS_PROF_NO_CUBE_TAP",         0,                              "prof",     "Diagnostic: skip the per-pixel cube-shadow tap in the deferred lighting kernel — resolveCubeAtten short-circuits to 1.0 (fully lit). Lets you measure how much of the receiver-side shadow cost is the tap itself vs the surrounding lighting math. Does NOT skip the shadow render (cube bake); only the runtime sample.")
// --- geometry front-end (Transform_Objects) profiling, XFRM campaign ---
FDS_FLAG_INT(xfrm_prof,                 "FDS_XFRM_PROF",                0,                              "prof",     "Fine-grained breakdown of the MAIN-VIEW Transform_Objects call (FDS/RENDER/Transform.cpp), printed to stderr as an average every N main-view frames. 0 = OFF (no clock reads, byte-null). Buckets: SETUP (per-mesh matrix/bsphere/frustum work for meshes that survive to the vertex loop), VERT (the per-vertex transform loops), SOA (the VertexFrame_DumpFromAoS AoS->SoA sweep), FACE (the per-face visibility + SortZ + FList build + tile-bbox stamp loop), OTHER (= TOTAL - the four, i.e. mesh culls that never reach the vertex loop, plus the omni/particle epilogue). Also reports meshes/verts/facesTested/facesPushed per frame. Instrumentation cost is ~5 steady_clock reads per surviving mesh (~30 us/frame at greets' ~250 meshes), charged to TOTAL and to whichever bucket each read closes; treat sub-100-us differences as instrument noise.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(xfrm_pass_prof,            "FDS_XFRM_PASS_PROF",           0,                              "prof",     "PER-PASS geometry front-end census, printed to stderr every N MAIN-VIEW frames. NEEDS A CENSUS BUILD (cmake -DFDS_VIS_CENSUS=ON); inert in the shipping build, where the instrument is compiled out entirely because merely carrying its never-taken branches inside Transform_Objects moved pixels under -ffp-contract=fast (docs/VISIBILITY_PLAN.md 8a). --xfrm_prof is main-view-only by construction (unsynchronised scalars); this one runs in EVERY Transform_Objects call and attributes ms / meshes / verts / faces to four pass kinds: MAIN (the on-axis camera pass), MIRROR-RTT (any pass with g_offAxisFrustumCull set: the serial mirror RTT bakes and the parallel shard pass), SHADOW (g_inShadowPass: the static + dynamic bakes and every omni cube face), OFFSCREEN (everything else inside an OffscreenViewScope: env / SH probe faces, shatter). Counters are atomic (shadow + shard passes are threaded), so the numbers are exact but the ms of THREADED passes is summed wall-clock across workers, not elapsed frame time - read SHADOW as core-ms, MAIN and MIRROR-RTT (serial) as frame-ms. The dump fires at the end of the MAIN call, so offscreen passes that run after it in the tick land in the next window; over N frames that is a constant off-by-one, not a bias. 0 = OFF, no clock reads, byte-null.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(mirror_cull_census,        "FDS_MIRROR_CULL_CENSUS",       0,                              "prof",     "MIRROR-CLONE SPLIT CEILING census, printed every N main-view frames. NEEDS A CENSUS BUILD (cmake -DFDS_VIS_CENSUS=ON) - see xfrm_pass_prof. A GreetsMirror clone is the WHOLE mirrored room in ONE TriMesh, so its bsphere is room-sized and the mesh-level frustum cull can never reject it — the same disease the greets Piramid chunk split cured for the source wall (clones are hidden inside the RTT bakes, so this is purely a MAIN-VIEW cost). This census changes no geometry: it re-runs the SAME sphere-vs-frustum test the mesh cull runs, once per per-source-mesh sub-range of each clone (tight AABB-centre + max-radius spheres, exactly the construction the Piramid split uses), and reports how many clone verts a split would let the existing cull reject. That fraction IS the ceiling for splitting the clone. 0 = OFF, nothing computed, byte-null.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(mirror_cull_census_cell, "FDS_MIRROR_CULL_CENSUS_CELL",  0.0f,                           "prof",     "--mirror_cull_census granularity (census build only). 0 (default) = one sub-sphere per SOURCE MESH, i.e. what the cheapest possible clone split (one clone TriMesh per source mesh) would deliver — capped by the fact that only greets's Piramid is chunked at source, so the statues / ceiling / robot arrive as a few room-sized ranges. > 0 = near-cubic SPATIAL cells of this many world units over the WHOLE clone, ignoring source-mesh boundaries: the granularity a real spatial split would have. Cells are built once from the primed clone and are NOT refreshed per frame (a cell's verts are not a contiguous range), so treat the dynamic robot's share as approximate. Diagnostic only; does not change what is rendered.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(xfrm_pass_mesh_prof,       "FDS_XFRM_PASS_MESH_PROF",      0,                              "prof",     "PER-MESH / PER-MATERIAL decomposition of the --xfrm_pass_prof census, printed alongside it every N main-view frames. NEEDS A CENSUS BUILD (cmake -DFDS_VIS_CENSUS=ON); compiled out of the shipping build for the same -ffp-contract=fast codegen reason (docs/VISIBILITY_PLAN.md 8). --xfrm_pass_prof answers WHICH PASS dominates; this answers WHAT IS IN IT, which is the question a caster-flattening decision turns on. Attribution is per TriMesh (grouped to the authored object name, so Piramid.lwo:cNN chunks and momy.lwo::body bins fold back together) via a lock-free pointer-keyed table - shadow passes transform on 12 workers and a mutex there would serialise them. The MATERIAL table is computed at DUMP time from each mesh face->Txtr histogram and attributes that mesh transformed verts to its materials in proportion to face count, so a chunk straddling rooms/floor is split rather than mis-assigned. 0 = OFF, byte-null.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(xfrm_soa_inline,          "FDS_XFRM_SOA_INLINE",          1,                            "render",   "SoA Phase 2a: the per-vertex transform loops store TPos_x/y/z + PY straight into the mesh's VertexFrame SoA arrays as they go, instead of the Phase-1 post-pass sweep (VertexFrame_DumpFromAoS) that walks the whole Vertex array a SECOND time and re-reads 16 of its 136 bytes per vertex. BIT-EXACT BY CONSTRUCTION - same values from the same source, stored one loop earlier; the VertexFrame's contents are identical, so every consumer (SortZ, IsFrontFacingInViewSpace, QuadAwareMaxViewZ, the shadow depth sort) sees byte-identical input. Verify with --soa-verify (audits the inline stores against the AoS the sweep would have copied). The win scales with vertex count: the sweep is a second pass over the mesh's cache lines, which is free while the mesh fits in L2 and expensive once it doesn't. MEASURED greets t=5780 --greets_displace, 1920x1080, per-frame min over 24: main-view Transform_Objects 7.93 -> 5.96 ms (-1.97, -25%); the SOA bucket goes 2.38 -> 0.00 and VERT rises 4.00 -> 4.41 (the inline stores). Flat scenes see the same mechanism at their own scale (tenths of a ms). DEFAULT ON since 2026-08-05: city pin 37e62845 exact, fountain pin 51fff7cd exact, greets t=1588 and chase 5+2 poses byte-identical off-vs-on, --soa-verify clean on all four scenes. --no-xfrm_soa_inline restores the Phase-1 sweep for A/B.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(xfrm_rcp,                  "FDS_XFRM_RCP",                 0,                              "render",   "Per-vertex 1/z in the projection. 0 = TODAY'S ARITHMETIC EXACTLY: the Inside loop divides in single precision, the Ahead/Regular loops write `1.0/z` which promotes the float to DOUBLE, divides, and narrows back. 1 = single-precision divide at every site (removes the double promotion; differs from 0 only in the rare double-rounding cases, so it moves pixels only where those land on a raster boundary). 2 = NEON reciprocal ESTIMATE plus one Newton-Raphson step (a real approximation - ~22 bits, so PX/PY shift sub-pixel on every projected vertex in the demo). MEASURE BEFORE ENABLING: the ablation that removes the ENTIRE projection block (1/z + PX/PY + their stores; UZ/VZ were part of it until they were deleted as dead, 2026-08-06, --xfrm_ablate=2) is worth only 0.55 ms of a 4.0 ms per-vertex loop at greets t=5780, so any reciprocal-only change is bounded well below that - the loop is cache-line-bound on the 140-byte Vertex, not divide-bound. Applies to the three SIMD loops (Inside/Ahead/Regular) plus ERegular.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(xfrm_ablate,               "FDS_XFRM_ABLATE",              0,                              "prof",     "DIAGNOSTIC ABLATION BITMASK for the main-view geometry front-end. CHANGES PIXELS - measurement only, never ship. Read together with --xfrm_prof, and attribute cost from the PER-BUCKET counter delta (not the frame time, which the ablations also perturb downstream). 1 = skip the per-vertex TN/TTangent transforms (2 of the 3 per-vertex mat-vecs). 2 = skip the per-vertex projection block (1/z + PX/PY). 4 = skip the AoS->SoA VertexFrame dump sweep. 8 = per-face loop runs the visibility/backface test then continues (no reflective stamp, no SortZ, no FList push, no bbox). 16 = per-face loop continues immediately (pure loop + Face walk overhead). 32 = skip the whole per-face loop. 64 = skip the per-face tile-bbox stamp (the 3 random Vertex derefs for PX/PY/TPos_z) and write the cover-all box instead. 128 = skip the per-vertex loops (census build only; complement of 32, so the two bracket the function's two halves). --- SoA PHASE 5 PRICING LADDER (census build only, 2026-08-16s; docs/SOA_VERTEX_REFACTOR.md): replicas of the per-vertex loop that differ ONLY in WHERE the read and the write land, so a layout split can be priced before it is built. SHADOW pass: 256 = control, read the per-light clone Vertex (140 B stride) and write it (this is what ships); 512 = read the clone, write a DENSE 32 B/vert array; 1024 = read the SHARED T->Verts instead of the clone; 2048 = read a compact SHARED 12 B/vert Pos array. MAIN view (run with --xfrm_par=0 or --xfrm_prof has no buckets): 4096 = control, read + write T->Verts; 8192 = write a dense 64 B/vert array; 16384 = also read a compact 36 B/vert in-array. Combine with 32 to isolate the vertex loop from the face loop. Measured verdict: 512 alone costs +16 %, 1024 alone +33 %, 2048 alone 0 %, and only 512|1024 together pays (-31 %) - the variable is stream count and per-stream footprint, NOT sizeof(Vertex).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(quarter_normal_cos,      "FDS_QUARTER_NORMAL_COS",       0.95f,                          "deferred", "Quarter-rate fill: minimum dot(N_center, N_neighbor) for the neighbor's shaded value to be averaged in. Wave-2 currently only checks matID equality; that misses curved surfaces (same hull material, different normals → different shading) and creates the cartoonish look on the robot. Threshold ~0.95 ≈ 18°. Lower → softer (more averaging, more artifacts). Set to 0 to disable the check entirely (legacy behavior).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(quarter_z_jump,          "FDS_QUARTER_Z_JUMP",           0.04f,                          "deferred", "Quarter-rate fill: maximum |zEnc_center - zEnc_neighbor| / zEnc_center accepted before falling back to full shading. Detects silhouettes / material creases (which produce sharp depth steps even when both sides share matID and normal). Default 0.04 ≈ 4% relative depth change. 0 disables the check.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(nmap_lod_fade_start,       "FDS_NMAP_LOD_FADE_START",      2,                              "deferred", "Normal map LOD fade: the miplevel at which bump strength FIRES — at this mip the bump is already scaled by (1 - step). Below this mip, full bump (1.0). Above, drops by step per mip until 0. At distance the texture mip chain averages cleanly, but the normal map's per-mip averaged direction has shorter length → bump becomes high-frequency lighting noise. Set to a very high value (e.g. 16) to disable the fade. Use --nmap-lod-fade-start=0 --nmap-lod-fade-step=1 to fully disable the bump everywhere (useful to verify the path is firing).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(nmap_lod_fade_step,      "FDS_NMAP_LOD_FADE_STEP",       0.33f,                          "deferred", "Normal map LOD fade rate per miplevel past --nmap-lod-fade-start. 0.33 → bump fades to zero over 3 mip levels. Lower = gentler fade.")

FDS_FLAG_INT(frame_tile_x,              "FDS_FRAME_TILE_X",             6,                              "render",   "FRAME RASTER TILE GRID, columns. renderFrame splits the screen into frame_tile_x * frame_tile_y rectangles and enqueues one G-buffer/forward raster job per rectangle (RENDER.CPP); the same grid bounds the transparent peel's per-batch composite (runTilePass) and is what --face_tile_bin bins faces into. DO NOT CONFUSE IT WITH THE 12x8 DEFERRED LIGHTING GRID (DEFERRED_NUM_TILES_X/Y in DeferredCommon.h) — they are separate grids, and subscripting one with the other's ordinal is the bug --xpar_tile_lights fixes. Default 6x5 = 30 tiles (1512x848 -> 248x168 px). Tile sizes are rounded DOWN to a multiple of 8 and the last row/column absorbs the remainder, so every INTERIOR boundary stays 8-aligned at any grid — which is what keeps adjacent clipper workers off the same 8x8 blendv RMW (see the comment at the top of renderFrame). WHY IT IS A KNOB: `gbuffer` runs at effPar ~5.5 of 12 workers on chase — half the pool idles waiting on the heaviest tile. A finer grid trades per-tile fixed cost for load balance. NOT BYTE-NULL off the default: tile boundaries move, so faces are rasterized in a different ORDER and the per-tile mip hysteresis / mirror-mask centroid gate see different sequences — expect seam-local pixel differences. Clamped to [1, 24].")
FDS_FLAG_INT(frame_tile_y,              "FDS_FRAME_TILE_Y",             5,                              "render",   "FRAME RASTER TILE GRID, rows. See --frame_tile_x. Default 5. Clamped to [1, 24].")

// ─── normals / textures ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(surf_smoothing_authored,  "FDS_SURF_SMOOTHING_AUTHORED",  0,                              "render",   "Honor the AUTHORED per-surface smoothing angle when MakeFacesIndependent rebuilds vertex normals, instead of the hardcoded global 30 deg crease + 'momy' special-case. Each surface's Material::MaxSmoothingAngle (radians, loaded from the LWO/FLD) is used as its smoothing threshold when its Surf_Smoothing flag is set (else that surface stays faceted). Averaging is restricted to the surface's own faces (a material boundary is always a hard edge, matching LightWave). Off by default (byte-identical to the legacy look, which relies on the 30 deg art choice). A per-surface sidecar 'surface|smoothAngle|value' override always wins over this. Greets/City/Crash authored ~89 deg on most surfaces, so turning this on makes the demo much smoother.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(texture_filter,            "FDS_TEXTURE_FILTER",           0,                              "render",   "Deferred opaque (city buildings) texture filtering. 0 = point sample (default; byte-identical to legacy — the G-buffer packs a 20-bit integer swizzled texel address so the lighting kernel point-samples at texel granularity → full-facade texture crawl as surfaces pan sub-texel). 1 = bilinear, 2 = trilinear. When >0 the Mekalele rasterizer samples the diffuse texel with the UV FRACTION (only present at raster time) and writes the FILTERED BGRA color to a new G-buffer albedo plane; the deferred kernel reads that instead of chasing the packed texel. Metal/rough/AO/normal maps stay point-sampled. Forward (glass/water/additive TheOtherBarry) path is out of scope — unaffected.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(no_nmap,                  "FDS_NO_NMAP",                  0,                              "nmap",     "Force flat normals everywhere")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(nmap_strength,           "FDS_NMAP_STRENGTH",            1.5f,                           "nmap",     "Sobel-bake strength: higher = more pronounced bump")
FDS_FLAG_INT(nmap_blur,                 "FDS_NMAP_BLUR",                4,                              "nmap",     "Box-blur passes (3x3) applied to luminance before Sobel (0 = no blur)")
// ─── per-material AO map (PBR) ────────────────────────────────────────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(ao_map,                   "FDS_AO_MAP",                   1,                              "ao",       "Enable per-material AO map sampling (Material::AoMap), multiplied into the deferred ambient term (direct light is shadow-occluded, not AO'd). Composes with screen-space SSAO/GTAO.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(ao_map_strength,         "FDS_AO_MAP_STRENGTH",          2.0f,                           "ao",       "AO map strength: ao' = 1 - strength*(1-ao). 0 = no occlusion, 1 = full map, >1 = exaggerated. Multiplied per material by Material::AoStrength (editor 'aoStrength').")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(ao_direct,                "FDS_AO_DIRECT",                0,                              "ao",       "S4b: apply the AO map to DIRECT diffuse light too, not just ambient (docs/OPTIMIZATION_BACKLOG.md S4b). Default OFF keeps the canonical AO-on-indirect-only behavior (byte-identical). ON MOVES the AO multiply from the ambient-only term to the FINAL combined diffuse (ambient + direct diffuse) after the per-light loop, using ao_direct_strength as its dial (specular is NOT AO'd — it's a highlight). This is a static, acne-free stand-in for the per-block mortar SELF-SHADOW that the single-shadow-id collapse (the greets displaced-stone acne fix) removed: without it the disco light rakes straight into the recessed mortar grooves and the blocks read flat (light 'bleeding' between stones). Applies to the OPAQUE kernel (scalar wave-1 = greets/fountain + the vec inner loop, which rejoin before the combine; the quarter/checker fill inherits it via the reconstructed lit seeds). Needs a material with AO available (Mat_AoInAlpha albedo-alpha, or an AoMap) + --ao_map on.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(ao_direct_strength,      "FDS_AO_DIRECT_STRENGTH",       1.0f,                           "ao",       "S4b (--ao_direct): strength dial for the AO applied to the FINAL combined diffuse: ao' = 1 - ao_direct_strength * Material::AoStrength * (1 - ao). Kept SEPARATE from ao_map_strength (the ambient-only dial, default 2.0) so the direct-light groove darkening is a distinct look call. Because --ao_direct MOVES the single AO multiply here (ambient is no longer occluded at its own step), this dial governs the occlusion of BOTH the ambient and the direct diffuse: 0 = the moved multiply is identity (no AO on either — so don't run --ao_direct at 0, just leave it off), 1 = full map, >1 = exaggerated. Only read when --ao_direct is on.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(nmap_viz,                  "FDS_NMAP_VIZ",                 0,                              "parallax", "Normal debug viz (deferred, post-tonemap): 0 = off; 1 = G-buffer geometric normal as RGB (n*0.5+0.5); 2 = final shaded normal with the material's normal map applied through the TBN (what lighting actually uses — flipped-G conventions show as inverted relief here); 3 = raw normal-map texel RGB at the pixel's sampled UV (no map → dim gray).")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(env_refl,                 "FDS_ENV_REFL",                 0,                              "parallax", "Deferred env-specular reflection: materials with Reflection > 0 add an equirect-panorama sample along the per-pixel reflected view direction (specular IBL tier). The panorama is baked ONCE per scene from the camera position at first use (EnvReflection_EnsureBaked — a static approximation, not a live RTT) and attenuated by the roughness map like the analytic highlight. Materials with Reflection == 0 pay nothing.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(env_refl_gain,           "FDS_ENV_REFL_GAIN",            1.0f,                           "parallax", "Env-reflection intensity multiplier on top of the material's Reflection percentage.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(metal_map,                "FDS_METAL_MAP",                1,                              "parallax", "Per-material metalness map (Material::MetallicMap): metal pixels lose diffuse, tint their specular + env reflection by the albedo, and reflect at near-full strength (F0→1). Needs --env_refl for the reflection part to show. --no-metal_map disables sampling (map still loads).")
FDS_FLAG_INT(env_refl_res,              "FDS_ENV_REFL_RES",             512,                            "parallax", "Env-reflection panorama resolution (square equirect, clamped 64-1024; cube faces render at half this). Superseded by env_bake_res when THAT is set explicitly. Applies to NEW bakes — rebake (editor button / EnvReflection_Invalidate) to change existing ones. Memory ~res²×4×1.33 per reflective surface.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(env_bake_res,              "FDS_ENV_BAKE_RES",             256,                            "parallax", "Env-reflection bake FACE resolution: per-face pixels of the deferred per-surface probe stores (auto-baked probes AND the city per-building registered stores; legacy equirect stores bake at 2x this). Power of two in 64..1024 — other values are clamped/rounded down with a stderr note (the mip chain + shift-indexed samplers need halvable dims). Default 256 = today's effective size; only an EXPLICIT set (CLI/env) overrides — unset keeps the legacy env_refl_res/2 sizing and the city stores' caller-chosen 256, byte-identical. City building stores cap at their 512² source bake. Applies to NEW bakes — rebake to change existing ones. Memory ~6×res²×4×1.33 per store.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(env_refl_scene_mode,       "FDS_ENV_REFL_SCENE_MODE",      0,                              "parallax", "LIVE/CLI override of the scene-wide env-reflection default (-1 none / 0 auto / 1 all) for materials whose per-surface envRefl is 0 (auto). The AUTHORED value lives in the FLD scene header (LWS 'FdsSceneEnvRefl' via tools/lwsread; Scene::EnvReflSceneMode); this flag wins over it only when EXPLICITLY set (CLI/env/editor). Per-surface envRefl +-1 always wins over both; the env_refl master switch still gates the whole env pass. 1 = every auto material bakes/publishes a probe, -1 = none do.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(env_bake_res_scene,        "FDS_ENV_BAKE_RES_SCENE",       0,                              "parallax", "LIVE/CLI override of the scene-wide default env-probe bake FACE resolution (pow2 64..1024, 0 = unset). The AUTHORED value lives in the FLD scene header (LWS 'FdsSceneEnvBakeRes'; Scene::EnvBakeResScene). Full sizing chain (envBakeResOverride): per-surface envBakeRes > explicit --env-bake-res > THIS flag when explicitly set > FLD scene header value > legacy env_refl_res/2 sizing. Applies to NEW bakes - rebake to change existing ones.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(env_bake_res_cap,          "FDS_ENV_BAKE_RES_CAP",         0,                              "parallax", "SAFETY CEILING on the per-probe env-bake FACE resolution (pow2 64..1024; 0 = no cap). Applied AFTER the whole sizing chain so a per-surface/scene/global wish larger than this is clamped DOWN at bake time — the store records the capped size and every consumer reads its own dims, so mixed-res stores still coexist. Exists for the 4GB wasm editor: greets sits near the ceiling and a full re-bake of every reflective surface at 512+ can spike a probe's transient (color+Z16+6 faces ~= 30*res^2 bytes) past 4GB -> 'memory access out of bounds'. Under __EMSCRIPTEN__ this defaults to 256 (~2MB/probe) even when unset; native defaults to 0 (uncapped) so the pinned baselines stay byte-identical, but --env-bake-res-cap can force it natively for testing.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(env_refl_viz,              "FDS_ENV_REFL_VIZ",             0,                              "parallax", "Env-reflection pano viewer: blit the Nth (1-based) baked panorama over the top-right corner, post-tonemap. 0 = off. Beyond-count clamps to the last pano.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(env_bake_fix,             "FDS_ENV_BAKE_FIX",             0,                              "parallax", "FIXED env-reflection auto-bake (EnvReflection_FramePrep probes): per-face projection actually published (legacy rendered every cube face with the MAIN camera's FOVX on the small bake surface — a few-degree telephoto sliver instead of a 90-degree face, i.e. mostly-void panos), FACE-level self-exclusion instead of whole-mesh (greets merges momy + the whole room into one TriMesh; the mesh skip emptied the probe), multi-instance exclusion radius (sibling statues stay visible in each other's probes), metal-without-store neutralization during bakes (siblings no longer bake black), and stale main-view mirror-mask neutralization. Default OFF to keep the pinned city baseline byte-identical (city vehicle glass probes bake through this path); the EDITOR always enables it, and importing a metallic map / a sidecar map auto-defaults it on.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(env_cube,                 "FDS_ENV_CUBE",                 1,                              "parallax", "Environment maps as six PADDED cube faces with a trig-free dominant-axis lookup (FDS/RENDER/EnvCube.h), replacing the equirect panorama in BOTH env consumers (deferred per-pixel --env_refl and the forward per-vertex reflective path CITY uses). Sharper reflections (no pole waste / stitch resample), no wrap seam, cheaper lookup (no atan2/asin). Default ON since slice D (validated: gates + flag-off pins byte-identical, no seams, bench <= equirect); --no-env_cube restores the equirect path bit-identical. Set FDS_ENV_CUBE_SELFTEST=1 to run the convention round-trip self-test at startup.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(city_env_pixel,           "FDS_CITY_ENV_PIXEL",           0,                              "parallax", "EXPERIMENT: city windows reflect PER PIXEL through the deferred kernel instead of the forward per-vertex TEXTURETEXTURE filler. Window faces skip Face_Reflective, so they enter the G-buffer with their per-building cloned matID; the deferred env-specular compose (--env_refl required) samples the cube store EnvReflection_FramePrep auto-bakes for each windows material — per-pixel reflected directions (no vertex interpolation, nothing to pop) + AABB parallax correction + roughness mips + Fresnel. Skips the per-building forward face bake entirely (city init faster; no city_envmap*.bin needed). Look differs from the legacy half-add composite by design.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(env_sphere_parallax,     "FDS_ENV_SPHERE_PARALLAX",      0.0f,                           "parallax", "Sphere-proxy parallax for centroid-anchored direction-only env stores (city noParallax): intersect the reflected ray FROM THE PIXEL with a sphere of this radius (world units) around the bake point, look up toward the hit. Positional reflections everywhere — low pixels see LOW content (neighbor bases, waterline) — with zero seams by construction (a sphere has no edges, unlike the whole-city AABB that banded, and no horizon blend, unlike the plane fake that tore). 0 = off. Try 1500-4000 in city (street-canyon scale); larger = weaker correction.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (env_ssr,                 "FDS_ENV_SSR",                  0,                              "parallax", "Short-range SCREEN-SPACE reflection for the deferred env compose (city glass): max march steps (spike default 8; 0 = off). Fills the self-reflections the centroid-anchored cube stores miss (a building's own ledges/wings — the bakes exclude the own building). Marches the reflected VIEW ray a few steps against the depth buffer; on a hit, blends the PREVIOUS-FRAME color (1-frame-stale — sampling live VPage races the concurrent tile writes) into the cube result BEFORE Fresnel, so the hit rides the same Fresnel/roughness/metal weighting. The sphere-corrected cube stays as fallback (SSR wins on hit, fades to the cube at every failure mode). Needs --env_refl (+ --city_env_pixel for city glass). Scalar-only compose while on (disengages the OuterVec env fast path). Requires renderFrame's prev-frame copy (~0.5-1ms).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(env_ssr_stride,          "FDS_ENV_SSR_STRIDE",           4.0f,                           "parallax", "env_ssr: screen-space pixels advanced per march step (short-range by design — total reach = env_ssr steps * this). A constant view-space dt is derived from the projected ray gradient at t=0 so each step advances ~this many px.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(env_ssr_thick,           "FDS_ENV_SSR_THICK",            40.0f,                          "parallax", "env_ssr: depth thickness (world/view units) for hit acceptance — a hit counts when the ray passes behind the stored surface by less than this. Also the continuous thickness fade: the SSR contribution ramps to zero as the crossing depth approaches this, so the hit boundary never seams.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(city_env_f0,             "FDS_CITY_ENV_F0",              60.0f,                          "parallax", "city_env_pixel: window glass F0 as a Reflection percent (Fresnel base at normal incidence; grazing still pulls toward 1). 40 ~= the authored half-add energy; raise for mirror glass, lower for subtle.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(city_env_lum,            "FDS_CITY_ENV_LUM",             0.45f,                          "parallax", "city_env_pixel: window glass emissive Luminosity (the texture base the reflection rides on). 0.45 ~= the authored texel*ambient brightness; the glass takes NO per-pixel dynamic lighting by design (the sweeping searchlight pools were the scripted-cam jumping).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(city_env_gloss,          "FDS_CITY_ENV_GLOSS",           24.0f,                          "parallax", "city_env_pixel: window glass Glossiness -> reflection mip depth (rough = sqrt(2/(gloss+2)), mip = rough*3). THE dancing-vs-sharpness dial: at ~17-20 fps the world steps several units per displayed frame, and sharp store content on glass strobes (temporal aliasing — no history buffer to filter with). Lower = deeper mip = frostier + calmer (24 ~= mip 1.3, 64px-tier content); raise toward 96 for sharp mirror glass if the strobing is acceptable.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(refl_correct,             "FDS_REFL_CORRECT",             1,                              "parallax", "PHYSICALLY CORRECT PLANAR WATER REFLECTION in chase and city (LOOK CHANGE, commissioned by Gil-Ad 2026-08-16; DEFAULT ON -- --no-refl_correct is the escape hatch back to the 1998 behaviour, proven byte-identical to the parent binary on all 12 pin recipes). Both scenes draw their water reflection by MIRRORING THE GEOMETRY: the tick runs Reflected_Transform -> Radix_Sort -> Render() and then Transform_Objects -> Radix_Sort -> Render(), and Render() IS renderFrame, so the reflected pass is a COMPLETE second deferred frame with its own G-buffer and its own light list. Two things in that pass were never mirrored with the geometry. (1) NORMALS: Reflected_Transform writes TPos/RZ/PX/PY/Flags and NO TN and NO TTangent -- it transforms the same Verts[] storage the main pass uses, so the reflected world shades from whatever the PREVIOUS Transform_Objects left there. At chase t=100 that is a load-zeroed array: 19 092 of 19 092 reflected triangles rasterize with all three corner normals at (0,0,0) (docs/OPTIMIZATION_BACKLOG.md 2026-08-16w). After the first tick the zero form goes away and the STALE form -- last frame main-view normals on mirrored geometry -- survives every frame, in city permanently. This flag writes MatrixXVector(IM, N, TN) and the tangent twin in ALL SIX vertex loops of each scene (the three non-Phong loops AND the three Tri_Phong else-branches: CHASE.CPP 459/483/510/552/576/605, CITY.CPP 641/665/692/732/756/785), IM being that pass model->reflected-view rotation, copied before the FOV row-scaling so it is the unscaled rotation. (2) LIGHTS: the deferred light list is built from Omni::IPos in MAIN world space and was never mirrored, so the mirrored normals met unmirrored lights and N.L FLIPPED where a real mirror preserves it. This flag reflects each light position, world position and spot axis about the SAME plane the geometry is mirrored about -- P - 2(P.N)N through the world ORIGIN, d=0, which is what Reflected_Transform actually does (CHASE.CPP:341, CITY.CPP:527); NOT city RflSurfOfs, which is the FastFog reflection plane -- in place, after the SoA build (FDS/RENDER/ReflMirror.cpp), so no light is appended and the light count, the tile binning and every per-pixel loop are untouched. DEFERRED-ONLY, BY CONSTRUCTION: both halves feed only the deferred pipeline, deferred defaults 0, and this flag is MEASURED byte-null in chase and city without --deferred. That is not a gap -- the FORWARD path shades the reflection from per-vertex Lighting() computed in TRUE WORLD SPACE before Reflected_Transform runs, and a mirror shows the real object real shading, so forward was already right. What is wrong is exactly the class of consumer that RECONSTRUCTS world space from the mirrored view through ctx.viewToWorld / ctx.cameraWorld*. MIRRORED FOR FREE, AND VISIBLY: cones, omni halos and the froxel/unified volumetric pass read ONLY ctx.lights (zero OmniHead/IPos reads in DeferredVolumetric.cpp / DeferredFastFog.cpp), so they inherit the mirror. VISIBLE CONSEQUENCE in chase, which does NOT pass skipVolumetric (city does, CITY.CPP:3880): the reflected pass used to paint a SECOND, unmirrored light shaft from each lighthouse across the SKY above the horizon; those ghost shafts now follow the mirrored light and leave the sky, so chase t=800 lighthouse beams read thinner and one sky shaft disappears entirely (docs/img/reflmir/chase_t000800_{before,after}.png). More correct -- a reflection has no business above the waterline -- but it IS a visible dimming of a signature element, flagged for the user eye rather than claimed as a win. NOT MIRRORED, DELIBERATELY, each a REMAINDER and not a claim of correctness: (a) the ENV-CUBE tap -- EnvSpecComposeScalar builds the reflected direction in view space and rotates it to world by the REAL camera viewToWorld, so in this pass it lands in MIRRORED-world coordinates and fetches a real-world cube, and the parallax step compounds it by intersecting a mirrored sample position against real-world box bounds. This is LIVE, not dormant: env_refl default 0 is overridden process-globally by greets PBR metallic import (MaterialImport.cpp:865 setDefault(env_refl,true)), greets inits FIRST, and setDefault is one-way -- so a full demo run has it ON in city and chase. City already routes its city_env_pixel window glass through the FORWARD filler for exactly this reason (cityMirrorGlassForward, whose own comment names the defect), and that helper takes the REAL camera eye rather than mirror(camera) -- a documented approximation of size 2*dist(camera, water). In city DEFAULT arm the mirrored glass is forward too but rasterizes with LAST FRAME main-view env UVs (Face_Reflective UVs are computed in Transform_Objects, which the reflected pass never calls) -- the same stale-state defect one field over, pre-existing and NOT fixed here. (b) --sh_ambient has the identical unmirrored-viewToWorld defect: a reflected pixel takes its ambient from the flipped hemisphere. Default 0 and nothing in DEMO/ sets it, so inert. (c) the SSR history (--env_ssr, default 0); note chase two-pass discrimination for that capture keys on skipVolumetric, which chase never passes, so it would store the reflected underlay as last frame image if ever enabled -- pre-existing. (d) city PER-FRAME shadow bakes (Render_DeferredShadowMaps at CITY.CPP:3856/3861) run after Reflected_Transform from UNMIRRORED light positions and are not re-run for the main pass; double-gated off by default (city_test_spots + shadows). (e) the reflected FLARE loops mirror themselves with a hardcoded ReflectedPos.y = -ReflectedPos.y (CHASE.CPP:685, CITY.CPP:868) OUTSIDE this flag, so --no-refl_correct still gives mirrored flares over unmirrored lighting; there are FIVE separate spellings of the mirror in the tree and they agree only while RflSurfNorm stays axis-aligned through the world origin. SPECULAR NEEDS NO MIRRORING and is correct as it stands: every path derives the eye vector from the pixel OWN view-space position, and the reflected geometry went through the same View->Mat, so V is already the real camera. NESTED BAKES ARE EXCLUDED: ReflMirror_MirrorLights returns early when g_offscreenViewDepth != 0, because renderFrame prologue runs EnvReflection_FramePrep inside the armed reflected pass and its six cube-face renders would otherwise bake MIRRORED lights into the ON-DISK probe cache -- a corruption outliving the process that no pin would catch. CITY NEEDS A SECOND TICK: city water carries NO mirrored content on the first tick of a process (measured: 1 tick byte-identical, 2 ticks differ; chase composites immediately), so the one-pose-per-process pin recipes render a frame in which this flag provably cannot act. City pins therefore barely move (t=1961 byte-identical, t=2400 22 px) while continuous play moves 277 215 px / 13.4 % at t=1961. Judge city from a warm multi-tick run, never from its pins. COVERAGE HONESTY: tools/render_gate.sh has ZERO coverage of this -- its four scenes have no Reflected_Transform; only chase and city do. greets and fountain have no Reflected_Transform and are byte-identical under this flag, proven by pin (5 greets recipes + fountain, unmoved). See docs/OPTIMIZATION_BACKLOG.md 2026-08-17.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(env_live_water,           "FDS_ENV_LIVE_WATER",           0,                              "parallax", "LIVE water in the baked env reflections (city windows): env-sample directions that hit the water plane (ray from the store's bake point, below horizon, and reading texels the bake marked as water) are perturbed by the SAME animated wave-slope field the main-view reflection ripple uses (pwater::WaveSlope, same clock/scale), so the frozen mirror content in window reflections undulates in sync with the live water. The tilt is gated by the bake's own PER-TEXEL WATER VERDICT BIT (one bit per baked texel at the full 512^2 face res, point-sampled at the tap's own texel) so ONLY the reflected water moves: the below-horizon test alone was not a water test -- a window at building-mid-height reflects a wholly below-horizon hemisphere, so 5d28db7 rippled the reflected skyline too. Applies to BOTH consumers, but NOT the same way, and 5f1ffa92's claim that carrying the mask into both was enough was measured on the DEFERRED one only. The deferred compose (scalar + OuterVec lane fetch) reads the mask PER PIXEL and is correct. The forward paraboloid-sheet path (Transform.cpp) perturbs a VERTEX, and the rasterizer interpolates the resulting UV affinely, so a corner whose reflection is water drags EVERY pixel of its triangle -- the reflected skyline included -- however right the mask is: measured under PRESETS/city-noir.flags (which renders FORWARD; `deferred` defaults off) at the t=1961 pin pose, 38 148 reflected-skyline pixels still moved between two wave clocks. So the forward path no longer perturbs the direction at all: EU/EV stay UNPERTURBED, Transform hands the filler a per-FACE UV offset (Face::LwDU/LwDV, the mean of the WET corners' full-tilt displacement), the sheets carry the verdict BIT in their ALPHA byte (0 or 255, stamped through the same gather table as the colour, so a pixel's alpha is the verdict OF THE TEXEL IT FETCHED), and TheOtherBarry gates the offset on each PIXEL's own bit -- read at the unperturbed lookup, structurally. That takes the skyline residual to 882 / 5 594 / 8 366 px at eye heights 190 / 423 / 800 (0.65 / 2.23 / 2.06 % of the reflected-skyline region, against the deferred path's 3.1 % at the same pose), with water motion undamped (105 / 111 / 102 % of the pre-fix motion). NOTE those three numbers were measured on the COVERAGE predecessor, whose residual was two thirds INTENDED soft ramp across the reflected waterline; with the verdict now a per-texel BIT there is no ramp left, and the sample-time bias knob that used to trade the ramp away is gone (see env_live_water_mask_bias, which moved to the bake). MEASURED ON THE DEFERRED PATH under the user's acceptance arm (--env_live_water --deferred --city_env_pixel, city t=1961, three eye heights): pixels whose env tap reads ONLY non-water baked texels move ZERO times, in the coverage arm AND the bit arm -- the deferred consumer never had the gross leak, because it always read the mask per pixel. What the exact bit buys THERE is boundary tightening: the band of pixels straddling the reflected waterline drops 45.2/59.7/63.5 % -> 41.2/53.9/57.6 % of its own region, 8 354 px stop being displaced against 1 412 that start, and water motion is 100.1-100.4 %. The residual band is the ENV TAP's own mip/bilinear footprint, not the mask: sharpen the tap (--city_env_gloss=4000, mip ~0) at the same pose and the band collapses 121 582 -> 42 457 px while the arms separate hard -- the coverage plane displaces 226 px that read ONLY non-water content and the bit displaces 0. At the default gloss 24 a tap averages ~8-16 baked texels, so displacing it necessarily drags whatever else is inside the filter; the lever for a narrower band is the tap, not the verdict. Static-bake content itself is untouched — this warps the lookup, exactly like the main view's dispMap ripple warps the RTT. No-ops when water_ripple is off or the scene never publishes a water plane (only CITY does).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(env_live_water_amp,      "FDS_ENV_LIVE_WATER_AMP",       0.10f,                          "parallax", "env_live_water direction-perturb amplitude: lookup dir += amp * waveSlope (slope range ~±2). 0.1 = a few degrees of wobble, comparable to the main-view ripple; higher = choppier reflected water.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(env_live_water_shade,     "FDS_ENV_LIVE_WATER_SHADE",     1,                              "parallax", "env_live_water: ALSO bake the city probes' water region as PROCEDURAL water instead of the static authored texture. The default bake composites the water mesh's static albedo over the mirrored world — dead against a --water_procedural main view. When env_live_water is on AND the city's procedural water is effective (water_procedural / sidecar tri-state), the bake hides the water mesh (pure mirrored underlay) and re-shades every unoccluded water-plane texel with the main view's formula: deep*(1-F) + underlay*F fresnel + the caustic-cell modulation (wave clock frozen at 0 — the sample-time perturb supplies motion). Both env consumers inherit (sheets + deferred stores derive from the same faces). The cube cache is BYPASSED for these bakes (fresh ~4s init; the on-disk cache stays the pristine static bake). --no-env_live_water_shade = perturb-only on the static bake.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(env_dynamic,              "FDS_ENV_DYNAMIC",              0,                              "parallax", "Live DYNAMIC-mesh env reflections (ENVDYN Workstream A, docs/ENVDYN_DISPLACEMENT_PLAN.md): the moving mech (and future movers) rendered LIVE into the env-reflection probes the user FLAGS via the per-surface 'dynamic env' editor checkbox (Material::EnvDynamic / RVSF bit 0x400). Each frame, before the main Transform, an overlay pass re-renders ONLY the dynamic meshes into each flagged probe's touched cube faces and composites them over the probe's retained static capture (A2), so the mech appears in the reflection instead of being structurally absent. Gated by a per-probe camera-frustum owner gate + mech-bsphere / padded-face-pyramid relevance cull (Foundation F). OFF (default) = byte-null: no flagged probes touched, no overlay runs. Needs --env_refl + --env_cube (default on). Overlay shading samples other probes' STATIC content only (no 2nd-bounce mech).")
FDS_FLAG_INT(env_dynamic_budget,        "FDS_ENV_DYNAMIC_BUDGET",       2,                              "parallax", "env_dynamic: max flagged probes refreshed by the dynamic-mesh overlay per frame (round-robin when more are relevant). Each touched probe costs one dynamic-only Transform + deferred raster/shade per touched cube face (typically 1-2 faces) + a per-face mip refilter. Lower to cap the per-frame overlay cost; 0 disables the overlay (like --no-env_dynamic).")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(draw_aabbs,               "FDS_DRAW_AABBS",               0,                              "cull",     "DEBUG overlay (Foundation F, docs/ENVDYN_DISPLACEMENT_PLAN.md): draw every TriMesh's world-space AABB as a wireframe over the final frame (post-tonemap), projected through the main camera. GREEN = static mesh (box computed once, post first transform), YELLOW = dynamic mesh (isDynamicForBake — box recomputed each frame by transforming the cached model-space AABB corners into world space), dim RED = mesh whose AABB the main-camera 6-plane frustum test rejects. For eyeballing the world-AABB build + frustum-vs-AABB reject that gates the dynamic env-reflection overlay (Workstream A). Off by default; when off, WorldAabb_UpdateScene is never called and nothing is drawn (byte-null).")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(parallax,                 "FDS_PARALLAX",                 1,                              "parallax", "Per-material PARALLAX (offset) mapping from Material::HeightMap. In the Mekalele G-buffer pass, the per-pixel UV is nudged along the tangent-space view direction by the sampled height before the texel pack, so albedo/normal/AO all sample the parallax-shifted texel — fakes surface depth (mortar joints, cobbles) with NO added geometry and no silhouette change. Off by default; needs a HeightMap on the material. Adds a height texture gather to the rasterizer's hot loop (the only pass with the smooth float UV).")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (nmap_16bit,              "FDS_NMAP_16BIT",               1,                              "parallax", "Pack normal maps to 16-bit RG (X,Y; Z reconstructed in-shader as √(1-x²-y²)) instead of 32-bit BGRA — HALF the normal-map memory + cache, visually equivalent (RG are the same 8-bit values; a unit normal's Z is determined by X,Y). The deferred kernel's decode branches on the texture's BPP, so 32-bit normals are byte-unchanged. Off by default; greets converts its authored stone normals via MakeNormal16 when on. The variable-texel-size win generalized from the 8-bit parallax height to normals.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (roughness_map,           "FDS_ROUGHNESS_MAP",            1,                              "parallax", "Per-material roughness map (Material::RoughnessMap): modulate per-pixel SPECULAR INTENSITY by the map (white=rough→dimmer highlight) so highlights break up across the surface (matte mortar vs glinty stone) instead of a uniform sheen. Cheap tier — intensity only, keeps the compile-time gloss exponent (no per-pixel pow). One 8-bit gather in the deferred kernel, only where the material has a roughness map AND specular is active. --no-roughness_map disables.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(roughness_strength,      "FDS_ROUGHNESS_STRENGTH",       1.0f,                           "parallax", "Roughness map strength: specMul = 1 - strength*roughness. 0 = ignore the map (uniform spec), 1 = full map, >1 = exaggerated matte/gloss contrast.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(parallax_strength,       "FDS_PARALLAX_STRENGTH",        0.3f,                           "parallax", "Parallax UV-offset scale (UV units at (height-0.5)×grazing). Offset = (h-0.5)*strength*tangentViewXY. Geometry/angle dependent: ~0.04 was sub-texel (invisible) on the greets wall at the test pose; ~0.15-0.4 reads as stone relief; too high smears/swims at grazing (offset parallax has no self-occlusion to mask the stretch). TUNE LIVE on a clean wall — greets snapshot frames are washed out by god-rays/bloom.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(pom_viz,                  "FDS_POM_VIZ",                  0,                              "parallax", "Parallax debug viz: materials with a HeightMap render the HEIGHT FIELD (grayscale, sampled at the final post-march UV) instead of their albedo — the parallax result made directly visible: block domes, mortar cuts, and any march terracing/step-banding show as terraces in the gray. Needs --texture_filter>=1 (rides the filtered-albedo path). Lighting still applies, so view in a plainly-lit pose. Run: ./DEMO --deferred --texture_filter --pom_viz [--parallax_pom ...]")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (parallax_pom,            "FDS_PARALLAX_POM",             8,                              "parallax", "Parallax OCCLUSION mapping step count (docs/HEIGHTMAP_POM_PLAN.md). 0 = off (single-shift, byte-identical). N>0 = N-step occlusion march that records the first ray/height-field CROSSING, anchoring relief to the true surface depth (self-occluding, NO swim like single-shift's float). DEFAULT drives the NAIVE linear march (N uniform steps, first rayH<=Hs crossing). N is the whole tap budget, so low N is coarse: measured on greets@t=5780, naive-8 lands ~32 texels (strength 0.3) to ~108 texels (strength 1.0) from the true crossing — cheap but under-converged. --parallax_pom_cone selects the CONE march instead (relaxed cone-step to BRACKET the crossing + binary-search refine to LAND on it; --parallax_pom_relax widens the steps, --parallax_pom_refine sets the bisection count). The cone converges to the true crossing sub-texel in ~8 cone taps (median ~1 texel at strength 0.3) — far fewer than the naive ~256 needs for equal quality — but each cone tap is ~2.7x a naive tap (2nd gather + divide), so the cone is a big win for CONVERGED/swim-free quality, not a raw-cost win over the coarse naive-8. Cone uses a per-material cone map (Material::ConeMap, baked once at setup when --parallax_pom_cone is on). Needs --parallax + a HeightMap. STEP-3 LOD fades to single-shift with distance/angle.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (parallax_pom_quarter,   "FDS_PARALLAX_POM_QUARTER",     0,                              "parallax", "Tier-2 STEP-2 quarter-res POM offset field: when set with --parallax_pom, run the cone march only on the even lanes/rows of each 8-wide raster block and reuse the resulting UV OFFSET for the odd neighbours (÷~4 the march cost). 0 = full-res march every pixel. The albedo/normal texel fetch stays full-res — only the offset SEARCH is subsampled. Bilateral (depth/height-aware) fill keeps recess silhouettes; a plain share is used where neighbours agree.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(parallax_pom_lod,       "FDS_PARALLAX_POM_LOD",         0.0f,                           "parallax", "Tier-2 STEP-3 POM level-of-detail fade distance (view-Z units). 0 = no fade (full march everywhere --parallax_pom is on). >0 = the cone-march result is blended toward the tier-0 single-shift as view-Z grows past this, reaching pure single-shift at ~2× this distance, so far/oblique parallax pixels (most of them) skip the march. Continuous (no pop). Try ~30-50 in greets (view-Z ~[5..80]).")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (parallax_pom_cone,      "FDS_PARALLAX_POM_CONE",        0,                              "parallax", "Select the relaxed CONE-STEP march for --parallax_pom instead of the default naive linear march. The cone brackets the first ray/height crossing then binary-refines onto it → converges sub-texel in ~8 taps (vs the naive's ~256 for equal quality), at ~2.7x per-tap cost. Off by default (the naive march is cheaper for the coarse tier). Needs --parallax_pom>0 + a baked Material::ConeMap.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (parallax_pom_refine,    "FDS_PARALLAX_POM_REFINE",      6,                              "parallax", "Cone march (--parallax_pom_cone) binary-search refine iterations after bracketing the crossing. Higher = tighter sub-texel landing. 6 is ~1/64-step accurate; diminishing returns past that. No effect on the naive march.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(parallax_pom_relax,     "FDS_PARALLAX_POM_RELAX",       4.0f,                           "parallax", "Cone march (--parallax_pom_cone) step-width relax factor: widens the conservative cone step so the ray brackets the crossing in fewer taps (the bisection then recovers the exact crossing inside the bracket). 1 = strict conservative (slow to bracket); higher brackets faster but overshoots more per step. 4 is the measured sweet spot on the greets stone relief.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (pom_height_mip,         "FDS_POM_HEIGHT_MIP",           -1,                             "parallax", "FIX for the diagonal seams on the greets stone walls (the height map is now real, non-flat content). The parallax height/cone gathers sample the material's HeightMap at the per-FACE albedo miplevel, and faces come from the mipmap-via-subdivision clipper whose sub-face edges are DIAGONAL — so adjacent sub-faces at different mip levels read the height at different resolutions and the resulting UV OFFSET jumps across the diagonal boundary (a faint diagonal seam). This PINS the height (and its cone) mip to ONE fixed level for every face, with the height map's own dims/mask/scale threaded through the rasterizer ctx (independent of the albedo mip), so the sampled height is continuous across sub-face boundaries — height is low-frequency, so a single level is enough. -1 (default) = legacy per-face mip (byte-identical to before). >=0 pins that level (clamped to the map's mip range); 0 = full-res height, 1-2 = slightly coarser/cheaper and just as seam-free. Only affects parallax materials (greets stone); flag-off byte-null.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(parallax_max_offset,    "FDS_PARALLAX_MAX_OFFSET",      0.0f,                           "parallax", "Clamp the per-pixel PARALLAX UV offset to at most this many TEXELS from the geometric UV (both single-shift and the POM march's final landing), mirroring --glass_refract_max for refraction. 0 = OFF (byte-identical, no clamp). The offset is (h-0.5)*strength*tangentViewXY; at strength >> the tuned ~0.3-0.4 it over-drives — the sampled UV streaks many texels and the stone blocks tear into diagonal smears crossing the mortar (the greets 'diagonal seam' at FDS_PARALLAX_STRENGTH=3 = 10x the tuned value; NOT a mip transition — the pose is ~all mip0 — and present with single-shift too, so not march banding). This bounds the streak length without disabling the effect: e.g. 6-12 texels keeps moderate relief while stopping the tear. The right fix is to keep strength in the tuned 0.15-0.4 band; this is the safety rail when a high strength is wanted. Applies to any parallax material (greets stone); needs --parallax + a HeightMap.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (pom_depth_write,        "FDS_POM_DEPTH_WRITE",          0,                              "parallax", "S1a depth-writing POM (docs/DISPLACEMENT_RESEARCH.md S1): the --parallax_pom march (naive or cone) ALSO writes the marched ray/height-field intersection's view depth into the Z buffer (ZPage16, 0xFF80-zscale*z encoding), instead of the flat polygon plane's depth — so every Z consumer (SSAO/GTAO contact darkening in the mortar grooves, fog, DoF, the deferred kernel's view-space reconstruction, snapshot z-dumps) sees the RELIEF surface, not the flat wall. Depth is derived from the crossing height the march already found, converted to world units through the face's actual UV texel density (per-face tangent solve), so the Z relief is exactly consistent with the parallax the texels show — offset-limited like the march itself, so it stays bounded at grazing (no divide, no explosion). The height convention is CENTERED (h=0.5 = the authored plane, matching the zero-mean geometric bake): mortar recesses write DEEPER z, block tops write slightly CLOSER z (up to +-0.5*strength*worldPerUV). Ordering note: the tiled raster is front-to-back with Z-early-reject — writing deeper-than-polygon Z lets a later face lying inside the relief band win groove pixels, which is what true displacement would do (see the write-site comment in Mekalele.h). Needs --parallax + --parallax_pom>0 + a HeightMap. Default OFF = byte-identical (Z keeps flat-plane semantics).")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (pom_normal,             "FDS_POM_NORMAL",               0,                              "parallax", "S1e HEIGHT-FIELD SHADING NORMAL for the parallax march (docs/S1_PIXEL_DISPLACEMENT_PLAN.md). The POM march only moves UVs -- nothing in it ever tilts a shading normal -- so the deferred kernel receives the FLAT polygon normal and the relief reads flat no matter how deep the marched Z is. The TESSELLATION path does not have this problem: its geometry carries the height map's low band, so its vertex normals tilt with the blocks. MEASURED with --no-nmap at greets t=5780: tessellation still shows bevelled blocks with dark mortar joints, the per-pixel path shows a featureless wall -- that gap is the user-reported 'the tessellated version's grooves sit in visibly deeper shadow'. The material's normal map does not close it because it carries the fine grain, not the block-scale relief that lives in the HEIGHT map. This flag takes central differences of the height map over +-1 texel of its own mip at the MARCHED hit UV and rotates the G-buffer normal by the resulting slope: dH/dU_world = dh * A_uv * heightUScale, where the per-face world-per-UV cancels exactly and A_uv is the same amplitude the march travelled with (the shell geometry's under --pom_shell, --parallax_strength x ParallaxScale otherwise), so the normal can never disagree with the parallax or the depth write. Costs 4 extra height gathers per covered pixel on top of the march's 8-14. The material's normal map is applied by the kernel ON TOP of this, in the rotated frame -- the same layering the tessellation path already has (bumped geometry + full normal map). Note --pom_horizon then takes its azimuth frame from the bumped normal, again exactly as the tessellation path does. Default OFF = the flat polygon normal, byte-identical.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(pom_normal_strength,    "FDS_POM_NORMAL_STRENGTH",      1.0f,                           "parallax", "--pom_normal: gain on the height-field slope before it rotates the G-buffer normal. 1.0 = the geometrically correct slope for the amplitude the march runs at (that is what makes it agree with the parallax and the depth); >1 exaggerates the relief shading, <1 flattens it. Use it to match the tessellation path by eye rather than by construction -- the tessellation path DOUBLE-COUNTS the low band (its geometry carries it AND it still samples the FULL normal map, because --greets_displace swaps only the HEIGHT map to the residual), so a strength of 1 will read slightly shallower than the bake at the same pose.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (pom_shell,              "FDS_POM_SHELL",                0,                              "parallax", "S1b SHELL POM — per-pixel displacement with TRUE SILHOUETTES (docs/S1_PIXEL_DISPLACEMENT_PLAN.md). Needs geometry built as a shell: at init the target material's faces are pushed OUT along their vertex normals by half the relief amplitude (PomShell_Build, DEMO/MeshOps.cpp) so the rasterized surface is the LID (top of the relief slab) and Material::PomShellUvAmp records the amplitude (in UV units) the geometry was built with. The march then runs from the per-pixel interpolated entry height (Vertex::ShellH, 1 = lid) DOWN through the slab along the TRUE view ray (not offset-limited — offset limiting caps lateral travel at parallax_strength regardless of angle, which is exactly the grazing travel silhouettes depend on; --pom_shell_cap bounds the 1/(V.N) blowup), writes the crossing's depth (arms pom_depth_write implicitly), and DISCARDS any lane whose ray leaves the authored face's UV domain before crossing the height field. That lateral-exit discard is the silhouette: at a wall's edge the lid covers more screen than the authored plane, and the rays that would have to travel past the wall's end leave the pixel to the geometry BEHIND. Default OFF = no shell geometry is built and the march keeps the legacy centered convention (byte-identical).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(pom_shell_cap,          "FDS_POM_SHELL_CAP",            8.0f,                           "parallax", "--pom_shell: cap on 1/(V.N) in the true-ray shell march (grazing guard). The shell ray's lateral UV travel per unit height drop is parallax-amp/(V.N), which explodes as the view grazes the surface; this bounds it (8 = up to ~83 degrees of incidence honoured, ~8x the offset-limited travel). Lower = flatter/cheaper rays and fewer discards at grazing, higher = more silhouette reach and longer marches (the march step count is unchanged, so a very high cap under-samples the ray). Also bounds the shell depth write (|dz| <= amp*cap).")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (pom_tbn_face_sign,      "FDS_POM_TBN_FACE_SIGN",        0,                              "parallax", "SOURCE of the mirrored-UV bitangent sign the POM march uses (see Mekalele.h MekaleleTbnHandedness). 0 (default) = Material::TbnHandedness, the engine convention DeferredSurfaceKernel and LightmapBake already apply, so the march agrees with the shading; it is maintained only for NORMAL-MAPPED materials and only from the first three vertices UV determinant. 1 = derive the sign PER FACE from that face own positions and UVs, sign((N x T_face) . B_face) -- the quantity the march actually needs, independent of the material bookkeeping. Measured on greets at t=5958 the two disagree on 60 percent of faces (the wall material rooms has 302 of 406 faces with a negative UV determinant sitting on a handedness +1 material), so this is an A/B, not a formality. Either way the march no longer uses a fixed-sign N x T, which walked mirrored charts backwards along V.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(pom_shell_cap_fade,      "FDS_POM_SHELL_CAP_FADE",       0.0f,                           "parallax", "GRAZING FADE for the --pom_shell true-ray march (default 0 = OFF, byte-identical). The shell march deliberately travels the TRUE view ray — 1/(V.N) UV per unit slab height — because grazing lateral travel is what silhouettes are made of; but 1/(V.N) diverges at grazing and --pom_shell_cap bounds it with a HARD CLAMP, which is a kink in the ray direction as a function of incidence and lets lateral travel reach cap x the offset-limited value right where the incidence is least stable. This blends the true ray toward the OFFSET-LIMITED ray (the non-shell march form, which does not swim) as incidence approaches parallel: w = smoothstep(0, VALUE, V.N), 1/(V.N)_eff = 1 + (capped - 1)*w. VALUE is the V.N at which the true ray is fully restored (0.3 ~= 72 deg off normal). Head-on pixels are unaffected either way (1/(V.N) is 1 there). Stacks with --pom_shell_cap, which still bounds what is left. Run: ./DEMO ... --pom_shell --pom_shell_cap=16 --pom_shell_cap_fade=0.3")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (pom_shell_stats,         "FDS_POM_SHELL_STATS",          0,                              "parallax", "--pom_shell diagnostic: print a per-triangle census (world-per-UV from the raster-time Lengyel solve, the resulting world amplitude, the authored UV domain, and the UV/screen area) for the first few hundred shell triangles a frame dispatches, plus the running min/max. This is where a degenerate post-clip sliver shows up as an exploding world-per-UV — the term the shell depth write and the march scale by. Dispatcher-side only (no hot-loop cost); off by default.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (pom_shell_pin,           "FDS_POM_SHELL_PIN",            0,                              "parallax", "--pom_shell build option: PIN (leave unmoved, ShellH 0.5) every shell vertex that non-target geometry also uses, so a cross-material junction cannot be pulled apart by the lid offset. Costs the relief near those borders — and on un-subdivided quads, whose every vertex is a border vertex, it can pin a whole face and leave no lid at all (the [POM-SHELL] census reports how many verts each rule moves vs pins). Default OFF: build the full lid and judge the junctions by measurement (the lid pokes at most amp/2 world into a neighbour, and what shows through a lateral-exit discard at an interior junction is usually the neighbouring wall, not background).")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (pom_shell_domain,       "FDS_POM_SHELL_DOMAIN",         1,                              "parallax", "--pom_shell: enable the lateral-exit DISCARD (the silhouette itself). The marched hit's UV is tested against the AUTHORED face's UV bounding box (F->U1..V3 — survives the frustum clipper and the mipmap poly-split, both of which pass the Face through unchanged); a hit outside it means the ray left the patch before crossing the height field, so the lane is killed and the geometry behind wins the pixel. --no-pom_shell_domain keeps the lid + marched depth but never discards — the ONE discriminating render for 'is a hole the discard or the geometry?'. Inert without --pom_shell.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (poly_viz,               "FDS_POLY_VIZ",                 0,                              "parallax", "PER-POLYGON OWNERSHIP VIZ: replace every textured face's albedo with a colour that says WHICH TRIANGLE OF WHICH SURFACE owns each pixel. HUE = material id (fixed 12-entry palette, so a fragment of foreign texture appearing mid-surface is instantly a different colour from the surface it sits on); BRIGHTNESS = whether that face is a --pom_shell LID face (bright) or not (45%); a per-TRIANGLE hash adds a +-20% brightness jitter so every triangle boundary is visible and 'which triangle' is answerable by eye. This exists because a rasterizer can only write inside the triangle it fills, so a stray fragment is EITHER another surface's triangle genuinely covering that screen area (geometry inflated through a neighbour) OR the surface's own triangle shading wrong (bad UV/height march) -- two completely different bugs with completely different fixes, and the campaign has repeatedly lost time deciding between them from ordinary crops. One render settles it. Lighting still runs (the shape reads); only the albedo is replaced. Default OFF.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (pom_shell_base_clip,    "FDS_POM_SHELL_BASE_CLIP",      1,                              "parallax", "--pom_shell: BASE-FOOTPRINT CLIP — the lid-overhang fix. PomShell_Build moves the patch's verts OUT along their normals by half the relief amplitude, so the rasterized lid is a rigid translation of the patch and at a patch BORDER it covers screen area the AUTHORED plane never covered. Rays entering there still march INWARD, still land inside the patch's UV box, so the lateral-exit test never fires and they shade as stone hanging past the wall's end (user-reported at greets t=6097 as 'pixels ran away to the left from the edge, floating mid air'; at t=5780 as background wrongly hidden behind a wall edge). Measured over-coverage vs the tessellation reference before this clip: 12 162 px at t=6097, 19 335 px at t=5780. The test asks where THIS pixel's view ray crosses the AUTHORED plane (h = 0.5): same affine UV chart, so uv_base = uv_lid + (V.T,V.B)*(0.5-hEnter)*A/(V.N), with A the slab's UV amplitude and an UNCAPPED 1/(V.N) (the overhang is pure geometry and knows nothing about --pom_shell_cap; capping it would under-clip exactly at the grazing angles where the overhang is worst). Outside the domain (own box OR a sibling's, same union as the lateral-exit test) means the flat wall does not cover this pixel, so the lane is lid overhang and is killed. This reproduces the TESSELLATION bake's convention exactly — that path pins its patch-border verts to zero displacement, so its relief never crosses the authored footprint either, which is why the reference has no overhang to compare against. Cost: 2 FMAs + one compare group per covered pixel, no per-step work. ON by default INSIDE --pom_shell (like --pom_shell_domain); --no-pom_shell_base_clip is the A/B. Inert without --pom_shell, so flags-off is byte-identical.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (pom_shell_base_clip_raw,"FDS_POM_SHELL_BASE_CLIP_RAW", 0,                              "parallax", "--pom_shell_base_clip A/B: take the base-plane crossing along the UNCAPPED view ray (1/(V.N) up to 64) instead of the march's --pom_shell_cap-limited one. Uncapped is the exact geometric footprint of an INFINITE affine patch, but at extreme grazing the crossing lands a world unit or more along the surface, where that model stops describing the authored geometry (the wall has ended, another patch has taken over, the UV chart is no longer the same one) and the clip eats real wall. Measured at greets t=5780, one 84-degree-incidence corridor wall: uncapped clipped 81 px PAST the flat wall's own edge. Default OFF = clip along the same capped ray the march shaded with, so the coverage test and the relief agree.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(pom_shell_merge_uv,     "FDS_POM_SHELL_MERGE_UV",       0.05f,                         "parallax", "--pom_shell MULTI-BOX DOMAIN (PomShell_Build; bake-time grouping, one extra compare group per sibling in the kernel and only for lanes that leave their own box). Edge adjacency alone leaves ONE physical surface split wherever the authoring interrupts it without sharing an edge — measured on greets, the floor is one plane cut into 6 patches by the doorway thresholds, and the residual grazing void sat exactly on those cuts (a ray crossing a threshold in UV left its patch and was discarded, though the stone physically continues). This groups patches that are on the SAME PLANE and whose UV rectangles abut/overlap within this many UV units (transitively), and gives each patch the others as SIBLING BOXES. The lateral-exit test then passes if the hit is inside the patch's own box OR any sibling's — the UNION OF THE BOXES, deliberately NOT their bounding box: the bounding box also swallows genuine OPENINGS between coplanar patches (a doorway in a wall), which measured at greets t=6097 killed the corner silhouette outright. 0 = off (edge adjacency only, one box per patch) — that is the pre-multi-box behaviour, kept for A/B. Default 0.05 UV; on greets the grouping is driven by patches whose boxes already OVERLAP, so 0.02 and 0.20 give the SAME grouping (rooms 67 patches -> 55 plane groups, floor 6 -> 1) and the same rendered result to the pixel — the default is not knife-edge. Measured effect at greets t=5780 (cone march, cap 2): grazing void 6175 px -> 404 px, while the t=6097 corner silhouette stays byte-identical to the single-box domain (the bounding-box variant destroyed it).")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (pom_horizon,            "FDS_POM_HORIZON",              0,                              "parallax", "S1c HORIZON-MAP RELIEF SELF-SHADOW (docs/S1_PIXEL_DISPLACEMENT_PLAN.md). Per texel of the height map, the elevation of the relief's OWN horizon in 8 tangent-space azimuths is baked to u8 sin(horizon) (MakeHorizonMap, disk-cached under Runtime/cache/, keyed on the height bytes + every bake parameter). At shading time each light's direction is taken into the pixel's tangent frame, its azimuth picks the two adjacent baked channels, and the light is attenuated where its elevation falls BELOW the interpolated horizon — so mortar cuts and block edges shadow themselves, and the shadow MOVES with the light. This is the only intra-wall shadow either displacement path can have: the PolyId shadow test is identity-only with one id per authored wall plane, so neither the tessellation bake nor the shell march can self-shadow relief. Being a pure SHADING term it serves BOTH paths, and it is baked from the FULL height field in both (under --greets_displace the POM input becomes the residual, but the geometry still carries the low band). Needs a HeightMap + a tangent (normal-mapped material). Default OFF = not baked, not sampled, byte-identical.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(pom_horizon_soft,       "FDS_POM_HORIZON_SOFT",         0.15f,                          "parallax", "--pom_horizon softness: the width, in sin(elevation), of the smoothstep between fully lit and fully shadowed around the baked horizon. 0 = a hard step (aliases badly on a quantized 8-azimuth bake); larger = a softer, more ambient-occlusion-like falloff that also hides the azimuth quantization. The transition runs from (sinHorizon - soft) to (sinHorizon + soft).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(pom_horizon_strength,   "FDS_POM_HORIZON_STRENGTH",     1.0f,                           "parallax", "--pom_horizon: how much of the light a fully-below-horizon pixel loses. 1 = full occlusion (the shadow goes to the ambient term only), 0 = the term is inert. Use it to dial the relief shadow against --ao_map_strength / --ao_direct, which cover the same groove darkening statically.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (pom_horizon_radius,     "FDS_POM_HORIZON_RADIUS",       128,                            "parallax", "--pom_horizon BAKE parameter: the mip-0 scan radius in TEXELS (halved per mip). It should reach about 1.5 block pitches — far enough that a block edge shadows across the next mortar cut, short enough that the bake stays seconds. Part of the disk-cache key, so changing it rebakes rather than reusing a stale file.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (pom_horizon_viz,        "FDS_POM_HORIZON_VIZ",          0,                              "parallax", "--pom_horizon diagnostic: replace the surface's lighting with the horizon TERM itself (white = unoccluded, black = fully below the horizon in every contributing light's azimuth), so the shadow's shape and its motion with the light are visible without albedo or ambient hiding them.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (pom_shell_patch_dump,   "FDS_POM_SHELL_PATCH_DUMP",     0,                              "parallax","--pom_shell diagnostic: print every built patch's id, face count, plane and UV box from PomShell_Build. This is how you see what --pom_shell_merge_uv did (patch count before/after, and which UV seams remain). Init-time only, off by default.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (pom_seam_census,        "FDS_POM_SEAM_CENSUS",          0,                              "parallax","S1d-1 PATCH-BOUNDARY TOPOLOGY + SEAM CLASSIFICATION (docs/S1D_CLOSED_SHELL_PLAN.md), DIAGNOSTIC, default OFF, no behaviour change. In PomShell_Build, after the patch union-find, walks every boundary edge of every patch and classifies it by POSITION-COINCIDENT adjacency against EVERY face in the scene (face indices are not comparable across arms and the mesh is angle-split before this point, so position is the only robust key): COPLANAR CONTINUATION (a neighbour lies in the same plane -- a ray must continue, a hole here is always wrong), ANGLED-IN (the neighbouring surface folds toward this patch's front side: a concave corner, the ray should enter the neighbour's shell), ANGLED-OUT (convex fold: the surface turns away, exit to the scene is a TRUE silhouette) and TRUE BOUNDARY (nothing continues; this is where side faces belong). Edges with no shared-edge neighbour get a geometric OUTWARD PROBE (the edge midpoint pushed into the plane beyond the patch, tested against every triangle) so T-junctions and non-edge-adjacent coplanar abutments -- the doorway thresholds -- are found too. Prints [POM-SEAM] per edge, [POM-SEAM-PATCH] per patch, [POM-SEAM-SUM] per class (count + total world length), [POM-SEAM-XFORM] the UV transform across each continuation seam (identity / scale / rotation / mirror, so S1d-2 knows what a hand-off costs) and [POM-SEAM-DOMAIN] the patch UV boxes and sibling lists in a machine-readable form. RUN IT UNDER --pom_recess_only (or --pom_shell_lid_probe): with the lid offset non-zero the angle-split corner vertices move in different directions and position coincidence -- the whole basis of the topology -- is destroyed. A warning is printed if it is not.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (pom_seam_viz,           "FDS_POM_SEAM_VIZ",             0,                              "parallax","S1d-1 SEAM VIZ, default 0 = OFF. Draws every classified patch-boundary edge (see --pom_seam_census, which it implies) over the final frame as a depth-tested coloured line: GREEN = coplanar continuation, ORANGE = angled-in (concave, should enter the neighbour's shell), MAGENTA = angled-out (convex, true silhouette), RED = true boundary (nothing continues -- where side faces belong). 1 = all classes, 2 = coplanar only, 3 = angled only, 4 = true boundary only. Main view only, post-tonemap, so it is byte-null with the flag off and never reaches an offscreen pass.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (pom_shell_side_faces,   "FDS_POM_SHELL_SIDE_FACES",     0,                              "parallax","S1d-2a CLOSED SHELL -- SIDE FACES at convex ridges and true boundaries (docs/S1D_CLOSED_SHELL_PLAN.md), default 0 = OFF, byte-identical. The shell rasters only the LID of the relief slab and substitutes a UV-BOX test for the missing sides, so a ray that leaves its patch has no answer: killing it gives the user-reported black gashes, clamping gives the grazing smear. S1d-1 measured where those pixels sit: 72.9% at a CONVEX (angled-out) ridge, 11.9% at a true boundary, 15.1% at a concave fold, 0.004% coplanar. At a convex ridge the side face of the slab IS THE NEIGHBOURING PATCH'S PLANE, and that plane LEANS OUTWARD WITH DEPTH: the solid is the INTERSECTION of the two half-spaces, so at depth d below the authored plane the material extends cot(fold)*d PAST the ridge line. The vertical UV box therefore cuts the shell short exactly where the user sees the defect. This flag replaces the box's four sides with the real side planes, derived at bake time from the same position-coincidence topology --pom_seam_census builds (no new authoring): per patch and per box side, the dominant boundary class and a LEAN in UV per unit slab height, lean = (N_nb . N_patch) * slabWorldAmp / |N_nb . dP/daxis|, clamped to [0, uvAmp * --pom_shell_cap] (a lean above the ray's own lateral rate can never bind, so the cap costs nothing). TRUE BOUNDARY and concave folds keep lean 0 = the vertical extrusion, which is the correct closed-shell side there (at a free edge the wall really ends; at a concave fold the material is a UNION and the neighbour's plane does not bound this shell at all -- that case needs the hand-off, S1d-2c). The domain test then reads uMax + lean*(h0-h) instead of uMax, evaluated at BOTH the marched hit height and the entry height (h0 = the slab height of the AUTHORED plane: 1 under --pom_recess_only, 0.5 under the lid). Under recess-only the entry term is identically zero and every lean is >= 0, so the test is PURELY ADDITIVE -- it can only rescue pixels the box killed, never kill one it kept. Under the LID the same formula also NARROWS the shell above the authored plane by the same lean, which is what --pom_shell_base_clip approximates with a single lateral offset; this flag therefore forces that clip OFF and does the job geometrically. 1 = lean from the DOMINANT class of each box side (by boundary length). 2 = CONSERVATIVE: the minimum lean over every segment on that side, so a side that mixes convex with anything else gets 0. 3 = mode 1's lean but ONLY BELOW THE AUTHORED PLANE (dh = max(0, h0-h)): above h0 the neighbour's own SHELL bounds this one, not the neighbour's authored plane, and with --pom_shell_weld the two lids already MEET at the ridge, so the correct side face above h0 is the plain box. Modes 1/2 narrow the shell above h0 instead and KILL lid rays that have real material under them -- measured at 67 816 px of pure black over the 13 review poses. Mode 3 is therefore PURELY ADDITIVE under the lid as well as under recess-only, and it makes --pom_shell_side_entry inert by construction (the domain at hEnter >= h0 is the plain box the pixel is inside anyway), so the two are alternatives, not a stack. Requires --pom_shell. Implies the seam-topology build (seconds of scene init, no per-frame cost) but not its per-edge printing.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (pom_shell_side_edge,    "FDS_POM_SHELL_SIDE_EDGE",      0,                              "parallax","S1d-2b PER-BOUNDARY-CLASS EDGE POLICY, default 0 = OFF (--pom_recess_edge applies globally, as before). S1d-1 measured that the right terminal action for a ray that leaves the shell is NOT the same at every boundary. At a TRUE BOUNDARY the wall genuinely ends and the geometry behind already fills the pixel -- all 95 546 such pixels void ZERO under a discard -- so the recess arm's CLAMP is the bug there: it paints flat wall over a hole you are meant to see through. At a convex ridge or a concave fold there is nothing behind in this scene, so a discard is the black gash. This flag makes the terminal action a table lookup on the class of the side the ray exited through (the same per-side table --pom_shell_side_faces bakes). 1 = DISCARD at TRUE BOUNDARY, --pom_recess_edge everywhere else. 2 = also LAND ON THE SIDE FACE at convex ridges and concave folds instead of falling back to the flat wall: the landed UV and the marched height are taken at the ray's own crossing of the leaning side plane, i.e. a real face at a real depth rather than the un-marched geometric UV. Needs --pom_shell_side_faces (that is what builds the per-side classes). Inert without --pom_shell.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (pom_shell_weld,          "FDS_POM_SHELL_WELD",           1,                              "parallax","S1d-2d WATERTIGHT LID -- weld the lid OFFSET DIRECTION across position-coincident vertices (docs/S1D_CLOSED_SHELL_PLAN.md). DEFAULT 1 = ON since S1d-2f: it is INERT unless --pom_shell (itself default OFF) selects the lid, so no shipping render and no pinned scene moves, and within a lid arm the unwelded mesh is TORN, not a different rendering choice -- measured 232 612 -> 14 163 void px over the 16 review poses (-93.9%), removing a FULL-HEIGHT BLACK GASH between wall panels at p9 t5958 and the wall/floor wedge at p5 t5963 (docs/img/s1d_2f/weld_gash_*.png). 0 = OFF restores the torn lid for A/B. Do NOT use 4 or 6: their true mitre optimises per-plane offset exactness, which ShellH already models, and pays for it by multiplying TANGENTIAL SLIDE by 1/cos(half-fold) -- measured x2.51 void on the weld=5-vs-6 pair (docs/S1D_CLOSED_SHELL_PLAN.md S1d-2f). PomShell_Build moves every target vertex along ITS OWN Vertex::N. On greets that is not a shared normal: MakeFacesIndependentByAngle has split the mesh so completely that 'rooms' owns 588 verts over 196 faces -- EXACTLY 3 per face, nothing shared -- so the two copies of one corner move along two DIFFERENT normals and the mesh TEARS OPEN at every convex ridge. Measured over the 13 review poses (void = z==0): the lid arm voids 413 100 px; with the domain kill AND the base clip both off it still voids 228 411; with --no-parallax (no march at all) 198 704; with --pom_shell_lid_probe (offset forced to 0, everything else identical) ZERO. Void against offset 0.02/0.06/0.18/0.36 world runs 19 416/58 665/198 131/383 364 -- LINEAR in the offset. The lid void is therefore a SLIT IN THE GEOMETRY whose width is the offset, not the march, not the domain test and not the base clip; side-face ENTRY cannot reach it because there is no fragment there to enter. This flag extrudes along a normal SHARED by every copy of a position (their mean, normalized), which is what shell maps do: adjacent prisms keep a common side and the offset surface stays watertight -- a MITRED corner instead of a wedge. Vertex::N is untouched, so shading is unchanged; only the offset direction is welded, and Vertex::ShellH already models the consequence (a corner vertex reaches only cos(half-fold) of the offset above its own plane, which is the ndv term the stamp already computes, so the march enters at the true geometric height). Inert under --pom_recess_only and --pom_shell_lid_probe (no offset to weld). Prints [POM-SHELL-WELD] with how many positions carry 2+ copies and how far the weld redirects them. 1 = weld inside the target material. 2 = also PIN (leave unmoved, ShellH 0.5) every position a NON-TARGET face shares, which is the junction the per-material weld cannot close -- greets' wall meets an UNSHELLED ceiling and a separately-built floor, and the wall moving into the room while the neighbour stays put opens a slit there (measured residue after weld=1: 13 986 px of 14 163, with the march disabled). That is what --pom_shell_pin was for; the older flag never fires because it keys on VERTEX SHARING inside one TriMesh and the angle-split mesh shares nothing (measured: 0 pinned on greets). 2 is NOT usable as an arm: on greets it pins 289 of 588 'rooms' verts and ALL 90 of 'floor', so the floor gets no shell at all. 3 (S1d-2e) = CROSS-MATERIAL WELD, the fix for that same residue without pinning anything. PomShell_Build runs ONCE PER MATERIAL, so mode 1's bucket can only average normals within the material being built and 'rooms'/'floor' still extrude their shared wall-floor corners in different directions. Mode 3 has Initialize_Greets call PomShell_WeldPrepare once, BEFORE the first build moves a vertex, to bucket vertex normals by POSITION across EVERY material that is about to be shelled; it also memoises the offset VECTOR applied at each position, so two materials with different amplitudes (uvAmp x world-per-UV is 0.18 vs 1.11 world on greets unless --pom_shell_world_amp_set pins them) still land a shared corner on exactly ONE point. Prints [POM-SHELL-WELD3] with the cross-material position count and how many verts reused a prior material's delta. Same inertness as mode 1 (recess-only / lid probe / flag off are byte-identical). 4 = mode 3 PLUS the TRUE MITRE. Modes 1 and 3 move a welded corner `off` along the MEAN normal, so each incident face's plane is only offset by off*cos(half-fold) and the boundary of every welded surface RETRACTS laterally; measured on greets, mode 3 alone pulls the whole floor 0.064 world in from its UNSHELLED neighbours and opens more slit than the wall/floor junction it closes (lid void 14 163 -> 24 334 over the 13 review poses). Mode 4 divides the offset by that cosine, which puts the vertex on the INTERSECTION of the offset planes: every incident plane is offset by exactly `off`, nothing retracts, and Vertex::ShellH returns to 1 because the vertex genuinely is on the lid. Clamped at 3x so a near-180-degree fold cannot fling a vertex across the room (a clamped corner degrades to mode 3 and its ShellH records the height it actually reached).")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (pom_shell_lid_edge,      "FDS_POM_SHELL_LID_EDGE",       0,                              "parallax","S1d-2d LID EDGE POLICY (docs/S1D_CLOSED_SHELL_PLAN.md), default 0 = OFF, byte-identical. Under the LID model every shell failure is a DISCARD, and the largest of them is the LATERAL EXIT -- measured with --pom_shell_weld and --pom_shell_side_faces on, the exit kill alone owns ~152 000 px of the arm void over the 13 review poses. That is the same defect --pom_recess_edge=0 removed from the recess arm: a hole punched through solid wall at an INTERNAL seam, where the wall demonstrably continues. The lid keeps it a discard only because the lid can cover screen area the authored wall does not -- and that population is already identified separately, by --pom_shell_base_clip (genuine overhang) and by --pom_shell_side_entry (a ray that is never inside the closed shell). 1 = CLAMP the lateral exit to the flat surface (geometric UV + entry depth, exactly --pom_recess_edge=0) and keep every other kill a discard: a ray that marched the WHOLE slab without crossing the height field still dies, which is the see-through in the mortar valleys and the entire reason the lid model exists; so does a side-entry miss and so does lid overhang. 2 (S1d-4) = mode 1 WITHOUT the crossed-first requirement: clamp EVERY lateral exit, crossed or not. WHY: at an INTERNAL fold (corridor bend, t=5518 user repro) both lids' grazing rays exit the chart laterally WITHOUT ever crossing -- the stone they would hit lives in the NEIGHBOUR'S chart, which this march cannot address -- so under mode 1 both walls' kill bands union into a black slit that the (edge-on, ~0 px) prism side quad cannot cover. The see-through populations are structurally untouched by the wider clamp: silhouette crenellation is BASE-CLIP discards (lid overhang, baseOK=false) and mortar-valley see-through is IN-DOMAIN no-cross (domOK=true); neither is a lateral exit. Cost: a clamped-to-flat band instead of relief continuity where charts meet -- flat wall texels, not black. 3 (S1d-4, THE FOLD FIX) = mode 2 PLUS: a lane whose march CROSSED the height field KEEPS ITS HIT, even when the domain box or the base-footprint clip disqualifies it. Path-code census at the t=5518 corridor-fold slit: ALL 2 078 defect px were coneHit + DISCARD(baseclip[+domain]) -- the march found stone (the texture tiling past the chart edge, which at a gentle fold is visually the neighbour panel's continuation) and the base clip threw the hit away; mode 2's clamp never fired because clampable requires baseOK. Both see-through populations are lanes that NEVER crossed (silhouette crenellation = lid-overhang no-cross, mortar-valley = in-domain no-cross), so keeping crossed hits cannot paint over them -- measured at t=5877 the crenellated silhouette survives, and t=5518 drops 2 092 -> 14 black px, t=2980 corner 848+531 -> 1+9. Ignored under --pom_recess_only (that mode has its own --pom_recess_edge). Inert without --pom_shell.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(pom_shell_keep_uv,      "FDS_POM_SHELL_KEEP_UV",        0.125f,                         "parallax","--pom_shell_lid_edge=3 OVERSHOOT GATE: the maximum UV distance (L-infinity) OUTSIDE the patch box a CROSSED hit may land and still be KEPT; farther hits fall back to the discard. WHY A GATE AT ALL: without it (equivalent to a huge value) mode 3 keeps GRAZING WRAPS -- rays that tiled many blocks past the chart before crossing -- and their marched Z is deep enough (dz up to amp*cap = 0.72 world at the review arm's 0.18/4) that the wall Z-LOSES to the geometry behind it: measured at t=5963 the grazing wall VANISHED (91 031 black px vs 776) and at t=5743 the doorway was PAINTED OVER (black 85 017 -> 62, stone where the opening should be). The fold hits the mode exists for land a FRACTION OF A BLOCK outside the box (the t=5518 census: ~25 texels); the destructive wraps land blocks away. Default 0.125 ~= half a block pitch on the greets stone (256 texels of a ~1024 chart -> 0.25 UV per block). Same UV units as the patch box (tiled, pre-scale). Only read at --pom_shell_lid_edge>=3.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (pom_shell_entry_flat,   "FDS_POM_SHELL_ENTRY_FLAT",     0,                              "parallax","S1d-7 DIAGNOSTIC, default 0 = OFF, byte-null: stamp Vertex::ShellH = 1 on every moved lid vertex instead of the corner-corrected 0.5 + 0.5*ndv, i.e. tell the march every pixel enters AT THE LID. This is the CAUSAL A/B for the QUAD-DIAGONAL CREASE the user has reported since the early shell work and that two investigations failed to reproduce (repro: t=2993 on the authored camera; --wire_viz=1 shows the block is ONE QUAD, TWO TRIANGLES and the crease lies EXACTLY on the triangulation diagonal; plain parallax has no crease, minimal --pom_shell does; not the normal map, not the prism, not --pom_shell_lid_edge, not displacement). MECHANISM UNDER TEST: PomShell_Build moves each vertex by off along its SMOOTHED normal, so the height it actually reaches above its incident faces planes is off*ndv with ndv = the average of N_v . N_face over the incident target faces, and ShellH records that as the march ENTRY HEIGHT. The rasterizer transports ShellH as a per-vertex interpolant (shZ1 / da[11] / db[11]), and a per-vertex scalar interpolated over a quad as TWO TRIANGLES is only C1 across the shared diagonal when h1 + h3 == h2 + h4 -- a bilinear-vs-two-linear kink. Where a quad corners carry different ndv the entry height therefore KINKS ON THE DIAGONAL, and the march starts at a different depth either side of it. Forcing ShellH = 1 removes the ndv dependence entirely: if the crease is this, it must vanish here, and if it does not the mechanism is refuted. NOT A FIX -- it is exact only where ndv == 1 (a flat patch interior); at a genuine corner it claims the ray enters at the lid when it really enters lower, so the march starts above the surface and the corner relief shifts. The real fix has to keep the corner correction while making the field CONTINUOUS: the entry height is an AFFINE function of world position (h(P) = 0.5 + N.(P - P_authored)/worldAmp) and both triangles of a quad share the authored plane, so stamping each corner against THAT face own normal rather than the incident-face AVERAGE reproduces one affine field across both triangles by construction. Inert without --pom_shell; ignored under --pom_recess_only (which stamps ShellH = 1 already).")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (pom_shell_lid_true_edge,"FDS_POM_SHELL_LID_TRUE_EDGE",  0,                              "parallax","S1d-6 PER-BOUNDARY-CLASS EXIT UNDER THE LID, default 0 = OFF, byte-null. DIAGNOSTIC: the shipping recommendation is 0 -- see the MEASURED REJECTION below. The per-class terminal action S1d-2b built for the recess arm (--pom_shell_side_edge) is never applied under the LID (its sideKill lives inside the --pom_recess_only branch), so a lid ray that leaves the patch is treated the same wherever it left. This brings the table to the lid so the idea could be MEASURED. Value is a BITMASK over the boundary class of the box side the landed UV left through -- bit0 = the TRUE-BOUNDARY SUB-INTERVAL (Material::PomShellSideTrue; the only entry that is not a dominant-class lookup, and the right one for free edges because a free edge is a MINORITY of the box side it lands on: measured on greets, 9.875 of 1847.73 world of the rooms boundary is TRUE), bit1 = class TRUE, bit2 = class CONVEX/angled-out, bit3 = class CONCAVE/fold, bit4 = class COPLANAR, bit5 = unattributed -- and a lane exiting across a selected class is FORCED TO DISCARD: removed from --pom_shell_lid_edge=3 crossed-hit keep AND from the lateral-exit clamp. MEASURED at the t=5877 silhouette (tools/greets_silhouette.py, 1920x1080): bit2 (CONVEX) is the class that owns it -- straightness std 0.35 -> 5.02 px, i.e. the crenellation comes back, and at that ONE pose void/black stay 1/1. But over the 19 review poses bit2 costs 127 043 void / 125 643 black (t=5743 62 495, t=5843 16 607, t=5854 12 869, the t=5958 grazing family ~32 000) -- because MOST convex ridges in greets are not silhouettes at all: the neighbouring wall is FRONT-facing and the shell legitimately continues past the ridge, exactly the black gash S1d-2b warned the discard would be there. So the CLASS ALONE IS NOT THE DISCRIMINATOR; what would be is whether the ridge partner is BACKFACING at this pose, which is view-dependent and is not baked today. bits 0/1 (genuine free edges) are the geometrically safe subset -- S1d-1 measured all 95 546 true-boundary px void ZERO under a discard -- but they do NOT reach the t=5877 silhouette: only 2 of 268 rooms box sides carry a TRUE sub-interval at all, because the greets walls are authored as CLOSED BOXES and that silhouette is an outside CORNER. Arms the side-table bake by itself; it does NOT arm --pom_shell_side_faces leaning side planes (pomSideFaces still gates on the LEAN pointer, which stays behind that flag). S1d-9 ADDS bit6 (64) = CONVEX AND ITS PARTNER BACKFACING, which is the missing discriminator: PomShell_Build now bakes each box side's convex-ridge PARTNER NORMAL in the owning face's own TANGENT FRAME (Material::PomShellSidePartner, 3 floats per side), so the kernel tests nT*VtT + nB*VtB + nN*VtN < 0 per pixel -- N_b.V < 0, the partner is backfacing, the wall really ends here -- with no camera and no matrix, so it stays correct in the mirror/env/shadow passes too. A side whose convex partners disagree (length-weighted coherence < 0.90) bakes to all-zero, the dot is then exactly 0 and the side falls back to KEEP, which cannot gash. Self-check: with N_b = N_a the expression collapses to VtN < 0, 'this face is itself backfacing'. Use 64, not 4. Requires --pom_shell; ignored under --pom_recess_only.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(pom_shell_keep_uv_overhang,"FDS_POM_SHELL_KEEP_UV_OVERHANG", -1.0f,                   "parallax","S1d-6 THE OVERHANG KEEP GATE -- the SILHOUETTE CRENELLATION fix (docs/S1D_CLOSED_SHELL_PLAN.md S1d-6). A SECOND --pom_shell_keep_uv used ONLY for lanes the BASE CLIP rejected, i.e. pixels the LID covers that the AUTHORED wall does not. <0 = one global gate for everything, byte-identical. THE DEFECT: at t=5877 cam 15.5497618,3.4823668,-59.5607719,-0.524191499,-0.0974417627,0.846008122 the near wall LEFT SILHOUETTE is a razor-straight vertical cut 28 px OUTSIDE the authored wall, where the tessellation bake at the same pose steps per block with background showing between them. Measured with tools/greets_silhouette.py (per-row first-near pixel off the z16 dump, residual about a Theil-Sen line, 1920x1080): straightness std FLAT 0.46 px, tessellation@0.18 2.43, tessellation@0.30 4.00, the S1d-5b.10 shell arm 0.35 -- THE SHELL IS STRAIGHTER THAN THE UNDISPLACED WALL. The void/black metric family is structurally blind to this (silhouette see-through against a LIT background is neither z==0 nor pure black), and S1d-5 crenellation PIXEL-IDENTICAL / see-through preserved compared shell mode 2 against shell mode 1 -- two arms carrying the SAME defect -- never against tessellation. MECHANISM, from the --pom_path_viz=2 plane over the 28-px band (17 080 px sampled): 10 719 read coneHit / action=KEEP / why=domain / exit=uMin. PomShell_Build offsets every lid vertex by worldAmp/2 along N, so at a patch border the lid covers screen the authored plane never did (+20 307 px of near-wall coverage vs flat here; the silhouette offset is EXACTLY LINEAR IN AMPLITUDE -- -14 / -28 / -56 px at --pom_shell_world_amp_set 0.09 / 0.18 / 0.36). Rays entering that band march inward, leave the UV box through uMin, and find stone in the height map TILING PAST THE WALL END; --pom_shell_lid_edge=3 crossed-hit keep then keeps every one of them, because its gate tests only pomCrossed and the --pom_shell_keep_uv overshoot and never asks whether the pixel is over authored wall at all. The wall does not end at a straight line because the blocks end there -- it ends at a straight line because THE LID does. Measured consequences: (a) --pom_shell_base_clip alone does NOT fix it (silhouette byte-identical with the clip on, std 0.35 / offset -28, because keepHit overrides baseOK by construction) and at --pom_shell_lid_edge=3 the clip is a NO-OP on the 19 review poses (10 void / 73 black with it on OR off, same binary) -- which retires S1d-5b.10 +43 black without it; (b) setting the SINGLE keep_uv to 0 fixes the silhouette (std 2.26) but reopens the internal-seam see-through the gate exists for (t=5877 void 1 -> 549). Hence the SPLIT. AT 0: in the overhang band a crossed hit survives only if it landed INSIDE the patch own UV box -- a genuine block of THIS wall standing proud -- and everything else discards, so the background shows BETWEEN the blocks. MEASURED at t=5877: std 2.26 / p95 5.00 / area 1142 against tessellation@0.18 2.43 / 5.00 / 1045 at MATCHED world amplitude -- the closest any arm gets. NOTE the shell does something the tessellation bake CANNOT: its blocks stand PROUD of the authored footprint (tessellation pins its patch-border verts to zero displacement, so its relief only ever cuts inward), which is why the fixed silhouette straddles the authored line instead of sitting inside it. STILL OPEN, stated plainly: over the 19 review poses this costs 152 662 void / 146 984 black, concentrated at the GRAZING poses (t=5743 60 668, the t=5958 family ~32 000, t=5843 18 127, t=5854 14 087), because the base clip walks the ray to the authored plane with 1/(V.N) and once that is cap-bound the crossing lands a world unit or more along the surface -- the same failure --pom_shell_base_clip_raw documents -- so out there the overhang band is most of the wall, not a border strip. NOT SHIPPABLE until that is separated; see docs/S1D_CLOSED_SHELL_PLAN.md S1d-6 for the candidate discriminators. Only read when --pom_shell_base_clip is on and --pom_shell_lid_edge>=3.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (pom_shell_side_entry,   "FDS_POM_SHELL_SIDE_ENTRY",     0,                              "parallax","S1d-2d SIDE-FACE ENTRY -- the missing half of the closed shell (docs/S1D_CLOSED_SHELL_PLAN.md), default 0 = OFF, byte-identical. --pom_shell_side_faces gave the slab four LEANING side planes but used them only as an EXIT test: a lid ray that starts OUTSIDE them is KILLED. Geometrically it does not miss the solid -- at a convex ridge the side plane leans OUTWARD with depth, so a ray outside it at the lid crosses INTO the shell lower down THROUGH THE SIDE FACE. Killing instead of entering is why side faces made the LID (protrusion) arm worse: void 413 100 -> 468 868 over the 13 review poses, and 933 535 with the base clip replaced. With this flag the ray and all four side planes -- both AFFINE in the slab height h -- are intersected as one convex slab clip (side k requires a_k + b_k*h <= 0; b>0 bounds the entry height from above, b<0 from below), and the march STARTS at that entry height with the UV at that crossing instead of at the lid. A ray whose interval is EMPTY misses the closed shell entirely: that is a TRUE silhouette and the lane is killed, which is the see-through the lid model exists for. The existing exit test is unchanged and still runs; only the ENTRY-side half of --pom_shell_side_faces' domain test is superseded (testing a lid point that is no longer where the ray enters is meaningless). Nothing serialises: hStart was already a per-lane Vec8f (hEnter is interpolated per pixel), so the 8-wide masked-store structure is untouched -- the cost is 4 divides + ~16 FMAs + 8 selects per covered shell pixel. Depth is untouched too: the S1a write is dz = (hitH - hEnter)*A*Vz/(V.N), i.e. relative to the RASTERED surface, and entry moves where the march begins, not where the fragment is. INERT under --pom_recess_only (hEnter == h0 == 1, so the entry test reduces to the plain box test the pixel passes by construction) and inert with every lean 0. 1 = on. Requires --pom_shell_side_faces (that is what bakes the leans); A/B it against --pom_shell_base_clip, which approximates the same narrowing with a single lateral offset.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (pom_shell_world_amp,    "FDS_POM_SHELL_WORLD_AMP",      0,                           "parallax","S1b P0 WORLD-CONSISTENT SHELL AMPLITUDE, default OFF (docs/S1_DISCREPANCY_INVENTORY.md S8). The shell's amplitude is authored in UV (--parallax_strength x Material::ParallaxScale) and each face converts it to WORLD by its own world-per-UV, so the relief's world depth follows the UV chart: measured on greets, the 'rooms' authored planes span 0.180..0.226 world (x1.26, the tilted walls' planar projection stretches their chart) while 'floor' sits at 1.113..1.130 world -- 6.2x the wall it meets, from two ParallaxScale numbers (0.10 and 0.25) that were tuned as UV OFFSET magnitudes for flat parallax and then reused as a slab DEPTH. The tessellation bake has the opposite semantics (--greets_displace_amp is WORLD and constant), so the two arms cannot agree on how deep the stone is. With this flag ON the builder picks each material's world amplitude as uvAmp x its AREA-WEIGHTED MEDIAN world-per-UV (so the material's overall slab depth is unchanged and only the per-face variance is removed -- the A/B is about the distribution, not the scale), offsets every lid vertex by exactly worldAmp/2 (one authored plane -> one lid plane) and the rasterizer derives each triangle's UV amplitude as worldAmp/w so the depth write's world amplitude is constant. Use --pom_shell_world_amp_set to force ONE world amplitude on every shelled material instead of the per-material median, which is what makes the wall and the floor agree. Default OFF = the UV semantics, byte-identical.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(pom_shell_world_amp_set,"FDS_POM_SHELL_WORLD_AMP_SET",  0.0f,                           "parallax","--pom_shell_world_amp: force this WORLD amplitude (full 0..1 height range, world units) on every shelled material instead of deriving each material's own from its median world-per-UV. 0 = derive per material. Set it to --greets_displace_amp (0.3) to put the per-pixel shell on the same world depth as the tessellation bake. Ignored unless --pom_shell_world_amp is on.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (pom_shell_census,       "FDS_POM_SHELL_CENSUS",         0,                              "parallax","DIAGNOSTIC PER-FACE AMPLITUDE CENSUS (docs/S1_DISCREPANCY_INVENTORY.md S8), default OFF, init-time print only -- changes no pixel. The shell's relief amplitude is authored in UV units (Material::PomShellUvAmp = --parallax_strength x ParallaxScale) and each face converts it to WORLD by its OWN world-per-UV density, so two faces of the SAME authored surface displace by DIFFERENT world distances whenever their UV charts differ in scale. This flag prints, per displaced/shelled material: per-face |dP/du|, |dP/dv|, their geometric mean w, the UV anisotropy sqrt(|dP/du|/|dP/dv|), the implied WORLD amplitude uvAmp x w, the lid offset actually applied, a histogram of the world amplitude, and -- grouped by AUTHORED plane -- how many distinct LID plane constants that one authored plane turned into and how far apart they are. The same census runs in the tessellation bake (DisplaceStoneSubdiv), where the amplitude is authored in WORLD units and is therefore constant, so the two tables are directly comparable: the bake's per-plane spread is the relief it CARRIES at its lattice, the shell's is the amplitude itself. Also prints the height-map mip each shell face's march actually sampled (a raster-time histogram, since the march takes the face's albedo mip unless --pom_height_mip pins it).")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (snapshot_ss,            "FDS_SNAPSHOT_SS",              1,                              "parallax","DIAGNOSTIC supersample factor for the HEADLESS SNAPSHOT path only (docs/S1_DISCREPANCY_INVENTORY.md): render at N times the rev.cfg resolution per axis, so a converged reference can be captured with 16x (N=2), 16x (N=4) etc. sub-pixel coverage and box-downsampled offline for comparison against the 1x arms. Never touches the live window. 1 = off. Memory and time scale with N squared, and the G-buffer planes are allocated at the supersampled size.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (pom_ref_march,          "FDS_POM_REF_MARCH",            0,                              "parallax","CONVERGED BRUTE-FORCE REFERENCE MARCH (docs/S1_DISCREPANCY_INVENTORY.md), DIAGNOSTIC ONLY, default OFF. Replaces both the naive and the cone march with --pom_ref_steps UNIFORM steps down the entire relief slab plus a secant solve on the bracketing pair, with no cone map, no --parallax_pom_lod fade and no --parallax_pom_quarter lane sharing. It exists because NEITHER shipping path is ground truth for the relief and the campaign kept using one as the yardstick for the other: the cone march reaches the crossing in a few taps but through an approximate baked cone map under a step budget; the tessellation bake carries the height field only at its subdivision lattice, samples it at --greets_displace_mip rather than mip 0, pins patch borders to zero displacement, and still applies the FULL normal map on top of geometry that already carries the low band. A uniform march with hundreds of steps has none of those approximations left: its residual is the height map own point sampling. Combine with --pom_shell (so the ray enters through the lid and the slab is the built geometry), --pom_shell_cap=64 (the cap is a grazing quality hack, not part of the surface definition, so a reference must not have one) and --snapshot_ss for a supersampled capture. Cost is roughly --pom_ref_steps height gathers per covered pixel, i.e. seconds per frame — which is why this is a reference and not a rendering mode. Default OFF = the shipping march, byte-identical.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (pom_ref_steps,          "FDS_POM_REF_STEPS",            512,                            "parallax","--pom_ref_march: number of UNIFORM march steps across the slab. The height quantisation of the landing is 1/N of the slab BEFORE the secant solve and float-exact after it, while the LATERAL quantisation, which is what actually limits the reference at grazing, is rayScale/N UV — at the greets walls (amp 0.030 UV, 1/(V.N) up to 64) 512 steps put the worst-case lateral step at ~0.004 UV, about 4 texels of the 1024 map, and the secant closes that too as long as no thin feature was stepped OVER. Raise it (768/1024) and re-render to check convergence: if the image and the depth stop moving, the reference has converged; if they do not, report that instead of picking a number.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (face_id_dump,           "FDS_FACE_ID_DUMP",             0,                              "parallax","DIAGNOSTIC per-pixel FACE-OWNERSHIP plane (docs/S1_DISCREPANCY_INVENTORY.md), default OFF. The G-buffer's matID byte is far too coarse to answer 'which surface owns this pixel': every greets wall shares the material 'rooms', so a (matID before, matID after) classification cannot separate 'the neighbouring wall won the pixel' from 'the same wall shaded differently', and it can never say whether the winner is a face that ought to have been OCCLUDED by a nearer one. This allocates GBuffer::faceId and has the rasterizer stamp, per pixel and on exactly the same p_mask as every other G-buffer plane, a stable per-TRIANGLE key: bits 31..4 = the Face pointer >> 4 (the Face* passes through the frustum clipper and the mipmap poly-split unchanged, so it names the AUTHORED polygon however it was subdivided), bits 3..0 = the fan sub-triangle index. A greets snapshot then writes greets_t<t>_face.u32 beside the z16/matID dumps and prints a [FACEID] resolution table (key -> material, mesh, face index, world plane, vertex positions), with any key collision reported rather than hidden. Costs one 32-byte store per covered 8-pixel group plus the plane's memory; nothing reads it at runtime. Default OFF = plane not allocated, write skipped, byte-identical.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (greets_displace_crease_normals, "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_CREASE_NORMALS", 0, "greets", "CREASE-GATED post-bake shading normals (2026-08-28) - BUILT, MEASURED, AND REFUTED AS A DEFAULT by tools/bulge_detect.py: with the groove-shade retarget in place, crease30 is WORSE than the 80-deg weld on every region (pier front GBI 3.50 vs 2.82, curved 13.74 vs 8.56, seam 84.3 vs 80.7) - the 80-deg smooth is the correct anti-facet heal for the rough relief, and the panel-scale bulge was the groove-shade TARGET (see greets_displace_groove_shade_plane), not the smoothing angle. Kept default OFF as an A/B instrument. Original hypothesis text: the displaced stone's re-smooth pass (DisplaceStoneSmoothNormals) runs at greets_displace_crease_deg (default 30) instead of greets_displace_smooth (80). At 80 the position-bucket weld re-merges every block TOP with its grout BEVELS (at bake density every top vert touches a bevel face), so vertex normals ROLL across each block face and the wall shades as pillowed cushions - the month-long bulge, measured by tools/bulge_detect.py at GBI 5-63 deg/100px vs 0.00 on the bare wall while GEOMBOW pins the geometry flat. At 30 the tops keep their facet-heal smoothing (intra-top cell angles are below it) but the top-to-bevel edges (~40 deg) split hard: flat block faces, crisp bevel steps, dressed stone. The curved wall's authored inter-panel angles (~10-20 deg) stay smooth. TBN follows for free - the same pass welds the tangent over the same gated bucket and re-Gram-Schmidts. --no- restores the 80-deg path byte-exactly.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(greets_displace_crease_deg, "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_CREASE_DEG", 30.0f, "greets", "--greets_displace_crease_normals: the crease threshold in degrees for the post-bake shading-normal weld. Neighbour faces within this dihedral smooth together; above it the edge stays hard. 30 = block tops smooth internally, top-to-bevel (~40 deg) and wall junctions split. Sensitivity measured 2026-08-28: see docs/SESSION_STATE.md.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (greets_displace_groove_front_majority, "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_GROOVE_FRONT_MAJORITY", 1, "greets", "--greets_displace_groove_shade_plane SIGN FIX (2026-08-28, the bulge fix part 3 - the seam-column inversions): orient the groove-blend's patch-plane target by the PER-PATCH area-weighted majority of authored Face::N (the same majority-front rule that fixed the mitre bisector) instead of per-vert dot-with-the-RIDE. The ride is the polluted pre-bake smoothed field; wherever lid/corner pollution tips it past 90 deg off the true front (measured worst at seam columns), the old sign rule flipped the blend target INWARD and the final vertex normals inverted - normchain stage D measured vertN-vs-faceN median 22.6 deg / max 176 (anti-parallel) on the pier's second wall with the old rule, vs 0.9 pre-weld. FLD windings disagree ~17% per face, so the majority is taken patch-wide (the mitre round measured 220:46 / 414:83 - unambiguous), keyed by the bake's own sign-canonical plane key. --no- restores the ride-sign path byte-exactly.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (greets_displace_corner_front_ride, "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_CORNER_FRONT_RIDE", 1, "greets", "CORNER-vert RIDE retarget (2026-08-28, the bulge fix part 4 - the seam-column geometry): junction-column verts (incident faces spanning >1 authored plane) are skipped by the patch-plane ride retarget and still DISPLACE along the polluted pre-bake smoothed normal - the tearhunt round's 293-vert population displacing along their own tilted planes (63 deg out-of-wall at 80% amplitude) and the seam column's GEOMBOW 0.40u wobble (2.5x relief scale). Each corner vert takes the normalized sum of its incident patches' area-weighted majority fronts (the area bisector at the junction), consumed ONLY when it deviates from the smoothed ride by more than greets_displace_corner_front_deg - healthy columns (the 207-deg jamb, ride dots +0.93) are byte-identical by construction. --no- restores the polluted ride byte-exactly.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(greets_displace_corner_front_deg, "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_CORNER_FRONT_DEG", 25.0f, "greets", "--greets_displace_corner_front_ride: minimum angle (deg) between the smoothed ride and the majority-front mean before the override fires. Below it the smoothed ride is healthy and kept (byte-identity for clean columns); above it the ride is pollution-steered and replaced.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (greets_displace_groove_shade_plane, "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_GROOVE_SHADE_PLANE", 1, "greets", "--greets_displace_groove_shade RETARGET (2026-08-28, the bulge fix part 2): the carved-band shading restore blends toward the vert's authored PATCH-PLANE normal (the bake's own per-vert plane accumulation, oriented by the ride sign) instead of the pre-bake SMOOTHED ride normal. The smoothed field rolls across panels (lid-averaged at seams, arc-averaged on the curved wall - the root-cause round's polluted field), and blending toward it at every grout band re-injected that roll panel-wide: measured SWEEPh 5.85 deg on the flat pier front with the old target vs 0.97 with groove shade off entirely (tools/bulge_detect.py). Plane-less verts (authored corners) keep the old smoothed target. --no- restores the old target byte-exactly.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (bulge_dump,             "FDS_BULGE_DUMP",               0,                              "greets",  "DIAGNOSTIC, changes no pixel: greets snapshot writes the deferred G-buffer SHADING-NORMAL plane (greets_t<t>_norm.u32, u32[xres*yres], oct 16.16 VIEW-space, decode = oct_decode_u32 Mekalele.h) beside the color PPM. Data path for the bulge-detector instrument (tools/bulge_detect.py, 2026-08-28): it overlays the normals as an arrow grid on the render, plots scanlines, and measures panel-scale normal rotation - a flat dressed wall reads as a per-block square wave, a bulge as a smooth baseline drift the eye misreads as texture. Snapshot-path only; nothing reads it at runtime; default OFF = no write, byte-identical.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (refplane_dump,          "FDS_REFPLANE_DUMP",            0,                              "greets",  "DIAGNOSTIC, changes no pixel: greets snapshot writes greets_t<t>_refplane.txt — the camera actually used (ISource, Mat rows, PerspX/PerspY, CntrX/CntrY, res) plus every rooms*/floor* FACE (A/B/C world pos, per-corner UVs, face N) and per-material height-map info (useMip after the bake's clamp, amp/mip flags, mipMean over that mip) with the deswizzled mip texels beside it (greets_hm_<mat>_mip<K>.u8, row-linear). Ground-truth feed for tools/refdiff_detect.py: an offline plane+heightfield reference rendered per pixel and diffed against the bulge_dump normal plane — detector validation is against HIS verdict corpus (docs/BULGE_CORPUS.md), not any in-engine field. Dump the FACES from a --no-greets-displace run (authored coarse quads); the displaced arm's faces are subdivided/displaced and are NOT the reference.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (pom_shell_lid_probe,    "FDS_POM_SHELL_LID_PROBE",      0,                              "parallax","DIAGNOSTIC PROBE, NOT A RENDERING MODE (docs/S1_DISCREPANCY_INVENTORY.md). --pom_shell normally does TWO things at once: (1) PomShell_Build physically MOVES the target material's vertices outward along their normals by half the relief amplitude, so the walls are genuinely thicker in world space for EVERY consumer (main pass, per-frame shadow bake, mirror RTT, env probes), and (2) the rasterizer runs the true-ray shell march with its lateral-exit discard, base-plane clip and depth write. Any difference measured against the tessellation reference is therefore ambiguous between moved GEOMETRY and the MARCH. This flag forces the lid offset to ZERO — the vertices stay exactly on the authored plane — while leaving Vertex::ShellH, Material::PomShellUvAmp, the patch domains, the sibling boxes and every kernel path bit-for-bit identical to the real shell. Rendering it and diffing against the real shell attributes each discrepancy class to one cause or the other: what survives the probe is the march, what vanishes is the moved vertices. The probe's own image is NOT a proposal (with the geometry unmoved the relief hangs entirely BELOW the authored plane instead of straddling it, so its depths are biased by the full amplitude); it exists to be differenced, not to be looked at as a candidate look. Inert without --pom_shell. Default OFF.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (pom_recess_only,        "FDS_POM_RECESS_ONLY",          0,                              "parallax","S1 P2-A RECESS-ONLY DISPLACEMENT, default OFF; a MODE OF --pom_shell (inert without it, since PomShell_Build is what stamps the amplitude and the patch domains). The shipping shell rasters the LID of a slab that straddles the authored plane: PomShell_Build moves every target vertex OUT along its normal by half the relief amplitude, so the wall is physically thicker for the shadow bake, the mirror RTT and the env probes (measured: 30% of the per-frame shadow depth cube changes, docs/S1_DISCREPANCY_INVENTORY.md C6), the lid overhangs its own patch border, and a ray that leaves the patch MUST be discarded because the lid covers screen the authored wall never did. That discard fires at INTERNAL patch seams too -- the surface continues there and the shell punches a hole in it. Measured at the user's t=5743 review pose, 1080p, void = z==0 pixels: tessellation 3, flat POM 0, shell at cap 2 68 122, shell at cap 16 97 329 (docs/S1_DISCREPANCY_INVENTORY.md S10; the coordinator's smaller counts for the same arms are recorded there as NOT REPRODUCED, with both recipes stated). The literature-correct fix is a CLOSED shell (Hirche'04 prism side faces + cross-patch march continuation) so a ray only exits at a genuine silhouette; this flag is the cheap alternative that removes the need to exit at all. With it on: (1) the vertices are NOT moved -- the authored wall stays exactly where the artist put it, so every offscreen consumer sees the true wall by construction; (2) the height field's MAXIMUM sits AT the authored plane (Vertex::ShellH = 1 exactly, no smooth-normal corner correction, because an unmoved vertex is exactly on its faces' planes) and the whole relief carves INWARD, so the march starts at the authored surface and only ever goes deeper; (3) the depth write is therefore always >= the authored plane's depth, which removes S1a's 'a protruding block writes closer than the true wall' ordering hazard entirely; (4) --pom_shell_base_clip is forced OFF (it exists to remove LID overhang outside the authored footprint, and with the geometry unmoved there is none -- leaving it on would test the domain at a half-slab lateral offset and clip real wall). What this model CANNOT do: stone can no longer stand PROUD of the mortar, so it cannot break the polygon silhouette at a grazing edge and it cannot occlude a neighbour -- the wall reads as CARVED, not as BUILT, and its outline is exactly the authored polygon's. See --pom_recess_edge for what happens to a ray that still leaves the patch.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (pom_recess_edge,        "FDS_POM_RECESS_EDGE",          0,                              "parallax","--pom_recess_only: what to do with a ray that leaves the patch's UV domain (or never brackets) before it crosses the height field. Under the LID model this must be a DISCARD -- the lid covers screen area the authored wall does not, so a lane that leaves has no geometry under it and shading it would paint stone into thin air. Recess-only inverts that: every shaded pixel is covered by the AUTHORED polygon, which genuinely exists, so killing the pixel is no longer required and a hole is never the right answer. 0 (default) = CLAMP TO FLAT: fall back to the geometric (un-marched) UV and the authored plane's depth, i.e. that pixel shows the flat wall instead of a void. Exact and always in-domain (the pixel's own interpolated UV lies inside its patch box by construction), and it is the only fallback that cannot invent a surface. 1 = CLAMP UV: keep the marched height/depth but clamp the landed UV into the patch's own box (an approximation of 'shade the last valid sample'; ignores the sibling boxes, so it is a box clamp, not a union clamp). 2 = DISCARD, the lid model's mandatory kill, kept as the DIAGNOSTIC that makes the clamp's value measurable (it is what turns the void count back on). Ignored unless --pom_recess_only.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (pom_march_earlyout,     "FDS_POM_MARCH_EARLYOUT",       0,                              "parallax","S1 P1 (docs/S1_DISCREPANCY_INVENTORY.md S9), default OFF and BYTE-EXACT when on. Break the POM march loop the moment EVERY lane in the 8-wide group has bracketed its crossing. It cannot change a pixel: a bracketed lane already has its step forced to zero and all of its bracket state frozen behind the `search` mask, so every remaining iteration is arithmetic whose results are discarded. What it changes is the COST of a large --parallax_pom budget: the measured error surface says the shipping 8 steps is the dominant C1 term at the wall poses (t=6097 colour >12/255 falls 217 217 -> 92 359 and depth off >0.05 world falls 201 213 -> 10 005 going from 8 to 64 steps) while most pixels bracket in far fewer, so without an early-out the whole frame pays the worst pixel's budget. Applies to the cone march, the naive march and --pom_ref_march alike. Default OFF so the byte-exactness claim stays falsifiable by A/B rather than assumed.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (pom_cone_exact,         "FDS_POM_CONE_EXACT",           0,                              "parallax","S1 P1 EXACT PER-TEXEL CONE BAKE, default 0 = OFF (the legacy bake, byte-identical). The shipping cone map (MakeConeMap) max-pools the height to a 128x128 coarse grid and nearest-upsamples the result, which has two measured consequences: the max over an 8x8 block of a stone height map is near 255 almost everywhere, so hardly any cell has a TALLER cell to be bounded by and the baked cone comes out enormous (measured mean byte 38.6 = cone ratio 0.61 BEFORE the x4 --parallax_pom_relax, against a competing dlen of at most ~0.7 -- so the march takes a near-full gap every step and the cone map barely steers it, confirmed by --parallax_pom_relax 1 / 4 / 16 moving the t=6097 error by under 5%); and lateral distance is quantised to 1/128 UV, so a texel one texel from a cliff is told the cliff is eight texels away. 1 = exact CONSERVATIVE per-texel bake (the cone contains no geometry), 2 = exact RELAXED (Policarpo GPU Gems 3 ch.18 as corrected by Ban/Valasek/Balint/Vad, 'Robust Cone Step Mapping', EGSR 2024: the cone may penetrate the field and is bounded by where the ray LEAVES it again, so it is wider yet still guarantees the first bracket is the first crossing). Both are adapted to the fact that our march POINT-SAMPLES the height field, so distance is measured to the nearest point of a texel's CELL rather than to its centre -- the paper's construction is for a bilinear field, where centre distance is right and ours would be wrong. Quantised by TRUNCATION over [0, kPomConeExactMax] (a cone rounded UP is exactly what makes a march skip geometry) and disk-cached under Runtime/cache/ keyed on the height bytes + mode + encode ceiling. Changing this changes the ConeMap ENCODE as well as its contents, and the rasterizer picks the matching decode scale from this same flag.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(pom_cone_min_step,      "FDS_POM_CONE_MIN_STEP",        0.0f,                           "parallax","S1 P1 minimum cone-march step, in TEXELS of lateral advance; default 0 = off (byte-identical). A quantised cone byte of 0 makes the cone step c*gap/(c+dlen) exactly ZERO, so the march FREEZES in place: the lane never brackets, keeps the un-shifted entry UV, and for --pom_shell that means it renders the FLAT LID at the lid's depth -- up to a full slab in front of the true surface. This floors the step at N texels of lateral travel (converted to a height step by dividing by the texels travelled per unit height), clamped so it never exceeds the gap the ray is currently standing over, so it can only ever override a frozen cone and never steps deeper into the surface than the cone itself would have allowed. This is the EGSR'24 'artifact-free minimum step size' and it also bounds the number of steps needed to cross the slab. Mainly matters with --pom_cone_exact, whose fine per-texel cones legitimately quantise to 0 near a cliff.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(pom_march_steps_auto,   "FDS_POM_MARCH_STEPS_AUTO",     0.0f,                           "parallax","S1 P1 PER-FACE MARCH BUDGET FROM THE MEASURED STEP RULE; default 0 = off, --parallax_pom's single global number is used exactly as before (byte-identical). The measured rule (docs/S1_DISCREPANCY_INVENTORY.md S9): what a march has to resolve is TEXELS OF LATERAL TRAVEL down the relief slab, T = uvAmp x texels-per-UV-tile x tan(incidence), and the quality knee sits at a roughly fixed number of texels PER STEP regardless of pose. The shell's --pom_shell_cap bounds tan(incidence) at sqrt(cap^2-1), so a face's worst-case travel is computable at raster setup from numbers it already owns, and this flag's VALUE is the texels-per-step target: N = ceil(T / value), clamped to [4, 256]. That makes the budget follow the SURFACE instead of a hand-set global -- greets' floor tiles its height map over ~15 world units and the walls over 6 while the floor's slab is 6.2x deeper, so the floor genuinely needs a larger budget than the wall it meets, and a distant face marching a higher height mip with half the texels automatically needs half. Computed once per FACE in the dispatcher; the per-triangle and per-pixel paths are untouched, so flag-off stays byte-exact. Pair with --pom_march_earlyout, which turns the budget into a CEILING only the pixels that need it pay for.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (pom_mip_viz,            "FDS_POM_MIP_VIZ",              0,                              "parallax", "DIAGNOSTIC for the stone-wall seam bug: tint each face's albedo by its per-FACE miplevel (a small color hash — mip0 red, mip1 green, mip2 blue, mip3 yellow, ...) so the mipmap-via-subdivision sub-face boundaries are directly visible. The diagonal color boundaries ARE the seam locations (--pom_viz shows the matching height-field discontinuity there); --pom_height_mip>=0 makes the height sampling ignore these boundaries. Rides the filtered-albedo path (needs --texture_filter>=1). Applies to every textured face, not only heightmap ones. Off by default.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (pom_path_viz,           "FDS_POM_PATH_VIZ",             0,                              "parallax", "MARCH PATH VISUALISATION (DIAGNOSTIC, default 0 = OFF, byte-null). Records, per pixel, WHICH BRANCH of the POM march ran and WHY it terminated, as a 32-bit path code: [3:0] kind (1 single-shift, 2 LOD row-far skip, 3 naive hit, 4 naive no-hit, 5 cone hit, 6 cone UNRESOLVED = ran out of steps with the ray still inside the slab, 7 cone MISS = passed under all the stone, 8/9 reference march hit/miss); [7:4] terminal action (0 keep, 1 clamp-to-flat, 2 clamp-into-box, 3 land-on-side-face, 4 discard); [11:8] failure reasons (no-cross, domain exit, base clip, side-entry miss); [15:12] which box side the final UV left; [16] side-face entry taken, [17]/[18] LOD fade partial/full, [19] quarter-res SHARED lane, [20] grazing 1/(V.N) cap active, [21] cone min-step floor fired, [22] max-offset clamp, [23] side-edge kill, [24] recess arm, [25] lid arm, [26] shell face, [31] sticky: some fragment at this pixel was shell-killed. MODE 1 = record + replace the albedo with a flat colour per (kind, action) so the paths are separable by eye; MODE 2 = record only, leaving the normal image, so a sweep can be diffed for PATH FLIPS (a pixel changing class between adjacent frames changes appearance discontinuously — a swim mechanism no continuous metric sees). The code plane is dumped beside the snapshot PPM as greets_tNNNNNN_path.u32 (uint32[xres*yres]); the census and the flip map are computed offline from it. Run: ./DEMO --snapshot=greets@t=5958 --pom_path_viz=1 <march flags>")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (pom_rebuild,            "FDS_POM_REBUILD",              0,                              "parallax","ARM THE LIVE DISPLACEMENT REBUILD (DEMO/DisplaceRebuild.cpp), default OFF and byte-null. The displacement flags split in two: the ones the rasterizer reads per face / per pixel (--parallax_pom, --pom_shell_cap, --pom_normal, --pom_recess_edge, ...) take effect on the next frame, and the ones Initialize_Greets consumes ONCE — --pom_shell, which MOVES the stone's vertices out to the lid via PomShell_Build, and --parallax_pom_cone / --pom_cone_exact / --pom_horizon, which select an offline BAKE the kernel then reads. Flipping one of the second group through the live param path either does nothing (the shell march is gated on Material::PomShellUvAmp, which only PomShell_Build stamps) or HALF-applies (--pom_cone_exact also picks the cone-byte DECODE scale, so it reinterprets the OLD bake), which is why the editor could not drive the displacement modes. With this on, scene init takes a PRISTINE snapshot of the stone's geometry (vertex positions + Vertex::ShellH, face normal/plane/PomShellGroup, mesh bspheres — measured 0.9 MB on greets) and a requested --pom_shell is DEFERRED past init and applied through the rebuild path instead, so the snapshot is pristine by construction and repeated mode switches cannot compound the lid offset. The editor sets this automatically; on the CLI it only matters with --pom_rebuild_test. Scope is the shell family (no shell / POM / recess-only / lid) — the tessellation bake (--greets_displace) subdivides in place before the chunk split and cannot be undone live, so the rebuild refuses when the scene was built with it.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (pom_rebuild_test,       "FDS_POM_REBUILD_TEST",         0,                              "parallax","IDEMPOTENCY GATE for --pom_rebuild, default 0 = off. Implies --pom_rebuild. After greets init, runs N extra restore+re-apply cycles before the first frame; the frame that follows must be identical to a fresh launch in the same mode. That is the acceptance test for 'restore pristine geometry': PomShell_Build MOVES vertices, so a rebuild that failed to restore would compound the offset by half a slab per toggle and N=10 would be unmistakable. Use as: render --pom_shell ... once with no test flag and once with --pom_rebuild_test=10 and compare the snapshots.")
#if FDS_DEV
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(ao_from_diffuse,          "FDS_AO_FROM_DIFFUSE",          0,                              "ao-dev",   "[dev] Stand-in AO from the diffuse texture's luminance (no authored AO files needed) — proves the AO path on existing scenes.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(nmap_from_diffuse,        "FDS_NMAP_FROM_DIFFUSE",        0,                              "nmap-dev", "[dev] Derive normal map from diffuse")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(viz_tangent,              "FDS_VIZ_TANGENT",              0,                              "nmap-dev", "[dev] Visualize per-pixel view-space tangent as diffuse color")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(viz_normal,               "FDS_VIZ_NORMAL",               0,                              "nmap-dev", "[dev] Visualize per-pixel view-space normal as diffuse color (POST normal-map perturbation)")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(viz_geonormal,            "FDS_VIZ_GEONORMAL",            0,                              "nmap-dev", "[dev] Visualize per-pixel GEOMETRY normal (the vertex-interpolated normal BEFORE the normal map perturbs it) as diffuse colour. A flat wall should be ONE solid colour; any variation (e.g. a diagonal kink along a quad's triangle split) is the geometry-normal interpolation. Pair with --viz-tangent to separate a normal crease from a tangent crease.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(viz_matid,                "FDS_VIZ_MATID",                0,                              "nmap-dev", "[dev] Visualize per-pixel matID as a deterministic colour (8-bit id hashed to BGRA) so you can see which face won z at each pixel. Use to verify reflected-floor pixels are actually the floor clone (and not some other face that happens to z-fight there).")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(viz_pmid,                 "FDS_VIZ_PMID",                 0,                              "nmap-dev", "[dev] Visualize per-pixel mirror id (gb.mirrorId post-commit) as a deterministic colour. Distinct from --viz-matid because original + clone faces share material/matID — pmid is the only way to tell them apart per pixel.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(nmap_as_diffuse,          "FDS_NMAP_AS_DIFFUSE",          0,                              "nmap-dev", "[dev] Paint baked normal map directly as diffuse — confirms swizzle round-trip")
#endif

// ─── anti-aliasing ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(aa,                       "FDS_AA",                       0,                              "aa",       "Edge-aware AA: a post-tonemap pass that blends a 5-tap cross only across G-buffer geometry edges (normal creases + depth/silhouette discontinuities). Removes jaggies without softening textures/interiors. Deferred path only.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(aa_strength,             "FDS_AA_STRENGTH",              1.0f,                           "aa",       "Max blend weight at a detected edge (0..1). 1 = full 5-tap cross average at the strongest edges, scaled down by edge strength elsewhere; lower = subtler.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(aa_viz,                   "FDS_AA_VIZ",                   0,                              "aa",       "Debug: paint the AA edge map — detected geometry edges in green over a dimmed frame — instead of blending. Shows exactly which pixels --aa touches (silhouettes + creases). Requires --aa.")

// ─── display / present ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(no_vsync,                 "FDS_NO_VSYNC",                 0,                              "display",  "Disable SDL_RENDERER_PRESENTVSYNC — lets SDL_RenderPresent return immediately for benching real flip cost")

// ─── volumetric / atmospheric ────────────────────────────────────────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(draw_cones,               "FDS_DRAW_CONES",               0,                              "atmos",    "Draw volumetric spotlight cones (additive). Off by default; scene driver opts in.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(city_test_spots,          "FDS_CITY_TEST_SPOTS",          0,                              "atmos",    "Install 6 test spotlights in city for cone-overlay experimentation")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(cone_strength,           "FDS_CONE_STRENGTH",            0.05f,                          "atmos",    "Volumetric cone density (per-step contribution × 1e-3). 0.05 = subtle shafts, 0.5 = pea-soup fog")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(volumetric_unified,       "FDS_VOLUMETRIC_UNIFIED",       0,                              "atmos",    "Use unified Beer-Lambert volumetric pass (replaces separate fog + cone passes; adds omni halos)")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(fog_sigma_mult,          "FDS_FOG_SIGMA_MULT",           3.0f,                           "atmos",    "Beer-Lambert extinction coefficient as multiple of 1/FZP. σ=mult/FZP → T(FZP)=exp(-mult). Default 3 = ~5% transmittance at FZP")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(omni_halo_strength,      "FDS_OMNI_HALO_STRENGTH",       0.0f,                           "atmos",    "Volumetric halo brightness scale for omnis (0 = no halos; 0.05 = subtle; 0.5 = strong). Runs in both unified and legacy pipelines (Render_OmniHalos standalone pass).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(omni_halo_range_mult,    "FDS_OMNI_HALO_RANGE_MULT",     1.0f,                           "atmos",    "Global multiplier on every Omni's halo sphere radius (multiplies HaloRange when set, IRange otherwise). 1 = no change; use to scale halo footprints across a scene without editing every light.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(omni_halo_force_range,   "FDS_OMNI_HALO_FORCE_RANGE",    0.0f,                           "atmos",    "Hard override: when > 0, every Omni's halo radius is forced to this value, ignoring IRange / HaloRange / omni_halo_range_mult. Cleanest way to tune halo footprints without compounding per-light settings.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(vol_prof,                 "FDS_VOL_PROF",                 0,                              "atmos",    "Per-frame timing for volumetric passes (cones/halos/unified). Prints averaged ms every FDS_VOL_PROF_INTERVAL frames.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(vol_n_samples,             "FDS_VOL_N_SAMPLES",            4,                              "atmos",    "Samples per pixel for volumetric ray-march. Lower = faster but noisier; 4 default (2× faster than 8 with only √2 more noise — best perf/quality knee per profiling).")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(vol_cone_half_y,          "FDS_VOL_CONE_HALF_Y",          0,                              "atmos",    "Volumetric cone pass at half VERTICAL rate (even rows computed, written to both rows). Halves the pass cost but thins narrow beams — the 1-2px bright core line can fall between sampled rows (user-observed). Off by default; available for wide-cone scenes.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(vol_vec,                  "FDS_VOL_VEC",                  1,                              "atmos",    "Vectorize the per-sample inner loop in the cone/halo/unified passes (8-wide SIMD). Default on; --no-vol_vec falls back to scalar for A/B.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(vol_rect_cull,            "FDS_VOL_RECT_CULL",            1,                              "atmos",    "Per-batch (vec) / per-pixel (scalar) screen-rect cull in the cone tile fn. Skips the per-spot quadratic when the pixel/batch falls outside the spot's cone-bbox screen projection. Default on; --no-vol_rect_cull disables for A/B.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(vol_halo_analytic,        "FDS_VOL_HALO_ANALYTIC",        1,                              "atmos",    "Replace the halo per-sample ray-march with a closed-form arctan integral. Drops the (1-d/r)² near-edge softness (sharper boundary) but is ~8 ops/pixel vs N×30 ops. Default on; --no-vol_halo_analytic falls back to N-sample ray-march.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(vol_cone_analytic,        "FDS_VOL_CONE_ANALYTIC",        1,                              "atmos",    "Spotlight equivalent of vol_halo_analytic: closed-form arctan integral over the cone-clipped segment [zLo,zHi], coneAtten approximated at midpoint. Default on. NOTE (corrected 2026-08-12, was documented backwards): there is NO per-spot fallback — useAnalytic is this flag and nothing else (DeferredVolumetric.cpp, `const bool useAnalytic = analyticCone;`). A shadow-casting spot takes the SAME analytic branch and just pays a midpoint shadow tap; only --no-vol_cone_analytic reaches the ray-march. The per-spot `shadowed` branch that this text described lives in the fast-fog pass, not here.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(vol_analytic_noise,      "FDS_VOL_ANALYTIC_NOISE",       0.08f,                          "atmos",    "Per-pixel multiplicative jitter strength applied to analytic halo / cone results. 0 = silky smooth analytic gradient; 0.08 = subtle dither matching ray-march visual noise; 0.2+ = heavy grain. Hashed from pixel coords so it's stable frame-to-frame.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(cone_turbulence,         "FDS_CONE_TURBULENCE",          0.0f,                           "atmos",    "Animated turbulent density inside volumetric spotlight cones (drifting smoke/dust structure instead of uniform density). 0 = off (byte-identical legacy integration); 0..1 = modulation strength (0.5 = visible wisps, 1 = strong churn with dark voids). Noise field is WORLD-space value noise drifting with scene time, so beams read as sweeping through a static animated medium. Applies per segment (analytic hybrid) / per sample (ray-march); wide cones route through the segmented integrator while on.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(cone_turb_scale,         "FDS_CONE_TURB_SCALE",          3.0f,                           "atmos",    "cone_turbulence noise cell size in WORLD units. Scene scale: greets beams span ~10-38 units -> 2-4 gives beam-width wisps; city beams are larger. Smaller = finer grain.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(cone_turb_speed,         "FDS_CONE_TURB_SPEED",          0.5f,                           "atmos",    "cone_turbulence drift rate in noise cells/second (wind advection of the density field, fixed world direction). Also scales the swirl rotation rate. 0 = frozen structure.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(cone_swirl,              "FDS_CONE_SWIRL",               0.0f,                           "atmos",    "Helical swirl of the cone turbulence around each beam's axis: samples rotate by angle = cone_swirl * (2*pi*axialDist/(8*cone_turb_scale)) + time phase before the noise lookup, producing corkscrew streaks that ride the beam. 0 = off; 1 = one full twist per 8 noise cells along the axis. Only active when cone_turbulence > 0.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(cone_turb_octaves,         "FDS_CONE_TURB_OCTAVES",        2,                              "atmos",    "Octaves of value noise for cone_turbulence (1 or 2). 2 adds a half-scale detail layer (~1.6x noise cost, richer wisps); 1 is the cheap single-scale look.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(deferred_skybox,          "FDS_DEFERRED_SKYBOX",          0,                              "atmos",    "Skybox-from-G-buffer pass. Paints pixels with zEnc==0 (no surface) by reconstructing view ray + sampling the sky cube. Off by default until visually validated. When on, the forward RenderSkyCube becomes redundant (perf win pending separate suppression).")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(fast_fog,                 "FDS_FAST_FOG",                 0,                              "atmos",    "Analytic exponential fog post-pass (iquilezles model) that composes with the analytic cone/halo passes. Independent of Scn_Fogged so it can be toggled on clear scenes (city). Mixes pixels toward the scene Ambient color by 1-exp(-tau).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(fast_fog_density,        "FDS_FAST_FOG_DENSITY",         3.0f,                           "atmos",    "fast_fog extinction as a multiple of 1/FZP: sigma = density/FZP. At the far plane tau~=density, so 3 => ~95% fogged. Higher = thicker.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(fast_fog_height,         "FDS_FAST_FOG_HEIGHT",          0.0f,                           "atmos",    "fast_fog height falloff k in 1/world-units. 0 = uniform density. >0 = denser at low altitude (ground thickest), density(y) ~ exp(-k*y). Try ~1/(scene height) e.g. 0.0005 in city.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(fast_fog_bottom,         "FDS_FAST_FOG_BOTTOM",          -1.0e9f,                        "atmos",    "fast_fog slab BOTTOM in world Y. Fog only fills the ray segment between bottom and top, so it reads as a bounded mass floating in the air rather than tinting every surface. Default -1e9 = no floor (unbounded below).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(fast_fog_top,            "FDS_FAST_FOG_TOP",             1.0e9f,                         "atmos",    "fast_fog slab TOP in world Y. Set finite (e.g. 1000 in city) to cap the fog layer so building tops emerge above a discrete bank. Default 1e9 = no ceiling (unbounded above).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(fast_fog_feather,        "FDS_FAST_FOG_FEATHER",         0.0f,                           "atmos",    "Softens the slab's top/bottom Y-cutoff over this many world units (smoothstep ramp) so the boundary doesn't show a razor-sharp horizontal line at grazing view angles. 0 = auto (20%% of slab thickness). Set explicit for tighter/softer edges.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(fast_fog_dist_dim,       "FDS_FAST_FOG_DIST_DIM",        0.0f,                           "atmos",    "Blend the legacy 1998 distance darkening into the froxel/screen fog composite: the deferred SCENE term is scaled by 1-s+s*sqrt(max(0,1-z/FZP)) before fog layers on top, so far surfaces dim toward black at any altitude (aerial perspective the slab-bounded blob fog cannot give above its top). 0 = off (current look). 1 = the full legacy sqrt ramp. Skips sky (zEnc==0) and forward-filler pixels (mat sentinel), like the legacy pass.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(fast_fog_dist_dim_far,   "FDS_FAST_FOG_DIST_DIM_FAR",    0.0f,                           "atmos",    "fast_fog_dist_dim reaches full darkness at THIS view distance (world units) instead of the far plane FZP. 0 = FZP (legacy). Lower to start the darkening earlier/steeper — e.g. 0.5*FZP makes mid-range visibly dimmer instead of compressing all the falloff near the horizon.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (mips,                    "FDS_MIPS",                     1,                              "render",   "Mip selection in MiplevelClipper. DEFAULT ON since 2026-08-08 (was 0, force-disabled since the legacy NO_MIPMAPS define). With this OFF every face rasterises at LEVEL 0, and that pins not just the albedo but the NORMAL, ROUGHNESS, METAL and AO chains too — the deferred kernel indexes all of them by this same miplevel, so levels 1..N of five map sets were being built, paid for in memory, and never read. THE OLD DOC ARGUED THE DEFAULT FROM A NON-SEQUITUR ('1998 textures are low-res and MAGNIFIED at modern resolutions, so mips barely engage'): magnification describes NEAR surfaces and says nothing about the distant ones minification aliases, and it predates the sidecar PBR sets greets now ships. MEASURED on current content (greets t=2993, 1080p, --deferred --texture_filter=1, via --mip_stats): OFF = 100%% of draws and 100%% of covered area at level 0. ON (with --mip_fix) = 7.6%% of draws / 83.3%% of AREA at level 0, the remainder spread across levels 1-8 with 48.8%% of DRAWS at level 6 — mips engage heavily on the many small distant faces even though near geometry still owns the pixel count. Frame deltas vs off: 2.02%% of pixels at t=2993, 12.75%% at t=2000, 29.39%% at t=4200 with the legacy ramp and 8.28%% once --mip_fix corrects it. Branch split at t=2993: 56115 faces entered, 55679 took the face-uniform fast path, 436 (0.78%%) took the subdivision path — the split path is RARE but owns the large near faces, so it is not dead code. PERF IS NEUTRAL: min-of-arm over 5 interleaved 20-iter rounds, greets t=2993 RNDR 39.855 ms off vs 39.965 ms on+fix; no measurable texture-cache win and no measurable cost, on a machine loaded enough that nothing below ~0.2 ms is resolvable. FLIPPING THIS MOVED ALL FOUR BYTE PINS (render_gate mirror/cone/halo, city, fountain, greets) — they were re-derived in the same commit. Combine with --mip_fix (correct depth ramp, default on), --mip_aniso (grazing/max-axis), --mip_bias, --mip_hysteresis, --mip_stats.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(mip_bias,                "FDS_MIP_BIAS",                 0.5f,                           "render",   "Forward/G-buffer mip selection bias added to the per-face 0.5*log2(texel/pixel) metric before truncation. 0.5 = historical default. LOWER = finer mips held longer (sharper at distance, smaller pops when a face crosses a level; more texture aliasing/shimmer in exchange). Applies to the face-uniform paths of MiplevelClipper.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (mip_stats,               "FDS_MIP_STATS",                0,                              "render",   "DIAGNOSTIC, changes no pixel: print a MIP-LEVEL HISTOGRAM to stderr at process exit — per selected level, the number of filler draws AND the covered screen area, plus the branch counters (fastUniform vs split), the Txtr_Nomip count, how many faces produced a NEGATIVE signed pixArea, and how many exceeded the 2%%-screen split threshold. This is the direct measurement of 'are mips actually engaging'; it replaces inferring levels from --pom_mip_viz tint chromaticity, and unlike the viz it needs neither a dev build nor --no-hdr nor --texture_filter. Levels are recorded AFTER the --mips gate, so with mips off it correctly reports 100%% at level 0. Cumulative over every frame the process rendered — use with --snapshot for a single pose.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (clip_stats,              "FDS_CLIP_STATS",               0,                              "render",   "DIAGNOSTIC, changes no pixel: print the PER-(FACE, TILE) CLIPPER CENSUS to stderr at process exit. `FrustumClipper::Render` runs once per (face, tile) pair, and its first act is to copy three whole 140-byte `Vertex` structs into the worker clipper's `C_Verts` scratch so it can stamp per-face UV/UZ and per-TILE visibility flags on them without touching the shared mesh vertex. This report says how often that copy is followed by any clipping at all: `entered` (total visits), `no-clip` (the OR-of-vertex-flags carried neither a Z nor a 2D bit, i.e. the face lies wholly inside this tile rect), `emitted` (the clip actually changed the vertex count away from 3, i.e. Near/Far/FInterpolator manufactured vertices), `rejected` (entered minus the other two: the tile clip threw the polygon away entirely, so the copy and the UV stamp bought no pixels), plus the `needZ`/`need2D` splits and the mip fastUniform-vs-split branch. The table is SPLIT BY DISPATCHER (`fds::ClipSrc`, stamped once per tile job by `FrustumClipper::SetClipSource`, never per face), and that split is the reason the flag exists rather than a single number: at greets t=5743 the tiled G-buffer pass contributes 16 %% of the entries and `Shadows.cpp`'s depth raster contributes 83 %%, of which 82 %% are clipped away to nothing. A `rejected` row that large is a MISSING PRE-REJECT, not a clipper cost. It is the precondition test for any copy-elision scheme: aliasing the source vertices instead of copying them is only interesting if the no-clip share is large, and it is only SAFE if nothing writes them -- which the per-face UV stamp and the per-tile `Calc_Flags` both do, from several tile workers at once. Priced with it 2026-08-16p: the copies are 0.03-0.35 %% of frame and cutting the payload to 52 of the 140 bytes is unmeasurable -- see docs/PERF_STATE.md 00e and docs/OPTIMIZATION_BACKLOG.md 2026-08-16p. Cumulative over every frame the process rendered -- use with --snapshot for a single pose. Cost when off: one bool load per face-visit.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (mip_aniso,               "FDS_MIP_ANISO",                0,                              "render",   "ANISOTROPIC mip selection: pick the level from the LONGER of the two texel-space axis vectors instead of the geometric mean of both. Inert unless --mips. The legacy metric is 0.5*log2(texelArea/pixelArea) = 0.5*log2(Lx*Ly*|sin t|), the GEOMETRIC MEAN of the two axis densities; at grazing incidence one axis is compressed far harder than the other and the geometric mean then sits log2(sqrt(aspect)) sharper than the compressed axis can support. Those are the pixels that alias/crawl under motion. This flag accumulates the area-weighted UV->screen Jacobian in the SAME fan sweep that already computes texArea/pixArea (5 extra MACs per fan triangle, no extra pass, no filler change) and uses log2(max(|dUV/dx|,|dUV/dy|)) in texels. Isotropic faces are bit-unchanged in the metric (both forms reduce to log2(s)); only anisotropic ones move, and they move BLURRIER. NOW MEASURED (2026-08-13), and the flag doc it replaces was guessing in two places. THE OLD DOC CLAIMED greets' corridor walls run ~16:1: WRONG as an area-weighted statement. Per --mip_aniso_stats (the census added with this measurement), the area-weighted TRUE anisotropy (singular values of the Jacobian, not the ratio of its two columns) is 1.80:1 at the user's t=5970 jamb pose, 1.91:1 at review pose p1 t=5743, 1.84:1 at the t=5799 long-wall pose. Only 18.3%% of covered area is >=2:1 and 9.2%% is >=4:1 at t=5970. The first cut of the census DID print 16.6 -- from one edge-on sliver with sigmaMin -> 0 owning the mean, which is a clamping bug in the instrument, not a property of the walls. WHAT IT ACTUALLY FIXES, at the pose the user reported (the 'texture discontinuity near the edge' report; the doorway jamb at FDS_GREETS_CAM=18.8969765,3.21025538,-58.888485,-0.896694958,-0.0735020638,0.436503887 t=5970): ONE face, and it is the right one. That face is 6.0:1 anisotropic, sigmaMax 1.73 texels/px, sigmaMin 0.29. The geometric-mean LOD is -0.50, which with the default 0.5 bias + truncation (= round-to-nearest) lands level 0 and therefore still samples 1.73 texels per pixel along the worst axis -- point-sampled, that IS the striping. Max-axis gives LOD +0.79 -> level 1 -> 0.87 texels/px, i.e. no longer undersampled. dLOD +1.29. --pom_mip_viz confirms it per-pixel: the whole jamb is mip 0 under the legacy metric and exactly that sliver turns mip 1 under this flag. Frame-wide the undersampled AREA (sigmaMax > 2^level) falls 71.8%% -> 44.0%% at t=5970 (82.4%% -> 53.6%% at p1, 76.1%% -> 57.0%% at t=5799), and the area undersampled by more than 2x falls 6.3%% -> 0.0%% at every pose measured. GROUND TRUTH, because 'sharper' and 'more correct' are not the same claim: scored against a 4x supersampled capture (--snapshot_ss=4, box-downsampled) this flag is CLOSER to the reference in the defect region -- RMSE 9.300 -> 8.726, MAE 6.330 -> 5.916 -- and marginally closer over the whole t=5970 frame (6.070 -> 6.042). The GpuBench Metal arm at the same pose agrees with the supersampled reference and NOT with the legacy grain (docs/img/mipaniso/his_t5970_four_way_legacy_aniso_ss4x_gpubench.png). THE COST IS REAL AND IS WHY THIS IS STILL DEFAULT 0: at review pose p1 t=5743 the whole-frame error against the same 4x reference gets WORSE (RMSE 7.070 -> 7.156), and the worst 128px window in the battery loses 23%% of its gradient energy -- distant low-contrast stonework flattens (docs/img/mipaniso/p1_t5743_BLURCOST_legacy_vs_aniso.png). Frame-wide gradient energy falls only 0-1.1%% over the 18 review poses, and the frames change 0.5%%-7.0%% of pixels, so all four byte pins move if this is defaulted on. A PARTIAL DIAL WOULD NOT HELP AND IS NOT WORTH BUILDING: lod = lodGeo + k*(lodMax-lodGeo) only flips the reported face once k*1.29 >= 1.0, i.e. k >= 0.78, so any setting mild enough to protect the distant stonework leaves the user's defect exactly as it was. TEMPORAL: it does NOT flicker. Over a 13-step dolly along the real camera path (t=5967..5987, identical poses in both arms, so motion is normalised out) the consecutive-frame mean|d| is LOWER with the flag on -- 14.048 -> 13.749 in the jamb band (-2.1%%), px>20 23.26%% -> 22.46%% (-3.5%%) -- and bit-identical on the walls it does not touch. --mip_hysteresis still engages on top of it (0.38%% of pixels under the legacy metric, 0.65%% under this one, measured through --repro with 16 frames of real history, since --snapshot cannot express Face::LastMip at all). PERF: it costs, it does not pay for itself. Interleaved x6 at t=5970, hardware counters (load-independent; the box was at load 18 and the wall column is not resolvable below ~1 ms): renderFrame 4.287 -> 4.292 Ginstr/f (+0.12%%), and the whole delta lands in the gbuffer phase, 0.655 -> 0.660 Ginstr/f (+0.76%%). Mechanism, both halves: the 5 extra MACs per fan triangle, plus the poly-SPLIT branch firing more often (166 -> 236 invocations at t=5970) because a higher LOD puts more faces across a level boundary. There is no measurable texture-cache win from the coarser mips. KNOCK-ONS CHECKED: the G-buffer mip:4 field does not overflow (max level 8 both arms, and the clamp to numMipmaps-1 is unchanged). POM is NOT entangled with it -- the height march takes the per-face albedo mip when --pom_height_mip is -1, but pinning it to 1 leaves this flag's effect essentially unchanged (5.04%% of pixels vs 4.91%%), so the change is albedo, not re-offset relief. DEFAULT 0 BECAUSE THE BLUR IS A TASTE CALL, NOT BECAUSE THE METRIC IS WRONG: max-axis is the correct metric for a point sampler and every objective score in the defect region agrees, but there is no aniso-tap filler to win the detail back, so the distant-surface softening is a look decision. Evidence pairs in docs/img/mipaniso/, full write-up in docs/SESSION_STATE.md. Measure with --mip_aniso_stats.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(mip_hysteresis,          "FDS_MIP_HYSTERESIS",           0.0f,                           "render",   "Per-face mip switching hysteresis (in mip-metric units). 0 = off (historical: a face near a level boundary flips mips frame-to-frame — visible texture-detail flicker under camera motion, since the point-sampled fillers switch levels hard). ~0.25: a face must cross the boundary by this margin before switching, so boundary-straddling faces hold their level. Only +-1-level transitions are damped; cuts/jumps switch immediately.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(fast_fog_blobs,           "FDS_FAST_FOG_BLOBS",           0,                              "atmos",    "Fill the fog slab with procedurally-scattered blobs (one jittered sphere per world grid cell, closed-form cubic density per blob, 3D-DDA along the ray). Turns the smooth slab into a lumpy volumetric mass. Off = smooth slab.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(fast_fog_cell,           "FDS_FAST_FOG_CELL",            400.0f,                         "atmos",    "Blob grid cell size in world units (= blob diameter). Smaller = finer, lumpier fog but more DDA cells/ray. Try ~1/4 of the slab horizontal extent.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(fast_fog_blob_jitter,    "FDS_FAST_FOG_BLOB_JITTER",     0.35f,                          "atmos",    "Per-cell blob center jitter as a fraction of the cell size (0 = regular lattice, 0.5 = blobs can sit on cell faces). Breaks up the grid regularity.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(fast_fog_worley,          "FDS_FAST_FOG_WORLEY",          0,                              "atmos",    "Froxel blob field uses inverted Worley F1 (distance to the nearest jittered per-cell feature point) instead of trilinear value noise: density peaks AT scattered points and falls off radially, so fog reads as puffy rounded cloud masses instead of caustic-like veins. Size-modulated by a large-scale value-noise octave so puffs vary instead of forming a polka-dot lattice. Froxel path only; ~27 cell hashes per sample vs 8 (costs more).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(fast_fog_worley_thresh,  "FDS_FAST_FOG_WORLEY_THRESH",   0.5f,                           "atmos",    "Worley puff cutoff: density reaches 0 at F1 = (1 - thresh) cells from a feature point. Higher = smaller, more separated puffs with clear air between (0.5 -> puff radius half a cell); lower = fatter merging masses. Range ~0.2-0.7.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(fast_fog_blob_overlap,   "FDS_FAST_FOG_BLOB_OVERLAP",    0.0f,                           "atmos",    "Metaball-style ADDITIVE blob field (overrides worley/value noise when > 0): each cell's jittered blob contributes (1-(d/R)^2)^2 and overlapping blobs SUM, so density piles up where they stack - the 'lots of overlapping big blobs' cloud look that inverted-Worley F1 cannot express (nearest-distance is bounded; bigger radii just fill space uniformly). Value = blob radius in CELL units: 0.7 = separated puffs, 1.0 = touching, 1.2-1.5 = overlapping cloud masses (capped at 1.5, the 3x3x3 search limit). fast_fog_cell stays the spacing knob; this decouples size from spacing. fast_fog_worley_thresh is reused as the iso-threshold (where fog begins out of the summed field).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(fast_fog_falloff,        "FDS_FAST_FOG_FALLOFF",         0.0f,                           "atmos",    "Distance (world units) over which fog density e-folds with view distance: density *= exp(-z/falloff). Makes fog a near-camera mass that thins out far away instead of forming a wall at the far plane. 0 = auto (= scene FZP). Smaller = tighter, closer fog.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(fast_fog_froxel,          "FDS_FAST_FOG_FROXEL",          1,                              "atmos",    "Froxel (view-frustum 3D grid) volumetric fog — THE fast_fog renderer. Populates density+in-scatter per froxel, integrates front-to-back along depth, trilinearly composites; temporal supersampling, distance-LOD, worley/metaball fields, glow compression and xpar fog all live on this path. Volumetric by construction (fog fills air, not painted on surfaces), no grazing speckle, cost decoupled from screen resolution. --no-fast_fog_froxel falls back to the LEGACY screen-space per-pixel march (value-noise only, glow speckles at grazing angles, saturates at high inscatter) — kept for A/B reference.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(fast_fog_froxel_x,         "FDS_FAST_FOG_FROXEL_X",        256,                            "atmos",    "Froxel grid width (columns). Coarse is fine — fog is low-frequency and the composite trilinearly upsamples.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(fast_fog_froxel_y,         "FDS_FAST_FOG_FROXEL_Y",        144,                             "atmos",    "Froxel grid height (rows).")
FDS_FLAG_INT(fast_fog_froxel_z,         "FDS_FAST_FOG_FROXEL_Z",        64,                            "atmos",    "Froxel grid depth slices (camera to fog far). More = finer depth resolution / less near-camera banding.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(fast_fog_froxel_temporal, "FDS_FAST_FOG_FROXEL_TEMPORAL", 1,                             "atmos",    "Temporal supersampling of the froxel grid: jitter the per-froxel sample position each frame (Halton 2/3/5), reproject each froxel's center into the previous frame's grid and blend the history in. Averages 8 sub-froxel positions over time, dissolving the XY stair/blockiness of the coarse grid without raising its resolution. Costs one history fetch per froxel; moving lights can show slight glow lag (lower fast_fog_froxel_blend if so).")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(rain,                     "FDS_RAIN",                     0,                             "atmos",    "Screen-space rain: procedural streaks composited as a tile-parallel post pass (3 depth layers with parallax, z-buffer occluded, attenuated by the froxel/SS fog at each layer's depth, sinusoidal wind gusts). Works in any scene with a z-buffer; scene time drives the fall so pause freezes it. Read per frame - scriptable (e.g. ramp a storm in via SCRIPTS/<scene>.params).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(rain_intensity,          "FDS_RAIN_INTENSITY",           1.0f,                          "atmos",    "Screen-space rain amount: scales streak density and opacity (0 = none, 1 = steady rain, 2+ = downpour). Per-frame / scriptable.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(rain_speed,              "FDS_RAIN_SPEED",               1.0f,                          "atmos",    "Screen-space rain fall speed multiplier. Per-frame / scriptable.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(fast_fog_refl_depth,      "FDS_FAST_FOG_REFL_DEPTH",      1,                             "atmos",    "Fog the water's reflections at their MIRRORED path length (pass-1 Z, displaced through the wobble) instead of uniformly at the fog far plane — near buildings stay crisp in the mirror, far ones drown, matching the world above the waterline. City only (the scene hands the engine its reflection Z).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(bolt_width,              "FDS_BOLT_WIDTH",               0.85f,                          "scene",    "Fountain lightning CORE ribbon half-width factor (px = POW * this * RZ * FOVX). Thin=0.8, fat=2.5. Per-strike / scriptable (SCRIPTS/fountain.params hot-reloads).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(bolt_kink,               "FDS_BOLT_KINK",                0.30f,                         "scene",    "Fountain lightning texture ROUGHNESS: midpoint-displacement amplitude of the baked bolt texture (fraction of width). ~0.18 smooth, ~0.3 jagged, >0.4 wild. Changing it re-bakes the texture set (live, slight hitch).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(bolt_seg,                "FDS_BOLT_SEG",                 1.0f,                          "scene",    "Fountain lightning BRIGHTNESS multiplier (additive). 1=default, <1 dimmer, >1 hotter/blown. Live / scriptable.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (bolt_geom,               "FDS_BOLT_GEOM",                1,                              "scene",    "Fountain lightning glow rendering: 1 = fully geometric untextured additive Gouraud ribbon (default — no texture bake/sample, clean at any zoom and at screen edges/forks), 0 = baked bilinear texture quad. Both share the geometric AA core line.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(bolt_interval,           "FDS_BOLT_INTERVAL",            220.0f,                         "scene",    "Fountain lightning strike cadence in Timer units (smaller = more frequent strikes). Strikes fire periodically from the first scripted time through the scene at random spires; deterministic per index so it stays scrub-safe. ~120 = frantic, ~500 = sparse.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(bolt_flash_peak,         "FDS_BOLT_FLASH_PEAK",          500.0f,                         "scene",    "Fountain strike-flash light ISize at full intensity (lights the froxel fog + nearby geometry). Keep it mostly BELOW --fast_fog_glow_max so the fog's 1/d^2 falloff shows (bright halo at the strike, dark away) instead of a flat bright wash that hides the bolt. 0 = no flash light.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(bolt_flash_range,        "FDS_BOLT_FLASH_RANGE",         500.0f,                         "scene",    "Fountain strike-flash light reach in world units. Smaller = tighter halo around the bolt; larger spreads it across more fog (too large = whole-screen wash).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(bolt_flash_decay,        "FDS_BOLT_FLASH_DECAY",         0.10f,                          "scene",    "Fountain strike-flash decay rate: intensity *= exp(-dTime*this) per frame. Larger = shorter/snappier flash (~0.05 ≈ a few frames); smaller = lingers.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(rain_lens,                "FDS_RAIN_LENS",                1,                             "atmos",    "On-camera droplets while --rain is active: refracting discs that stick to the 'lens', dwell, then trickle down and shrink. Spawn rate follows rain_intensity; scene time drives the motion (pause freezes). Deterministic (hash-seeded, no rand).")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(city_rain,                "FDS_CITY_RAIN",                0,                             "scene",    "The 1998 city rain: 3000 camera-tracking drops (1px + transparent motion trail), sinusoidal wind gusts, spline-lookahead respawn. Read once at scene init (allocates the particle pool) — CLI/env only, not hot-scriptable.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(city_headlights,          "FDS_CITY_HEADLIGHTS",          0,                             "scene",    "RETIRED code scheme (LEGACY single nose spot per vehicle, re-aimed along motion each tick). Default OFF since 2026-07-11: the city headlights are AUTHORED lights now — 46 parented 'city headlight L/R' spots baked into Authoring/city/CITY1.LWS -> CITY.FLD — and turning this on would double-light every vehicle nose (and re-add the police strobe). Kept for A/B against the authored look. Volumetric beams when --draw_cones is on. Scriptable per-frame (city.params).")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(city_headlights_front,    "FDS_CITY_HEADLIGHTS_FRONT",    0,                             "scene",    "RETIRED code scheme (front-row PAIR of spots per vehicle on the local-AABB front face, ±35% width, 15°/30° cones, warm white, parented wrappers). Default OFF: this exact scheme is what got baked as AUTHORED lights into Authoring/city/CITY1.LWS -> CITY.FLD (46 'city headlight L/R' spots, Omni_SceneAuthored -> editable in the editor lights list, grouped under their vehicles). Turning it on double-lights every vehicle front. Kept for A/B; the intensity/range knobs still shape the CODE lights only — the authored lights are edited per-light via the editor/LWS.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(city_headlight_intensity,"FDS_CITY_HEADLIGHT_INTENSITY", 1.2f,                          "scene",    "Headlight beam intensity (spot ISize), both schemes. 0 also disables; ~2-4 reads well against the streetlights.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(city_headlight_range,    "FDS_CITY_HEADLIGHT_RANGE",     1600.0f,                       "scene",    "Front-row headlight beam range in world units for a reference 50-unit-radius vehicle; each vehicle scales it by its world bounding radius / 50 (clamped 0.5x-1.5x, so the x18.9-scaled SHIP1 doesn't floodlight the city). The legacy nose scheme keeps its fixed 1600.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(fast_fog_froxel_taps,      "FDS_FAST_FOG_FROXEL_TAPS",     1,                             "atmos",    "Density samples per froxel per frame when temporal is on (1 or 2). 2 averages two opposite phases of the jitter cycle each frame — halves the temporal cycle's amplitude and doubles convergence, for roughly double the noise-field cost in foggy froxels. Diagnostic / quality knob for residual fog shimmer.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(fast_fog_froxel_blend,   "FDS_FAST_FOG_FROXEL_BLEND",    0.9f,                          "atmos",    "Temporal history weight (0..0.95): new froxel value = lerp(current, history, blend). The jitter cycle's residual flicker scales with (1-blend), so HIGHER = LESS shimmer (camera motion is handled by reprojection, not by this); the cost of high values is fog/glow response lag (~1/(1-blend) frames). Lower = more responsive but more shimmer; 0 disables blending (jitter then shows as raw per-frame noise).")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(fast_fog_xpar,            "FDS_FAST_FOG_XPAR",            1,                             "atmos",    "Fog the transparent peel from the froxel grid: each transparent pixel's lit color becomes lit*T(z) + inscatter(z) at ITS OWN depth before the alpha blend (out = a*(C*T+acc) + (1-a)*Bg — exact under the froxel model, one trilinear grid sample per transparent pixel). Off = transparents keep the legacy per-light sqrt ramp (no aerial perspective on water/glass).")
FDS_FLAG_INT(fast_fog_glow_grid_div,    "FDS_FAST_FOG_GLOW_GRID_DIV",   4,                             "atmos",    "Froxel glow (light in-scatter radiance) is evaluated on an XY grid this many times coarser than the fog grid (full z resolution) and bilinearly upsampled - light radiance is low-frequency in XY, and this divides the per-light glow cost (the #1 froxel cost in many-light scenes: 30-omni city glow ~26ms -> ~1.6ms at div=4) by div^2. 1 = exact per-column glow (the A/B reference). Softens shadow boundaries INSIDE fog glow by ~div fine froxels; surface shadows unaffected.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(fog_composite_tile_align8,"FDS_FOG_COMPOSITE_TILE_ALIGN8",1,                    "atmos",    "Round the froxel COMPOSITE's per-tile X span up to a multiple of 8 so every tile starts and ends on an 8-lane group boundary. The tile width is ceil(XRes/12), which at 1512 is 126 = 15 groups + 6 leftover pixels, and those leftovers take the per-pixel scalar path for no reason but arithmetic: 61 056 px per composite pass at 1512x848, 4.76 % of the frame. Bit-exact by construction - Froxel_CompositeTileVec8 is a lane-for-lane port of Froxel_CompositePixel and the two are pinned identical, so which of them a pixel goes through cannot change its value. --no-fog_composite_tile_align8 restores the unaligned split (the A/B reference).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(fast_fog_glow_max,       "FDS_FAST_FOG_GLOW_MAX",        0.0f,                          "atmos",    "Soft-knee compressor for the froxel fog's per-slice in-scatter radiance (8-bit output units): EXACTLY linear below half this value, then compressed, asymptotically approaching it — tames the white-clipping cores near lamps (radial kernel peaks ~20x, times fast_fog_inscatter) WITHOUT dimming the glow's dim reaches or touching density/extinction. Hue-preserving (max-channel scaled). 0 = off. Try ~250-400; lower = flatter glow, higher = only the hottest cores compressed. Reduce fast_fog_inscatter instead if you want the WHOLE glow dimmer.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(fast_fog_halfres,         "FDS_FAST_FOG_HALFRES",         1,                              "atmos",    "Compute fast_fog at half resolution and bilaterally (depth-weighted) upsample. Fog is low-frequency so the quality cost is tiny; ~4x cheaper, makes the blob field affordable. --no-fast_fog_halfres for full-res.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(fast_fog_adaptive,        "FDS_FAST_FOG_ADAPTIVE",        0,                              "atmos",    "Hierarchical fast_fog: compute on a coarse grid (fast_fog_adaptive_step), interpolate where neighbouring coarse samples agree, recompute per-pixel only where they disagree (occluder/shadow/depth edges). Cheaper than half-res in smooth regions, full-res-sharp at edges. Overrides fast_fog_halfres when set.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(fast_fog_adaptive_step,    "FDS_FAST_FOG_ADAPTIVE_STEP",   4,                              "atmos",    "Coarse-grid spacing (pixels) for fast_fog_adaptive. 4 = compute 1/16 of pixels then refine. Larger = cheaper base but more refinement at edges.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(fast_fog_adaptive_thresh,"FDS_FAST_FOG_ADAPTIVE_THRESH", 0.06f,                          "atmos",    "Adaptive refine trigger: if the 2x2 coarse fog-amount spread exceeds this (or depth disagrees), recompute the pixel exactly instead of interpolating. Lower = more recompute (sharper, slower).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(fast_fog_inscatter,      "FDS_FAST_FOG_INSCATTER",       0.0f,                           "atmos",    "In-scatter glow: brightness scale for scene lights (omnis/spots) lighting the fog medium, so fog blooms around lamps. Integrated along the fog segment (fast_fog_inscatter_samples points), scaled by fog amount. 0 = off (fog only occludes toward Ambient). Try 0.3-1.0.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(fast_fog_inscatter_analytic,"FDS_FAST_FOG_INSCATTER_ANALYTIC",1,                          "atmos",    "Integrate the in-scatter glow in closed form (arctan integral of the 1/dist^2 radial kernel over the fog segment, cone/edge factors at the segment's closest-approach point) instead of point sampling. Matches vol_cone_analytic/vol_halo_analytic. Shadow-casting spots can't be integrated (per-sample shadow tap) so they fall back to fast_fog_inscatter_samples point sampling. Default on; --no-fast_fog_inscatter_analytic forces sampling for all lights.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(fast_fog_inscatter_samples,"FDS_FAST_FOG_INSCATTER_SAMPLES",6,                             "atmos",    "Stratified samples used to integrate in-scatter for lights the analytic path can't handle (shadow-casting spots), or for all lights when --no-fast_fog_inscatter_analytic. 1 = the old single-midpoint sample, which resonates with the lamp's 1/dist^2 near-field and prints a bright disc/shell. >=4 converges to the smooth finite integral. 6 is a good quality/cost balance.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(fast_fog_inscatter_jitter,"FDS_FAST_FOG_INSCATTER_JITTER", 0,                             "atmos",    "Per-pixel interleaved-gradient-noise sample-offset for the sampled in-scatter integral. Default OFF: with the analytic path on, the shadowed integral is importance-sampled by the radial CDF so its summand (cutoff*cone * PCF-visibility) is smooth — centered samples are already terrace-free and pattern-free, so jitter only ADDS a visible weave. Turn ON only for the brute-force path (--no-fast_fog_inscatter_analytic), where uniform samples of the 1/dist^2 spike + binary shadow DO terrace without it.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(fast_fog_shadow_analytic, "FDS_FAST_FOG_SHADOW_ANALYTIC", 0,                             "atmos",    "Analytic shadowed in-scatter: bisect the shadow map for lit/shadowed transitions, sum the analytic radial integral over lit sub-intervals (cutoff^2*cone pinned per interval at clamp(z*,a,b)). Exact for fully-lit/convex; an approximation in bright partially-shadowed regions, and no faster here (path is compute-bound, not shadow-tap-bound). Experimental A/B; default off.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(fast_fog_shadow_earlyout, "FDS_FAST_FOG_SHADOW_EARLYOUT", 1,                             "atmos",    "Fully-lit early-out for the shadowed in-scatter: probe PCF visibility at z* and 5 points across the segment; if all read fully lit, skip the per-sample integral and use the analytic brightness. Wins in LIT-heavy views (most of the cone unoccluded); a wash in shadow-heavy views (the probes just add cost to penumbra pixels that still loop), and can slightly over-brighten a thin shadow that falls between probes (~3 levels). Default on; --no-fast_fog_shadow_earlyout for the exact per-pixel integral everywhere.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(fast_fog_shadow_pcf,       "FDS_FAST_FOG_SHADOW_PCF",      1,                              "atmos",    "PCF radius (shadow-map texels) for the sampled in-scatter shadow tap. 0 = single binary tap (hard edge -> the lit-sample count steps by 1 across neighbours, printing ns horizontal terraces unless ns is huge). 1 = 3x3 box -> fractional visibility -> soft penumbra that reads smooth at low fast_fog_inscatter_samples. 2 = 5x5 (softer/slower). Cost is (2r+1)^2 depth taps per in-scatter sample, so only the shadowed-spot path pays it.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(fast_fog_dither,         "FDS_FAST_FOG_DITHER",          1.0f,                           "atmos",    "Triangular dither amplitude (in 8-bit levels) added to the fog composite to break up the contour banding that 8-bit output quantization gives on smooth low-contrast fog gradients. 0 = off. 1.0 = ~+-1 level, enough to dissolve bands. The banding is NOT from fastPow2 (verified: std::exp bands identically).")

// ─── Cinematic preset ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (cinematic,               "FDS_CINEMATIC",                0,                              "atmos",    "Cinematic preset: each scene turns on HDR + hdr_linear + sane bloom + the filmic post stack (anamorphic, chromatic aberration, vignette, colour grade, film grain) at a PER-SCENE exposure tuned so nothing blows out (bright water scenes get low exposure, dark scenes high). Applied via setDefault in each scene's factory, so any individual --flag you pass still overrides it. Does NOT enable deferred_quarter (greets keeps that to itself). Greets keeps its own hotter HDR/bloom and just gains the post stack.")

// ─── HDR (prototype, docs/fountain_hdr_plan.md) ───────────────────────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (hdr,                     "FDS_HDR",                      0,                              "atmos",    "HDR prototype: deferred passes accumulate unclamped radiance into an f32 buffer, then a tonemap pass maps it to the 8-bit framebuffer instead of clamping per-pass (replaces the glowMax soft-knee). Lets additive glow/fog/flash roll off instead of clipping to white. Fountain-first; no-op until the deferred writes are wired (Phase 1).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(hdr_exposure,            "FDS_HDR_EXPOSURE",             1.0f,                           "atmos",    "HDR exposure multiplier applied before the tonemap. 1.0 = neutral; raise to brighten, lower to darken before the highlight roll-off.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(hdr_white,               "FDS_HDR_WHITE",                1.0f,                           "atmos",    "HDR post-tonemap chroma scale: 1.0 = neutral (ACES filmic's own highlight desaturation only); <1 pulls colors toward the tonemapped luminance (less saturated, e.g. 0.8 to tame neon glow); >1 boosts saturation. Applied after the ACES curve.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(hdr_glow_scale,          "FDS_HDR_GLOW_SCALE",           0.25f,                          "atmos",    "HDR auto-compensation: glow inputs tuned against glowMax's cap (fast_fog_inscatter, bolt_flash_peak, cone_strength) run hot once --hdr disables that cap, so in HDR mode they're scaled by this factor. Lets the glowMax-tuned flags Just Work under --hdr instead of hand-dividing each. ~0.25 (=÷4) start; lower if fog/glow still too hot. 1.0 = no compensation.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(hdr_glow_softknee,        "FDS_HDR_GLOW_SOFTKNEE",        0,                              "atmos",    "HDR fog in-scatter: the physically-grounded alternative to the flat hdr_glow_scale fudge. Instead of disabling the soft-knee under --hdr and scaling inscatter by a constant (which over-dims faint glow and under-tames hot cores — a scalar can't match the knee's gain curve g=(2x-1)/x²), run the SAME soft-knee in HDR-linear with fast_fog_glow_max as the radiance ceiling: faint glow preserved (gain~1), hot cores rolled off (gain→0), auto-tracking the actual glow level; ACES tonemaps on top. Tune fast_fog_glow_max (the physical ceiling; falls back to 300 if unset) rather than re-tuning a ratio. Affects ONLY the froxel/screen fog in-scatter, NOT volumetric cones (no soft-knee there — they keep hdr_glow_scale). Default off for A/B vs hdr_glow_scale.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(hdr_cone_softknee,        "FDS_HDR_CONE_SOFTKNEE",        0,                              "atmos",    "HDR volumetric cone/halo glow: the physically-grounded alternative to scaling cone_strength by hdr_glow_scale. Applies a soft-knee (linear below cone_glow_max/2, asymptote at cone_glow_max, same gain curve g=(2x-1)/x² as the fog knee) to the cone+omni-halo in-scatter at its composite into the linear HDR buffer, so hot shaft cores roll off smoothly toward the ceiling (faint shafts preserved) before ACES, instead of a flat hdr_glow_scale that over-dims faint shafts and under-tames cores. When on, the cone term skips the hdr_glow_scale fudge. Tune cone_glow_max (falls back to 200 if unset). HDR-only; affects cones AND omni halos (they share VolCompositeAdd). Sibling of --hdr-glow-softknee (fog in-scatter). Default off for A/B.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(cone_glow_max,           "FDS_CONE_GLOW_MAX",            0.0f,                           "atmos",    "Radiance ceiling for --hdr-cone-softknee's soft-knee on the volumetric cone+halo glow, in the same linear-radiance units as the rest of the HDR buffer (cones composite UNCLAMPED into g_hdrBuf and ACES rolls off above this). Try ~150-400; lower = more roll-off. 0 = use the 200 fallback when --hdr-cone-softknee is on.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (hdr_linear,              "FDS_HDR_LINEAR",               0,                              "atmos",    "HDR Phase 3 Stage A (look experiment): tonemap in LINEAR space. Decodes the accumulated buffer gamma->linear (cheap gamma-2.0: square), applies exposure+ACES in linear, then encodes linear->sRGB (sqrt) on output. Does NOT make the surface lighting itself linear (that's Stage B) -- this just lets you A/B whether linear tonemapping looks better before committing to the hot-kernel rewrite. Off = the original gamma-space ACES. Toggle live via the tune server.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (bloom,                   "FDS_BLOOM",                    0,                              "atmos",    "Full-screen HDR bloom: bright-pass the linear radiance buffer (g_hdrBuf, the true unclamped source), blur a downsampled pyramid, and add the glow back BEFORE the tonemap so it rolls off through ACES with everything else. Requires --hdr (no float buffer otherwise). Subsumes the localized disco-ball bloom. Threaded (6x4 tiles). Off = no spatial glow (HDR still rolls highlights off, just doesn't bleed).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(bloom_threshold,         "FDS_BLOOM_THRESHOLD",          200.0f,                         "atmos",    "Bloom bright-pass knee, in the linear-radiance 0-255 scale (255 = reference white; lit surfaces sit near there, lights/flash/disco exceed it). Only radiance above this blooms. Lower = more of the scene glows; raise to restrict bloom to the brightest highlights.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(bloom_intensity,         "FDS_BLOOM_INTENSITY",          0.6f,                           "atmos",    "Bloom add-back strength: the blurred bright-pass is scaled by this before adding into g_hdrBuf. 0 = off (same as --no-bloom), ~0.5-1.0 a natural glow, higher for a dreamy/overblown look.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(hdr_refl_gain,           "FDS_HDR_REFL_GAIN",            2.5f,                           "atmos",    "HDR brightness gain for forward Mat_HdrEmissive surfaces (the disco ball): when their colour is lifted from the 8-bit forward buffer into g_hdrBuf, it's scaled by this so the reflection exceeds 255 and over-blooms (8-bit forward can't otherwise be a true HDR source). 1.0 = no boost (just HDR-correct). Only affects flagged surfaces, so other reflective env is untouched.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (anamorphic,              "FDS_ANAMORPHIC",               0,                              "atmos",    "Anamorphic lens streaks (Star Trek 2009 look): bright-pass the linear g_hdrBuf, build a long HORIZONTAL light streak (+ a faint VERTICAL cross) off every hot source via exponential doubling-blur, tint it cool blue-white, and add it back BEFORE the tonemap so it rolls off through ACES. Requires --hdr. Independent of --bloom (does its own bright-pass at bloom_threshold). Threaded.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(anamorphic_intensity,    "FDS_ANAMORPHIC_INTENSITY",     0.5f,                           "atmos",    "Anamorphic streak add-back strength (scales the streak before adding into g_hdrBuf). 0 = off.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (anamorphic_passes,       "FDS_ANAMORPHIC_PASSES",        6,                              "atmos",    "Horizontal streak length in exponential doubling passes — reach ~2^passes quarter-res px (6 ~ a 256px streak at 1080p). Each pass is 3 taps; cost is log(length).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(anamorphic_decay,        "FDS_ANAMORPHIC_DECAY",         0.85f,                          "atmos",    "Per-pass streak falloff weight (higher = longer, brighter tail). ~0.8-0.9.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(anamorphic_vert,         "FDS_ANAMORPHIC_VERT",          0.25f,                          "atmos",    "Faint vertical cross-streak intensity as a fraction of the horizontal (0 = pure horizontal anamorphic; 0.25 = a subtle JJ lens-array cross). The vertical streak uses 2 fewer doubling passes so it stays shorter than the horizontal.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (lens_ghosts,             "FDS_LENS_GHOSTS",              0,                              "atmos",    "Screen-space lens-flare ghosts: a chain of colored discs + a halo ring marching along the line from each bright source through screen-centre, sampled from the linear g_hdrBuf bright-pass and added BEFORE the tonemap. Completes the anamorphic flare. Requires --hdr. Threaded.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(lens_ghost_intensity,    "FDS_LENS_GHOST_INTENSITY",     0.4f,                           "atmos",    "Lens-ghost add-back strength. 0 = off.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (lens_ghost_count,        "FDS_LENS_GHOST_COUNT",         4,                              "atmos",    "Number of ghost discs along the centre axis.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(lens_ghost_dispersal,    "FDS_LENS_GHOST_DISPERSAL",     0.32f,                          "atmos",    "Ghost spacing as a fraction of the screen toward/through centre (bigger = ghosts spread farther apart).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(lens_ghost_halo,         "FDS_LENS_GHOST_HALO",          0.4f,                           "atmos",    "Halo-ring intensity (the soft circle the ghosts sit on), as a fraction of ghost_intensity. 0 = no halo.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (chromatic,               "FDS_CHROMATIC",                0,                              "atmos",    "Chromatic aberration: radial per-channel split (R outward, B inward) that grows toward the frame edge — the lens-glass tell. Post-tonemap on the 8-bit image. Cheap.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(chromatic_amount,        "FDS_CHROMATIC_AMOUNT",         2.5f,                           "atmos",    "Max channel split in pixels at the frame corner (0 at centre). ~1-4 subtle to strong.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (vignette,                "FDS_VIGNETTE",                 0,                              "atmos",    "Vignette: darken the frame toward the corners (cinematic focus). Post-tonemap on the 8-bit image.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(vignette_strength,       "FDS_VIGNETTE_STRENGTH",        0.4f,                           "atmos",    "Vignette darkening at the corner (0 = none, 1 = black corners). ~0.3-0.5 natural.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (dof,                     "FDS_DOF",                      0,                              "atmos",    "Depth-of-field: circle-of-confusion bokeh blur off the G-buffer depth, applied to the linear g_hdrBuf BEFORE bloom so defocused highlights bloom into discs. Gather blur with a golden-angle disc kernel + scatter-as-gather weighting (no foreground bleed). Requires --hdr. Threaded.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(dof_focus,               "FDS_DOF_FOCUS",                0.0f,                           "atmos",    "Focus-plane distance in view-space z units. 0 (default) = auto-focus on the screen-centre depth each frame.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(dof_range,               "FDS_DOF_RANGE",                0.06f,                          "atmos",    "Depth over which sharpness falls to full blur, as a FRACTION of the scene far-plane (so one value auto-scales across scenes: a small room and an outdoor vista both work). Smaller = shallower depth of field. ~0.03 shallow, ~0.1 gentle.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(dof_max,                 "FDS_DOF_MAX",                  10.0f,                          "atmos",    "Maximum blur radius at full circle-of-confusion, in pixels at a 540p reference height (auto-scaled to the actual resolution so the blur looks the same at 540p and 1080p). 0 = off.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (dof_downscale,           "FDS_DOF_DOWNSCALE",            2,                              "atmos",    "DoF compute resolution divisor (same idea as --ssao_downscale): 1 = full-res gather (original path); 2 = half-res gather + depth-aware bilinear upsample, blended toward the sharp original as CoC shrinks so the focus boundary stays clean. ~4x less gather work; defocused regions are blurry by definition so the res cut is invisible except at the in-focus transition band, which the CoC blend covers. Default 2.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (grade,                   "FDS_GRADE",                    0,                              "atmos",    "Colour grade on the final 8-bit image: temperature, contrast, saturation, and a teal-orange split-tone (shadows → teal, highlights → orange — the blockbuster look). Post-tonemap, cheap, works with or without bloom/HDR effects in frame.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(grade_teal_orange,       "FDS_GRADE_TEAL_ORANGE",        0.3f,                           "atmos",    "Teal-orange split-tone strength. 0 = neutral, ~0.3 tasteful, ~0.6 strong cinematic.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(grade_contrast,          "FDS_GRADE_CONTRAST",           1.05f,                          "atmos",    "Contrast around mid-grey. 1 = none, >1 punchier, <1 flatter.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(grade_saturation,        "FDS_GRADE_SATURATION",         1.1f,                           "atmos",    "Saturation. 1 = none, >1 richer, 0 = greyscale.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(grade_temp,              "FDS_GRADE_TEMP",               0.0f,                           "atmos",    "Colour temperature shift. 0 = neutral, + = warmer (more red/less blue), - = cooler. ~±0.3 subtle.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (grain,                   "FDS_GRAIN",                    0,                              "atmos",    "Animated film grain: per-pixel luminance noise hashed on x,y,frame and added to the final image. Post-tonemap, cheap.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(grain_strength,          "FDS_GRAIN_STRENGTH",           6.0f,                           "atmos",    "Film-grain amplitude in 8-bit levels (peak ± offset). ~4-10 subtle to coarse.")

// ─── scene-specific ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(skip_cubemap,             "FDS_SKIP_CUBEMAP",             0,                              "city",     "City: skip per-building env-map bake")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(city_envmap_cache,        "FDS_CITY_ENVMAP_CACHE",        1,                              "city",     "Cache per-building env-map panoramas to Runtime/cache/city_envmap.bin keyed by CITY.FLD + panorama dims. Default on — slashes city bench/PGO init from ~4.8s to ~0.4s. --no-city-envmap-cache forces a fresh bake (and rewrites the cache).")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(water_ripple,             "FDS_WATER_RIPPLE",             1,                              "city",     "City: animate the water-reflection displacement map with cross-axis travelling ripples (was a frozen static sin/cos warp). The mirrored world below the water surface now moves. --no-water_ripple restores the legacy static warp. Per-frame dispMap rebuild is row-parallel across the pool.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(water_ripple_amp,        "FDS_WATER_RIPPLE_AMP",         30.0f,                          "city",     "Water-ripple displacement amplitude in pixels (peak screen-space offset of the reflection). Higher = choppier; at low HDR exposure the reflection is dark so it needs more amplitude to read. Legacy static warp was ~15.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(water_ripple_speed,      "FDS_WATER_RIPPLE_SPEED",       1.0f,                           "city",     "Water-ripple animation speed. 0 = frozen (but cross-axis organic, unlike the legacy warp).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(water_ripple_scale,      "FDS_WATER_RIPPLE_SCALE",       1.0f,                           "city",     "Water-ripple spatial frequency scale. Larger = tighter, choppier ripples; smaller = broad swells.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(water_scroll_speed,      "FDS_WATER_SCROLL_SPEED",       1.0f,                           "city",     "Water SURFACE-texture scroll speed (the tiled albedo sliding, distinct from the reflection ripple). 1.0 = original rate; scale up for a faster flow, 0 = frozen.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (water_bump,              "FDS_WATER_BUMP",               1,                              "city",     "Bump-mapped specular GLINTS on the water surface (distinct from the reflection ripple): an animated WORLD-space ripple normal catches a key light → view-dependent sparkles across the water. Masked on the transparent G-buffer (front == water matID) with opaque-Z occlusion, so it covers all visible water and never paints over foreground geometry. --no-water_bump disables.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(water_bump_strength,     "FDS_WATER_BUMP_STRENGTH",      0.6f,                           "city",     "Water-bump glint brightness (peak add in 0-1 → ×255). 0 = off. ~0.3 subtle shimmer, ~0.8 strong sparkle.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(water_bump_shininess,    "FDS_WATER_BUMP_SHININESS",     14.0f,                          "city",     "Water-bump glint tightness. Higher = smaller, sharper sparkles; lower = broad sheen.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(water_bump_scale,        "FDS_WATER_BUMP_SCALE",         6.0f,                           "city",     "Water-bump normal spatial frequency (independent of the coarse reflection ripple). Higher = finer, denser sparkles; lower = broad coherent sheen.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (water_detile,            "FDS_WATER_DETILE",             1,                              "city",     "De-tile the water ALBEDO: warp the water grid's face UVs by a low-frequency function of position at init, so the tiled texture stretches/swirls across the surface instead of repeating as a regular grid. --no-water_detile restores the plain tiling.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(water_detile_amp,        "FDS_WATER_DETILE_AMP",         0.6f,                           "city",     "Water de-tile warp amplitude in UV units (fraction of a texture tile the mapping is displaced; varies across the surface). Bigger = more distortion, but too big smears the texture.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(water_detile_scale,      "FDS_WATER_DETILE_SCALE",       3.5f,                           "city",     "Water de-tile warp spatial frequency. Higher = more, smaller warp cells (breaks local tile repetition); lower = broad swells.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (water_procedural,        "FDS_WATER_PROCEDURAL",         0,                              "city",     "Procedural water composite (Stage B): replace the tiled albedo with a deep-water colour mixed with the reflection by a view-angle FRESNEL term (more reflection at grazing, deep colour looking straight down). Coherent with the shared wave field. Off = legacy albedo+reflection blend.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(water_deep_b,            "FDS_WATER_DEEP_B",             58.0f,                          "city",     "Procedural deep-water colour Blue (0-255), seen looking straight down.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(water_deep_g,            "FDS_WATER_DEEP_G",             44.0f,                          "city",     "Procedural deep-water colour Green (0-255).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(water_deep_r,            "FDS_WATER_DEEP_R",             26.0f,                          "city",     "Procedural deep-water colour Red (0-255).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(water_reflectivity,      "FDS_WATER_REFLECTIVITY",       0.9f,                           "city",     "Procedural water max reflection at grazing angle (fresnel peak). 1 = full mirror at the horizon, 0 = no reflection.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(water_fresnel_base,      "FDS_WATER_FRESNEL_BASE",       0.65f,                          "city",     "Procedural water reflection floor looking STRAIGHT DOWN (fresnel base). For this mirror water the reflection underlay IS the see-through, so this is effectively the base transparency: higher = more reflection/transparency at all angles, lower = more opaque deep colour. ~0.65 gives the reflection clear weight; 1 = full mirror everywhere.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (water_fresnel_composite, "FDS_WATER_FRESNEL_COMPOSITE",  1,                              "city",     "Procedural water kernel composite: replace the lit water texel with deep-colour x (1-fresnel) + reflection x fresnel (the city look — assumes a BRIGHT reflection underlay). Off = keep the forward-style lit-texel + reflection/2 blend and take only the procedural field (ripple/glints/caustics) on top; chase turns this off because its night-sky reflection underlay is black and the deep colour reads as a dark view-angle band.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(water_albedo_mix,        "FDS_WATER_ALBEDO_MIX",         0.45f,                          "city",     "Procedural water: strength of the generated CAUSTIC texture (bright cell-network lines), modulating the water brightness, sampled per-pixel at a UV WARPED BY THE WAVE FIELD so it ripples with the same waves as the glints/reflection (coherent). 0 = none, ~0.45 visible caustics, higher = stronger.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(water_tex_scale,         "FDS_WATER_TEX_SCALE",          0.06f,                          "city",     "Procedural water caustic tiling: world-units→texel frequency. Higher = smaller/denser caustic cells; lower = larger cells.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(water_tex_warp,          "FDS_WATER_TEX_WARP",           5.0f,                           "city",     "How hard the wave field distorts the water texture's sampling (in texels). Makes the texture ripple/flow WITH the waves & glints; 0 = flat (just tiled), too high = over-churned/funhouse.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(water_tex_flow,          "FDS_WATER_TEX_FLOW",           1.0f,                           "city",     "How fast the caustic texture TRANSLATES with the wave field. 1 = matches the glint scroll velocity (caustics ride the waves), 0 = anchored (warp ripples it in place only), >1 = faster than the glints.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (water_variation,         "FDS_WATER_VARIATION",          0,                              "city",     "Break the procedural water's REGULARITY (default OFF → city/existing byte-identical). ON layers extra structure on the wave field + caustics so the sea reads as a varied natural surface instead of a uniform repeating field: (1) waterWaveSlope adds a low-frequency world-space SWELL (two crossed long swells + a slow macro roll) so there is large-scale structure the 128-texel tile can't hold, plus a third rotated ripple octave to break the tile's periodicity; (2) the caustic-cell sampling sums THREE scales/rotations (base + a finer rotated octave + a coarse macro octave) so the Worley network stops visibly tiling at water_tex_scale. Chase's factory turns this ON (createChaseScene); city/fountain leave it off. Read once into a static at BuildField, so it's a per-scene bake choice — flip needs a scene re-init.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (water_slope_vec8,        "FDS_WATER_SLOPE_VEC8",         1,                              "city",     "8-WIDE waterWaveSlope (default ON; --no-water_slope_vec8 falls back to the scalar, same pixels). pwater::waterWaveSlope is the 4th-hottest symbol of the city acceptance frame (--env_live_water --deferred --city_env_pixel) and is called OUT-OF-LINE from both screen passes, so there is exactly one scalar codegen to match. The vector form reproduces it op-for-op under an explicit FP_CONTRACT_OFF: rx/rz fused, the four scroll coords NOT fused, the wrap fused (fmls), the bilinear with NO fma at all, the layer mix fused (fmla) — the 2026-08-16b modulo round moved 4 px purely because an edit let clang re-contract that bilinear, so it is pinned here rather than left to the optimizer. The 129-stride halo (same round) is what makes the gather cheap: (i0,i0+1) are contiguous and the two components interleaved, so a row's corner pair is ONE 128-bit load. Consumers batch their LIVE pixels (the ray-cast rejects never reach the field) and flush in eights; the env_live_water tilt still calls the scalar per glass pixel, because that one goes through a function POINTER inside the deferred kernel's per-lane loop and batching it means restructuring the kernel. Byte-null at every pin; the hatch exists so a future contraction regression can be localized in one run.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (water_census,             "FDS_WATER_CENSUS",             0,                              "city",     "Instrument: classify every pixel the water passes scan (glints + city ripple dispMap) into reject/live buckets and print a [WCENSUS] table at exit. Runs a separate classification-only sweep so the shading loops themselves are untouched. Answers the denominator questions: what fraction of the full-screen scan is above the horizon, what fraction is occluded by opaque geometry, and how many pixels actually get a wave-slope evaluation.")
#if FDS_DEV
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(debug_panorama,           "FDS_DEBUG_PANORAMA",           0,                              "city-dev", "[dev] City: synthesize debug panoramas with gridlines")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(dump_panorama,            "FDS_DUMP_PANORAMA",            0,                              "city-dev", "[dev] City: dump baked panoramas to PPM")
#endif
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(greets_mat_labels,        "FDS_GREETS_MAT_LABELS",        0,                              "greets",   "Greets: overlay one '<matID>:<name>' label per material at the centroid of its closest-to-camera on-screen face. Diagnostic for picking mirror/effect target materials by name.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(greets_stone_tex,         "FDS_GREETS_STONE_TEX",         1,                              "greets",   "Greets: swap the wall ('rooms') + floor ('floor') diffuse to the de-lit 1024 Egyptian sandstone textures (TEXTURES/greets_wall.jpg, greets_floor.jpg). For the normal map, prefers a dedicated AUTHORED tangent-space map (TEXTURES/greets_*_n.png) if present; otherwise Sobel-bakes one from a height map (greets_*_h.png). Off by default for A/B.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(greets_nmap_flip_g,       "FDS_GREETS_NMAP_FLIP_G",       0,                              "greets",   "Greets stone-tex: flip the green channel of an AUTHORED normal map (greets_*_n.png) on load. Authored maps come in two conventions — OpenGL (+Y up) and DirectX (+Y down); the engine's tangent-space decode expects one of them. If lit bumps look inverted (mortar grooves bulge out / bricks cave in), toggle this. No effect on the Sobel-baked-from-height path.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(greets_tbn_fix,           "FDS_GREETS_TBN_FIX",           1,                              "greets",   "Greets: fix the normal-map relief seam at boundaries between faces of opposite UV handedness (mirrored mapping). The deferred kernel reconstructs the bitangent as a fixed-sign N×T, which is wrong on negative-UV-determinant faces; this splits those faces onto a handedness=-1 material clone so the kernel flips B per pixel (B = TbnHandedness·(N×T)). Default on; the 'rooms' wall has ~92 mirrored faces out of ~196. Off = the legacy seam.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(greets_floor_uv_scale,   "FDS_GREETS_FLOOR_UV_SCALE",    1.5f,                           "greets",   "Greets stone-tex: scale the floor's planar UVs so tiles are this much BIGGER (fewer repetitions). 1.0 = authored size; 1.5 = tiles 1.5x larger. Applied to the floor face UVs after load (the planar projection is baked at FLD load, so this rescales it in code).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(greets_floor_detile,     "FDS_GREETS_FLOOR_DETILE",      0.06f,                          "greets",   "Greets stone-tex: low-frequency world-space UV warp on the floor to break the obvious tile grid (same de-tile trick as city water). Amplitude in UV units; 0 = off. Per shared world position so adjacent faces stay seamless.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(greets_mirror,            "FDS_GREETS_MIRROR",            0,                              "greets",   "Greets: build a one-shot world-mirrored clone of every mesh across the teleporter wall plane and hide the wall faces, so the teleporter rectangle reveals a Z-buffer-portal view of the mirrored room. Static-only — dynamic meshes (robot) reflect frozen at init.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(greets_mirror_debug_mask, "FDS_GREETS_MIRROR_DEBUG_MASK", 0,                              "greets",   "Greets: after Render(), overlay gb.mirrorId * 64 onto the framebuffer (red/green/blue channels rotated by mirror id) so you can see which pixels each mirror's wall pre-pass actually stamped. Diagnostic for tuning the per-pixel mask path.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(greets_mirror_cam,        "FDS_GREETS_MIRROR_CAM",        0,                              "greets",   "Greets: after building mirrors, override the camera to a fixed free-cam pose squarely in front of the teleporter mirror (computed from its wall centroid + plane normal). Lets a snapshot capture the teleporter reflection directly without hunting for a scripted-camera timestamp that happens to face it.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(mirrortest_skip_mirror,   "FDS_MIRRORTEST_SKIP_MIRROR",   0,                              "mirror",   "mirrortest snapshot: build the bare floor+wall+mirror+test-object scene WITHOUT invoking BuildMirror or the mask pre-pass. Use to bisect whether the Render() hang is in the mirror layer or in the base scene render path. Run: ./DEMO --deferred --snapshot=mirrortest --mirrortest-skip-mirror")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(greets_spot_height,      "FDS_GREETS_SPOT_HEIGHT",       0.0f,                           "greets",   "Greets: override robot spotlight height (0 = scene default)")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(greets_blasters,          "FDS_GREETS_BLASTERS",          0,                              "greets",   "Greets: test the blaster-bolt system — periodically fire bright additive energy bolts across the room (Star-Wars style, bloom into the HDR buffer). Off by default; dev harness for the BlasterBolts module destined for the chase scene.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (blaster_light,           "FDS_BLASTER_LIGHT",            1,                              "blaster",  "Blaster bolts EMIT light into the scene: each live bolt drives a transient, non-stationary, coloured Omni at its position (deferred surface lighting → moving light pools on nearby geometry, and fog in-scatter for free where fog is on). Scene-agnostic; works with or without fog. --no-blaster_light = the old draw-only billboard (no scene illumination). Capped at a fixed pool of simultaneous bolt-lights (excess live bolts glow but don't light).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(blaster_light_intensity, "FDS_BLASTER_LIGHT_INTENSITY",  160.0f,                         "blaster",  "Blaster bolt-light peak intensity (Omni ISize; radiance ≈ boltColour × this × linear-falloff). Scene-scale dependent — ~100-300 at the greets/chase scale. 0 = no emission (≈ --no-blaster_light).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(blaster_light_range,     "FDS_BLASTER_LIGHT_RANGE",      14.0f,                          "blaster",  "Blaster bolt-light range in VIEW-SPACE (world) units — how far the moving pool reaches. Scene-scale dependent (these scenes live in view-Z ~[5..80]); ~10-20 reads as a tight travelling glow, larger washes the room.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(no_greets_spots,          "FDS_NO_GREETS_SPOTS",          0,                              "greets",   "Greets: skip the code-installed robot spotlights (g_RobotSpot + orbit lights). FLD omnis still load.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(greets_omni_shadows,      "FDS_GREETS_OMNI_SHADOWS",      0,                              "greets",   "Greets: mark every FLD-loaded Light_Omni as Omni_CastsShadow|StaticShadow so CubeShadowMaps_Rebuild bakes 6-face shadow cubes for them. Disabled by default — N omnis × 6 faces × shadowMapRes² adds up quickly.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(greets_omni_shadow_res,    "FDS_GREETS_OMNI_SHADOW_RES",   256,                            "greets",   "Greets: per-face cube shadow-map resolution for omnis flagged by --greets-omni-shadows. Should be a power of two (128/256/512/1024). Total memory per omni = 6 × res² × 4 bytes.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(greets_moving_omni_shadow_res, "FDS_GREETS_MOVING_OMNI_SHADOW_RES", 0,                       "greets",   "Greets: separate per-face cube shadow-map resolution for omnis flagged as MOVING (Omni_CastsShadow without Omni_StaticShadow — typically mech-attached). 0 = use --greets-omni-shadow-res. Moving omnis pay full per-frame cube re-bake (no static cache, no lightmap), so dropping their res is the cheapest way to reclaim shadow time when they sit close to the camera.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(greets_piramid_chunk_grid, "FDS_GREETS_PIRAMID_CHUNK_GRID", 8,                              "greets",   "Greets: spatial-grid resolution per axis for Piramid.lwo chunking. Greets's wall mesh is one giant TriMesh (bsphere r≈60) that the cube-face cull can't reject; splitting it at scene init into N³ smaller TriMeshes (most cells empty → ~50-150 chunks in practice) lets mesh-level culling actually fire. 0 = disable chunking. Overridden by --greets_chunk_size when that is > 0.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(greets_chunk_size,       "FDS_GREETS_CHUNK_SIZE",        0.0f,                           "greets",   "Greets: SIZE-based Piramid chunk granularity (Phase A of the chunk-occlusion experiment, docs/VISIBILITY_PLAN.md §7). When > 0, the wall mesh is partitioned into near-CUBIC cells of ~this many world units per side (per-axis grid = ceil(span/size)) instead of the uniform N³ grid — so the cell size is resolution-independent and does NOT grow 20× when --greets_displace multiplies the face count. 0 = OFF: use the legacy greets_piramid_chunk_grid N³ grid, BYTE-IDENTICAL to before. Smaller = more, tighter chunks (better frustum + occlusion rejection, more per-chunk overhead). Chunking is look-neutral scene content (a spatial partition of the same faces), not a byte-gated engine change; only the default (0) is proven byte-null.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(chunk_occlusion,          "FDS_CHUNK_OCCLUSION",          0,                              "greets",   "Phase B (docs/VISIBILITY_PLAN.md §7): MAIN-VIEW-ONLY software occlusion cull of chunks/meshes BEFORE transform, using the PREVIOUS frame's opaque depth (ZPage16) as the occluder. The tick clears ZPage16 before Transform, so the depth is captured at END of frame (post-Render) with its camera; next frame each mesh's world AABB is projected with that CAPTURED camera and skipped if fully behind the farthest occluder over its screen footprint by more than --chunk_occl_bias + 2x the camera translation delta. Occluders = the REAL scene (displaced walls, mummies, robot) for FREE — no occluder-raster prepass, works on ANY scene with a Z-buffer, no --greets_shadow_proxy needed. Conservative in coverage (sky/untouched texel in a block = far plane = never cull; near-plane straddle / off-prev-buffer (incl. rotation-revealed) → keep). NOT byte-identical / NOT pin-safe: 1-frame-stale occluder → a chunk revealed between frames can be wrongly culled for one frame (temporal pop) — the deliberate trade for a free occluder set; audit with --chunk_occl_verify (must stay 0 over a camera sweep). Snapshot pin dumps force it inert. Default 0 = OFF (byte-null).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(chunk_occl_bias,         "FDS_CHUNK_OCCL_BIAS",          0.5f,                           "greets",   "chunk_occlusion conservative depth margin in view-Z (world) units: a chunk is culled only when its nearest point is at least this far BEHIND the farthest occluder over its screen footprint (2x the inter-frame camera translation is ADDED automatically). Absorbs dynamic-occluder motion, min-pool block coarseness and projection rounding. Larger = safer / fewer culls / less temporal pop; smaller = more catch.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(chunk_occl_res,            "FDS_CHUNK_OCCL_RES",           256,                            "greets",   "chunk_occlusion hi-Z WIDTH in texels (height keeps the frame aspect); the end-of-frame ZPage16 is min-pooled by ~XRes/this into it. Smaller = cheaper min-pool + coarser (min-pool + 1-texel rect expansion stay conservative — just less catch near silhouettes). 256 ≈ /7.5 of 1920. Min-pool cost is reported by --vis_stats.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(chunk_occl_verify,        "FDS_OCCL_VERIFY",              0,                              "greets",   "chunk_occlusion OVER-CULL AUDIT: record every chunk the cull skipped and, at end of frame, re-test it against the FINAL current-frame depth from the current camera. A culled chunk that is not hidden there would have contributed a pixel = a violation (upper bound: the farthest-over-rect test is loose at silhouettes — a hidden chunk whose rect overlaps a doorway showing far background is flagged; pair with the ground-truth pixel diff via chunk_occl_snapshot_force to separate). Reported per frame in --vis_stats and accumulated in g_occlViolationsTotal. Costs one extra min-pool + one AABB test per culled chunk; measurement runs only.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(chunk_occl_snapshot_force, "FDS_OCCL_SNAPSHOT_FORCE",     0,                              "greets",   "[dev] Override the snapshot harness's forced chunk-occlusion inertness (g_occlSnapshotInert) so a MULTI-timestamp snapshot recipe exercises the prev-frame cull: tick 1 captures depth, tick 2+ cull with it and DUMP the result — the ground-truth pixel-diff rig for temporal pops (compare against the same recipe without --chunk_occlusion). Never use while re-pinning gates.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(vis_stats,                "FDS_VIS_STATS",                0,                              "greets",   "Per-frame visibility diagnostic (docs/VISIBILITY_PLAN.md instrumentation): stderr [VIS-STATS] line with main-view meshes seen / transformed / frustum-rejected, verts seen / transformed, and (when --chunk_occlusion) chunks tested / occlusion-culled + verts/faces culled + occluder-face count + prepass ms. Default OFF; counters are behind a cached bool so the hot loop pays nothing when off. Diagnostic only — culls nothing.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(greets_stone_subdiv,       "FDS_GREETS_STONE_SUBDIV",      0,                              "greets",   "Greets: LINEAR-subdivide the stone wall ('rooms') + floor ('floor') materials N levels (1→4 faces per level) at init — the tessellation-density knob for the stone displacement bake (docs/ENVDYN_DISPLACEMENT_PLAN.md workstream B) and its cost spike. Pure density split: midpoints stay linear (no PN rounding — flat walls must stay flat), base geometry bit-unchanged. Runs at the FDS_MOMY_SUBDIV hook point: after Preprocess, before MakeFacesIndependentByAngle + the Piramid chunk split (so chunk bounds wrap the finer mesh). 0 = off (byte-identical); with --greets_displace on, 0 means 'use the B2-measured default density' (L=2: +3.4k faces, +2.3 ms threaded / +14.7 ms serial at the t=5780 wall pose; L=3 costs +11.5 ms threaded — needs the B5 tile pre-reject first).")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(greets_displace,          "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE",          0,                              "greets",   "Greets: STONE DISPLACEMENT bake (docs/ENVDYN_DISPLACEMENT_PLAN.md B3) — at init, subdivide the wall ('rooms') + floor ('floor') (see greets_stone_subdiv) and push each interior vertex along its smooth vertex normal by greets_displace_amp*(h-mipMean) (zero-mean relief, no DC bulge against the pinned borders), h bilinear-sampled from the material's HeightMap at mip greets_displace_mip at the per-FACE UV (averaged over the vertex's incident target faces). Real silhouettes + true self-shadowing for the block-scale relief; POM carries the finer detail (residual map, B4). Patch-BORDER verts (edges used by only one target face — wall/ceiling junctions, doorway jambs, wall-floor lines) are pinned at zero displacement so no T-junction or cross-material crack can open; relief fades to the authored edge there. Face normals + NormProd re-derived here; per-vertex normals re-derived by the MakeFacesIndependentByAngle pass right after (same as today). Needs the height maps loaded (--greets_stone_tex + --parallax, both default on). Default 0 = bake never runs (byte-identical).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(greets_displace_amp,     "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_AMP",      0.3f,                           "greets",   "Greets displacement amplitude in WORLD units: vertex offset = amp*(h-mipMean) along the vertex normal (mipMean = the sampled mip's average, so the relief is zero-mean), carving up to amp*(max-min) of peak-to-valley relief. The UV-space ParallaxScale is deliberately NOT reused (different units). Scene scale: the mummy is ~3.2 units tall (~1.8 m), so 0.1 ≈ 3 cm of stone relief.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(greets_displace_mip,       "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_MIP",      2,                              "greets",   "Greets displacement: height-map mip level the BAKE samples for vertex heights AND for the B4 residual split (they must match or POM double-counts). 1024² map: 2 = 256², 4 = 64² texels per UV tile. This is the geometry/POM frequency split point: geometry carries the relief at scales COARSER than this mip (real silhouette), POM's residual map carries the finer surface micro-texture. Default 2 (was 4): mip 4 box-blurred the block edges cell-wide, so displaced stone read as pillowy DOMES with wavy 'melted' mortar (the geometry carried a smoothed low band, sharp block edges left to POM's parallax fakery); mip 2 resolves the mortar grooves (~2-3 texels wide, ~32 texels/block) so the blocks pop out with crisp planar faces and STRAIGHT mortar. mip 1 is marginally crisper but risks aliasing sub-block surface noise into the geometry — mip 2 is the robust sweet spot (block-scale relief fully carried, micro-texture stays parallax). Verified A/B at frontal t=1867 + grazing poses; face count essentially unchanged (mip4 9590 → mip2 9667, +0.8%). Clamped to the map's block-tiled mip range.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(greets_displace_adapt,   "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_ADAPT",    1.0f,                           "greets",   "Greets displacement ADAPTIVE-DEPTH gain: each stone quad's subdivision level (0..5) is the smallest whose HONEST refinement error passes — a cell is 'bad' when its bilinear-of-corners deviates from the true height by > eps anywhere in the cell's FULL texel footprint at greets_displace_mip (not a sparse stencil, which used to alias and read flat) — bounded above by the per-quad block-pitch cap (greets_displace_cpb). This scales the error eps by 1/adapt: >1 = stricter = deeper, <1 = shallower/cheaper. 1.0 = the tuned default (genuinely flat quads stay shallow; block-bearing quads climb to block resolution). Only read when --greets_displace is on AND greets_stone_subdiv is 0 (a nonzero greets_stone_subdiv forces that UNIFORM level through the symmetric retriangulation instead — the cost-comparison baseline). Level-boundary edges are crack-free: the finer side's inserted edge verts are pinned onto the coarser side's straight displaced segment.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(greets_displace_cpb,     "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_CPB",      1.0f,                           "greets",   "Greets displacement CELLS-PER-BLOCK: the target subdivision density, in subdivision cells per stone BLOCK, that the adaptive bake drives each quad toward. The block pitch (mortar period) is estimated per material from the height map by gradient autocorrelation, so this is map-relative: however many UV tiles a wall quad spans, its cells land at block scale (the fix for 'cells span 2-3 blocks → ON looks like OFF'). 1.0 = one cell per block (each block a centre-fan dome, mortar recessed at the shared cell borders — the shipped default; blocks pop with the lowest face cost). 2.0 = half-block cells (crisper flat-topped blocks, but ~4x the faces and ~2x the frame cost — measured +70 ms at t=5780, use only for hero close-ups). <1 coarser/cheaper. The honest full-footprint refinement metric still keeps genuinely flat quads shallow; this only sets the ceiling. Only read when --greets_displace is on and greets_stone_subdiv is 0.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(greets_displace_edge,     "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_EDGE",     1,                              "greets",   "Greets displacement EDGE-ALIGNED tessellation (the flat-top fix, docs/ENVDYN_DISPLACEMENT_PLAN.md): subdivision cell borders SNAP onto the height map's mortar-groove lines instead of slicing a uniform 2^L grid, so block plateaus become FLAT cells at the plateau height (4 corners, 2 triangles, zero interior tessellation) and the step down/up happens in a narrow transition band at the groove (~1.25-texel shoulder pads at the bake mip). Groove lines are detected per material in MAP space: the per-row mean-height profile gives the horizontal mortar runs, and the per-BAND (between horizontal grooves) column profile gives each band's vertical runs — running bond, where vertical mortar phase alternates per block row, lands naturally as per-band groove positions. Fixes the proven centre-fan DOME defect (--scene-displacetest: FLAT-TOP 0% at cpb=1 — every block a pyramid, square-wave silhouette FAILS). Only applies to quads whose UV mapping is axis-aligned (up to u/v swap + flips) when the block-pitch estimate succeeds AND the groove profile is bimodal; everything else (lone triangles, rotated/skewed UV charts, structure-free maps like the smooth control) keeps the adaptive centre-fan path, and greets_stone_subdiv>0 (the uniform baseline) bypasses it entirely. Cross-quad/T-junction crack discipline: groove params quantized to a 1/2048 lattice + the generalized side-pin (finer side snapped onto the coarser side's displaced polyline). COST (measured t=5780 1080p threaded, iters=40): flags-off 52.5 ms; dome path (--no-greets_displace_edge) 42.9k faces / 72.5 ms; edge path 86.6k faces / 107.0 ms — same ms-per-face curve, the edge carve just makes more (and correctly-placed) faces: ~24 tris per block (flat top + 4 step walls + corners) vs the dome's 4-triangle fan that failed the square-wave test outright. greets_displace_cpb is NOT read by the edge path (it has no free density dial — the groove graph sets the cells); it still drives the fan fallback. Only read when --greets_displace is on. 0 = legacy dome tessellation (A/B + perf baseline).")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(greets_displace_seam_union, "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_SEAM_UNION", 1,                            "greets",   "S4a fan<->edge SEAM-HOLE fix (docs/OPTIMIZATION_BACKLOG.md S4a). The displacement bake's cross-patch heal only REPOSITIONS the finer side's edge verts onto the coarser side's polyline; where two adjacent stone patches' shared-side param lists have NO subset relation (a fan/lone patch's i/2^L side meeting an edge-aligned patch's mortar-groove side), the repositioned chord bypasses the other side's groove KINK -> a hairline background hole at the junction. Fix: for every interior side shared by two patches whose param lists are NEITHER a subset of the other, UNION both lists and give BOTH patches a vertex at every union param (fan-splitting the boundary triangle of each segment that lacks them), so both boundary polylines pass through the identical shared-vertex set = watertight; the heal then no-ops on those sides. SUBSET sides (level boundaries) are untouched (adaptive-level tessellation stays byte-identical). Default ON; --no-greets_displace_seam_union reproduces the pre-fix holey heal for A/B. Only read when --greets_displace is on.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(greets_displace_fold_relax, "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_FOLD_RELAX", 1,                            "greets",   "FOLD RELAXATION for the stone displacement bake — the fix for the user-reported SLIVER GAP (see-through seam at a displaced wall's top edge, repro FDS_GREETS_CAM=18.45,5.16,-57.65,... t=6097). ROOT CAUSE (measured at the repro pose, docs/ENVDYN_DISPLACEMENT_PLAN.md): a narrow authored RETURN face (a jamb/pilaster side strip about one shoulder-band tall) has verts whose smoothed vertex normals point in very different directions; the mean-centered relief recesses them by very different amounts (0.02 vs 0.135 world measured), TWISTING the strip past 90 degrees. DisplaceStoneSubdiv commits face N/NormProd with the AUTHORED orientation sign, so the folded strip's plane equation opposes its actual winding and the backface cull (Transform.cpp plane test) rejects it when viewed from the FRONT -> a see-through slit through topologically sealed geometry (z-scan: 52 background px through the wall; both lips the same 'rooms' surface; all position-keyed edges sealed; scales with amp, gone at recess-free clamp). Same defect class as B1's SubdivideMaterialFaces fold-over (fixed there by fold-relaxation) — this is that pass adapted to the displaced mesh: iteratively HALVE the displacement of every vert of a face whose displaced winding normal OPPOSES its own PRE-displacement winding normal (g_disp*g_base < 0 = the face rotated past 90 degrees from its undisplaced self; convention-FREE, so FLD-loaded walls and SceneBuilder rigs — which have opposite winding-vs-N conventions, measured — are both handled, and an undisplaced face can never be marked). These are exactly the faces whose committed N ends up opposing their winding, so the Transform plane cull rejects them while they still front the camera (proven by force-two-sided closing the sliver with no other change). Iterate until no target face is inverted (offsets -> 0 restores the base plane, so convergence is monotone; 8th pass zeroes stragglers; ~4k of 63k rooms faces at the repro), BEFORE the cross-patch heal so healed polylines land on relaxed anchors. Relief loss is confined to the folded slivers. Default ON (correctness, sibling of --greets_displace_seam_union); --no-greets_displace_fold_relax restores the pre-fix fold for A/B. Only read when --greets_displace is on. Prints a one-line [STONE-FOLD] census.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(greets_displace_shadow_planes, "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_SHADOW_PLANES", 1,                      "greets",   "PARENT-PLANE ShadowMatID inheritance for the displaced greets stone — the LIGHT-BLEED fix. The displaced-wall acne fix collapsed ALL 'rooms' (and 'floor') faces to ONE shadow id so facets self-match everywhere; but the deferred PolyId identity test then ALSO matched every rooms-vs-rooms OCCLUSION: a disco/mech omni sitting behind a backfaced rooms wall lit the next rooms wall straight through it (the shadow pass renders two-sided, so the occluder IS in the shadow cube — it just carried the receiver's own id and was skipped as 'self'). This flag keys each displaced facet's shadow cluster by its PARENT (pre-displacement authored) plane instead: DisplaceStoneSubdiv stamps every emitted face with its parent plane's registry ordinal (transient, resolved by the greets clustering; MeshOps_StoneParentPlane), so facets of one flat wall still share one id (the acne stays fixed WITHIN a wall — same collapse semantics per plane as the pre-displacement flat clustering) while DIFFERENT walls get different ids and occlude each other again. The S1 offscreen proxy gets per-face plane-matched ids (it is the parent geometry). Default ON (correctness — restores inter-wall shadowing the collapse silently removed); --no-greets_displace_shadow_planes restores the whole-material collapse for A/B. Only read when --greets_displace is on.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(greets_displace_offscreen_skip, "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_OFFSCREEN_SKIP", 1,                     "greets",   "S1 offscreen proxy: skip a 100 %-displaced Piramid chunk from EVERY offscreen pass, not just the shadow bake. Those chunks already get Tri_NoShadowCast at scene init, which Transform_Objects gates on g_inShadowPass -- so the mirror RTT bakes and the env/SH probes kept transforming them in full and then dropped every one of their faces on Face_MainOnly, because the flat Tri_OffscreenProxy stand-in is what casts and reflects for them. This flag also stamps Tri_AllFacesMainOnly, which the mesh loop tests against the OFFSCREEN predicate. BYTE-EXACT BY CONSTRUCTION: every face of such a chunk carries Face_MainOnly, so in an offscreen pass the face loop `continue`s on all of them and the mesh emits not one FList entry -- the same \"no faces => no output\" invariant the FIndex == 0 skip rests on. MEASURED greets t=5743, his acceptance arm, --xfrm_pass_prof: the mirror RTT transformed 54 073 of its 100 618 verts/frame and 31 894 of its 33 866 face-visits (94.2 %) on these chunks, the env probes 151 500 of 257 604 verts. The hatch lives at scene init, NOT in the mesh loop: a runtime flag read inside that -ffp-contract=fast function is not byte-null even when never taken (docs/VISIBILITY_PLAN.md 8). Inert unless --greets_displace built the chunks. Default ON.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(greets_displace_neighbor_pin, "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_NEIGHBOR_PIN", 1,                        "greets",   "CROSS-MATERIAL SEAM fix for the stone displacement bake (docs/ENVDYN_DISPLACEMENT_PLAN.md workstream B; the user-reported sliver-gap). The bake's authored-border pinning (which verts stay at zero displacement) classified borders by shared vertex INDEX within the mesh (non-target-face incidence) + patch-border edges (an edge used by exactly one target face). But a displaced wall ('rooms') meeting a lintel/ceiling of a DIFFERENT, non-displaced material is authored as SEPARATE geometry: its own duplicate verts at the SAME coordinates, often in another mesh, coincident-by-POSITION not shared-by-index. Those boundary verts were never classified as borders, so they displaced and the seam tore open a long thin gap (widest mid-span, closing at the ends — the mid-span subdivision verts along the edge moved while the pinned quad corners held). This fix builds a scene-wide position index of every NON-DISPLACED vertex and face-edge (once per bake) and treats a target vertex coincident with one, or a target edge lying on a non-displaced edge, as an authored border -> pinned to zero (the created verts ALONG such an edge pin too, closing the mid-span). DISPLACED siblings are excluded (greets rooms<->floor stays coincident-but-relieved — that junction already renders correctly and must not flatten). Default ON (a correctness fix, same spirit as --greets_displace_seam_union); --no-greets_displace_neighbor_pin restores the pre-fix classification for A/B. Only read when --greets_displace is on; prints a one-line [STONE-SEAM] audit of the pinned population per material.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(greets_displace_line_height, "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_LINE_HEIGHT", 1,                        "greets",   "CONSISTENT GROOVE-LINE displacement for the stone bake — the GRAZING ZIGZAG fix (user repro: FDS_GREETS_CAM=-7.387,2.725,-50.824,0.818,-0.114,0.564 t=2845, a vertical mortar joint sawtoothing +-25 px at a grazing view; POM/fold-relax exonerated by the diagnosis A/B matrix). ROOT CAUSE (measured, causally — zero-displacement box bisection at the repro): the artifact wall's authored UV chart is a 4:1 TRAPEZOID (non-affine), so the edge-aligned tessellation rejects it (non-parallelogram) and it falls to the block-pitch FAN lattice, where a few-texel mortar groove is UNREPRESENTABLE: each lattice vert near the groove sampled the mid-slope independently and the smeared pseudo-carve (recessed lattice column + alternating field-following diagonals) shears into a per-cell chevron sawtooth under the ~20x grazing magnification. Making those heights merely CONSISTENT does NOT straighten it (measured); NOT carving there does (measured: zeroing the strip's displacement = straight, look intact — the joint reads via albedo/normal map/POM exactly as before). FIX (bake-time only, zero per-frame cost), one principle - verts tracing a groove displace consistently - two populations: (1) EDGE-ALIGNED patches: every vert on a detected groove line (<=0.45 texel, wrap-aware; incl. S4a seam-union inserts) takes the line's representative height = MEDIAN of the height field along that line (min wins at crossings — mortar recess dominates); keeps the edge path's clean carve, kills its along-line variance. (2) FAN/LONE fallback patches (charts the edge path rejected): verts inside a groove's influence zone (<=2.5 texels of any line) pin to the groove's PLATEAU reference (median along an inset line pad+1.25 texels outside the groove — beyond the mip blur; the pad lines themselves read mid-slope 0.37 vs true plateau 0.60 on the repro wall) — the fallback simply does not carve grooves it cannot represent. Plateau-interior verts everywhere keep their own samples: block pitch + carve depth on the edge path unchanged. Measured at the repro: zigzag lateral deviation 8.3 px std -> 4.4 (straight no-displace reference 3.1); fold-relax census 3882->3818 rooms / 62->57 floor, still converges. Default ON (correctness, sibling of --greets_displace_fold_relax); --no-greets_displace_line_height restores the independent per-vert sampling for A/B. Only read when --greets_displace is on. Prints a one-line [STONE-LINE] census per material.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(greets_shadow_proxy,      "FDS_GREETS_SHADOW_PROXY",      0,                              "greets",   "S1 OFFSCREEN PROXY (docs/ENVDYN_DISPLACEMENT_PLAN.md): render the displaced stone (rooms/floor detail, ~81k faces) ONLY to the main camera; every offscreen consumer (per-frame shadow bake, mirror RTT, env probe, disco/shatter) rasterises a FLAT pre-displacement stand-in (~226 faces) instead. Built at init as a separate Tri_OffscreenProxy mesh with the displaced walls' single shadow-cluster id stamped on so the deferred PolyId self-shadow test matches; the displaced detail is tagged Face_MainOnly and skipped in offscreen passes, and chunks that are 100%% displaced are marked Tri_NoShadowCast so their per-vertex transform is skipped too. Measured (t=5780, 1080p, threaded, 30-iter p50, interleaved): BAKE 27.3->21.5 ms, frame 92.8->88.1 ms (-4.7; the win is bounded because the cube-face cull already limited per-frame wall rastering). TRADE-OFF: the flat caster's shadows differ from the displaced walls' on the looked-at wall (~1%% of pixels shift by the displacement offset — measured, NOT invisible), and a flat caster carries no mortar relief so it does NOT restore per-block groove self-shadow. DEFAULT 0 so --greets_displace alone keeps its reviewed look; set 1 to opt into the perf (a look call). Only read when --greets_displace is on. RE-MEASURED 2026-08-06 on the post-799c808 tree (t=5780 wall bench, 1080p, 12 threads, iters=20, 4 interleaved rounds, min-of-arm, load 7-10): frame 67.95->62.05 ms, BAKE 9.18->3.45 (flat POM's BAKE at the same pose is 3.26, i.e. the proxy removes essentially the WHOLE displaced-caster bake). Colour divergence over the 16 review camera/t pairs: byte-identical at 5, elsewhere 1282-110668 px >4/255 with the worst at t=6097 (58021 px >12/255, 14604 >32/255, max 144, concentrated at the corner junction; mean |d| on changed px 1.0-4.3). GreetsApplyInitDefaults now setDefault()s this ON whenever --greets_displace is on (one flag = the good tessellated arm) and prints a [STONE-PROXY] note saying so; --no-greets_shadow_proxy still wins.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(greets_displace_flat_mirror, "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_FLAT_MIRROR", 0,                        "greets",   "MIRROR CLONES REFLECT THE FLAT STONE (docs/VISIBILITY_PLAN.md 8d/9e, re-opened for the tessellated arm 2026-08-06). GreetsMirror clones the whole scene per mirror as ordinary MAIN-VIEW geometry, and Face_MainOnly is only honoured in OFFSCREEN passes — so under --greets_displace the reflection re-transforms and re-rasterises the full tessellated wall. MEASURED at t=5780 (1080p, 12 threads, iters=20, min-of-arm): the clone costs the TESSELLATED arm 11.40 ms/frame (RNDR 7.19 + XFRM 2.17 + SORT 0.34) versus 3.31 ms in the flat-POM arm, and it pushes 42870 clone faces while the direct view pushes only 28598 displaced ones -- the reflection draws MORE displaced wall than the camera does, because the clone is culled by the frustum and not by the mirror WINDOW (0.04-3.9%% of screen). With this ON the clone sources rooms/floor from the FLAT --greets_shadow_proxy mesh instead: Face_MainOnly faces are skipped, the Tri_OffscreenProxy mesh is included, and a source mesh that contributes no clone face has its VERTS skipped too (so the saving lands in the transform phase, not only in RNDR). NEEDS --greets_shadow_proxy (which --greets_displace now defaults ON) or the reflection loses its walls entirely. LOOK TRADE, stated not hidden: the reflected masonry keeps its albedo/normal map and its POM march (measured to read in the mirror: 96.7%% of the mirror's 32674 px move without the march) but loses the GEOMETRIC block relief and its silhouettes, inside a mirror window that is 1.58%% of the screen at t=1588. Default 0 = today's behaviour, byte-null. Inert without --greets_displace + --greets_shadow_proxy.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(greets_displace_smooth,  "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_SMOOTH",   80.0f,                           "greets",   "Greets displacement SMOOTH-NORMAL angle (degrees, S1 faceting fix): after the displacement bake + MakeFacesIndependentByAngle, the displaced stone is re-smoothed by a scene-wide position-bucket weld that averages the DISPLACED face normals over neighbours within THIS angle (area-weighted), rebuilding a continuous normal AND tangent basis so the triangulation stops reading as facets/hairline seams. Higher = smoother relief (rounder stone); MUST stay below 90 so authored wall-to-wall 90-degree corners stay hard (they are ~90 apart, so the gate won't average across them); material boundaries (wall/floor/ceiling) are hard for free. 0 = leave the per-face facet normals (debug: shows the raw triangulation). Only read when --greets_displace is on.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(zero_normal_census,       "FDS_ZERO_NORMAL_CENSUS",       0,                              "engine",   "[dev] Census of ZERO VERTEX NORMALS (|N| <= EPSILON), printed from Compute_Vertex_Normals for every mesh that has any, in EVERY scene. Classifies each one by WHY it is zero, which is the only question that matters for whether the geometry is reachable: ORPHAN (the vertex is in Verts[] but no face references it, so the accumulator never ran and N is still the zeroed init value), ALL-DEGENERATE (every incident face is zero-area, so Compute_Face_Normals deliberately left its N as the un-normalized zero cross — the greets Piramid case, 2026-08-16u), or CANCEL (real-area faces whose area-weighted normals sum to zero — a zero-thickness fin or a fully doubled surface, i.e. REACHABLE geometry cornering fillable triangles). Prints per vertex: incident face count, how many carry a zero N, the largest incident area, |Sum(area*N)| / Sum(area) as the cancellation residual, and the most-opposed pair dot; plus the incident faces themselves (area, N, material, and whether the two are position-COINCIDENT) for the first few of each material. Default 0 = nothing walked, nothing printed, byte-null.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(tangent_nan_census,       "FDS_TANGENT_NAN_CENSUS",       0,                              "greets",   "[dev] Census of NON-FINITE Vertex::Tangent (NaN/Inf) across the scene, printed at the named init stages of the greets displacement bake (post-DisplaceStoneSubdiv, post-MakeFacesIndependentByAngle, post-DisplaceStoneSmoothNormals, post-Piramid-chunk-split) plus a final scene-wide sweep. Prints per-mesh counts, then up to 32 offending verts as (mesh, vert index, Pos, N, |N|, the material of an incident face) so the producing stage and the degenerate input are both identifiable. Also arms a one-shot diagnostic inside the two normalize sites that can emit a NaN tangent — Compute_Vertex_Tangents' perpendicular-axis fallback (FDS/MISC/PREPROC.CPP) and DisplaceStoneSmoothNormals' matching fallback — each of which divides by the length of a vector that is zero when the vertex normal is zero. Default 0 = nothing walked, nothing printed, byte-null.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(displace_viz,              "FDS_DISPLACE_VIZ",             0,                              "greets",   "DEBUG overlay for the stone-displacement bake (docs/ENVDYN_DISPLACEMENT_PLAN.md B). MODE selector (needs an explicit value, e.g. --displace_viz=1): 0 = OFF (byte-null, nothing recorded or drawn), 1 = MAGNITUDE wireframe, 2 = HEIGHT-ERROR field, 3 = DIRECTION/HEIGHT combination, 4 = NEEDLES. MODE 3 (the corner-debug view, 2026-08-27): fills each displaced triangle by the WORST corner ANGLE between the displacement vector the bake ACTUALLY applied (final-base, recorded per vert) and the triangle BASE-plane normal (base positions reconstructed as final-vec, i.e. the pre-bake wall plane): GREEN 0deg = rode the wall normal (all interiors must read this), YELLOW ~45deg = a legitimate mitre at a square corner, RED 90deg = slid ALONG the wall; brightness = |displacement| vs the bake max, and a corner carrying <2%% of max reads SOLID BLUE = FLUSH (no height carried - a DIFFERENT defect than a wrong direction). MODE 4 draws the applied vectors as x3-scaled depth-tested needles, same colours. Both answer the corner question directly: which normal did each vert ride and how much height did it carry. All modes project the displaced 'rooms'/'floor' post-bake triangles through the main camera over the final frame (post-tonemap), depth-tested against ZPage16 so occluded far walls don't bleed through. MODE 1 draws the triangle EDGES tinted by per-vertex displacement magnitude (COOL blue = 0 at pinned borders + flat verts, green, WARM red = the bake's max push) — shows the added/subdivided geometry and how far each vertex moved. MODE 2 tints each displaced triangle by the SIGNED height error truth − carried, where truth = the height map at the BAKE mip and carried = the barycentric interp of the vertices' applied displacement, taken as the WORST error anywhere in the triangle's full texel footprint (not a sparse stencil, which aliased and read everything 'matched'). The value is an ABSOLUTE fraction of the map's peak-to-valley relief: MATCHED cells (|err| < 15%) get NO fill — only the thin wireframe — so the eye is drawn to failures; above that the fill fades in and tints RED = geometry UNDER-carries (missing relief — cell too coarse to follow the map, raise greets_displace_cpb) / BLUE = OVER-carries. Reads as a heat map of where the displaced GEOMETRY fails the HEIGHT MAP, so it's the tuning dial for greets_displace_cpb / _mip / _amp. Needs --greets_displace ON to have any baked geometry; with the bake OFF the overlay is empty and prints a one-line stderr hint. Run: ./DEMO --scene-greets --greets_displace --displace_viz=2")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(shadow_cube_face_cull,    "FDS_SHADOW_CUBE_FACE_CULL",    1,                              "shadows",  "Per-cube-face bsphere cull in Transform_Objects: skip meshes whose world-space bsphere doesn't intersect the 90°-FOV pyramid of the cube face being baked. Reuses sphereOutsideSpotCone with a circumscribed cone (half-angle 54.7°, cos ≈ 0.577) to keep the test conservative. Big win for moving omnis with 6 active faces — typically each face sees only ~⅙ of the scene.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(shadow_bbox_cull,         "FDS_SHADOW_BBOX_CULL",         1,                              "shadows",  "S2/B5 pre-reject for the SHADOW DEPTH RASTER — the same 4-compare screen-bbox test --tile_bbox_cull gives RenderInner/RenderInnerMekalele, transplanted onto Shadows.cpp's per-(light, tile) face walk, which never had one. That walk hands EVERY survivor of its four material/degenerate/behind rejects straight to FrustumClipper::Render; measured 2026-08-16q at greets t=5743 (--deferred --hdr --hdr-linear --texture-filter=2 --ssao --ssao-gtao --greets-displace) it issued 231 735 clipper entries a frame, 83.4 %% of the whole frame's, and 81.8 %% of them (189 567) were clipped away to NOTHING — three 140-byte Vertex copies, the UV stamp and Calc_Flags bought zero pixels. The bbox is ALREADY on the FListEntry: the per-light FList is built by Transform_Objects, whose stamp is unconditional on the shadow path (its ablation gate is main-view-only), and PX/PY there are SHADOW-MAP pixels because the light's CameraContext drives the projection — so the box and the tile rect are in the same space and the test is RenderInner's verbatim. BYTE-EXACT by the same S2 argument, re-derived for the light frustum: the box is a conservative superset of the un-clipped triangle, valid only when all three verts are in front of the light camera's near plane (behind-near faces keep the cover-all sentinel and are NEVER rejected, which is what makes it safe where 6.5 %% of entries need a Z clip); the FAR clip manufactures vertices on the 3-D segment between two in-front verts, whose projection stays on the projected 2-D segment, hence inside the box; and the 2-D clip only ever SHRINKS coverage. Verified with --shadow_plane_hash: every packed shadow plane byte-identical at every pose. Inert when --no-tile_bbox_cull (boxes stay cover-all). Default ON; --no-shadow_bbox_cull restores the un-culled walk for A/B.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(shadow_plane_hash,        "FDS_SHADOW_PLANE_HASH",        0,                              "shadows",  "DIAGNOSTIC, changes no pixel: after every shadow-bake drain, FNV-1a the PACKED PLANE (depth+polyId, all 4 bytes of every texel) of every map the bake just wrote, in the deterministic tick-thread map order, and print one [SPH] line per bake invocation plus a process-cumulative digest. This is the gate for any change to the shadow raster or its culls: shadow maps feed every scene's lighting, so a wrong reject shows up as acne/popping over TIME, not as a pin diff at one pose — comparing final frames at four poses does not prove the planes are the same. Compare the [SPH] streams parent-to-child; equal streams mean every light's plane was byte-identical at every baked frame. Cost when off: one bool load per bake invocation (not per face, not per texel).")
FDS_FLAG_INT (clone_refresh_inputs,     "FDS_CLONE_REFRESH_INPUTS",     0,                              "shadows",  "CLONE INPUT INVALIDATION. fds::PerTriMeshClone (FDS/Base/VertexScratch.h) snapshots a mesh's WHOLE Vertex[] and Face[] the FIRST time a clone-backed pass (the 42 per-frame shadow bakes; the shatter's per-worker reflection renders) touches that mesh, and NEVER refreshes it -- nothing in the tree clears VertexScratch::clones or resets `initialized`. Transform_Objects only rewrites the projection OUTPUTS (Vertex offsets [0,52): PX, PY, Flags, TPos_AOS, RZ, TN, TTangent), so every INPUT byte from offset 52 on (Pos, N, Tangent, BGRA, UZ/VZ, EUZ/EVZ, U/V, EU/EV, i, OrigBary, ShellH) plus the entire cloned Face[] is frozen at first-use forever. 0 (DEFAULT, and what always shipped) = frozen. 1 = re-copy the 88-byte vertex INPUT block from the live mesh on every clone REUSE. 2 = also re-copy the Face[] (A/B/C re-remapped). WHY IT IS OFF: measured with --clone_stale_census, no mesh that reaches a clone-backed pass mutates those bytes after its first bake -- rigid animation moves IPos/RotMat, which the transform reads off the TriMesh and not off the clone, and the two greets meshes that DO rewrite vertex state every tick (mirror clones rewrite Pos in UpdateMirror, the disco ball rewrites LR/LG/LB in UpdateDiscoBall) both carry Tri_NoShadowCast and are skipped by every shadow occluder pass. Level 1 is therefore byte-null today and is here as the ARMED FIX: if you ever animate vertex data on a caster, or clear Tri_NoShadowCast on one of those two, turn this on and the shadows follow the geometry again. Level 2 additionally resets the clone's per-face LastMip hysteresis, so it is NOT byte-null and is a diagnostic, not a fix. Cost at level 1 is the 88-byte copy x VIndex on every (shadow map x mesh) pair -- 1269 pairs x 88 B = 138 MiB/frame of extra copy traffic at greets, which is why the shipping answer is the census + this switch and not an unconditional refresh.")
FDS_FLAG_INT (clone_stale_census,       "FDS_CLONE_STALE_CENSUS",       0,                              "prof",     "[INSTRUMENT, default OFF, changes no pixel, needs -DFDS_VIS_CENSUS=ON] Answers 'is any clone stale, and by how much' directly instead of by argument. On every REUSE of a fds::PerTriMeshClone (i.e. every clone-backed Transform_Objects call after the mesh's first), compare the clone's read-only input fields against the LIVE TriMesh -- Pos, N, Tangent, the 52-byte tail, and every Face field the pass does not itself rewrite -- and count the divergences per mesh NAME. Prints [CLONE-STALE] lines every N main-view frames: one per mesh that ever diverged plus a TOTAL line. A clean run is `stale=0` with a non-zero reuse count; a non-zero reuse count is what proves the instrument is actually looking (a run whose every clone is fresh proves nothing). Companion switch: --clone_refresh_inputs, which makes the divergence go away so you can see whether it reached pixels.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(shadow_cube_vert_cull,    "FDS_SHADOW_CUBE_VERT_CULL",    0,                              "shadows",  "Per-vertex pyramid cull in the cube-face shadow xform, gated on TriMesh::worldVerts being pre-computed. For meshes that pass the mesh-level cube-face cull but only partially overlap the face pyramid, skip the view matmul for world-space vertices outside. Off by default: in practice (greets chunk grid=8) the mesh-level cull is tight enough that surviving meshes have nearly all verts inside; the per-vertex test cost + un-fused perspective in the survivor path (~36 ops vs 18 ops M34) exceeds the savings. Useful if you have one giant moving mesh that can't be spatially split.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(shadow_swizzle,           "FDS_SHADOW_SWIZZLE",           0,                              "shadows",  "[experiment] Store shadow-map depth/polyId in 8x8-tiled (swizzled) layout so a 2x2/3x3 PCF footprint lands in one cache line instead of straddling rows, and read them swizzled in the lighting-pass PCF. A timed swizzle pass converts the freshly-baked linear maps to tiled each frame; --shadow_bake_time reports the swizzle cost separately so we can weigh swizzle-cost vs PCF-read speedup.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(shadow_lm_dynamic,       "FDS_SHADOW_LM_DYNAMIC",        0,                              "shadows",  "Keep the static-shadow LIGHTMAP fast path active when --shadow-dynamic is on, compositing lightmap(static) x dynamic-only cube tap (resolveCubeAtten already implements the composite). Previously --shadow-dynamic disabled the lightmap entirely, forcing full cube taps for every static light at every pixel. Faces whose dynamic buffer was not baked this frame (no moving mesh visible: ShadowMap::dynBaked) skip the dynamic tap. MEASURED NEUTRAL-to-NEGATIVE on greets (planar sampler +0.8ms w1; mech keeps dynBaked true on ~40% of faces; moving-omni taps can never use lightmaps) -> default OFF, kept for configs with sparse dynamics.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(greets_omni_default_range, "FDS_GREETS_OMNI_DEFAULT_RANGE", 0.0f,                          "greets",   "Greets: HARD OVERRIDE on every Light_Omni's range, in world units. 0 (default) = inert, use the AUTHORED range. Ranges are authored per light in Authoring/greets/JENINPYR-new-2.LWS ('LightRange <f>' inside each AddLight block) and flow LWS -> tools/lwsread -> the FLD Range envelope -> Omni::Range, which Animate_Objects evaluates into Omni::IRange every frame; all ten currently author 30. When > 0 this rewrites the spline KEYS (not just IRange, which the next Animate_Objects would overwrite) so the forced value survives, and it hits every Light_Omni alive at that point, including the disco-ball lights -- so it is a tuning dial, not a fallback. HISTORY: this used to default to 30 and fire whenever IRange == 0, which at scene-init time means 'the spline has not been evaluated yet', NOT 'no authored range' (see FDS/FLD/FLD_CONV.CPP) -- so it flattened all ten authored ranges on every run. Its old help text claimed the FLD format omits the envelope; that was wrong, every greets omni has always carried one.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(greets_mirror_min_area,  "FDS_GREETS_MIRROR_MIN_AREA",   2.0f,                           "greets",   "Greets: secondary area gate, applied AFTER the explicit surface-name allowlist (see kMirrorScreenMats in GREETS.CPP) — it only picks the front face of a marked screen's box vs its thin side caps; it no longer decides WHICH screens reflect (that's the explicit name list now). Box edge strips 0.30-0.32, column panels ~1.0, small amudim screens ~6.4, big display 172.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(greets_mirror_rtt_min_area, "FDS_GREETS_MIRROR_RTT_MIN_AREA", 1.5f,                        "greets",   "Greets: minimum coplanar P_TEXT cluster area to become a FIRST-ORDER RTT mirror (below the clone threshold). The central column panels (~0.98-1.00 area) + box edge strips (~0.30) are barely-visible half-silvered screens that add little but each cost an RTT slot + the per-frame RTT bake. Default 1.5 drops them (only end-screen CLONES at >=2.0 survive; second-order/recursive RTT is unaffected). Set 0.2 to re-include the columns + strips.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(mirror_rtt_trace,         "FDS_MIRROR_RTT_TRACE",         0,                              "greets",   "DIAGNOSTIC. Record, per frame and IN MEMORY (no per-frame stderr, so worker scheduling is not perturbed), the greets mirror RTT scheduler's decisions and the CONTENT it produced: which slots the 2-jobs/frame round-robin picked, their selection priority, the adaptive resolution each was baked at, and an FNV-1a hash of every baked slot texture. MirrorRttTrace_Report() prints the whole run in one burst (the --repro harness calls it just before it dumps the frame). WHY: the greets mirror is per-launch NON-DETERMINISTIC at ~1 flip in 15 full-history --repro_prescenes runs (2026-08-15f), and the panel is exactly where the user's reported band lands. This flag separates the two candidate causes by construction: if the SCHEDULE lines differ between a canonical and a flipped launch the round-robin is the mechanism; if the schedule is identical and only a texture HASH differs, the non-determinism is inside the RTT render and the scheduler is exonerated. 2026-08-16: the record now carries a digest after EVERY stage of the bake, in execution order -- camera, the shared xpar peel floor at entry, the transformed+sorted draw list and every scene vertex, the animated omni state, g_shadowMaps (planes AND the viewToLight transforms), the G-buffer after the surface kernel, the surface after deferred lighting, after cones -- plus the frame-1 surface VERBATIM ([RTTPIX]). A launch diff then names the first stage that MOVED and, just as important, every stage before it that did not; that is what turned 'the RTT render is non-deterministic' into 'ShadowMap::viewToLight is uninitialised at the frame-1 bake'. The whole-run FULL digest also stopped hashing the record's alignment holes, which were indeterminate and made the instrument report differences the render never made.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(mirror_rtt_probe,         "FDS_MIRROR_RTT_PROBE",         0,                              "greets",   "Per-frame probe for second-order mirror pairs: for every active mirror A whose reflection contains another mirror B's panel on screen, log the projected pixel footprint (= required RTT resolution), the doubly-reflected virtual camera position, and B's panel window extents. Diagnostic groundwork for the order-2 render-to-texture path; no rendering changes.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(mirror_rtt,               "FDS_MIRROR_RTT",               0,                              "greets",   "Second-order mirror reflections via render-to-texture: panels of mirror B seen inside mirror A's reflection display a per-frame low-res forward render of the real scene from the doubly-reflected camera, through B's panel window (off-axis projection, near plane at the mirror plane). Up to 2 visible pairs re-rendered per frame, picked by projected footprint area.")
FDS_FLAG_INT(mirror_recurse_depth,      "FDS_MIRROR_RECURSE_DEPTH",     0,                              "greets",   "Recursive mirror reflection depth (0 = legacy order-1 clones + order-2 RTT). N>0 enables the recursive portal-render path: a mirror seen in a mirror ... resolves N levels deep. See docs/MIRROR_RECURSION_PLAN.md. Campaign in progress — slices land incrementally; validate on --scene-cloaktest.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(mirror_rtt_alpha,        "FDS_MIRROR_RTT_ALPHA",         0.75f,                          "greets",   "First-order RTT panel blend: panel weight in the transparent kernel's lerp (panel*a + behind*(1-a)). 0.75 = mostly mirror with a hint of see-through; lower is more windowy. 0 falls back to the legacy additive texel + behind/2.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(mirror_rtt_gain,         "FDS_MIRROR_RTT_GAIN",          0.55f,                          "greets",   "First-order RTT reflection gain (reflectance): the mirrored scene is scaled by this before the text composite. The offscreen pass is forward-lit (StaticLighting bake on walls), which runs brighter than the deferred main pass — <1 re-balances, and real mirrors absorb anyway. 1.0 = no attenuation.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(greets_disco,             "FDS_GREETS_DISCO",             1,                              "greets",   "Mirrored disco ball hanging in the greets corridor: faceted env-mapped sphere + rotating spotlight dots.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(shard_deferred,           "FDS_SHARD_DEFERRED",           0,                              "greets",   "Mirror-shard reflections bake through the DEFERRED kernel (shadowed, lit exactly as the main deferred view) instead of the forward filler. Each shard owns a per-worker G-buffer fanned across the pool (MirrorShatter Slice 6). ~20ms vs ~6ms forward on greets's 238 shards — shadowed quality for the cost. Off → forward bake. FDS_SHARD_REFL_SERIAL forces the legacy serial path either way. Read once at shatter time. Run: ./DEMO --shard-deferred --scene-greets")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(mirror_rtt_density,      "FDS_MIRROR_RTT_DENSITY",       256.0f,                         "greets",   "RTT mirror texel density (texels per world unit, pow2-rounded per axis, capped at 512x512). Replaces the old constant 64K budget that gave big panels visibly lower resolution than small ones.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(mirror_rtt_adaptive,      "FDS_MIRROR_RTT_ADAPTIVE",      1,                              "greets",   "View-dependent RTT resolution: bake each mirror panel at the pow2 size matching its PROJECTED on-screen footprint (clamped to the density-derived max), instead of a fixed world-size*density texture. A panel seen small/oblique/across-the-room bakes cheap; a panel you're standing in front of keeps full res. Zero visible loss (never more texels than screen pixels) and the dominant greets-central-room ANIM cost (RenderSecondOrderMirrors). Off = legacy fixed size.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(mirror_rtt_adaptive_scale,"FDS_MIRROR_RTT_ADAPTIVE_SCALE", 1.0f,                          "greets",   "Multiplier on the on-screen footprint before pow2-rounding for --mirror-rtt-adaptive. 1.0 = ~1 texel per screen pixel (pow2of already rounds up within 1.5x); raise toward 2 if oblique panels look soft, lower for more savings.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(greets_mirror_tint,      "FDS_GREETS_MIRROR_TINT",       0.0f,                           "greets",   "OPTIONAL cool-silver cast on ALL greets mirrors via ONE composite: out = silver*tint + reflection/2. The half-silvered look is the HALVED WARM reflection (reflection/2); silver is an extra cast on top. tint=0 = dimmed warm reflection, matching the display screens (#2) with no cast; raise for a cool silver cast on the portal (#1, kernel XparBlendAlpha=0 + Lum=tint) and shatter shards (#3, same formula baked into the atlas). LIVE. (Replaces greets_shard_silver / greets_portal_tint.) FDS_TINT_RED=1 swaps silver for red to verify.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(greets_shard_randomness, "FDS_GREETS_SHARD_RANDOMNESS",  1.0f,                           "greets",   "Greets shatter: irregularity of the shard pieces. Scales BOTH the non-uniform grid line spacing (varied shard SIZES) and the per-intersection 2D jitter (irregular SHAPES). 0 = a clean uniform grid; 1 = natural cracked glass; >1 = increasingly chaotic (jitter is capped at ~0.45 of a cell so pieces never cross). Read once at shatter build.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(greets_shard_fall_speed, "FDS_GREETS_SHARD_FALL_SPEED",  1.5f,                           "greets",   "Greets shatter: gravity multiplier on the falling shards. 1.0 = original pace; >1 falls faster (default 1.5 = a bit snappier). Applied per-frame to the downward integration.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (greets_shard_lay_flat,   "FDS_GREETS_SHARD_LAY_FLAT",    1,                              "greets",   "Greets shatter: when a shard comes to rest, rotate it to lie FLAT on the floor plane (its face normal -> +Y, with a random yaw) instead of freezing at its tumbled orientation. Correctly aligns the panel normal regardless of the screen's facing (the old fixed-pitch settle assumed a -Z panel and left greets's -X panel standing on edge). 0 = freeze tumbled pose (legacy).")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (greets_shard_res,        "FDS_GREETS_SHARD_RES",         64,                             "greets",   "Greets shatter: per-shard reflection texel resolution (each shard owns one res² cell of a reflection atlas). Rounded up to pow2. Independent of --mirror-rtt-density (that only drives the room mirror RTT panels). Shards are split across as many ≤1024² atlas textures as needed (the block-tiled sampler can't address beyond 1024/axis), so res can exceed the old single-atlas ceiling of 64: 128 = 4x the texels (sharper falling shards) at ~4x the bake + memory. Read once at scene load (prepareReflectionAtlas).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(greets_shard_refl_gain,  "FDS_GREETS_SHARD_REFL_GAIN",   1.0f,                           "greets",   "Greets shatter: brightness of the reflection baked into each mirror shard, applied in the silver glaze (out = silver*tint + reflection*gain) BEFORE the text composite, so the text always rides on top at full strength. This is the break's CONTINUITY dial: at the break frame the shards are still coplanar and cover the panel exactly, so they must show what the intact half-silvered screen was showing one frame earlier. MEASURED at the shatter screen (t=3122, square-on): main deferred pass from the shard's own reflected eye = 73.75 luma over the panel window, intact panel = 78.81, i.e. the unbroken mirror shows essentially the WHOLE reflection. The old hardcoded halving showed 34.94 — a -56% brightness step in one frame, ~40x the scene's own frame-to-frame motion, and the thing that made the break 'pop'. 0.5 = the legacy halved look. Independent of --greets-mirror-tint (that only sets the ADDED silver cast, and it never gated the halving — which is why toggling it measured null).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(disco_bloom,             "FDS_DISCO_BLOOM",              1.5f,                           "greets",   "Disco-ball bloom strength: screen-space threshold+blur+add over the ball's projected disc. 0 disables.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(mirror_bounce,            "FDS_MIRROR_BOUNCE",            1,                              "greets",   "Mirrors throw beams back into the room: virtual bounce spots at mirrored light positions (volumetric shaft + bounced dot pools). Off = no bounce pool activity.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(mirror_bounce_gain,      "FDS_MIRROR_BOUNCE_GAIN",       0.55f,                          "greets",   "Bounce reflectance: bounce spot intensity = source x gain.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(mirror_bounce_range,     "FDS_MIRROR_BOUNCE_RANGE",      1.6f,                           "greets",   "Bounce range multiplier over the source's range. 1.0 = physical (the surviving stub past the glass is a few units and barely reads); 1.6 gives the reflected shaft a visible body.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(mirror_prof,              "FDS_MIRROR_PROF",              0,                              "greets",   "Per-frame mirror-pass timing: accumulate UpdateAllMirrors / StampMirrorMasks / RenderSecondOrderMirrors wall time and print averages every 120 frames (FDS_MIRROR_PROF_EVERY overrides the interval). Also reports avg active mirror count and RTT jobs/frame.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(fountain_particle_omni_pct, "FDS_FOUNTAIN_PARTICLE_OMNI_PCT", 0.0f,                      "fountain", "Fountain: percentage of particles spawning omnis")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(chase_legacy_omni_hack,   "FDS_CHASE_LEGACY_OMNI_HACK",   0,                              "chase",    "Chase: apply the 1998 load-time omni fixup SceneCorrections() (Size spline x0.1, Range spline x100000, FallOff=5000). RETIRED — the effective values are now AUTHORED in Authoring/chase/CHASE.LWS (LgtIntensity x0.1, LightRange x100000 baked into the 7 lights), so this defaults OFF and the shipped scene needs no hack. The scaling is idempotent-unsafe: turning this ON against the shipped (already-baked) FLD DOUBLE-applies it (wrong). Only meaningful against an un-baked legacy LWS/FLD, kept as an A/B / re-derivation escape hatch.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(sky_gradient,             "FDS_SKY_GRADIENT",             0,                              "chase",    "Paint the authored backdrop GRADIENT into void/sky pixels (where no geometry drew) before the fog composite, instead of leaving them black. The four vertical stops come from the scene's authored LWS ZenithColor/SkyColor/GroundColor/NadirColor (FLD Scene_SkyColors payload → Scene::Sky*, HasSkyGradient) — a scene without them is untouched. Generic (chase today; city/crash also render void sky). Zenith at the top of the frame blends down through sky/ground to nadir at the bottom, keyed on screen Y. Default OFF — flag-off is byte-identical (the paint pass is skipped entirely). Deferred path.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(chase_engine_mod,         "FDS_CHASE_ENGINE_MOD",         0,                              "chase",    "Chase: modulate the 4+2 engine-glow omnis' ISize (which drives BOTH the light contribution AND the flare sprite = ISize x FlareScale) by the ships' maneuver intensity, so the engines PUNCH during the evasive clusters and the finale climb (afterburner feel). The signal is a DETERMINISTIC function of CurFrame — the ship's rotation-spline angular rate (heading/pitch/bank deg per frame, central difference), which spikes exactly at the authored jinks (incl. the M1.1 banking) and the finale pitch-up. Normalised per ship by the P90 rate over the scene, so glowMul = 1 + chase_engine_mod_gain*clamp(rate/ref,0,1). NO accumulation (pure f(CurFrame)) → snapshot-safe. Each glow is attributed to its ship by local mount-z (-88 Ship1 / -64 ship2). Default OFF → ISize untouched → chase pins byte-identical.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(chase_engine_mod_gain,   "FDS_CHASE_ENGINE_MOD_GAIN",    1.0f,                           "chase",    "chase_engine_mod punch strength: glowMul = 1 + this * clamp(angularRate/P90ref, 0, 1). 1.0 = up to 2x ISize (flare + light) at the sharpest maneuver; 0 = no punch (glows stay at authored size even with chase_engine_mod on). Only consulted when chase_engine_mod is on.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(chase_blasters,           "FDS_CHASE_BLASTERS",           0,                              "chase",    "Stage B1 (docs/CHASE_UPGRADE_PLAN.md §B1): ship2 fires blaster bolts at Ship1. Wires the BlasterBolts module into the chase driver, driven by a DETERMINISTIC fire table (DEMO/ChaseEvents) keyed on CurFrame — bursts aligned to Ship1's authored evasive clusters (t≈1408/1495/1623). Bolt/flash state is RECONSTRUCTED pure-t every frame (§8.B), never accumulated, so the jump-to-t chase snapshot reproduces it byte-for-byte. Bolts are occluded by terrain/ships (the additive filler's ZPage16 Z-test, §8.C), light the scene + froxel fog (transient omnis), flash at muzzle/impact (fountain bolt_flash envelope, hdr_glow_scale-parity), and reflect on the water (§8.E, streaks only). Independent of greets_blasters. Default OFF → all chase pins byte-identical (no texture, no omnis, no draw).")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(blaster_flash_peak,      "FDS_BLASTER_FLASH_PEAK",       950.0f,                         "blaster",  "Muzzle/impact flash light ISize at full envelope (chase B1). Pooled non-stationary Omni_FogTransient at each muzzle + impact point — lights nearby geometry + froxel fog, then decays (--blaster_flash_decay). Chase-scale (ships live in view-Z hundreds+, far larger than the greets/fountain scale). Auto-scaled by hdr_glow_scale() under --hdr (glowMax cap parity with the fountain strike-flash). 0 = no flashes.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(blaster_flash_range,     "FDS_BLASTER_FLASH_RANGE",      420.0f,                         "blaster",  "Muzzle/impact flash light reach in world units (chase B1). Smaller = a tight halo at the strike; larger spreads it across more fog. Chase-scale (~hundreds of units); tune with --blaster_flash_peak.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(blaster_flash_decay,     "FDS_BLASTER_FLASH_DECAY",      0.12f,                          "blaster",  "Muzzle/impact flash decay rate: intensity = exp(-decay·ageFrames) over a fixed ~26-frame window (chase B1). Larger = snappier/shorter flash; smaller = lingers. Pure function of CurFrame (no accumulation) so snapshots reproduce it. See --blaster_flash_peak.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(chase_spark_size,        "FDS_CHASE_SPARK_SIZE",         0.00011f,                       "blaster",  "Stage B2 (chase): impact-spark sprite size. TBR_Sprite screen size = ImageSize(1000)·RZ·PerspX·FlareSize, so FlareSize ≈ world-disc-radius/1000; this is multiplied by the target ship's world bounding radius, so a spark disc ≈ chase_spark_size·shipRadius·1000 world units. ~0.00005 reads as small hot sparks; raise for fatter sparks/splash. Only active with --chase_blasters.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(chase_spark_bright,      "FDS_CHASE_SPARK_BRIGHT",       255.0f,                         "blaster",  "Stage B2 (chase): impact-spark peak additive brightness at spawn (per-channel, faded linearly to 0 over the particle life). Each spark's colour = (0.5 + 0.5·boltColour)·this·fade — a hot-white core tinted by the bolt. Too high blows the additive buffer to white; ~200-255 is a bright spark. Only active with --chase_blasters.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(chase_cam_fx,             "FDS_CHASE_CAM_FX",             0,                              "chase",    "Stage C1 (chase): camera SHAKE + FOV punch on bolt impacts. A post-Animate_Objects pose modifier — reconstructed pure-t from the impact table (amplitude decays ~12 frames after each hit, scaled by camera proximity), perturbing View->ISource (positional jitter, deterministic multi-sine on CurFrame), rolling View->Mat, and kicking IFOV inward (--chase_cam_fov_kick). NO accumulation → snapshot-safe; flag-off OR no active hit leaves the authored dolly untouched (pin-identical). Needs --chase_blasters (drives off its impact events). Default OFF.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(chase_cam_shake_gain,    "FDS_CHASE_CAM_SHAKE_GAIN",     0.04f,                          "chase",    "chase_cam_fx shake displacement gain: camera jitter magnitude = amp·this·shipRadius world units (amp ≈ summed hit proximity, ~0..2). 0 = no positional shake (roll + FOV punch still apply). Raise for a heavier action-cam.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(chase_cam_fov_kick,      "FDS_CHASE_CAM_FOV_KICK",       5.0f,                           "chase",    "chase_cam_fx FOV punch: degrees the field of view kicks INWARD (zoom) at hit peak (amp≥1), recovering as the hit fades. 0 = no FOV punch (shake still applies). ~3-8 reads as a hit jolt; too high whips distractingly.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(chase_cam_dump,           "FDS_CHASE_CAM_DUMP",           0,                              "chase",    "Chase debug: print one [CHASECAM] line per rendered frame to stderr — scene tick t, CurFrame, camera ISource/ITarget/IRoll/IFOV captured AFTER the full per-frame camera chain (Dynamic_Camera + chase_cam_fx), plus both ships' spline centres. Works identically in --scene-chase, the full demo, and --snapshot=chase (each snapshot t prints one line, so a t-sweep gives a framing trace). grep '\\[CHASECAM\\]' the stderr log to debug camera/framing/clipping questions. Pure observer: no state touched, default OFF, pins unaffected.")

// ─── SSAO (screen-space ambient occlusion, deferred path only) ────────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (ssao,                    "FDS_SSAO",                     0,                              "ssao",     "View-space SSAO over the deferred G-buffer (Render_SSAO, runs after the lighting kernel and before fog). Darkens contact creases by hemisphere depth sampling. Deferred path only; no-op in forward mode. Output target is automatic: under --hdr/--cinematic it multiplies the LINEAR radiance in g_hdrBuf before the tonemap (physically correct, glow added later is NOT occluded); without HDR it multiplies the 8-bit VPage colour. See --ssao_downscale for the cost/quality lever and --ssao_samples for sample count.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (ssao_downscale,          "FDS_SSAO_DOWNSCALE",           2,                              "ssao",     "SSAO compute resolution divisor: 1 = full-res (sharpest, most expensive); 2 = half-res per axis (~1/4 the AO work, depth-aware bilinear upsample) — DEFAULT since 2026-08-16; 3/4 = coarser/cheaper. The depth buffer is always sampled at full res, so geometry edges stay crisp; only the AO field is reduced + upsampled. Biggest cost lever after --ssao_samples. DEFAULT 1 -> 2 COUNTERSIGNED BY GIL-AD 2026-08-16, verbatim: \"ssao downscale 2 is ok (no downscale looks much better, but too slow)\" — i.e. full-res is preferred aesthetically and rejected on cost. The price of =1 on his greets acceptance arm is +9 ms of frame (docs/PERF_STATE.md 00a); the look cost of =2 against =1 is 44-63 % of pixels moved at mean |delta| 0.53-0.87/255, peaks local to contact creases (crops: docs/img/ssaoperf/dial_t*_crop_d1_d2_d4.png). Pass --ssao_downscale=1 to restore full-res exactly.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (ssao_samples,            "FDS_SSAO_SAMPLES",             16,                             "ssao",     "SSAO hemisphere samples per pixel (1..64). 16 = default (clean on flat grazing surfaces like the greets floor; the depth-only denoise resolves the interleaved-gradient dither). 8 ≈ half the compute but leaves a faint directional 'hatch' on large flat areas the denoise doesn't fully clear; 32 = marginally smoother. The compute pass scales linearly with this.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(ssao_radius,             "FDS_SSAO_RADIUS",              4.0f,                           "ssao",     "SSAO sampling radius in VIEW-SPACE (world) units. SCENE-SCALE DEPENDENT and small: these scenes live in view-Z ~[5..80], so ~3-5 gives crisp contact AO; ~6-12 gives a broad soft drop-shadow halo; >20 overshoots all geometry into the background (less occlusion, not more). Set FDS_SSAO_STATS=1 to print the live view-Z range for a scene.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(ssao_strength,           "FDS_SSAO_STRENGTH",            1.5f,                           "ssao",     "SSAO darkening gain. 1.0 = subtle, 2-3 = dramatic. AO = 1 - occlusion*strength.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(ssao_bias,               "FDS_SSAO_BIAS",                0.1f,                           "ssao",     "SSAO depth bias (view units) subtracted before the occlusion compare to kill self-occlusion acne on flat lit surfaces. Scaled to the small view-Z range; raise toward ~0.3 if you see banding/shimmer on walls.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(ssao_power,              "FDS_SSAO_POWER",               1.0f,                           "ssao",     "Contrast exponent on the AO term (ao = ao^power). >1 deepens creases and lifts open areas toward white; 1.0 = linear.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (ssao_blur,               "FDS_SSAO_BLUR",                2,                              "ssao",     "Bilateral denoise radius applied to the AO field before compositing (0..8, in compute-grid pixels). Weighted by depth AND normal similarity (cos^4) so it kills the per-sample rotation grain WITHOUT smearing across creases — normal-awareness is what keeps panel-gap detail under --ssao_downscale. 0 = raw (dithered); 2 = default. Runs in the (reduced) compute resolution, so it's cheap even at downscale 1.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (ssao_debug,              "FDS_SSAO_DEBUG",               0,                              "ssao",     "Visualize the AO term as greyscale (white = unoccluded, black = fully occluded) instead of modulating the lit colour. Writes straight to VPage, so pair it with --no-hdr (under HDR the tonemap overwrites VPage and the viz is lost). Use to judge the AO itself in isolation.")

// ─── SSAO: GTAO + Visibility Bitmask compute path (drop-in for the hemisphere) ─
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (ssao_gtao,               "FDS_SSAO_GTAO",                0,                              "ssao",     "Use the GTAO + Visibility-Bitmask AO compute (Therrien/Levesque 2023) instead of the hemisphere point-sampler. Horizon-based: per slice it marches a screen-space direction and accumulates a 32-sector visibility bitmask, integrating the TRUE visible horizon. Fixes the hemisphere sampler's 'halo' (fat over-occlusion band + bright silhouette rim) because background is occluded only by the occluder's real angular extent, and the thickness param lets light pass behind thin objects. Drop-in: shares --ssao_radius/strength/power/downscale/blur and the denoise+apply. Default off.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (ssao_gtao_slices,        "FDS_SSAO_GTAO_SLICES",         2,                              "ssao",     "GTAO: number of hemisphere SLICES (screen-space directions) per pixel (1..8). Each slice is one horizon integration; more = smoother azimuthal coverage, linear cost. 2-3 + the 4×4-tiling rotation + matched denoise is the usual sweet spot.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (ssao_gtao_steps,         "FDS_SSAO_GTAO_STEPS",          4,                              "ssao",     "GTAO: ray-march STEPS per side per slice (1..16). Samples taken outward along the slice direction within --ssao_radius (both directions → 2× this per slice). More = finer horizon, linear cost. The biggest GTAO cost lever.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(ssao_gtao_thickness,     "FDS_SSAO_GTAO_THICKNESS",      1.0f,                           "ssao",     "GTAO bitmask THICKNESS in view-space units: each occluder blocks the sector range between its front face and a back face `thickness` deeper, so beyond that light passes behind it (the visibility-bitmask fix for thin-occluder over-occlusion). Larger = thicker/solid occluders (more occlusion); ~0.5-2 at this scene scale. Scene-scale dependent like --ssao_radius.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (ssao_temporal,           "FDS_SSAO_TEMPORAL",            0,                              "ssao",     "Temporal AO accumulation: reproject last frame's (blended) low-res AO through the camera delta, depth-reject, and blend it with the current frame (--ssao_temporal_blend). To fund the win it HALVES the per-frame sampling (GTAO steps / hemisphere samples) and cycles the 4x4 rotation tiling per frame, so the accumulated result converges back to (or past) full quality over ~1/(1-blend) frames. Risks: AO cast by MOVING objects onto static surfaces lags by the blend window (the receiving pixel's depth doesn't change, so the reject can't fire); disocclusions fall back to the current frame's noisier field for a few frames.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(ssao_temporal_blend,     "FDS_SSAO_TEMPORAL_BLEND",      0.85f,                          "ssao",     "History weight for --ssao_temporal (0..0.98). 0.85 ~ a 6-7 frame accumulation window: smoother AO + shorter ghost trail; higher = smoother but laggier on moving-object AO.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(ssao_radius_zfloor,      "FDS_SSAO_RADIUS_ZFLOOR",       48.0f,                          "ssao",     "PER-SCENE SSAO/GTAO RADIUS PIN -- DEFAULT 48 SINCE 2026-08-25, ON GIL-AD'S ORDER (\"the moire was fixed - we need to pin the per-scene radius\"), after he approved the chase zfloor=48 arm by eye. Raises the EFFECTIVE --ssao_radius to a floor of (this value) x the ZPage16 depth QUANTUM, 1/g_zscale = FZP*1.1/0xff00. **--ssao_radius_zfloor=0 is the OFF switch and the exact pre-2026-08-25 arm** -- every gate value moved by this flip reproduces its old hash under it, which is what proves nothing but the default moved. MECHANISM, measured at chase t=1105: the AO radius is authored in view units but the depth GTAO integrates is a 16-bit LINEAR encoding of [0, FZP*1.1], so what actually decides whether the horizon march sees a SURFACE or a STAIRCASE is the ratio radius/quantum -- and that ratio is set by the SCENE's far plane, not by the flag. At the shipped --ssao_radius=4.0 chase's entire sampling sphere spans under five discrete depth levels; the decoded depth comes back as ~4-px-wide treads (measured: 197 distinct z values in a 256x256 patch, step 0.84253 exactly) and the horizon angle can only take a handful of values, which beats against the fixed march offsets. That is the moire -- the isoline contours and the herringbone weave are the same staircase seen along and across the slope -- and it is why greets is clean and chase is not: a 333x far-plane scale gap, not a defect in the AO math. THE PER-SCENE PIN THIS DEFAULT ESTABLISHES (FZP, quantum, quanta spanned at radius 4.0, then the EFFECTIVE radius at k=48; float32, as the code computes it): greets FZP 150, q 0.00252757, 1582.6 quanta -> 4.000000 (FLOOR INERT, byte-null); crash 2000, q 0.03370098, 118.7 -> 4.000000 (FLOOR INERT, byte-null); fountain 5000, q 0.08425245, 47.5 -> 4.044117 (+1.10 %); city 7500, q 0.12637867, 31.6 -> 6.066176 (+51.7 %); chase 50000, q 0.84252453, 4.75 -> 40.441177 (x10.11, the defect and the approved fix). So the flip is BYTE-NULL in greets and crash BY CONSTRUCTION (k*q never reaches 4.0 there), a hair in fountain, a real broadening in city, and the whole fix in chase. It is also byte-null in every arm that does not pass --ssao, which is every pinned gate row except the greets acceptance x4 -- and that row is one of the two the construction exempts. PROVEN BY CONSTRUCTION, not inferred: holding --ssao_radius=4.0 and sharpening ONLY the depth quantum via --ssao_zprec_probe collapses the moire monotonically (probe 1/4/8/16 -> R1 moire rms 0.02898 / 0.00660 / 0.00371 / 0.00254, an 11.4x drop with the radius untouched). WHAT IT IS NOT: not the 8-wide path's un-refined _mm256_rsqrt_ps -- forcing the NR-refined scalar path with FDS_SSAO_NOSIMD=1 reproduces the moire to four significant figures (0.02899 vs 0.02898), which EXONERATES the standing backlog suspect; and not --ssao_downscale, whose full-res arm keeps 95 % of it. COST adds ZERO samples -- the march step count is unchanged, only its world-space extent -- but a larger radius does mean the dl2>r2max reject discards fewer marched samples, so more of them reach the two gtaoAcos calls, and the cost is therefore not zero A PRIORI. RE-MEASURED at the default flip, chase t=1105, 1920x1080, --ssao_downscale=2, 14 runs per arm INTERLEAVED, [ssao] pass wall: k=0 min 4.15 / median 4.25 / mean 4.264 ms; k=48 min 4.07 / median 4.26 / mean 4.238 ms -- delta min -0.08, median +0.01, mean -0.03. THE TWO ARMS ARE INDISTINGUISHABLE at this sample size and the sign of the difference flips between statistics, so the honest quote is NO RESOLVABLE COST, |delta| <= 0.08 ms on a ~4.25 ms pass -- NOT a claim of zero, a claim that 14x14 interleaved cannot see it. The hunt's original +0.17 ms min / +0.05 ms median (6 runs per arm, un-interleaved) DOES NOT REPRODUCE and should be treated as run-to-run noise, not as the price. MEASUREMENT TRAP: do NOT take [ssao] timings from a run carrying --ssao_dump. The dump forces the scalar apply loop and inflates the pass to 14-20 ms, ~4x the real cost; the numbers above are from non-dumping runs.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(ssao_zprec_probe,        "FDS_SSAO_ZPREC_PROBE",         1.0f,                           "ssao",     "DIAGNOSTIC, default 1.0 = byte-null. Multiplies g_zscale (RenderPipeline::setCurrentScene, g_zscale = 0xff00/(FZP*1.1)) so the 16-bit ZPage16 depth quantum shrinks by this factor WITHOUT touching --ssao_radius. It exists to separate the two candidate mechanisms for the chase moire: 'the AO radius is too small for the scene' versus 'the depth the AO reads is too coarsely quantized for that radius'. Chase ships FZP=50000, so the z quantum is 0.8425 view units and the default --ssao_radius=4.0 spans just 4.75 quanta; greets ships FZP=150, quantum 0.00253, and the SAME radius spans 1583 quanta -- which is why greets is clean and chase moires. Setting this to 4 or 8 holds the radius fixed and makes the depth field smooth. WARNING, this is a PROBE not a shippable lever: Z is written as 0xFF80 - Fist(g_zscale*z), so anything past z = 0xFF80/(g_zscale*probe) wraps and the FAR half of the frame corrupts. Only read patches whose view-Z is inside the surviving range.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (ssao_dump,               "FDS_SSAO_DUMP",                0,                              "ssao",     "DIAGNOSTIC, default OFF. Write the FULL-RES applied AO MULTIPLIER (the exact scalar Render_SSAO's pass-3 apply multiplies g_hdrBuf/VPage by, after the denoise and the depth-aware upsample) to a float32 file: the 4 bytes 'AOF2', int32 width, int32 height, then width*height float32 AO in row-major top-left-origin order, then width*height float32 VIEW-Z with < 0 meaning sky. AO is 1.0 on sky (the pixels the apply skips); the Z plane is there because the two arms' rasterisers do not agree pixel-for-pixel on coverage, and without it a comparator cannot tell 'AO 1.0 because open' from 'AO 1.0 because sky'. Destination is FDS_SSAO_DUMP_PATH, default /tmp/fds_ssao_ao.f32 -- a PATH, which the flag table has no type for, and the only reason it is an env var. WHY IT EXISTS: the GPU arm (GpuBench --ssao_dump=PATH) writes the identical file, so the two renderers' AO fields can be differenced pixel by pixel instead of being inferred from a lit frame, where D1/D3/D6/D7/E6/E7 all move the same pixels. INSTRUMENT STATE, stated because the trap is real: turning this on FORCES the generic scalar apply loop instead of the aarch64 vld4/vst4 fast path (they are bit-identical by construction -- x*1.0f and the f16 round-trip are the same round-to-nearest -- but the fast path has nowhere to write the plane), and it allocates one XRes*YRes float plane. It does NOT touch the compute or the denoise, so the AO field itself is what the un-instrumented frame would have applied. No-op unless --ssao.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (ssao_hdr_transport,      "FDS_SSAO_HDR_TRANSPORT",       1,                              "ssao",     "DEFAULT ON -- THE FIX for docs/OPTIMIZATION_BACKLOG.md 2026-08-25b (\"SSAO IS SILENTLY DISCARDED IN CITY AND FOUNTAIN UNDER --hdr\"). Render_SSAO's pass-3 apply picks its write target from the frame's ACTUAL radiance transport instead of from Hdr_WritableFor(), which only answers 'is g_hdrBuf sized for this view'. THE DEFECT: on a PreferOuterVec scene (city CITY.CPP:2586, fountain FOUNTAIN.CPP:1029, crash CRASH.CPP:25) the OUTER-VEC lighting kernel stores 8-bit VPage ONLY and leaves the HDR coverage lane at 0 -- its pack IS the HDR transport, lifted afterwards by the froxel composite (`h[3] > 0 ? h : VPage`) or by Hdr_ActivateNoFog. Between the kernel and that lift g_hdrBuf is sized and CLEARED, so the old predicate sent the AO multiply into a buffer of zeros and the lift then seeded it from the UN-OCCLUDED VPage. MEASURED at city t=1961 and fountain t=2500 under `--deferred --hdr --hdr-linear --texture-filter=2 --ssao --ssao-gtao`: byte-identical to --no-ssao, to --ssao_strength=8 and to --ssao_radius=200, while [ssao] still reported ~5 ms; one instrumented render shows `cov=0 maxFinite=0` in g_hdrBuf right after the kernel in city/fountain against `cov=2072779 maxFinite=617` in greets. Same class as the wave-2 fill kernel's 'WAVE-1 TRANSPORT MUST MATCH' warning, one call site further downstream. WITH THE FIX the apply takes the VPage arm on those scenes -- the SAME 8-bit buffer their whole frame is shaded into -- and the lift carries the occlusion into the radiance. Byte-null on greets and chase (scalar kernel, PreferOuterVec 0, still the HDR arm) and byte-null under --no-hdr everywhere. --no-ssao_hdr_transport restores the old predicate EXACTLY and is the pre-fix A/B arm.")

// ─── demo / debug ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(skip_to_fountain,         "SKIP_TO_FOUNTAIN",             0,                              "demo",     "Skip Glato + City and jump straight to Fountain")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(scene_mirrortest,         "FDS_SCENE_MIRRORTEST",         0,                              "demo",     "Run the interactive mirror-test scene instead of the demo sequence. Floor + back wall + reflective panel + a test cube; uses the SceneBuilder API + the planar mirror system. TAB/WASD for free-cam, ESC/Backspace to exit. Run: ./DEMO --scene-mirrortest")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(scene_cloaktest,          "FDS_SCENE_CLOAKTEST",          0,                              "demo",     "Run the 'invisible mirror' (Sphinx cabinet) test scene: four 45-degree mirror panels in a W, hidden cubes in the wedges, checkered room. Renders 4 preset poses to /tmp/cloak_view_*.ppm and exits. The central 90-degree seam is the recursive-mirror (--mirror-rtt) A/B target. Run: ./DEMO --scene-cloaktest")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(scene_greets,             "FDS_SCENE_GREETS",             0,                              "demo",     "Skip Glato/City/Chase/Fountain/Crash and run only the greets scene. Useful for iterating on greets-specific code (mirror system, lightmap bake, text rasterizer) without sitting through the rest of the demo each time. Run: ./DEMO --scene-greets [--greets-mirror]")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(scene_conetest,           "FDS_SCENE_CONETEST",           0,                              "demo",     "Run the interactive volumetric-cone test scene (single spot over a ground plane) with free-cam, instead of the demo. The rig for tuning cones / fast_fog / in-scatter live. WASD/QE move, arrows look, ESC exit. Run: ./DEMO --scene-conetest --deferred --draw_cones [--fast_fog --fast_fog_blobs ...]")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(displace_dump,            "FDS_DISPLACE_DUMP",            0,                              "greets",   "DIAGNOSTIC, changes no pixel: with the --displace_viz recorder armed (any mode, or --viz_arm), write EVERY recorded bake vertex to Runtime/displace_dump.txt on the first overlay frame - world+local position, applied displacement vector + magnitude, deviation angle vs the reconstructed base wall plane (worst and best over the vert's incident faces - a large SPREAD means the vert's faces disagree about their base plane, the tear signature), incident-face count and material name. Ranks offline: sort by magnitude for oversized-needle hunts (the 2026-08-28 out-of-wall vectors), by devMax for direction hunts, by spread for sheet-divergence hunts. One shot per process. Run: ./DEMO --scene-greets --greets_displace --displace_viz=3 --displace_dump + snapshot.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(normchain_dump,           "FDS_NORMCHAIN_DUMP",           0,                              "greets",   "DIAGNOSTIC, changes no pixel: dump the NORMAL CHAIN at init. Stage A: normchain_ride_<mat>.txt, per-vert position + Vertex::N at the top of DisplaceStoneSubdiv — the bake ride input, i.e. PREPROC Compute_Vertex_Normals' ungated area-weighted average, which corner-averages across walls of ANY dihedral. Stage B (the applied ride vector) is --displace_dump. Stage C: normchain_shading.txt, per face corner position + corner N + face winding N after MakeFacesIndependentByAngle(30) — what shading interpolates. The 2026-08-28 root-cause instrument for the month's wall-seam bulge: diffing A/B/C against the wall planes names the stage that owns a bad normal. Files land in CWD (Runtime).")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(scene_displacetest,       "FDS_SCENE_DISPLACETEST",       0,                              "demo",     "Run the minimal stone-DISPLACEMENT test scene: ONE flat 8x8 quad with an 8-bit height map, running the EXACT production bake (DisplaceStoneSubdiv, docs/ENVDYN_DISPLACEMENT_PLAN.md B) greets uses on its wall — an isolated, analytically-checkable rig for 'is the subdivision honouring the height map?'. Height map via FDS_DISPLACETEST_MAP=0(synthetic 4x4 blocks, default)/1(ramp)/2(real greets_wall_h.png)/3(running bond); FDS_DISPLACETEST_SPAN=N tiles the map across N UV tiles per axis (N*4 blocks/axis — span 3 ~= the real multi-tile greets wall). Reuses --greets_displace_amp/_mip/_adapt/_cpb + --greets_stone_subdiv VERBATIM (amp defaults up to 0.8 so relief reads on the test wall). Default = interactive free-cam (WASD/QE move, arrows look, G dump pose, ESC exit; --displace_viz=1/2 overlays the bake live). FDS_DISPLACETEST_DUMP=1 = headless: prints the [DTEST] metric matrix over (map,span), then renders the selected combo from 4 poses (frontal/45/grazing/edge-on silhouette) x 3 styles (lit/viz1/viz2) to /tmp/displacetest_*.ppm. Run: ./DEMO --scene-displacetest  |  FDS_DISPLACETEST_DUMP=1 ./DEMO --scene-displacetest")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(scene_chase,              "FDS_SCENE_CHASE",              0,                              "demo",     "Skip Glato/City and run only the chase scene (then exit). For interactively judging chase authoring work (loop alignment, light drama) without sitting through the intro. Music (if enabled) starts at song start, so audio sync differs from the full run — this is a look-review mode, not a timing reference. Run: ./DEMO --scene-chase --deferred [--cinematic]")
FDS_FLAG_INT(chase_start,               "FDS_CHASE_START",              0,                              "demo",     "--scene-chase only: start the chase clock at this t instead of 0 (same units as --snapshot=chase@t=; loop at ~500-580, canyon/island stretch ~1200+). Debug/preview seek — a cold jump skips any state that would have accumulated before t (bolts, events), which is acceptable for look review and byte-irrelevant to the normal run.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(mirrortest_multi_dump,    "FDS_MIRRORTEST_MULTI_DUMP",    0,                              "demo",     "mirrortest one-shot multi-view sanity dump: render the interactive scene from 6 preset camera poses (front, side, high, low, close, far), write each as /tmp/mt_view_N.ppm, then exit. Used to verify the mirror reflection is sane from multiple angles without manual fly-through.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(chase_event_test,         "FDS_CHASE_EVENT_TEST",         0,                              "demo",     "S0 music-sync foundation (docs/CHASE_UPGRADE_PLAN.md §8.B). Default OFF -> chase pins byte-identical. When ON, the chase snapshot reconstructs which events are active at the current scene-tick as a PURE FUNCTION of t (no accumulation) via DEMO/ChaseEvents, and stamps a deterministic marker for each. Sources: a beatmap+event-table pair (FDS_CHASE_BEATMAP / FDS_CHASE_EVENTS files, musical order:row resolved to scene-ticks at load, offset by FDS_CHASE_MUSIC_START_TICK) if present, else a self-contained throwaway table active only at t=800. Proof: t=800 marker (3x byte-identical), t=799/801 no marker. This is the determinism contract B1/B2 blasters/particles reconstruct against.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(dump_mats,                "FDS_DUMP_MATS",                0,                              "debug",    "Dump material info on load")

// ─── runtime debug-viz cycle (X / Shift+X) + the whole-scene wireframe ────────
FDS_FLAG_INT  (wire_viz,                "FDS_WIRE_VIZ",                 0,                              "viz",      "WHOLE-SCENE TRIANGLE WIREFRAME (default 0 = OFF, byte-null). A post-tonemap overlay that strokes every visible mesh triangle's three edges, DEPTH-TESTED against the frame's opaque Z (ZPage16, the line z pulled 1% nearer so an edge lying on its own surface wins instead of z-fighting) — so wireframe on a far wall does NOT draw over a near statue. Unlike --displace_viz=1 (scoped to the displacement bake's materials) this covers the whole scene, so it answers 'what topology am I actually looking at': with --greets_displace ON the tessellator's added faces appear as real edges, with it off the authored quads do. MODES: 1 = white edges over the normal image; 2 = white edges over the scene DIMMED to --wire_viz_dim (topology-only read, nothing competes with the lines); 3 = edges coloured by MATERIAL (12-hue palette) with a per-MESH brightness step, over the dimmed scene — says which surface and which chunk owns each face; 4 = FACING: back-facing triangles RED then front-facing GREEN over the dimmed scene (no back-face cull, front drawn last so shared edges read green) — a red patch on a closed surface is an inverted normal / open shell. Main view only, never in an offscreen pass. Cost is per-triangle-drawn, not per-pixel: measure with --profiler. Run: ./DEMO --scene-greets --deferred --wire_viz=2, or press X in any run to cycle it live.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(wire_viz_dim,            "FDS_WIRE_VIZ_DIM",             0.25f,                          "viz",      "--wire_viz modes 2/3/4: how much of the rendered image survives under the wireframe (0 = black background, 1 = untouched). 0.25 keeps enough shading to place the lines in the scene without competing with them.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (mat_probe,              "FDS_MAT_PROBE",                0,                              "viz",      "CENTRE-PIXEL MATERIAL PROBE (greets, default OFF). Prints \"[MAT@(x,y)] id=N <name>\" bottom-left for whatever the G-buffer says owns the exact centre pixel, updated every frame, so flying the crosshair over a surface names it. It is how a material gets identified from a screenshot without guessing, and it is why the greets tick used to carry it UNCONDITIONALLY -- which also put it in every capture the campaign has taken, bottom-left, on top of whatever was there. Now opt-in: pass --mat_probe when you are hunting a material and leave it off for captures you intend to keep or compare. FDS_GREETS_FACEFIND=\"x,y\" (the point-in-triangle face census on stderr) implies it, since that is the same investigation. Forward-rasterised pixels report \"forward-rendered (sentinel)\" -- TheOtherBarry stamps 0xFFFFFFFF to tell the deferred pass it already shaded them, which decodes to matID 255 and is not a real material. Costs one G-buffer read and one snprintf per frame when on; nothing at all when off.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (viz_legend,             "FDS_VIZ_LEGEND",               1,                              "viz",      "ON-SCREEN LEGEND for the active debug viz (default ON). Draws a few short lines bottom-left, above the mode name, saying what the colours MEAN -- with the colour CHIPS themselves, not a prose description of them. It exists because naming a mode is not a reading: \"POM march paths\" does not tell you that magenta is the cone march running out of steps with the ray still inside the slab and therefore applying NO shift, and \"WIRE facing\" does not tell you that a surviving red patch means back-facing on BOTH sides, i.e. an inverted normal. Every row is DERIVED from the code that colours the pixels -- --pom_path_viz chips come from meka::pom_path_color(), --wire_viz / --displace_viz / --pom_seam_viz rows are built beside their overlays in WorldAabb.cpp and quote the live --wire_viz_dim, the fill-match threshold and the bake's MEASURED max push / worst error -- so a legend cannot drift from the picture next to it. Applies to CLI-set vizzes as well as X-key cycled ones (that is the case that needs the key most); no gate recipe passes a viz flag, so no pin is affected. The block is clamped to the lower half of the screen so it can never collide with the resolution readout or the profiler column, and a legend too tall to fit drops its tail and says how many rows it dropped. The FULL legend is also printed once per selection as [VIZ-LEGEND] lines on stderr, with hex colours. --pom_path_viz=2 deliberately gets NO legend: it records the path plane while leaving the image untouched so a sweep can be diffed for path flips, and painting text into those frames would corrupt the instrument. Byte-null with every viz off.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (viz_arm,                 "FDS_VIZ_ARM",                  0,                              "viz",      "ARM every runtime-switchable debug viz (default OFF). Several vizzes need storage or a recorder that is committed BEFORE the frame the flag is read on, so flipping the flag live is a silent no-op: the filtered-albedo G-buffer plane (--pom_viz, --pom_mip_viz, --poly_viz, --pom_path_viz write into it; normally allocated only for --texture_filter>0) and the displacement bake's per-vertex/per-triangle records (--displace_viz 1/2, normally recorded only when the flag was already set at bake time). --viz_arm allocates/records all of them unconditionally so the X-key viz cycle can switch into those modes mid-flight. Costs one extra full-res dword plane (~3.5 MB at 1280x720) plus the displacement record maps; changes no rendered pixel by itself. Pass it once when you intend to review with the viz cycle.")

// ─── XFRM: parallel main-view Transform_Objects (docs/SOA_VERTEX_REFACTOR.md) ──
FDS_FLAG_INT  (xfrm_par,                "FDS_XFRM_PAR",                 -1,                             "render",   "PARALLELISE THE MAIN-VIEW Transform_Objects across the ThreadPool. DEFAULT ON (-1 = 2 blocks per worker). 0 = OFF, the old serial tick-thread path. N > 0 = exactly N blocks. The object list is cut into CONTIGUOUS mesh-index BLOCKS balanced on a cost estimate (VIndex + 2*FIndex, from the measured 4.44 ns/vert vs 9.09 ns/face), and the blocks are WORK-STOLEN off a shared cursor by the pool plus the tick thread. Block count is deliberately decoupled from worker count: the cost model cannot see which meshes the frustum + occlusion culls will discard, and most of them are discarded (greets: 471 TriMesh objects on the object list, 108 reach a vertex loop), so one-block-per-worker with a static assignment leaves ~15 % on the table. DETERMINISM BY CONSTRUCTION, and it is the whole design: each block appends into a segment of the shared FList RESERVED IN MESH ORDER (the prefix sum of per-mesh FIndex, an exact upper bound on what a block can push), so EXECUTION order is free while OUTPUT order is pinned; a compaction pass in block order then closes the gaps and the result is bit-identical to serial insertion whatever order the workers finished in. No atomic bump allocator on a shared cursor - that would make FList order a function of thread scheduling, i.e. a different image run to run. Verify the property cheaply: the block COUNT does not move the image (par=0/5/8/12/24/52/104 all hash the same). MEASURED greets 1920x1080, per-frame min over 24, min-of-arm over interleaved reps, box shared (1-min load 12-25): displaced t=5780 --greets_displace 1.718 -> 0.470 ms (-1.25, 3.7x); shipping t=5743 0.442 -> 0.236 ms (-0.21, 1.9x). Driver overhead measured with par=1 (one block, inline, no dispatch): its WORK equals the serial TOTAL to within noise, so PLAN + COMPACT + EPILOGUE cost ~10-35 us. The two arms are limited by DIFFERENT things and both are at their measured mechanism: shipping is at the per-mesh LPT bound (one mirror clone is 55 % of its main-view verts AND 55 % of its faces -> predicted 0.230, measured 0.252 WORK), displaced is at an AGGREGATE-BANDWIDTH bound (53.7 MB of streamed vertex traffic in 0.42 ms WORK = ~125 GB/s, about 2x the single-core 64 GB/s ceiling this loop sits at serially - not 12x). Excluded passes: shadow bakes, mirror RTT, off-axis shard passes, env/SH probes and any resolution-overridden or VertexScratch (clone) render - those already run on pool workers and would nest dispatches. Interacts with --bake_tick_overlap / --shadow_gbuffer_overlap: with either of those on, the pool is busy when this dispatches and the blocks queue behind bake tasks (still correct, possibly slower). Both default OFF.")

// ─── mirror clone: bounding-sphere tightening (docs/VISIBILITY_PLAN.md) ──
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (mirror_clone_tight_bsphere, "FDS_MIRROR_CLONE_TIGHT_BSPHERE", 0,                       "greets",   "TIGHTEN A MIRROR CLONE'S BOUNDING SPHERE to the vertices the clone actually carries. A clone is built as ONE TriMesh and its sphere is stamped from the bbox of every source vertex it walked, INCLUDING the ones no clone face references (faces are dropped four ways: the mirror surface itself, a null corner, --greets_displace_flat_mirror's Face_MainOnly skip, and the 'in FRONT of the plane' side test). The clone now carries only referenced vertices, so that bbox is stale-by-construction: mirror 'P_TEXT.JPG#11' keeps 1926 of 27416 vertices in one corner of the greets room, yet its sphere spans the whole mirrored room and NO camera pose can reject it -- which is the mechanism behind 'a clone is culled by the frustum and never by the mirror window'. With this ON the sphere is the bbox of the KEPT vertices, which is the CORRECT sphere (a clone cannot draw a vertex it does not carry), so a clone the mesh cull now rejects had no vertex in the frustum. Default 0 = the old sphere, byte-identical: turning it on changes cull outcomes, and with them the Tri_Inside/Tri_Ahead classification that selects the clipped-vs-unclipped vertex path, so it is measured as a perf/divergence PAIR rather than assumed null.")

// ─── R2 mitre root-cause: geometric slit census (docs/S1D_CLOSED_SHELL_PLAN.md) ──
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (pom_shell_slit_census,  "FDS_POM_SHELL_SLIT_CENSUS",    0,                              "parallax", "R2 GEOMETRIC SLIT CENSUS for the lid weld (docs/S1D_CLOSED_SHELL_PLAN.md S1d-2f), DIAGNOSTIC, default OFF, init-time print only, byte-null. Answers why --pom_shell_weld=4's geometrically CORRECT mitre measures WORSE than mode 3's mean-normal approximation, WITHOUT rendering anything: it snapshots every vertex position before the first PomShell_Build and, after the last one, attributes the delta actually applied at each POSITION and scores it against the only two surfaces the lid can tear from. (A) shelled-vs-shelled TEAR = max pairwise |d_a - d_b| among the copies at one position; the cross-material memo drives this to exactly 0 at --pom_shell_weld>=3 and it is nonzero at mode 1, where each material welds its own mean. (B) LIFT OFF AN UNSHELLED NEIGHBOUR = |d . N| for every unshelled face incident on a moved position, edge-length weighted into a world^2 SLIT AREA. (B) is the term the mitre makes worse: a corner on a fold of half-angle T moves off*sin(T) laterally under the mean normal and off*tan(T) under the mitre, so dividing by cos(T) fixes the offset of every SHELLED incident plane -- which is what the mitre guarantees -- while multiplying the gap against every UNSHELLED one by 1/cos(T). Prints [POM-SLIT]. Lives wholly outside PomShell_Build so that function's -ffp-contract=fast vertex move is textually untouched.")

// ─── S1d-3 PRISM: closed-shell SIDE GEOMETRY (docs/S1D_CLOSED_SHELL_PLAN.md) ──
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (mip_fix,                "FDS_MIP_FIX",                  1,                              "render", "MIP-SELECTION CONSISTENCY FIX for MiplevelClipper (FDS/FRUSTRUM/FRUSTRUM.CPP), default 0 = OFF, byte-null. The clipper picks a mip two different ways and THE TWO DISAGREE. Small faces (screen area < XRes*YRes*0.02) take a whole-face average: mip = 0.5*log2(texArea*W*H/pixArea) + bias. Larger faces fit a per-vertex ramp level(rz) = _C - 2*log2(rz) and SUBDIVIDE the polygon at integer crossings, where _C = 0.5*log2(ratio) + mean(log2 rz) + bias. DERIVED: for a plane at depth z a screen pixel covers world extent proportional to z, so texel AREA per pixel goes as z^2 and the correct ramp is mip = 0.5*log2(k*z^2) = 0.5*log2(k) - log2(rz) -- COEFFICIENT 1, not 2. Evaluate the split branch at uniform depth rz0 and it returns 0.5*log2(ratio) + bias - log2(rz0) while the small branch returns 0.5*log2(ratio) + bias: the branches differ by log2(z0), i.e. 1 to 6 LEVELS over greets' z range of ~2-60, in the blurrier direction, and a face crossing the 2%-area threshold POPS by that amount. The doubled slope also over-steepens the ramp INSIDE a subdivided face (near part too sharp, far part too blurry) and puts the subdivision cut lines at the wrong depths. Rounding disagrees too: the small branch truncates (level L active for the metric in [L, L+1), which is what the trilinear fraction assumes) while the split branch's Fist(ml-0.99999) lands one level SHARPER. This flag sets the ramp coefficient to 1, matches the split branch's rounding and its trilinear fraction to the small branch's, and solves the cut-line crossover from the same relation (limrz = 2^(_C - level), successive cuts halving rather than dividing by sqrt2). DEFAULT 1 since 2026-08-08 (same commit that defaulted --mips on). CORRECTION to this flag's original claim: it is NOT inert while --mips is off. The mips gate zeroes the mip LEVEL but does not suppress the SUBDIVISION, and this flag moves the cut lines (limrz), the level range rounding (iml/iMl) and hence the number and shape of the sub-polygons handed to the rasteriser — so with --mips off it still changes geometry and therefore pixels. MEASURED: greets t=1588 pin reproduces the pre-change f1297141611c484bac7cc10a8bdcf630 only under --no-mips --no-mip_fix; --no-mips alone gives 90c38ab4fd769ac8ffba6a35270d17a6. city t=1961 and fountain t=2500 happened to be byte-exact either way, which is what made the original 'inert' claim look true. VERIFICATION HISTORY, because this flag carries two retracted claims: (a) it was committed asserting a correctness it had not been measured to have; (b) it was then reported as MEASURABLY BROKEN / byte-identical-to-mips-off. Claim (b) was a MEASUREMENT ARTIFACT — zsh does not word-split unquoted parameter expansions, so a '--mips --mip_fix' shell variable arrived as ONE argv token, DEMO logged an unknown-flag warning and ignored it, and the arm silently rendered with NO flags at all. Re-measured with explicit argv: the ramp coefficient K=1 is INDEPENDENTLY RE-DERIVED as correct (a screen pixel at depth z covers world extent proportional to z, so texel AREA per pixel goes as z^2 and mip = 0.5*log2(k*z^2) = const - log2(rz): coefficient 1), and evaluating the split branch at uniform depth rz0 now returns EXACTLY the small-face branch value, which K=2 missed by log2(z0). Measured effect, greets t=4200 vs mips-off: --mips alone changes 29.39%% of pixels, --mips --mip_fix changes only 8.28%% — i.e. TWO THIRDS of what plain --mips did to the frame was the K=2 ramp OVER-BLURRING large near faces, not legitimate mip selection. It also cuts subdivision work: split invocations 477 -> 157 at that pose (436 -> 156 at t=2993). Shared engine machinery: with --mips off, every scene is byte-identical either way. NOT addressed here and still true with the flag on: the area-ratio metric is the GEOMETRIC MEAN of the two axis densities, so at grazing (greets' walls compress ~16:1) it selects ~2 levels too SHARP on the compressed axis -- that is anisotropy, and it needs a max-axis metric, not this fix.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (pom_prism,              "FDS_POM_PRISM",                0,                              "parallax", "S1d-3 PRISM SIDE GEOMETRY for the --pom_shell lid (docs/S1D_CLOSED_SHELL_PLAN.md S1d-3, DISPLACEMENT_RESEARCH_II 8.6). DEFAULT 0 = OFF, byte-null: no face, vertex or material is touched. The lid shell pushes the shelled surface out by half the relief slab, so wherever the surface it moves away from does NOT move with it -- an unshelled ceiling or column, a free edge, a T-junction, or (weld off) a torn corner -- a slit exactly the offset wide opens, and MEASURED that slit is 100% of the void the weld cannot reach (every extra void pixel is --pom_path_viz code 0: no fragment was ever rasterised). Hirche 2004's answer is a PRISM per base triangle -- the extrusion keeps a real SIDE QUAD at every edge, so the shell stays watertight while the lids move apart. This builds those side quads as REAL GEOMETRY in one new TriMesh ('pom_prism_sides', no 'Piramid' in the name so the chunk split skips it), spanning lid (the moved position) to base (pristine - delta) so the quad passes exactly through the AUTHORED edge and seals against a static neighbour whatever the weld's tangential slide did. ShellH runs 1 -> 0 down the quad so the march enters at the fragment's true slab height; N/Tangent are the PATCH's, so the tangent frame, the UV domain and the material (including its ::mirUV handedness clone) are the neighbouring lid's. 1 = SEAL: emit only where no partner face shares the authored edge with the SAME delta at both endpoints -- under the default weld that is the boundary skirt, since interior edges already agree. 2 = FULL: emit at EVERY shelled edge, the literal per-triangle prism, as the A/B that says whether the prism replaces the weld or only completes it. Inert without --pom_shell.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (pom_prism_flat,         "FDS_POM_PRISM_FLAT",           1,                              "parallax", "S1d-4 FLAT (NON-MARCHING) PRISM SIDE QUADS, default 1 = ON; inert without --pom_prism (itself default 0), so shipping stays byte-null. WHY (measured at the 2026-08-07 user-reported poses): with --pom_shell_weld=0 --pom_prism=1 the side quads seal the wall GEOMETRICALLY -- kill every march discard and the corner pose t=2980 drops 848+531 defect px -> 1+9, the corridor-fold pose t=5518 drops 2 568 -> 14 -- yet with the march ON the same slits render BLACK VOID, and --pom_path_viz=1 shows NO path code there: the quads' fragments are rasterised, march, and are DISCARDED TO THE LAST PIXEL. Mechanism: a side quad's UV chart is CONSTANT along the extrusion (plus a 1e-4 nudge), so its fragments march across a height field that never changes under the ray -- the no-cross discard ('honest see-through' under the lid model) is a mathematical certainty for them, and the sealing geometry erases itself. THE FIX: emit the quads on a per-material CLONE ('<mat>::prismside', appended to MatLib before Scene_RebuildMatTable so it gets a matID) whose HeightMap AND NormalMap are null -- the rasterizer's march gate (parallax() && Txtr->HeightMap) never fires, the fragments take the plain textured path, and the quads render as LIT, FLAT-SAMPLED stone, exactly how the tessellation bake's step sides render. The quads no longer march, so Vertex::N no longer needs to be the lid's (the march parametrisation was the ONLY consumer that wanted that): they now carry their TRUE outward normal ('out', previously computed and used only for winding), which also fixes the confirmed side-quad shading defect (MeshOps.cpp gave all four side verts the LID's normal -- differencing --pom_prism=1/0 under --viz_geonormal at t=5963 showed the 33 441 prism px shading with the wall's normal, no distinct side normal at all). NormalMap is nulled because the quad's degenerate chart (1e-4 texel span down the extrusion) makes any tangent-space sample a stretched 1-texel stripe; geometric-normal lighting is the honest answer. Known limitation, deliberate: the albedo down the quad is a stretched texel column (same as the tessellation arm's step sides). 0 = legacy marching quads (owner material verbatim, lid normals) for the A/B.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (pom_prism_free,         "FDS_POM_PRISM_FREE",           0,                              "parallax", "S1d-4 FREE-EDGE (TRUE-BOUNDARY) prism quads, default 0 = DO NOT EMIT when --pom_prism_flat is on. A FREE edge (no partner face shares the authored edge -- census: 87 of 352 quads on greets) is where the wall GENUINELY ENDS: the see-through past the last block's silhouette is the lid's no-cross discard revealing the geometry behind (user-confirmed correct at t=5877, and S1d-1 measured all 95 546 true-boundary px void ZERO under a discard). Under legacy --pom_prism_flat=0 the free-edge quads were invisible BY ACCIDENT -- their constant-UV march always discarded -- but a FLAT quad there would be a solid curtain nailed over the see-through. So with flat quads the free class is simply not emitted: open by design, not by discard. 1 = emit free-edge quads anyway (they are flat/solid under --pom_prism_flat=1, marching-and-invisible under 0). Ignored (always emitted, the legacy geometry) when --pom_prism_flat=0.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (pom_prism_march,        "FDS_POM_PRISM_MARCH",          0,                              "parallax", "S1d-5 PRISM-CLIPPED MARCH (docs/S1D_CLOSED_SHELL_PLAN.md S1d-5, DISPLACEMENT_RESEARCH_II 2.2d/8.6): the side quads become MARCHING ENTRY GEOMETRY and 'ray left the domain' becomes a DEFINED event -- the ray is bounded to the prism volume and on exit it is DISCARDED, unconditionally, because the neighbouring prism's own fragment answers the pixel, arbitrated by Z (Hirche 2004; Shell Maps). DEFAULT 0 = OFF, byte-null: every code path this flag arms is behind a per-face ctx bool that is false without it. Needs --pom_shell + --pom_prism + the cone march; ignores --pom_prism_flat (the quads must march -- flat scenery cannot answer a continuing ray) and --pom_shell_cap_fade (measured 3-4x worse slip at matched reach, S1d-4.3a). MODE 1 = SIDE-QUAD ENTRY MARCHING only: quads keep the owner's material and lid tangent frame (the march parametrises the ray in the OWNER's chart -- that is a documented trade, not an oversight; under --pom_normal a HIT shades with the marched field's own normal, which is what makes the S1d-5 'lid normal on the side quad' defect moot for every crossing ray), the 1e-4 UV nudge points INTO the patch box so a hit AT the entry is in-domain (the handedness-matched nudge could point outside and turn every in-stone entry into a lateral exit), and the rasterizer gains PER-LANE SIGNED 1/(V.N): a side-quad fragment can be seen from behind the owner lid's plane (V.N < 0 -- the doorway-curtain case), where the ray ASCENDS through the slab; the march direction and the cone step both carry the sign (ascent cone step is c*gap/(dlen - c); dlen <= c means the ray outruns every cone and exits through the lid = MISS). Exit policy is UNCHANGED in mode 1 (--pom_shell_lid_edge et al. still apply) so the entry change is measurable in isolation. MODE 2 = mode 1 + THE PRISM EXIT: every failure (lateral exit, no-cross) DISCARDS -- --pom_shell_base_clip / --pom_shell_lid_edge / --pom_shell_keep_uv / --pom_shell_side_faces / --pom_shell_side_entry are all FORCED OFF for shelled faces (superseded: base clip existed to kill clamped overhang paint, the clamps existed because a discard used to be a hole; with marching side quads the neighbour answers) -- and PomShell_BuildPrism emits side quads at every edge that is not CHART-CONTINUOUS-watertight: same deltas AND coplanar AND same material (a ::mirUV clone boundary is a chart tear) AND equal UVs at both endpoints. That adds the interior quads at welded FOLDS and handedness/chart tears which the geometric rule skips as watertight -- geometrically sealed, but a marching ray crossing them needs the neighbour's ENTRY fragment, which only exists if the quad is rasterised. T-junction edges are emitted split at each interior T-vertex with the WELDED delta at the split point (offset along an edge is watertight iff affine -- RESEARCH_II 8.6 5b; the split makes the long side's wall coincide with the two short sides' walls by construction), and an init-time census prints how many T-points were non-affine before the split. Precondition (8.6 req 1/2): one extrusion direction per POSITION -- run with --pom_shell_weld=3; ordering is keyed on PomWeldQPos on both sides of every shared wall.")

// ── Headless INTERACTIVE repro harness (--repro=, DEMO/ReproHarness.cpp) ────
// Diagnostic-only: every flag here is read ONLY by the --repro harness, so all
// of them are inert in a normal run and in every --snapshot pin. See
// docs/INTERACTIVE_REPRO.md.
FDS_FLAG_INT  (repro_from,             "FDS_REPRO_FROM",               0,                              "repro",    "--repro: scene time (Timer ticks, centiseconds) the scripted interactive session STARTS at, before it scrubs forward to the --repro= target. THIS IS THE DIAGNOSTIC DIAL, not a convenience knob: --snapshot is blind to any defect that ACCUMULATES over frames, and repro_from measures how much history a defect actually needs. Bisect it — if the defect reproduces from 0 but not from (target-100), the cause needs more than ~10 paused scrub frames of history, which rules out everything that converges in one frame and points at per-frame carried state (Face::LastMip under --mip_hysteresis, the mirror RTT, --fast_fog_froxel_temporal's reprojected history, a one-shot probe bake whose result depends on where the camera was when it first ran, an async shadow bake joined a frame late). 0 = start at scene start, the most faithful and the slowest (~300 paused scrub frames to reach t=2993).")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (repro_settle,           "FDS_REPRO_SETTLE",             8,                              "repro",    "--repro: frames to hold at each dump target with NO transport keys down before writing the PPM. A user who reports a defect is parked at the pose, not mid-scrub, and anything with per-frame history keeps converging while he sits there — so the dumped frame must be a settled one, not the first frame that happens to reach the target time. Under the default paused-scrub mode these frames re-render the SAME scene time repeatedly (pauseMode pins Timer), which is exactly the interactive condition. 0 = dump the first frame at or past the target.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (repro_seq,              "FDS_REPRO_SEQ",                0,                              "repro",    "--repro: after the settled dump, write N MORE consecutive frames at the same pose and same scene time as repro_greets_t<T>_s00.ppm, _s01.ppm, ... The frame-to-frame diff of that series is the direct test for a defect that only appears on SOME frames — temporal reprojection phase, an async bake joined a frame late, a threading race — none of which a single dump (or a --snapshot) can express. 0 = off.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (repro_play,             "FDS_REPRO_PLAY",               0,                              "repro",    "--repro: reach the target with the clock FREE-RUNNING (TimerInit(100) SDL timer thread + the driver's play path) instead of the default paused F2 scrub. This is the most faithful reproduction of an unattended interactive session — the scene plays at wall-clock rate and g_FrameTimeF runs its real load-elastic EMA — and it is deliberately NONDETERMINISTIC: frame pacing depends on machine load, so two runs will not match byte-for-byte. Use it to catch anything that depends on real frame timing; use the default paused scrub for anything you need to A/B. NB the target is reached in real time, so t=2993 costs ~30 s of wall clock.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (repro_max_frames,       "FDS_REPRO_MAX_FRAMES",         20000,                          "repro",    "--repro: safety budget on total ticks for the scripted session, so a target that can never be reached (past the scene's partTime, or a scrub that stalls) fails loudly with a [REPRO] STOPPED line instead of spinning forever. Raise it for a long --repro_from=0 session at a late scene time.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (repro_xres,             "FDS_REPRO_XRES",               0,                              "repro",    "--repro: render width override, 0 = use rev.cfg's ResolutionX. A user's window is usually NOT the configured resolution (his 1512x848 vs rev.cfg's 1920x1080), and resolution changes per-pixel work — mip selection, the checkerboard lattice, POM step counts, every reduced-res ladder. Reproducing his pose at YOUR resolution is not reproducing his frame. Exists so the harness can match a reported window WITHOUT editing rev.cfg (which is shared state and has burned a diagnosis before). Pair with --repro_yres.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (repro_yres,             "FDS_REPRO_YRES",               0,                              "repro",    "--repro: render height override, 0 = use rev.cfg's ResolutionY. See --repro_xres.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (repro_late_cam,         "FDS_REPRO_LATE_CAM",           0,                              "repro",    "--repro: hold FDS_GREETS_CAM back until the target t is REACHED, letting the scene's own scripted camera drive the whole scrub, instead of pinning the free camera from frame 0. THIS IS THE TAB THE USER ACTUALLY PRESSES. Animate_Objects skips the camera spline while View == &FC, so pinning at frame 0 does not merely choose a viewpoint — it FREEZES Scene::CameraHead at its INIT pose for the entire session, while a real user watches the scripted camera fly to t and only then hits TAB, leaving CameraHead parked at the t pose. Any renderer that reads the scene camera rather than the active View therefore sees a completely different camera in the harness than in the user's session, and — because CameraHead stops moving the moment TAB is pressed — the discrepancy is IDENTICAL across every free-cam pose he then flies to, which reads as a screen-locked artifact. Turn it ON whenever a defect is screen-locked across camera moves, or when the user reported it after a TAB.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (repro_run_fountain,     "FDS_REPRO_RUN_FOUNTAIN",       0,                            "repro",    "--repro: RENDER this many real FOUNTAIN frames before greets starts, the way REV.CPP's director does. THE HARNESS'S THIRD BLIND SPOT, and the one that hid the greets mirror band: --repro_prescenes reproduces the other scenes' INIT history but never their RENDER history, and the fountain is the only scene in the tree that runs the transparent DEPTH PEEL in reverse mode (FOUNTAIN.CPP:1083 sets Scene::XparPeelPasses = 4, and the isSet() gate in xparPeelPassesEffective means an explicit --xpar-peel-passes on the CLI DISABLES that per-scene value). Everything the reverse peel touches -- the deep-layer xpar G-buffer slices, the shared g_xparPeelFloor, and the per-strip dirty-column bookkeeping -- is ENGINE-GLOBAL and outlives the scene, so greets inherits whatever the fountain left. Implies the fountain's Initialize_ (no need to also pass --repro_prescenes, though the two compose). 30 frames is plenty; the spray is fully populated after a handful.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (repro_prescenes,        "FDS_REPRO_PRESCENES",          0,                            "repro",    "--repro: run the OTHER scenes' Initialize_* after Initialize_Greets, in REV.CPP's real order (Greets, City, Chase, Fountain, Crash), instead of initialising greets alone. THE HARNESS'S SECOND BLIND SPOT, and the one that hid the greets mirror band: --repro reproduces a live frame's per-frame HISTORY but not its INIT HISTORY, and the demo director inits five scenes into the same process-wide engine globals before greets ever ticks. Anything a later Initialize_ leaves in a shared global — a texture/material table slot, a scene-independent render buffer, an FDS global sized by whoever touched it last — is present in the user's session and absent from a greets-only repro, so the two disagree while every per-frame pin matches. Costs the other scenes' init time (~15 s) and nothing else: no scene but greets is ticked or rendered. Turn it ON whenever a defect survives --repro_from=0, which is the signature of init-carried rather than frame-carried state.")

// ─── argv hygiene ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (strict_flags,           "FDS_STRICT_FLAGS",             1,                              "engine",   "ABORT (exit 2) on an unknown ARGV flag instead of warning and continuing. Default ON. WHY IT IS ON: an ignored flag makes a run render with FEWER flags than intended, and the result is a perfectly plausible image that answers a DIFFERENT question — a silent false negative, which is the most expensive failure mode this project has. It has bitten repeatedly, most reliably through the shell: zsh does NOT word-split unquoted parameter expansions, so `ARM='--pom_shell --pom_normal'; ./DEMO $ARM` (or the same through a function's positional `$4`) passes the ENTIRE string as ONE argv token, every flag is dropped, and the render silently comes back as bare defaults. On 2026-08-08 that manufactured three byte-identical 'stage-off' results for one investigation and voided several conclusions in another, including a 'the defect does not reproduce in --snapshot' finding that sent work down a wrong path for hours. --warn-unknown-flags (default on) still controls the MESSAGE; this controls whether the run DIES. Same exemptions: tokens owned by the other argv parsers (--snapshot/--bench/--out/--material-import*/--print-completion) never trip it. ARGV ONLY — a --flags-file line, FDS_BAKED_ARGS, a SCRIPTS/*.params entry or a live tune-console edit still only warns, since killing a running demo on a stale script line would be worse than the typo. Set 0 (or FDS_STRICT_FLAGS=0) if you genuinely need to pass through an unrecognised token; prefer fixing the token.")

// ─── deferred phase profiler ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
FDS_FLAG_INT  (deferred_prof,          "FDS_DEFERRED_PROF",            0,                              "prof",     "PER-PHASE BREAKDOWN OF THE DEFERRED FRAME (value = leading WARMUP frames to exclude; 1 is the right default, see below), printed as ONE table at process exit (so --snapshot and --bench both get it with no plumbing). The frame profiler's RNDR section is a single bracket around the whole of renderFrame — at greets t=5743 that is ~90%% of the frame with no interior structure, so every optimisation proposal was a bet on which part dominates. This instrument (FDS/RENDER/TailProf.h) splits it: G-buffer clear + fill, the light-list / per-tile cull, the lighting waves, SSAO, fog/froxels, the transparent peel (and its clear/raster/composite split), TBR, volumetric cones, halos, the bloom chain, tonemap+post, edge AA — plus an explicit OTHER row = renderFrame minus the sum of the phases, so the table can never quietly fail to add up. TWO DISTINCT NUMBERS, never conflate them: `wall/f` is ELAPSED time on the tick thread and is what sums to the frame; `thrsum/f` is the sum of per-tile-task durations gathered at the barrier — CORE-milliseconds, ~W x larger — and `effPar = thrsum/wall` is the average number of workers the wave kept busy (≈ pool size = balanced and compute-bound; ≪ pool = a load-imbalanced barrier tail). Everything is normalised by MAIN-VIEW FRAMES, not by invocations: renderFrame runs many times per tick on greets (mirror RTT, shard bakes, env/SH probe cube faces) and the older per-invocation form averaged one 1920x1080 main pass together with dozens of 64² offscreen ones, under-reporting the main-view G-buffer wall by ~5x. Offscreen passes are kept in their own columns; `calls/f` exposes any phase that runs more than once per frame instead of hiding it in a mean. Cost when ON is a handful of clock reads per phase boundary (never per pixel or per triangle) plus one atomic add per tile task; DEFAULT 0 = no clock reads at all. THE VALUE IS A WARMUP COUNT, and it matters: the FIRST main-view frame pays every lazy one-shot (the env-reflection panorama bakes, the SH ambient probe, mip first-touch) — ~230 ms inside one frame on greets, which averaged over a 30-frame bench read as a steady 7.4 ms/frame 'frame-prep' phase that does not exist. Frames 1..N are therefore excluded from the steady-state columns; they are kept in separate buckets and reported (loudly labelled) only if the run was too short to have any steady frame, which is what a single --snapshot pose is — a snapshot CANNOT give a usable breakdown, use --bench=scene@scene=<city|fountain|greets>,t=<t>,iters=N. The legacy FDS_TAIL_PROF=1 env still selects the older rolling every-60-invocations prints.")

// ─── conductor diffuse kill in the HDR frame (docs/SHADING_CONTRACT.md D1) ───
FDS_FLAG_INT  (hdr_metal_kill,         "FDS_HDR_METAL_KILL",           2,                              "deferred", "CONDUCTOR DIFFUSE KILL for the --hdr_linear frame. DEFAULT 2 since 2026-08-08 (user decision after reviewing both looks); 0 = OFF restores the historical buggy behaviour and the old byte-pins. THE BUG: a conductor has no diffuse lobe, and this kernel KNOWS it — the metallic map drives `dk = 1 - metalM; fdB *= dk` at DeferredSurfaceKernel.cpp:2521. But `fdB` feeds only the LDR combine. The SHIPPED --hdr_linear frame is built at :2625 from the RAW accumulator `lB` (`rlB = aB*aB*lB + sB`), which the kill never touched, so metals render with a full diffuse term they should not have. Found by the standalone Metal arm disagreeing (docs/GPU_BENCHMARK_PLAN.md 6.2g, docs/SHADING_CONTRACT.md D1) and confirmed as a CPU bug, not a GPU one: on greets the only full-metal materials are momy/momy2/screen_emiter (metallic map constant 255) plus amudim (mean 1.263/255), and the GPU measured them 51.76 (momy) and 50.53 (projector) luma DARKER than the CPU, of which this recovers +19.41 and +28.93. MODES: 1 = kill the WHOLE accumulator (`lB *= 1-metalM`) — the minimal one-line form, but `lB` is SEEDED WITH EMISSIVE (`Mat->Luminosity * (Mat->Txtr ? 255 : BaseCol)` at :1753-1769), so this also darkens a material that is both metallic and self-luminous; on greets that is momy alone and worth 0.15 luma. 2 = kill only the NON-EMISSIVE part, re-deriving the same seed and preserving it — physically the correct form, since emission is not a reflectance term. The two agree exactly wherever Luminosity is 0 (the projector). CHANGES THE SHIPPING LOOK of every metal surface, so it is left OFF pending the user's own review; the scene byte-pins move when it is on.")

// ─── S1d-7 quad-diagonal crease ──────────────────────────────────────────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (pom_shell_lid_planar,   "FDS_POM_SHELL_LID_PLANAR",     0,                              "parallax", "S1d-7 A/B for the QUAD-DIAGONAL CREASE: give every lid face of ONE AUTHORED PLANE one single lid plane instead of the per-triangle plane PomShell_Build re-derives. Default OFF, byte-null. WHY: --pom_shell moves each lid vertex by `off` along its SMOOTHED normal, and `off` carries the corner correction, so four corners of an authored-FLAT quad can move by different amounts in different directions — the lid quad becomes NON-PLANAR. The re-planing loop then re-derives F.N and F.NormProd from EACH TRIANGLE's own three moved vertices, so the two halves of one quad get genuinely different plane equations, and every per-face quantity the march takes from the plane (the march frame, the depth write's Vz/(V.N), the tangent frame) steps across the triangulation diagonal — exactly where the user's reported crease lies, and why --pom_shell_entry_flat (which removes the OTHER per-triangle discontinuity, the ShellH entry height) attenuates the crease without removing it. This flag replaces both halves' planes with the area-weighted mean normal of their union-find PATCH — which the shell already builds as 'the faces sharing one authored plane' — and re-fits the plane constant to the patch's area-weighted centroid, so no vertex moves and only the per-face plane the march reads changes. Measure the population first with --pom_shell_census ([POM-SHELL-QUADPLANE] prints the per-pair lid-normal angle and the plane gap at the partner's centroid). Note this is a LID-PLANE unification only: it does not touch the prism side quads, the entry height, or the base clip.")

// ─── env probe content: gamma LDR vs linear radiance (the 1.8x conductor gap) ─
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (env_bake_linear,        "FDS_ENV_BAKE_LINEAR",          1,                              "parallax", "Bake the env-reflection probe faces in LINEAR radiance instead of the LDR VPage combine. MEASUREMENT/FIX CANDIDATE, default OFF = byte-null. THE BUG: a probe face is captured from the bake render's 8-bit VPage (EnvBake.cpp renderSixFaces memcpy of surf.Data), and that VPage is the LDR combine `texel*light/256 + spec` (DeferredSurfaceKernel.cpp:2509) — GAMMA albedo at power 1. But the shipped --hdr_linear frame is `albedo^2*light + spec` (:2649), and the env compose adds the probe texel STRAIGHT into that linear accumulator (`sB += ecB*ek*tB`, EnvSpecComposeScalar). So the reflection is brighter than the thing it reflects by ~255/albedo: MEASURED on greets' 'screen emiter' probe, the six CPU faces mean Y 42.53 against the standalone Metal arm's 23.32 for the same probe at the same 256^2 and the same 0-255 scale — 1.82x, which is the whole conductor disagreement (docs/GPU_BENCHMARK_PLAN.md 6.2k). Same failure class as --hdr_metal_kill / the (1-F) asymmetry: a term written for the pre-hdr_linear gamma composite that was never converted. ON captures the kernel's own linear g_hdrBuf radiance for each probe face (the bake's nested renderFrame already sizes g_hdrBuf to the face res, so this is the kernel's real output, not a re-derivation) and stores it clamped into the same 8-bit face store, so NO consumer changes: EnvSpecComposeScalar keeps reading the store as 0-255 radiance and is then correct. Costs 8-bit LINEAR quantization in the darks (the store was gamma-ish before) — the precision-correct form stores sqrt(radiance/255)*255 and squares at fetch, which needs the consumer edit. Uncovered texels (sky/void, h[3]==0) keep the LDR value. Needs --hdr --hdr_linear; inert otherwise. SCOPE 2026-08-08, SPLIT: this flag used to cover TWO captures, because renderSixFaces also feeds the SCENE-CENTRE SH AMBIENT PROBE (EnvBake.cpp, the --sh_ambient source), which carries the identical gamma-in-a-linear-composite defect on EVERY pixel. That half is now --sh_bake_linear and this flag is REFLECTION PROBES ONLY, so the two looks can be judged apart. Priced at the greets projector pose (t=2000, --no-bloom, whole-frame / 73,831-px conductor-mask luma): neither 101.43/102.13, THIS FLAG ALONE 91.79/69.05, --sh_bake_linear alone 95.99/102.13, both 86.17/69.05 -- so of the -15.27 combined whole-frame move this half owns -9.64 and the ambient half -5.44. (The previously recorded '-27% [SHAMB] DC ambient, most of the flag's ~15-luma move' belongs to --sh_bake_linear, and 'most of' was wrong: measured apart, reflections are the larger half.) CHANGES THE SHIPPING LOOK of every reflective surface, so it is left OFF pending the user's own review; the scene byte-pins move when it is on.")

// ─── ENVDYN A4: screen-priority scheduling for the dynamic env overlay ───────
FDS_FLAG_INT  (env_dyn_sched,          "FDS_ENV_DYN_SCHED",            0,                              "parallax", "SCHEDULER for the --env_dynamic overlay. 0 (default) = the legacy whole-PROBE round-robin: a hard off-screen skip, then the first env_dynamic_budget relevant probes in cursor order. 1 = SCREEN-PRIORITY, four TIERS against the same probe budget plus a face budget. WHY: the legacy form updates unevenly because (a) the cursor advances by processed+1 over ALL stores including the non-flagged ones, so the phase drifts and a probe's interval is not its share of the budget; (b) an off-screen owner is skipped OUTRIGHT, so its content goes arbitrarily stale (MEASURED max interval 228 frames on greets) and the moment the camera turns onto it the mech jumps to wherever it now is; (c) the budget is whole probes, so a probe filling half the screen and one filling 8 px are equal. TIERS, highest first: 0 = never overlaid at all (must run once); 1 = VISIBLE and waiting >= env_dyn_max_stall frames, which is the hard bound on what a viewer can see stale, ordered by wait; 2 = VISIBLE, ordered by screenArea x wait, which is proportional-share — a probe with 4x the screen area gets 4x the updates — where screenArea is the owner faces' world AABB projected through the main camera (8 corner transforms, the projection --draw_aabbs already uses, no new pass); 3 = OFF-SCREEN and waiting >= env_dyn_offscreen_period, ordered by wait, so an off-screen probe refreshes slowly instead of never and cannot pop when the camera turns. An off-screen probe younger than the period is not a candidate AT ALL — refreshing it just because the budget had slack is pure cost for nothing visible, which is what made the first cut of this 3.6x more expensive than the legacy path. Probes are taken whole (never a partial face set: a half-updated cube would show the mech at two positions across its own faces). Changes nothing unless --env_dynamic is on (itself default OFF), so the shipping frame cannot move.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (env_dyn_face_budget,    "FDS_ENV_DYN_FACE_BUDGET",      6,                              "parallax", "env_dyn_sched=1: max probe-FACE renders per frame, on top of the env_dynamic_budget PROBE cap (both bind; the probe cap is what keeps the cost envelope equal to the legacy path's). Six is one whole cube, i.e. the most a single probe can ever ask for, so the highest-priority probe is never truncated. Each face costs one dynamic-only Transform + deferred raster/shade at the probe's face res, plus that face's share of the mip refilter. 0 disables the overlay under sched=1 (like --no-env_dynamic).")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (env_dyn_max_stall,      "FDS_ENV_DYN_MAX_STALL",        3,                              "parallax", "env_dyn_sched=1: the WORST-CASE bound, in frames. A VISIBLE probe that has waited this many frames is promoted to tier 1 and outranks every screen-area consideration, so 'the biggest reflection updates fastest' can never starve a small one the viewer is also looking at. This is the dial that trades average interval against worst case, and the whole point of the scheduler is that the worst case is BOUNDED rather than emergent: with greets' 5 flagged probes and the default budget the MEASURED worst visible stall is 2 frames at 3, and pure proportional share (--env_dyn_max_stall=999, the control) puts 'screen emiter' — the smallest of the five on screen — at 33. Quote both numbers when changing it — --env_dyn_stats prints the mean, p50, p95, max interval AND the worst visible stall per probe.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (env_dyn_offscreen_period,"FDS_ENV_DYN_OFFSCREEN_PERIOD", 30,                            "parallax", "env_dyn_sched=1: how many frames an OFF-SCREEN probe may go without a refresh before it becomes a (lowest-tier) candidate. Bounds the staleness the camera can turn onto: the legacy path skips off-screen owners outright, so the mech in a reflection can be MEASURED 228 frames out of date when the surface comes back on screen. Cost is bounded by probes/period per frame — at the default 5 probes / 30 frames that is 0.17 probe updates per frame, which is why this is nearly free while never refreshing them is what makes the reflection pop. 0 = never refresh off-screen probes (the legacy behaviour, minimum cost, unbounded pop).")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (env_dyn_stats,          "FDS_ENV_DYN_STATS",            0,                              "parallax", "env_dynamic INSTRUMENT: every N overlay frames, print a per-probe UPDATE-INTERVAL histogram to stderr and reset. 'Jumpy' is a complaint about TIME, so this is the metric a scheduler change has to move: per probe it reports how many frames wanted an update (flagged + a mover in one of its cube-face pyramids), how many got one, the mean / p50 / max interval between updates, the max run of consecutive frames where the owner was ON SCREEN and wanted an update but did not get one (the worst case a viewer can actually see), the faces rendered, and the overlay's total ms. stderr only — no effect on any rendered pixel, so it is byte-null by construction. 0 = off.")

// ─── asset-root chdir (DEMO/REV.CPP ChdirToAssetRoot) ───────────────────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (chdir_assets,           "FDS_CHDIR_ASSETS",             1,                              "engine",   "Chdir into the asset root at startup so the demo's CWD-relative fopen()s (rev.cfg, SCENES/, TEXTURES/, FONTS/, Revival.xM, cache/) resolve wherever you launched it from. Default 1 = the historical behaviour. WHAT IT ACTUALLY DOES, and why you may want it OFF: ChdirToAssetRoot resolves the running EXECUTABLE's own path and walks <bindir>, <bindir>/../Runtime, <bindir>/../../Runtime for the first directory containing rev.cfg — so the assets a run reads are decided by WHERE THE BINARY LIVES, not by your shell's cwd. The build copies the binary to <tree>/Runtime/DEMO, so a build in a git worktree renders THAT worktree's assets and writes THAT worktree's cache/, silently, no matter what you cd to first. That is a measurement hazard, not a convenience: the byte pins were taken against the user's UNCOMMITTED authoring assets (GREETS.FLD, the momy textures, Piramid.lwo), so a worktree build reproduces none of them, and an instruction to 'run with cwd = the main Runtime/' is unsatisfiable that way. It also cost a full investigation on 2026-08-08, where a worktree build cold-baked its own 426 MiB city env cube and the resulting hash looked like an unexplained pin drift (see docs/SESSION_STATE.md, commit ad55e94). Set 0 (or FDS_CHDIR_ASSETS=0) to leave CWD exactly where you launched from, so `cd /path/to/main/Runtime && /path/to/worktree/build/DEMO/DEMO ...` renders the MAIN tree's assets with the WORKTREE's binary — which is what pinning a code change against fixed assets actually requires. PARSED FROM ARGV BEFORE the flag table exists (the chdir is the first thing main() does), so only the exact tokens `--no-chdir_assets` / `--chdir_assets=0` and the env var are honoured for it; it is registered here so --help lists it and --strict_flags does not reject it. Prints a one-line [CHDIR] note when it fires, so a run is self-identifying.")

// ─── conductor DIRECT-specular Fresnel (docs/SHADING_CONTRACT.md S5/M3) ─────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (metal_spec_f0,          "FDS_METAL_SPEC_F0",            1,                              "deferred", "CONDUCTOR F0 for the DIRECT specular lobe. MEASUREMENT/FIX CANDIDATE, default OFF = byte-null. THE DEFECT: the direct GGX Fresnel is the fixed DIELECTRIC `F = 0.04 + 0.96*(1-VoH)^5` (DeferredSurfaceKernel.cpp:2437) on every surface, and the metalness map reaches the direct term only as a post-hoc GAMMA-albedo TINT on the accumulated lobe (`sB *= 1-m + m*texB/255`, :2543). A conductor's F0 IS its albedo -- that is the whole content of a metalness workflow -- so a metal highlight is being rendered at 4% reflectance and then tinted, i.e. ~1/20 of its energy at normal incidence. The metalness pull EXISTS on the ENV side already (`f0 + (0.98-f0)*metalM`, :1255), so the two specular paths of the same kernel disagree with each other about what a metal is. ON computes the standard `F0 = mix(0.04, albedo, metalness)` per channel with the LINEAR albedo (albedo/255)^2 -- linear because the --hdr_linear frame this feeds squares the albedo everywhere else -- evaluates Schlick per channel, and SKIPS the post-hoc gamma tint, which would otherwise double-count the albedo. Dielectrics are bit-unchanged (metalM==0 collapses to the old scalar expression). NOTE what this does NOT fix and is not: at greets' projector pose (`screen emiter`, the only conductor in frame) the direct term is ~0 because --greets_omni_shadows' cube tap rejects 98.6% of the pixel-light pairs and drives the rest to shadowAtten < 1e-6, so this flag moves 0 px there; its measurable target is `momy-1`, which IS directly lit. Also note the standalone Metal arm uses the SAME dielectric F0 (GpuBench/shaders/deferred.metal:575), so this moves the CPU toward PHYSICS and AWAY from the current GPU oracle -- the GPU needs the same fix. Needs --pbr (inert on the Blinn-Phong fallback) and --metal_map. CHANGES THE SHIPPING LOOK of directly-lit metal, so it is left OFF pending the user's own review; the scene byte-pins move when it is on.")

// ─── conductor ENV-lobe tint + the SH-ambient half of the probe bake ────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (env_metal_tint_linear,  "FDS_ENV_METAL_TINT_LINEAR",     1,                              "deferred", "CONDUCTOR TINT for the ENVIRONMENT specular lobe. MEASUREMENT/FIX CANDIDATE, default OFF = byte-null. THE DEFECT (docs/SHADING_CONTRACT.md 8.2 row S-d, the last GAMMA-LIVE row in the --hdr_linear migration audit): EnvSpecComposeScalar tints the probe radiance by `t = 1 - m + m*texB/255` (DeferredSurfaceKernel.cpp:1309-1311), and texB is the GAMMA 0-255 albedo texel being used as a LINEAR reflectance. The shipped --hdr_linear composite squares the albedo everywhere else (`rlB = aB*aB*lB + sB`, :2708) and this lobe lands in sB, which enters that composite UNSQUARED -- so the error is PER-CHANNEL, not a brightness scale: squaring an sRGB-ish albedo separates the channels non-uniformly, so leaving it gamma pulls them together and DESATURATES the conductor. That matters because with --no-env_refl a conductor renders at 0.00, i.e. essentially all of its appearance is this lobe. ON squares the normalised albedo, which is the same linear reflectance --metal_spec_f0 puts into the DIRECT lobe's F0 and the same quantity the standalone Metal oracle already uses (`mix(1, S.baseColor, metal)` with `S.baseColor = alb*alb`, GpuBench/shaders/deferred.metal:738). Dielectrics (metalM == 0) are bit-unchanged. MEASURED on greets t=2000 at the projector pose (FDS_GREETS_CAM=\"43.0,3.4,-62.85,1.0,0.0,0.0\", --no-bloom, conductor mask = the --hdr_metal_kill 0-vs-2 change set at that pose, 73,831 px; all figures read off the final tonemapped 8-bit frame, so ACES+sqrt are already in them). Per-channel R/G/B on the mask: 127.42/101.32/39.95 -> 127.42/88.75/20.37, against the standalone Metal arm's 87.38/57.78/13.99. NOTE THAT R DOES NOT MOVE AT ALL: 'screen emiter' authors albedo (255,206,104), so squaring the normalised R is the identity -- a brightness bug cannot do that, and it is the signature that this is a per-CHANNEL defect. Chromaticity (R,G,B as fractions of their sum) (0.474,0.377,0.149) -> (0.539,0.375,0.086) against the GPU's (0.549,0.363,0.088): distance to the GPU falls 0.097 -> 0.016. Saturation 0.687 -> 0.840 against the GPU's 0.840 (exact). Hue 42.1deg -> 38.3deg against 35.8deg. Luma: mask 102.13 -> 92.52, whole frame 101.43 -> 100.89. COMPOSED WITH --env_bake_linear the 6.2k conductor residual falls 1.120x -> 1.014x on LUMA, but the chroma then OVERSHOOTS to (0.587,0.344,0.069): the CPU's linear-captured probe CONTENT is itself more saturated than the GPU's (mean face B/G/R, CPU 10.86/21.30/29.87 vs GPU 12.93/22.69/27.85), which is a SEPARATE divergence and not this row. It is a DARKENING fix on top of a brightness gap the probe bake owns (--env_bake_linear); the two are independent and compose. CHANGES THE SHIPPING LOOK of every metallic reflective surface, so it is left OFF pending the user's own review; the scene byte-pins move when it is on.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (sh_bake_linear,         "FDS_SH_BAKE_LINEAR",           1,                              "parallax", "LINEAR capture for the SCENE-CENTRE SH AMBIENT probe -- the other half of what --env_bake_linear used to do in one flag. MEASUREMENT/FIX CANDIDATE, default OFF = byte-null. THE BUG is identical to --env_bake_linear's (docs/SHADING_CONTRACT.md 8.1 row M2): SHAmbient_EnsureBaked projects its 9 SH coefficients from the SAME renderSixFaces 8-bit face store, and by default that store is the LDR VPage combine `texel*light/256 + spec` -- GAMMA albedo at power 1 -- while the shipped frame multiplies E(n) by the SQUARED albedo (`aB*aB*lB`). So the ambient irradiance is gamma content in a linear composite, on EVERY pixel rather than only on reflective ones. ON makes the SH probe's six faces capture the kernel's own linear g_hdrBuf radiance, exactly as --env_bake_linear does for the reflection probes. MEASURED on greets: [SHAMB] DC ambient B/G/R 148.3/158.8/154.5 -> 107.8/116.7/118.3 (-27%). SPLIT RATIONALE 2026-08-08: these two captures were one flag, and the combined -15.3 whole-frame luma was being attributed to reflections when the ambient half owns most of it. Priced apart at the greets projector pose (t=2000, --no-bloom; whole-frame / 73,831-px conductor-mask mean luma): NEITHER 101.43 / 102.13; --env_bake_linear ALONE 91.79 / 69.05; --sh_bake_linear ALONE 95.99 / 102.13; BOTH 86.17 / 69.05. So the REFLECTION half owns -9.64 of the -15.27 whole-frame move and this AMBIENT half -5.44 -- which CORRECTS the earlier claim that the ambient half was 'most of' it. The conductor mask does not move under this flag at all, because --hdr_metal_kill=2 already removes the entire diffuse/ambient term from a metalness-1 surface. [SHAMB] DC ambient B/G/R across the same 2x2: 148.3/158.8/154.5, 147.7/158.1/153.8, 108.4/117.3/119.0, 107.8/116.7/118.3 -- i.e. --env_bake_linear now moves the ambient by only -0.4%, through reflective surfaces appearing in the SH probe's own faces. CHANGES THE AMBIENT LEVEL OF EVERY PIXEL, so it is left OFF pending the user's own review; the scene byte-pins move when it is on.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (shadow_noncaster_depth,  "FDS_SHADOW_NONCASTER_DEPTH",   1,                              "shadows",  "PolyId cube shadows: give a NON-CASTING receiver the BIASED DEPTH test instead of the identity test. MEASUREMENT/FIX CANDIDATE, default OFF = byte-null. THE BUG: RENDER/Shadows.cpp excludes a material from the shadow BAKE when it is Transparent|Additive|SkipZ or its NAME contains \"lamp\"/\"emi\" (the heuristic that stops a lamp housing from occluding the omni inside it). Under ShadowMode::PolyId -- the production default -- a cube tap is occluded iff the stored id is non-zero and differs from the receiver own id, i.e. 'the closest thing to the light along this ray must be ME'. A material that was never rasterised into the cube can NEVER satisfy that, so whatever the bake did store along the ray (the room BEHIND the surface) reads as an occluder and the surface is shadowed FOR EVER, by every shadow-casting omni. Greets' 'screen emiter' -- the amber projector housing, Specular 0.62 at Glossiness 128 -- matches on \"emi\" and is exactly this case. MEASURED at the projector pose (greets t=2000, FDS_GREETS_CAM=\"43.0,3.4,-62.85,1.0,0.0,0.0\", --no-bloom, --no-env_refl so only the direct term remains, 73,831-px conductor mask): default R/G/B 0.000/0.000/0.000; ON 0.397/0.348/0.000 -- BIT-IDENTICAL to --no-greets_omni_shadows and to --no-shadows, i.e. this recovers 100% of the lost direct term and nothing more. It agrees with the standalone Metal arm, whose biased depth compare reads LIT here, and with that arm's ray-cast ground truth (--probe: lights 5 and 6 RAYCAST clear). Depth ACNE is not a risk for a non-caster: acne comes from a surface being compared against its OWN stored depth, and this one never wrote any. The receiver also skips the static shadow LIGHTMAP, whose atlas was baked through the same identity test. RELATED TRAP: --no-shadow_polyid on the COMMAND LINE cannot move g_shadowMode at all -- it is a namespace-scope dynamic initialiser that runs before argv is parsed -- so only FDS_SHADOW_POLYID=0 or F3 flips the mode, and an investigation that used the argv form concluded PolyId was innocent. CHANGES THE SHIPPING LOOK of every non-casting material lit by a shadow-casting omni, so it is left OFF pending the user's own review; the scene byte-pins move when it is on.")
// ─── S1d-9 silhouette slack (docs/S1D_CLOSED_SHELL_PLAN.md) ───
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(pom_shell_sil_slack,    "FDS_POM_SHELL_SIL_SLACK",      0.0f,                           "parallax", "S1d-9 SILHOUETTE SLACK for --pom_shell_lid_true_edge bit6 (64), in units of the patch's UV amplitude; default 0 = the EXACT partner half-space test, and inert unless bit6 is set. bit6 discards a lid exit only where the marched HIT has left the convex partner's solid, i.e. overshoot > lean*(h0 - hitH) + slack*uvAmp. lean*(h0-h) is exact geometry (at a convex ridge the partner's plane IS the side face, so below the authored plane the material really does reach that far past the ridge line); the slack widens it, trading silhouette crenellation for coverage. THE TRADE IS MEASURED and it is the point of the flag: at slack 0 the t=5877 silhouette scores std 2.76 / p95 7.00 against tessellation@0.18's 2.43 / 5.00 at matched world amplitude (the unfixed arm is 0.35, STRAIGHTER THAN FLAT's 0.46), and the residual cost is concentrated at ONE pose, t=5743, where the near-right wall panel loses a 230 px band. Raising the slack buys that band back and straightens the silhouette again. Read docs/S1D_CLOSED_SHELL_PLAN.md S1d-9 before picking a value.")

// --- reflection-probe AMBIENT CONTENT (docs/SHADING_CONTRACT.md 9.4) --------
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (env_bake_sh_first,      "FDS_ENV_BAKE_SH_FIRST",        1,                              "parallax", "Bake the SCENE-CENTRE SH AMBIENT probe BEFORE the env-REFLECTION probes, so a reflection probe's six faces are rendered with the same COLOURED SH irradiance the shipped frame uses instead of the flat neutral Sc->Ambient constant. MEASUREMENT/FIX CANDIDATE, default OFF = byte-null; inert unless BOTH --sh_ambient and --env_refl are on. THE DEFECT, and it is an ORDERING one: renderFrame triggers EnvReflection_FramePrep at RENDER.CPP:491 and SHAmbient_EnsureBaked at RENDER.CPP:511, in that source order, on the same first frame. SHAmbient_Coeffs (EnvBake.cpp:2135) returns null until SHProbe::baked is set at :2223, so every reflection-probe face is shaded through the kernel's FLAT-ambient fallback (`lB = Lum*255 + Mat->Diffuse * Sc->Ambient.B`, DeferredSurfaceKernel.cpp:1799) rather than its SH branch (:1789-1793). Greets authors Sc->Ambient = (32,32,32) -- an ACHROMATIC grey -- so the CPU's reflection probes carry a colourless ambient floor where the frame, and the standalone Metal oracle (deferred.metal:785, AmbientRadiance, bound with shBuf during its own cube bake at Deferred.mm:2156), carry a coloured directional one. That is a per-CHANNEL difference in probe CONTENT, upstream of and unreachable by --env_metal_tint_linear, and it is the recorded cause of the chroma residual that flag opens: mean face B/G/R under --env_bake_linear, CPU 10.86/21.30/29.87 vs GPU 12.93/22.69/27.85, luma agreeing to 2 % while the colour does not. The ordering is INCIDENTAL, not a dependency: SHAmbient_EnsureBaked reads nothing the reflection bake produces (it recomputes its own scene AABB), and the anti-recursion guards (g_envBakeInProgress, g_offscreenViewDepth) are symmetric, so either order is legal. ON calls SHAmbient_EnsureBaked from the top of EnvReflection_FramePrep. WHAT IT COSTS, stated because it is a real trade and not free: with the SH probe baked first, the SH probe's OWN faces no longer see the reflection probes' output -- the reverse coupling recorded on --sh_bake_linear as a -0.4 % ambient move. One bake either way; no extra render.")

// --- the MECH rows E6 / E7 / P2 (docs/SHADING_CONTRACT.md 11) ---------------
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (env_bake_include_animated, "FDS_ENV_BAKE_INCLUDE_ANIMATED", 1,                          "parallax", "Let ANIMATED meshes into the ordinary (static) env-reflection probe bakes. MEASUREMENT/LOOK CANDIDATE, default OFF = byte-null. THE DIVERGENCE (docs/SHADING_CONTRACT.md 11 row E6): EnvBake.cpp's renderSixFaces sets g_envBakeSkipDynamic for every ordinary probe bake, Transform.cpp:1274 folds it into inStaticBake, and :1559 continues past every mesh isDynamicForBake calls dynamic (Pos-spline extent > 0.1 u or Rotate quaternion extent > 0.01, on the object OR ANY ANCESTOR). On greets that excludes the WHOLE MECH -- Hull.lwo, Hull2.lwo, L_leg1/2.lwo, R_leg1/2.lwo -- so the mech is structurally absent from its own canopy reflection, while the standalone Metal arm (which has no such rule) has it in five of six faces of the same probe. ON removes the exclusion, so an animated mesh is captured at whatever pose it holds AT BAKE TIME -- one frozen pose, baked once into a cached probe. That is correct for the MECH'S OWN geometry in the MECH'S OWN probe (the 'cockpit' probe sits at the mech's centroid, so the hull/barrels/legs do not move relative to it); the probe's view of the ROOM does drift as the mech travels, which is a pre-existing property of every static probe and is what --env_dynamic exists for. This flag does not make that worse and does not interact with the ENVDYN overlay (which skips STATIC meshes and composites over the retained static capture). MEASURED on greets t=4871 at the user's mech pose, over the 33,478-px canopy mask. CORRECTED 2026-08-11 for ONE case: the 'sits at the mech's centroid, so the hull/barrels/legs do not move relative to it' argument is about the POSE and says nothing about the DISTANCE. The capture point is the owner surface's area centroid, i.e. a point ON the canopy, so those parts are at ~0 units and render as near-plane-clipped slabs. That stayed tolerable only while the probe was FROZEN (one ghost, baked at init, left behind); under --env_probe_follow_owner the point rides the mech and the slabs are re-burnt every re-bake, so a followed probe forces the animated skip back on for its own bake regardless of this flag. See env_probe_follow_owner.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (env_mip_chain,          "FDS_ENV_MIP_CHAIN",            9,                              "parallax", "VIRTUAL DEPTH of the env-probe mip chain used by the roughness->level select, i.e. the divisor in `lvlF = rough * (N - 1)`. 0 (default) = N is the store's real numMips (EnvPanoLinear::kMaxMips = 4), byte-null. THE DIVERGENCE (docs/SHADING_CONTRACT.md 11 row E7): the select FORMULA is identical on both arms but the divisor is not. The CPU store is a FIXED 4-level chain whatever the face res, while the standalone Metal arm builds the FULL hardware chain (mipmapped:YES + generateMipmaps -> 8 levels at 128 face res). So 'cockpit' at gloss 64 -> rough = sqrt(2/66) = 0.1741 lands at lvlF = 0.522 on the CPU (between 256 and 128, effective ~178 sq) and lvl = 1.219 on the GPU (between 64 and 32, effective ~55 sq) -- the SAME roughness selecting a 3x WIDER lobe on the GPU. That is the mechanism behind 'the GPU melts the canopy's window-frame ribs into a smooth mirror sweep'. Setting this to log2(faceRes)+1 reproduces the GPU's full-chain select at that same face res: at the shipped --env_bake_res=256, --env_mip_chain=9 gives lvlF = rough*8 = 1.39 = ~97 sq effective, matching the GPU's --env_res=256 row (98 sq); with --env_bake_res=128 as well, --env_mip_chain=8 is an EXACT emulation of the GPU default (~55 sq). Values above the store's real numMips still CLAMP to numMips-1, so the reachable floor is faceRes>>3 (32 sq at res 256) -- enough for the mech's roughness but not for rough ~ 1. Applies at SAMPLE time, so no rebake is needed. Clamped to 2..16; <= 1 is treated as unset.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (deferred_checker_env_full, "FDS_DEFERRED_CHECKER_ENV_FULL", 1,                          "deferred", "Shade ENV-REFLECTIVE pixels at FULL RATE in wave 1 under --deferred_checkerboard / --deferred_quarter, instead of letting the wave-2 fill's full-shade fallback do it. Default OFF = byte-null. THE DEFECT: the fill already refuses to AVERAGE an env-reflective pixel (`envForceFull`, DeferredSurfaceKernel.cpp:5003 -- both averaging models break on reflections) and re-shades it with the SCALAR FALLBACK at :5254. That fallback is a REDUCED kernel: it has no --pbr Cook-Torrance GGX lobe (it runs Blinn-Phong `pow(NdotH, gloss)` at :5420), no shadow maps at all (no computeMapShadowAtten, no resolveCubeAtten, no static lightmap, no PolyId, no bias), no AO map, no normal-map LOD fade, and no --hdr_metal_kill; and it applies the spot-cone penumbra to SPECULAR where wave 1 does not. greets sets --pbr and --shadows ON, so alternate pixels of every reflective surface are shaded by two DIFFERENT BRDFs and print a STATIC lattice -- the phase is `(px ^ py) & 1` with no frame term, so it never averages out under motion. MEASURED on greets t=4871 over the 33,478-px canopy mask, as the mean luma of the wave-2 cells minus the wave-1 cells (0 if the reconstruction were unbiased): SHIPPED +6.82; --no-shadows +5.51; --no-pbr +0.90; --no-pbr --no-shadows -0.01; --deferred_checkerboard=0 +0.04; the standalone Metal arm -0.05. So --pbr owns ~5.9 luma of it and the shadow terms ~0.9-1.3, and with both removed from wave 1 the two kernels agree to a hundredth of a luma. ON costs NO EXTRA SHADED PIXELS -- the fill was already full-shading exactly this set -- only the terms the fallback was skipping (shadow taps, AO, the GGX lobe) on half of them.")

// --- SHADING_CONTRACT 12: the wave-2 fill's OTHER reduced-kernel trigger ----
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (deferred_checker_edge_full, "FDS_DEFERRED_CHECKER_EDGE_FULL", 0,                        "deferred", "Shade the EDGE cells at FULL RATE in wave 1 under --deferred_checkerboard / --deferred_quarter, instead of letting the wave-2 fill's full-shade fallback do it. Default OFF = byte-null. THE DEFECT (docs/SHADING_CONTRACT.md 12, the sibling of --deferred_checker_env_full): the fill averages a dropped cell from its shaded neighbours only when at least one is COMPATIBLE (same matID + dot(N,Nn) >= --quarter_normal_cos + relative |dZ| <= --quarter_z_jump); when none is, it falls through to its own scalar re-shade at DeferredSurfaceKernel.cpp:5310. That fallback is a REDUCED kernel and this is the exhaustive list of what it does NOT compute, against the wave-1 scalar kernel: no --pbr Cook-Torrance GGX lobe (it runs Blinn-Phong std::pow(NdotH,gloss)); NO shadow term of any kind (no computeMapShadowAtten -> no 2-D spot map, no mirror-clone source map/cube; no resolveCubeAtten -> no cube tap, no static shadow lightmap, no PolyId identity test, no --shadow_bias / --shadow_slope_bias, no --shadow_noncaster_depth); no AO at all (no Mat_AoInAlpha - it never even fetches the albedo ALPHA - no Mat->AoMap, no --ao_direct); no normal-map LOD fade (--nmap_lod_fade_start/step); no --no_nmap gate; no --pom_horizon relief self-shadow; no --metal_spec_f0; no --hdr_metal_kill (its HDR composite uses the RAW lB, so a conductor keeps the diffuse the default hdr_metal_kill=2 removes in wave 1); no --texture_filter / --poly_viz albedo plane read; no --deferred_no_spec / --prof_no_spec / --prof_no_tex. And it ADDS a term wave 1 lacks: it multiplies SPECULAR by the spot-cone smoothstep (contract D4). The trigger set is every material / normal / depth EDGE, i.e. silhouettes and creases, which is exactly where shadows matter most. Predicate is ONE shared function (fillFallsBackHere) called from both waves, so they cannot drift. Costs no extra SHADED pixels - the fill was already full-shading exactly this set - only the terms the fallback skipped, plus one G-buffer neighbour probe per dropped cell in wave 1.")

// --- THE ENV-MAP INSPECTOR (FDS/RENDER/EnvBake.cpp, X-cycle + F/Shift+F) ----
FDS_FLAG_INT  (env_map_viz,            "FDS_ENV_MAP_VIZ",              0,                              "parallax", "ENV-MAP INSPECTOR: draw a BAKED environment probe's own texels over the frame, post-tonemap, so the probes can be LOOKED at in the interactive window instead of only through FDS_ENVBAKE_DUMP's offline PPMs. 0 (default) = off, byte-null (the whole painter early-outs on this one compare). 1 = FACES: the selected probe's six PADDED cube faces as a 3x2 atlas at mip 0, in the SAME cell order as FDS_ENVBAKE_DUMP and GpuBench --dump_env_cube (+X -X +Y / -Y +Z -Z), each cell labelled, with a header naming the material(s), the world bake point, the face res, the mip chain and whether the probe is a live --env_dynamic one. 2 = MIP CHAIN: every stored level of that probe left-to-right at native relative size, so the roughness lobe the kernel picks can be SEEN rather than inferred (see --env_mip_chain). 3 = CPU|GPU: the same 3x2 atlas beside the Metal arm's atlas for the same material, read from <dir>/gpuenv_<material>.ppm as written by `GpuBench --dump_env_cube` (default dir /tmp) -- the probe-CONTENT comparison of docs/SHADING_CONTRACT.md 11 row E6 as one keypress. A legacy equirect store (--no-env_cube) is drawn as the single panorama image with a note, never as a fake cube. A probe with no pixel data is drawn as an EMPTY framed panel that says so -- never as black texels. Reads the stores, re-bakes nothing, and paints VPage only.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (env_map_probe,          "FDS_ENV_MAP_PROBE",            0,                              "parallax", "env_map_viz: WHICH probe, 1-based (0 = the first). greets bakes ~6 (momy-1, momy-2, stairs, stairs::mirUV, screen emiter, amudim, cockpit), city registers 71 per-building panoramas, so the viewer is useless without paging: F steps forward, Shift+F back, both wrapping, and the value written here is exactly what those keys write (FeatureFlags::setParamFromText, the same path the tune console uses). Out-of-range values WRAP by modulo rather than clamping, so a stale --env_map_probe from a bigger scene still lands on a real probe.")

// --- greets shatter: scope the break's face-hide to the SCREEN's own geometry -
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (greets_shatter_screen_mat_only, "FDS_GREETS_SHATTER_SCREEN_MAT_ONLY", 1,                 "greets",   "Greets shatter: require the SCREEN MATERIAL ('screen*') on the break's rule-(A) 'box body' collector in BuildGreetsShatter, so the mirror can only take its OWN geometry with it. THE BUG (user-reported, 'when the mirror breaks it takes the wall behind it with it - it's caused by the displace flag'): rule (A) collected ANY face whose three verts fall inside the glass (u,v) footprint AND lie behind the front plane, with NO material filter, over a depth slab of 0.5*min(panel span) = 5.93 world units at the greets screen. With flat geometry the stone wall 'rooms' behind the screen is 2 room-spanning quads, so no face ever fits inside the 14.55x11.86 footprint and rule (A) collects nothing the material-scoped rules (B)/(C) did not already have. Under --greets_displace, DisplaceStoneSubdiv turns 'rooms' into ~68.5k faces and 2 436 of them now fit entirely inside the footprint -> collected -> the break's `F->Txtr = g_greetsScreenHideMat` swap makes the WALL invisible along with the mirror, leaving a black hole into the void once the shards fall. MEASURED at BuildGreetsShatter (FDS_GREETS_SHATTER_BOXDUMP=1): hide faces 450 flat / 2886 displaced before, 450 / 450 after; the extra 2436 are all material 'rooms' (FDS_GREETS_SHATTER_SCAN census). The flat arm is UNCHANGED because rule (A)'s unique contribution there was already zero (450 collected, 450 of them matched by rule (B)'s material test), so this is byte-null everywhere the shatter is not triggered and byte-null in the flat arm even when it is. Default ON = the repair; --no-greets_shatter_screen_mat_only restores the unscoped geometric collector for A/B. Trade: a future screen whose box body carries a NON-'screen*' material would keep a dark strip after its break instead of being hidden — under-hiding a rim is a far cheaper failure than deleting the room's wall.")

// --- mirror-clone FLARES: extent, not a centre pixel --------------------------
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (mirror_flare_bbox,      "FDS_MIRROR_FLARE_BBOX",        1,                              "mirror",   "Decide a MIRROR-CLONE FLARE by the sprite's SCREEN EXTENT instead of its centre pixel (user: 'flares rendering in mirrors should be decided by their bbox, no center point'). THE DEFECT: a clone flare is a sprite tens-to-hundreds of px across, but FILLERS.CPP's two gates (The_MMX_Scalar's immediate path, TBR_Sprite's deferred insert) sampled g_gbuffer->mirrorMask at ONE pixel — int(PX),int(PY) — and returned outright on a mismatch, so the entire sprite vanished the instant its centre crossed the mirror's edge while most of its disc was still over the reflection; the same all-or-nothing verdict was taken by the Face_PointZTest CENTRE occlusion reject, where the pixel under the centre is the WALL BESIDE the mirror and occludes a sprite that is mostly over the (farther) reflection. Symptom: flares popping in and out along mirror edges as the camera moves, and reflections missing flares that belong in them. THE FIX IS TWO-PART and both halves are required: (1) ADMISSION by extent — the sprite's screen bbox vs the mirror's stamped footprint bbox, published per frame by StampMirrorMasks as fds::MirrorMaskScreenBBox from the SAME clipped wall polygons it rasterises (so gate and mask cannot disagree; it is a superset of the stamped pixels, and O(1)); (2) THE DECISION per pixel — Spriter's new MaskMirror lane test kills every lane whose gb.mirrorMask byte is not this face's tag. Part (2) is what keeps the ORIGINAL defect family dead: every mirror clones every omni, so a clone sits at an arbitrary reflected world position and can pass a plain z-test inside ANOTHER mirror's footprint ('omni shows twice in the mirror') or out in the open room ('flare through walls'); widening the admission WITHOUT the per-pixel clip resurrects both, which is why they ship together and why the centre-point z reject can be dropped for clones (Spriter's per-pixel z test, EnableZTest=true, already runs and is exact — only the early-out is lost). SCOPE: clone flares only (Face::mirrorMaskTag != 0). Non-mirror flares — city beacons, fountain/chase particles — take the identical Spriter instantiation with a null mask pointer and are byte-identical; so is every scene with no mirrors. NOT ADDRESSED (measured, stated, unfixed): Transform.cpp's omni loop still admits a flare on its CENTRE's view-space z alone (nearZ..farZ), so an omni just behind the near plane is dropped even though its sprite would still cover screen area; fixing that needs real 3D sprite clipping and moves every scene's flares, not just mirrored ones. --no-mirror_flare_bbox restores the centre-point gates exactly.")

// --- ENVDYN perf: the reflected-hemisphere face cull -------------------------
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (env_dyn_face_cull,      "FDS_ENV_DYN_FACE_CULL",        0,                              "parallax", "--env_dynamic PERF: skip the dynamic-mesh overlay on any cube face that a reflection off the probe's OWN OWNER SURFACE can never reach. MEASUREMENT/PERF CANDIDATE, default OFF = byte-null (the mask is not even computed). THE PROPERTY (user: 'the env map wont show any dynamic data from the camera pos - we need to take advantage of it'): a probe is only ever READ by the deferred kernel at pixels of its owner surface, so a face outside the reflected set is one whose overlay NOBODY can see. THE BOUND is exact and camera-INDEPENDENT: for a front-facing shaded pixel n.v > 0, and the reflected direction r = 2(n.v)n - v has n.r = n.v > 0, so every reflected direction lies in the open hemisphere on the outward side of its own normal, for EVERY eye position. Face f is kept iff n.fwd + pad*(|n.right| + |n.up|) > threshold for some owner normal n (the face's whole padded direction square, three dots and two abs). The mask is the OR over every normal of every material mapped to the store, so a curved or two-sided owner reaches all six and the cull is a no-op -- the safe direction. Because there is no camera term, nothing here can pop when the camera moves. Parallax (the kernel indexes with (hit - bakePoint), not r) and --env_live_water's tilt are absorbed by env_dyn_face_cull_slack. Composes with the existing mover-relevance mask (it ANDs into it) and with both schedulers. Prints one [ENVDYN-CULL] line per probe stating how many of the six faces survived. MEASURED ON GREETS AND IT IS A NO-OP THERE, which is why it ships OFF: all five envDynamic-flagged probes report 6/6 reachable (momy-2 and momy-1 have 332 owner normals each, stairs 54, screen emiter 38), 0 of 396 face renders removed over a 496-frame --repro fly-through, 0 ms saved, final frame byte-identical (7cb72939 both arms). None of greets' flagged reflectors is planar. AND THE CEILING IS LOW EVEN WHEN IT BITES: a SINGLE normal's reflected hemisphere still touches FIVE of the six cube faces (only the face most opposite the normal can fail n.fwd + pad*(|n.right|+|n.up|) > 0, and only when that normal is near-aligned with a cube axis), so the best case is 1 face in 6 and any slack > 0 erodes it. The flag is kept for the [ENVDYN-CULL] CENSUS -- it is the evidence that this idea has no purchase on this geometry, so the next person does not re-derive it -- and because a genuinely planar, axis-aligned reflector (a city window) would still take the one face. THE VERSION WITH REAL HEADROOM is camera-DEPENDENT (which faces the viewer's pixels actually sampled) and needs a per-store 6-bit sampled-face mask published by the deferred kernel's env lookup; that hook is in DeferredSurfaceKernel.cpp and is REPORTED, NOT TAKEN.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(env_dyn_face_cull_slack,"FDS_ENV_DYN_FACE_CULL_SLACK",  15.0f,                          "parallax", "env_dyn_face_cull: the angular SLACK, in degrees, that the reflected-hemisphere test widens the owner's normals by before deciding a face is unreachable. It exists to cover the two things sitting between the ideal reflected direction r and the direction the kernel actually indexes with: (1) PARALLAX -- the lookup uses (hit - bakePoint) where hit = P + t*r; for a PLANAR owner the bake point is on the plane and the sign of n.(hit - bakePoint) is exactly the sign of n.r, but for a non-planar owner the error is the owner's own extent over the parallax hit distance; (2) --env_live_water, which tilts the direction by at most its amp. Both are angular. The widened test admits any normal within this angle, and |d(value)/d(n)| <= sqrt(1 + 2*pad^2), which sets the threshold. 0 = the exact hemisphere test with no margin (only correct for a perfectly planar owner with no live water). Raising it makes the cull weaker and never wrong; the default 15 covers the greets momy panels (owner extents of a few units against parallax hit distances of tens).")

// ─── vec GGX precision (docs/SHADING_CONTRACT.md, the SIMD row) ─────────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (vec_ggx_refine,         "FDS_VEC_GGX_REFINE",           1,                              "deferred", "Refine the vec (SIMD) GGX lobe's reciprocals with one Newton-Raphson step, default 1 = ON. THE BUG: run_vec_ggx_loop put D, Gv, Gl and 1/(4*NdotV) through FIVE bare _mm256_rcp_ps / _mm256_rsqrt_ps. Those are ~12-bit on x86, and simde lowers them to vrecpeq/vrsqrteq on arm64, which is COARSER still — so the SAME scene shaded through --deferred_vec produced a DIFFERENT IMAGE on the two architectures, and since that flag defaults ON for x86 and OFF for arm64, x86 and arm64 users were not seeing the same render. Not cosmetic in a GGX lobe: D divides by (NdotH^2*(a^2-1)+1)^2, a small number squared at low roughness, so relative error compounds. MEASURED (audit a1b2408, greets t=4871, --deferred_vec against the scalar reference): shipped 32 967 px differ, mean |dY| 7.931, max 164, 4 643 px >10 luma; --prof_no_spec collapses it to max 6 with 0 px >10 (so every diffuse and shadow term already agreed — it is the lobe and nothing else); and the roughness sweep names the mechanism outright — rough 0.2 -> mean 8.379 / max 178 / 5 091 px, rough 0.5 -> 0.421 / 6 / 0, rough 1.0 -> 0.374 / 6 / 0, i.e. the gap vanishes exactly where the GGX denominator becomes 1. The refinement is the same estimate+NR shape rsqrt8_nr already used for the OuterVec normal decode, whose comment names this identical failure. NOTE FOR ANYONE PINNING: --deferred_vec is default OFF on arm64, so on Apple silicon this flag is INERT at defaults and the byte gates cannot see it; it changes the x86 image, which is the point. 0 = the bare estimates, for the A/B.")

// ─── startup phase timeline (init concurrency diagnostics) ──────────────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (init_timeline,          "FDS_INIT_TIMELINE",            0,                              "prof",     "STARTUP PHASE TIMELINE, default OFF, byte-null (stderr only). Prints one `[INIT-T] +<ms since process start> (dt <ms>) [tid] <label>` line at each init milestone: the demo director's Initialize_* chain on thread t1, Run_Glato, the t1.join, and inside Initialize_Greets the individual phases (FLD load, Preprocess, the --greets_displace DisplaceStoneSubdiv bake, the crease/re-smooth passes, the shadow proxy, chunking, mirror clone build, the cube shadow bake, the env-reflection probe bake, the lightmap-bake thread spawn). WHY IT EXISTS: `Initialize_Greets` runs on the t1 worker so it OVERLAPS Run_Glato, and the demo only stalls if it outlasts the intro — a stall is therefore a DURATION question, not a threading question, and the two are indistinguishable without per-phase timestamps. Under `--greets_displace` the greets init is several seconds longer than flat and the timeline says which phase owns it. Timestamps are steady_clock relative to the first mark; `dt` is since the previous mark ON ANY THREAD, so read the absolute column when marks interleave.")

// ─── static lightmap: size the per-face atlas by AREA, not by face count ────
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(shadow_lightmap_texel_density, "FDS_SHADOW_LIGHTMAP_TEXEL_DENSITY", 0.0f,             "shadows",  "Per-MESH static-lightmap atlas resolution derived from MEAN FACE AREA, in texels per WORLD UNIT. DEFAULT 0 = OFF = every mesh gets the flat --shadow_lightmap_res, which is the historical behaviour and BYTE-NULL. THE DEFECT IT FIXES: StaticShadowLightmap::data is `numFaces * res² * numOmnis` BYTES, allocated AND TOUCHED (allocate() fills 255) per mesh, so with a FIXED per-face res the store scales with FACE COUNT and not with SURFACE AREA. greets sets res=128 (GREETS.CPP GreetsApplyInitDefaults) — correct for its authored wall quads, catastrophic the moment those quads are tessellated. MEASURED at greets t=5743, /usr/bin/time -l peak footprint: 6.93 GB flat (33 396 baked faces) vs 21.41 GB under --greets_displace (115 346 faces) on a 64 GB machine, with the bake itself going 1.2 s -> 7.1-11.7 s and max RSS 14.0 GB (i.e. macOS was already compressing/evicting). The displaced cells are ~1/300 the AREA of the quad they replace, so every one of them was carrying ~300x the shadow texels per world unit that the FLAT wall ships with — pure waste, and it is paid twice: once as bake time on the startup path and once as a 20 GB array the deferred kernel then samples per pixel. WITH THE FLAG ON: res = clamp(ceil(sqrt(meanFaceArea) * density), 8, shadow_lightmap_res) per mesh. Because it is CAPPED at shadow_lightmap_res the flag can only ever REDUCE an atlas, never sharpen one, and because the unit is world-relative a tessellated surface keeps the SAME shadow resolution per world unit as the untessellated one it replaced. The floor of 8 keeps bilinear (sampleBilinear needs res >= 2) and a usable gradient. The runtime sampler was already per-mesh (StaticShadowLightmap::lmRes is a member, read by sampleBilinear / sampleBilinearPlanar / sampleNearest), so nothing downstream needed changing. WHO TURNS IT ON: greets, and only under --greets_displace, alongside --greets_shadow_proxy and --greets_displace_flat_mirror in the same companion-default block — so the BYTE-PINNED flat path never takes the branch at all and the greets pin cannot move. The [LM] summary prints the resulting atlas size in GB on every run so a regression here is visible without being asked for.")

// ─── env probe CAPTURE POINT: area-weighted, over the whole instance ────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (env_probe_center,       "FDS_ENV_PROBE_CENTER",         0,                              "parallax", "Derive an env-reflection probe's CAPTURE POINT from the reflective footprint's AREA, not from its VERTEX COUNT, and over the WHOLE instance rather than one greedy cluster of it. THE DEFECT (user: 'I can't see the mech in the up-looking dynamic bake, even when the mech is directly above the stairs - I think the camera is offset to one of the stairs' side'), MEASURED on greets: materialCentroid (EnvBake.cpp) averages the world POSITION OF EVERY VERTEX of every face using the material, so the point is dragged wherever the mesh happens to be finely tessellated; then, for a multi-instance surface, it greedy-clusters at an 8-world-unit radius and re-centroids on the HEAVIEST cluster. The greets 'stairs' surface is ONE pair of flights 9.5 u long, and 9.5 > 8, so the clustering SPLINTERS each flight into a top cluster (n=22) and a bottom cluster (n=8) -- the function's own comment already admits 'the greedy clustering splinters a single statue into several' -- and 'heaviest' then parks the probe on the TOP LANDING END: bake point (45.4, 2.3, -54.9) against an owner-faces AABB of [35.9,0.0,-70.9]..[49.1,3.8,-54.8], i.e. ON the z-extreme face of its own footprint (-54.9 vs a -54.8 boundary), 7.95 u from the footprint centre on a 16.2 u extent. The mech ends its walk at (44.4, 4.7, -62.2), directly over that footprint centre; from the AUTHORED probe point its direction is (-1.0, +2.4, -7.3), i.e. 72 degrees off vertical, so it lands in the -Z cube face and the +Y (up-looking) face never sees it. From the footprint centre the same mech is 36 degrees off vertical -- inside +Y. Nothing is wrong with the draw set: the overlay composites 1754 mech texels into this probe every frame, just into the wrong faces. ON = two changes, both general (every probe, every scene): (1) AREA weighting -- each face contributes its own centroid weighted by its world area, so the point is tessellation-INDEPENDENT; (2) INSTANCE-GROUP UNION -- after the same greedy clustering (unchanged, so instance DETECTION is untouched), the heaviest cluster is unioned transitively with every cluster within the 2x cluster radius the self-exclusion logic ALREADY calls 'fragments of the probed instance', and the capture point is the area centroid of that union. Genuinely separate instances (the two greets mummies, 24 u apart) stay separate and still get the heaviest-instance probe. Composes with the per-surface authored override (Material::EnvBakeOfs, 'probe offset' in the editor), which is added on top of whichever derivation is active. NOT read by imported/registered stores (CITY's per-building cube faces carry their own bake point).")

// ─── the LIVE (post-overlay) probe dump ────────────────────────────────────
FDS_FLAG_INT  (env_dyn_dump,           "FDS_ENV_DYN_DUMP",             0,                              "parallax", "DIAGNOSTIC, default 0 = off, byte-null: after --env_dynamic's overlay composites the movers into probe N (1-BASED store index, the same numbering --env_map_probe uses), write that store's LIVE mip-0 cube to /tmp/envdyn_<material>.ppm as the standard 3x2 atlas (+X -X +Y / -Y +Z -Z -- the FDS_ENVBAKE_DUMP / --dump_env_cube cell order) and print a per-face census. FDS_ENVBAKE_DUMP can only show the STATIC capture, which by construction has no mover in it; this is the only way to see what the overlay actually produced, which is what 'the mech is not in the up-looking bake' is a claim about. Writes a file per overlay of that probe (the path is fixed, so the last one survives) and touches no rendered pixel.")

// ─── probes that FOLLOW their owner when it moves ───────────────────────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (env_probe_follow_owner, "FDS_ENV_PROBE_FOLLOW_OWNER",   0,                              "parallax", "Re-derive and re-bake an env-reflection probe when its OWNER SURFACE MOVES. MEASUREMENT/FIX CANDIDATE, default OFF = byte-null (the tracking is not even evaluated). THE DEFECT (user: 'env dynamic bakes don't take into account that the object itself can move - the static meshes need to be re-baked, where the dynamic not (?)'). Half right, and the half that is wrong is the expensive one. A probe's capture point is derived from its owner's geometry ONCE, at first sight, and EnvReflection_FramePrep's `if (env.byMat.count(M)) continue;` then never revisits that material - so (a) the STATIC cube keeps the room as seen from where the owner USED to be, and (b) the DYNAMIC overlay is NOT exempt: overlayComposite renders the movers from S.view.bake*, the same frozen static bake point, so it faithfully re-renders the mech every frame into a cube captured from the wrong place. MEASURED ON SHIPPING GREETS, and the witness is a probe on the mech itself: 'Hull.lwo::cockpit_upper' bakes at the mech's pose on the first frame and stays there while the mech walks the length of the room - at t=7100 the mech's hull is at (43.1, 4.5, -62.1) and its own canopy probe is still capturing from (-8.0, 2.1, -53.0), 51.8 world units away, in a different part of the scene. TWO-STAGE DIRTY TEST so a still scene pays nothing: stage 1 is an FNV signature over the owner meshes' RotMat+IPos (12 floats each, a handful of meshes) and short-circuits every frame nothing moved; only when that changes does stage 2 re-run materialCentroid + the authored EnvBakeOfs and compare against the point the live store actually holds, requesting a re-bake only past env_probe_follow_eps world units of DRIFT (the thing being throttled is a full cube bake). EDITOR MOVES ARE IMMEDIATE AND UNCAPPED, RUNTIME MOVERS ARE THROTTLED, and the discriminator is the engine's existing WorldAabb_MeshIsDynamic - the same predicate the overlay uses to decide what a mover is. An owner no spline animates can only have been moved by a person, once. A spline-animated owner moves every frame and is capped at env_probe_follow_budget re-bakes per frame, ordered by the SAME priority --env_dyn_sched=1 uses (tier 0 = never followed, then visible by ownerScreenAreaFrac x wait, then off-screen by wait) so the file carries ONE notion of which probe matters most, not two. The re-bake itself is the editor's own targeted drop (EnvReflection_InvalidateSurface): the whole 4-unit sharing group leaves byMat and FramePrep re-derives + re-bakes it from the current point, so the authored probe offset, the per-surface bake res and the ENVDYN static retention all come back through their normal paths. Shipping scenes are unaffected with it OFF; with it ON only a scene that HAS a moving probe owner can move at all. ONE THING THIS FLAG ALSO SWITCHES, and it has to: a followed probe keeps the MOVERS OUT of its own static capture, overriding --env_bake_include_animated for that bake alone. That flag argues the mech belongs in the mech's own canopy probe because 'the hull/barrels/legs do not move relative to it' - true of the POSE, false of the DISTANCE once the capture point is glued to the canopy's own area centroid. From a point ON the owner its own limbs are near-plane-clipped slabs filling every cube face, re-burnt at every re-bake (the user: '--env_probe_follow_owner fucks up the env bake for the mech - random polys rendered'). MEASURED at greets t=7100, followed point (44.3 5.0 -62.2): movers in = six faces of slabs, movers out = six faces of clean room; and DETERMINISTIC 24/24 on the live cube md5, so it was self-geometry and not a torn-vertex race. The movers are not lost - --env_dynamic's overlay draws them live at the CURRENT pose (2338 texels over 6 faces at that same frame).")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (env_probe_follow_budget,"FDS_ENV_PROBE_FOLLOW_BUDGET",  1,                              "parallax", "--env_probe_follow_owner: max SPLINE-ANIMATED probe owners whose store is re-baked per frame (editor moves are never capped - a person is waiting). One store re-bake is one full cube bake at that probe's face res, which is orders of magnitude more than one --env_dynamic overlay, so this is the knob that decides what the feature costs. 0 disables following for animated owners entirely while leaving editor moves live. Probes over the budget stay dirty and are re-ranked next frame by the shared --env_dyn_sched=1 priority, so an off-screen animated owner cannot starve the one filling the screen.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(env_probe_follow_eps,   "FDS_ENV_PROBE_FOLLOW_EPS",     1.0f,                           "parallax", "--env_probe_follow_owner: minimum CAPTURE-POINT DRIFT, in world units, before a moved owner's probe is re-baked. The dirty test is not 'the transform changed' but 'the point the bake would now use is more than this far from the point the live store actually holds' - a rotating owner whose area centroid barely moves is not worth a cube bake, and a mesh that jitters sub-unit would otherwise re-bake for ever. Raise it to trade reflection accuracy for bakes; 0 re-bakes on any measurable drift.")

// ─── --mem_census: what is resident, and WHICH VARIABLE is it a product of ──
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (mem_census,             "FDS_MEM_CENSUS",               0,                              "prof",     "DIAGNOSTIC, default OFF, byte-null (reads sizes, writes stderr, allocates nothing): walk every large allocation the engine holds and print one [MEM] line each — resident bytes, whether every byte is TOUCHED, and THE FORMULA IT SCALES WITH. That last column is the point. Two memory-size defects were found by hand in August 2026 and neither was visible in a profile: 943d644, the static shadow lightmap at `numFaces x lmRes^2 x numOmnis` BYTES fully touched at init — 19.4 GB displaced / 5.6 GB shipping, because the size scaled with FACE COUNT and a flat 128^2 per face instead of with SURFACE AREA, ~300x oversized for what it bought; and af1f8f8, one logical shadow texel (depth+polyId) scattered across four parallel u16 planes 512 KB apart, 8 cache lines per tap, worth -1.0 ms/frame once packed. A byte total alone would have caught neither quickly — `0.5 GB` is not actionable, `0.5 GB BECAUSE it scales with face count` is. The report fires ONCE at the end of scene tick --mem_census_frame (default 1: after the first full frame, by which point init allocations AND the lazy first-frame bakes — env panoramas, the SH probe, mip first-touch — have all happened); ask for a later frame to catch anything that grows over time. It also prints the process phys_footprint and the UNCENSUSED RESIDUAL (footprint minus the sum of what it walked), so a subsystem nobody has taught it about shows up as a gap rather than as silence. Covered today: the three G-buffers + their optional planes and the xpar Z/peel planes, the cube+2D shadow maps and their swizzle copies, the HDR buffer and the whole post chain (bloom/anamorphic/ghost/DoF/CA scratch), the glass-refraction snapshots, the static shadow lightmap atlas, the env-reflection panorama mip chains, the framebuffer + Z-buffer, per-thread clipper/tile scratch, scene geometry (verts/faces, and the mirror + shadow-proxy CLONES separately), and texture pixel data + mip chains. Cost when off: one relaxed bool load per tick. ADDING A SUBSYSTEM IS ONE FUNCTION PLUS ONE LINE — see the header comment in FDS/Base/MemCensus.h.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (mem_census_frame,       "FDS_MEM_CENSUS_FRAME",         1,                              "prof",     "Which scene TICK --mem_census reports at (1-based, default 1 = the end of the first full frame). Only meaningful with --mem_census. Use a larger value to catch buffers that GROW after startup — anything lazily sized by content rather than by resolution (per-tile face lists, the offscreen mirror RTT slots, an env probe refreshed on a later frame). A run that never reaches the requested tick prints nothing at all, which is itself the answer to `does this scene get that far`.")

// ─── --hw_prof: WHY a phase is slow, not just how long it took ──────────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (hw_prof,                "FDS_HW_PROF",                  0,                              "prof",     "DIAGNOSTIC, default OFF, byte-null (reads counters, writes stderr, allocates nothing): add HARDWARE COUNTERS and os_signpost intervals to the phase boundaries --deferred_prof already brackets. Use it WITH --deferred_prof=N; alone it does nothing, because it adds columns to that table rather than a table of its own — so every phase name, the main/offscreen split and the warmup exclusion are shared by construction and can never drift apart. Three new columns per phase: Ginstr/f (retired instructions), Gcyc/f (core cycles), and IPC = their ratio. WHAT THE NUMBERS ARE: both counters come from proc_pid_rusage(RUSAGE_INFO_V4), which is the kernel's per-task PMU accounting — real hardware counters, not a model — and they are TASK-WIDE, summed over every thread, so a parallel wave's counters cover all 12 workers exactly like thrsum does and unlike wall. They are also LIVE for threads still on-core (verified: a mid-flight read of a task with 4 spinning threads returns ~4 core-seconds per wall-second), which is what makes bracketing a wave work at all. IPC IS THE COLUMN TO COMPARE, not the ms ones: a descheduled worker burns wall time but retires no instructions and no cycles, so on a loaded machine (other agents benching concurrently) IPC stays put while every ms column inflates. Calibrated on this M2 Max by docs/HW_PROFILING.md's ladder: IPC ~2.4 = compute-bound, ~1.0 = L1-latency-bound, ~0.14 = L2, <0.05 = DRAM-latency-bound. WHAT IT CANNOT DO: cache-miss counters are unreachable without root on Apple silicon — kperf.framework is present and kpc.pc_capture_supported is 1, but writing kpc.counting returns EPERM — and Instruments/xctrace needs Xcode.app, which is not installed (Command Line Tools only). IPC is the substitute for a miss rate, and it is a ratio of two genuine counters rather than an estimate, but it conflates cache stalls with every other stall and with instruction mix: it adjudicates a BEFORE/AFTER on the same kernel cleanly (fewer bytes touched, same instructions, cycles fall => IPC rises) and should not be read as an absolute memory-boundedness score across different code. Phases booked through mark()/addMs() have no counter stamp to difference against and print '-' rather than a wrong number. The os_signpost intervals go to subsystem com.revival.fds, category PointsOfInterest, all sharing the literal name 'phase' with the real phase name as the message (os_signpost requires a compile-time-literal interval name and every phase name here is a runtime pointer); Instruments would put them on its Points of Interest track, and headless `log show --signpost --predicate 'subsystem == \"com.revival.fds\"'` reads the same records with per-thread TIDs. COST when on, measured with 12 threads spinning: 808 ns per proc_pid_rusage and ~40 phase boundaries x2 reads = 0.065 ms/frame, ~0.07%% of a 90 ms greets frame; signposts are skipped entirely unless a tracing tool is attached (os_signpost_enabled). Cost when OFF: one cached bool, and not a single counter read. See docs/HW_PROFILING.md for the recipes (per-stage counters, allocation audit, before/after diff) and for what each tool on this box can and cannot do. 2026-08-12: Xcode.app IS now installed, so xctrace/Instruments are REACHABLE and give per-symbol attribution this flag cannot — but xcode-select still points at CommandLineTools, so you must prefix DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer. Use xctrace to find WHICH function, then this flag to find how many instructions it retires; instruction counts are deterministic and survive machine load, wall times do not.")

// ─── --greets_displace_junction_census: WHY a wall junction seals ───────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (greets_displace_junction_census, "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_JUNCTION_CENSUS", 0, "greets", "DIAGNOSTIC, default OFF, byte-null (walks the ORIGINAL mesh at bake time and writes stderr; allocates nothing that survives the call). Answers the 2026-08-11 question 'why does the mortar groove open a visible gap where two wall faces meet at a shallow angle and seal where they meet at a sharp one'. The displacement bake's border classifier is PURELY TOPOLOGICAL - an edge used by exactly ONE target face is an authored patch border and both its endpoints pin to EXACTLY zero displacement (crack safety) - so it cannot see an angle at all. This census measures the bridge: for every edge of the target material it reports whether the edge is index-INTERIOR (two target faces share the vertex INDICES - displaces freely), a SPLIT-VERTEX seam (two target faces meet at the same POSITION but with distinct indices, so BOTH sides classify as single-use borders and BOTH pin), or a genuinely OPEN border (no target face on the other side). Each population is histogrammed by the DIHEDRAL ANGLE between the two faces' normals, which is what turns the topological rule into the angle rule the eye sees. Prints [STONE-JUNC] lines per material.")

// ─── mirror-shard reflection: the cone cull — 0 off / 1 legacy per-vertex / 2 per-FACE ──
FDS_FLAG_INT  (shard_cone_cull,        "FDS_SHARD_CONE_CULL",          0,                              "greets",   "THE MIRROR-SHARD REFLECTION BAKE'S CONE CULL. Three-valued since 2026-08-12 (it was a bool for one day); DEFAULT 0 = NO cone cull, which is the correct and the shipping behaviour. Each shard bakes the room through its own off-axis window, and this decides what that bake may skip. 1 = the LEGACY PER-VERTEX test (Transform.cpp, g_reflVertCull). WRONG BY CONSTRUCTION, kept only as an A/B lever: it decides FACE visibility from VERTEX positions, stamping each out-of-cone vertex TPos=(0,0,1)/PX=PY=-1 with every frustum-out bit set, so a wall quad whose INTERIOR covered the entire shard view had all three corners rejected and VANISHED, and a quad with only some corners rejected rasterized THROUGH those fake positions. Measured at the shatter screen (t=3122, square-on, 1258x767 panel window): 24.74 luma against the 73.86 the MAIN deferred pass renders from the same reflected eye, 86.37 with no cull -- two thirds of the reflection MISSING, not dim (ddb1d15). 2 = THE PER-FACE TEST this flag was three-valued for: reject a face iff its own world BOUNDING SPHERE lies entirely outside the cone. Conservative by construction in exactly the two ways 1 is not -- a face that reaches the cone survives WHOLE, and a face that survives is rendered with its vertices UNTOUCHED -- and MEASURED BYTE-IDENTICAL to 0: the 1024x1024 reflection atlas at the bracket is 0 of 1 048 576 pixels different, with 84.3%% of face tests rejected and the face list going 9 921 -> 8 678 entries. IT NEEDS A LAB BUILD (cmake -DFDS_SHARD_BAKE_LAB=ON) and in the shipping build it prints a note and behaves as 0. TWO reasons, both measured, both recorded in the top-level CMakeLists.txt: (a) carrying its call inside Transform_Objects' per-mesh body moves ALL THREE scene pins through -ffp-contract=fast alone, on frames that never shatter a mirror (greets 778fa6ac->7a6370a1, fountain 8db68ccb->eebf68e6, city 3cbe42b1->80583b85; bisected -- the branch it adds to the face loop is byte-null, the CALL is not, at either call site tried); and (b) IT IS NOT WORTH A RE-PIN, because the cost it was built to remove is not in the geometry. WHAT THE BAKE ACTUALLY COSTS, [SHARD-PHASE] core-ms summed over 12 workers, min-of-6 interleaved at the t=3122 bracket: Transform_Objects 6.5, G-buffer fill 27.4, RENDER_DEFERREDLIGHTING 124.4, cones 0.2 -- the geometry front-end is 4%% of the pass and the deferred shading of the reflection's own pixels is 78%%. Wall, same runs: 14.1 ms with no cull, 14.7 with the per-face cull, 6.8 with the broken per-vertex one, i.e. the per-face cull is a wash inside the +-1.5 ms spread this box has at load 10-22, and mode 1's speed was never culling, it was not drawing. ddb1d15 recorded 'the right accelerator is a per-FACE test'; the test is right and it is built, and it recovers none of the 4.0 -> 14.5 ms, because that 10.5 ms is the reflection appearing. The CLI form needs a value (--shard_cone_cull=0|1|2); the env form FDS_SHARD_CONE_CULL=0|1|2 is unchanged, and --no-shard_cone_cull no longer parses. This also corrects 983cdb4's recorded 20.4 ms deferred / 188.3 ms forward shard-bake costs, both timed with mode 1 eating the geometry.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (greets_displace_seam_weld, "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_SEAM_WELD", 0, "greets", "SPLIT-SEAM WELD for the stone displacement bake — the CONSISTENCY fix for 'the mortar groove opens a visible gap between stones where two wall faces meet at a shallow angle, and seals where they meet at a sharp one' (user, 2026-08-11: 'it flips where the angle between the walls jumps to > 90 degrees'). The bake's border rule is purely TOPOLOGICAL: an edge used by exactly ONE target face is an authored patch border and every subdivision vertex along it pins to EXACTLY zero displacement, so no groove can cut through it. Whether a junction between two faces of the SAME material presents as one shared edge or as two single-use ones is a property of the AUTHORED MESH, and --greets_displace_junction_census measures that in greets' stone it correlates exactly with the dihedral: all 211 welded interior edges of 'rooms' run 0.00-90.00 deg, all 12 split-vertex seams sit at 91.10 deg, nothing in between. This flag removes the inconsistency at its source — BEFORE any topology is derived, position-coincident ORIGINAL vertices used only by TARGET faces are merged onto one canonical vertex with the mean normal, so the junction becomes index-INTERIOR and takes the same path the shallow junctions already take. No new crack class: the two sides now share one vertex and cannot separate. The hard shading crease survives, because MakeFacesIndependentByAngle (30 deg) runs after the bake and the 80 deg re-smooth keeps hard creases and material borders. Cross-material seams are untouched (verts incident to any non-target face are excluded and keep --greets_displace_neighbor_pin).")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (greets_displace_border_pin, "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_BORDER_PIN", 1, "greets", "A/B KNOB for the AUTHORED-PATCH-BORDER half of the stone displacement bake's zero-pin (default 1 = today's behaviour, byte-null). An edge used by exactly ONE target face is an authored patch border, and every subdivision vertex created along it is pinned to EXACTLY zero displacement so the un-subdivided neighbour on the other side cannot open a T-junction crack. That rule is also what SEALS a wall junction: no mortar groove can cut through a line held at zero, which is why the same groove that opens a visible gap between stones on a shallow junction dies at a sharp one (see --greets_displace_junction_census and --greets_displace_seam_weld). --no-greets_displace_border_pin drops the rule so the border displaces with the rest of the patch — it PRICES the crack safety, it is not a fix: the cross-material neighbour pin (--greets_displace_neighbor_pin) still runs, but genuinely open borders and T-junctions against unsplit neighbours are exposed.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (greets_displace_free_edge, "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_FREE_EDGE", 0, "greets", "FREE-SILHOUETTE-EDGE RELIEF for the stone displacement bake — the fix for 'the gap between stones shows on some wall junctions and not on the corner I am looking at' (user, 2026-08-11). The bake pins every subdivision vertex created along an AUTHORED PATCH BORDER (an edge used by exactly ONE target face) to zero displacement, so the relief ramps to nothing and the junction reads as a smooth sealed edge. That pin exists to stop a T-junction opening against a neighbour that is not subdivided the same way — an argument that needs a neighbour. A single-use target edge with NOTHING on its far side (no coincident non-displaced edge, no position-coincident target edge) is a FREE SILHOUETTE edge: a doorway jamb, an arch reveal, the open end of a wall patch. There is no second surface for it to crack against, so pinning it buys no safety and costs the relief exactly where the eye reads it - on the silhouette. This flag lets those edges displace with the rest of the patch while every edge that DOES have a far side keeps the pin. TWO CHANGES ON 2026-08-12 (user: 'it makes most of the sites better, but for the specific pose I sent you, it adds a bulge similar to the gpu one - which is less than optimal'). (1) NO SLIDE ALONG THE BORDER - this is the one that fixes the reported defect, and the diagnosis is measured, not argued. The displacement rides the SMOOTHED vertex normal, which at a patch border is averaged with whatever else the authored mesh joins there: at the greets doorway jamb the freed verts on the wall plane x=17.898 carry N ~ (+0.894,+0.419,-0.155), 26.5 deg out of their own plane, and the tilt is mostly DOWN THE EDGE (the border runs in y). So even a pure recess of -0.10 slid the vertex ~0.045 world units ALONG its own border line - that slide is what swelled the doorway reveal and raised the tab at the lintel. A freed vertex now moves only ACROSS its border line, never along it, so the border stays where it was authored and only its profile changes; worst case measured, 1579 of 1594 'rooms' verts were sliding, one of them with |cos| 0.968 against its own edge (i.e. 97% pure slide). Removing the WHOLE tangential component instead (riding the face plane normal) was tried and REJECTED on measurement: it tears the border off neighbours that share its position without sharing an edge - 1408 background pixels at t=5967 against 46. (2) RECESS-ONLY: the displacement is zero-mean, d = amp*(h - mipMean), so freeing a border let it swing BOTH ways and a stone PLATEAU (h > mean) could push the border OUTWARD, the same bulge GpuBench had before --tess_border_ramp. A freed border now keeps the pin's CEILING (d <= 0) and drops only its floor. HONEST SIZE: this half is small here - 134 of 1594 'rooms' verts and 204 of 698 'floor' verts were positive, and at t=5967 the sign clamp alone left every row above 687 byte-identical, i.e. it did not touch the jamb at all. It is kept because it cannot cost anything (a freed vertex can never add silhouette the pinned arm did not have) and it is what the border pin guaranteed. Reports [STONE-FREE] with the freed/clamped/de-slid counts and the deepest carve. Measure the populations with --greets_displace_junction_census; price the whole pin with --no-greets_displace_border_pin. CHANGED 2026-08-13 (user, on the full arm at t=5970: 'the shit part is that the displacement doesn't work at the edge, and also we see a texture discontinuity near the edge'; and the t=1088 wall-corner slit): (3) GEOMETRIC ABUTTAL VETO - 'nothing on the far side' was tested by VERTEX COINCIDENCE only, and the t=1088 corner refuted it: two walls that meet geometrically but never share vertex positions (different segmenting along the corner) both classified OPEN, both freed, both recessed - a full-height slit. A candidate free edge now probes a scene-wide face soup at 5 interior samples; any foreign face within 0.05 world units means there IS a far side and the edge stays pinned. Only narrows the freed set. (4) FULL FIELD AT FREED BORDERS under --greets_displace_border_mean: the straight-line level constant no longer overrides freed borders - they carry amp*(h-mean) with BOTH signs, so stones stand proud, grooves notch through, and the border crenellates with the relief instead of dying into a flat band (the flat band and its transition line were the named defects). The level constant still sets PINNED borders.")

// ─── pbrtest: a synthetic STUDIO DOME env probe, so a conductor can be judged ──
FDS_FLAG_INT  (pbrtest_studio,         "FDS_PBRTEST_STUDIO",           0,                              "parallax", "PBRTEST ONLY: replace the scene-baked env probes with a SYNTHETIC STUDIO DOME, so a metal can be looked at against a known environment instead of against the checkerboard room. 0 (default) = off, byte-null (nothing is registered, FramePrep bakes the room probes exactly as before). 1 = the calibrated studio dome. WHY IT EXISTS: a conductor has essentially no appearance of its own -- metalness kills diffuse, so the surface IS its reflection -- and pbrtest's room is a black-and-white CHECKERBOARD lit to a 6-face mean radiance of 99.3 with its UP face at 52.0, i.e. dim, top-dark and maximum-variance. That is the worst possible environment to judge a metal in: the same material that reads as smooth bright silver under a studio dome reads as dark charcoal with salt-and-pepper speckle there (MEASURED: local contrast inside the metal 13.6% of mean, against 2.6% for the same material in a vendor studio render; --no-nmap moves that 13.61 -> 13.58, so the speckle is the PROBE CONTENT, not the normal map). THE DOME: an analytic sky, evaluated per cube texel and registered through EnvReflection_RegisterCubeFaces as an imported store (so FramePrep skips the room bake for those materials, and the store is marked noParallax -- a dome is at infinity). Radiance is LINEAR 0-255, matching the store's contract. Ground 0.03, horizon 0.10, zenith 0.55, smoothstepped over d.y in [-0.25, 1.0], plus one broad soft key lobe pow(max(dot(d,K),0),8)*1.6 about K = normalize(0.50, 0.80, 0.33) -- upper-front-right, the direction the reference's own specular hot spot puts it. All six faces come from one closed-form function of direction, so the store is exactly reproducible from these numbers and carries no scene state: that is what makes it usable as a RENDERING TEST fixture (tests/pbr_import_test.cpp + the pbr_studio_render CTest). Registered for every material in the scene that qualifies for a probe (Reflection > 0 or a metalness map); the first one allocates the store and the rest alias it, so the dome costs one store however many surfaces use it.")

// ─── --env_dynamic: the overlay is the SOLE source of movers in a probe ─────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (env_dyn_static_exclude, "FDS_ENV_DYN_STATIC_EXCLUDE",   1,                              "parallax", "A/B KNOB, default 1 = the fix. With --env_dynamic live, keep MOVING meshes OUT of the STATIC capture of every probe the overlay composites onto, so each mover appears in a probe EXACTLY ONCE. Without it a flagged probe holds the mech TWICE: once frozen into the static master at bake time (--env_bake_include_animated, default ON, lets the movers into ordinary bakes) and once drawn live by the overlay every frame. That is not a cosmetic duplicate — overlayComposite resolves the two by DEPTH (`win = rendered && mZ >= sZ`), and the frozen copy contributes its own depth to sZ, so wherever the ghost is nearer the probe than the live mech it WINS and the live mech is thrown away behind it. The reported symptom: 'only some mech parts are actually changing the height - I see some mech parts changing height, while some don't' — the parts that do not are the frozen copy showing through. MEASURED, greets 'stairs' probe, 60 overlays over t=7000..7060 on `--greets-displace --scene-greets --env_probe_center --env-dynamic`: the live mech rasterises 688,339 texels in BOTH arms (same live population — the control), of which 54,104 lose the depth test with the duplicate in and 5,899 with it out, i.e. 48,205 texels of live mech were hidden behind its own ghost. The frozen copy is also the WORSE of the two: greets' flagged probes sit within a mech-radius of the walk, so it bakes as near-plane-clipped slabs (see the 'stairs' static capture). Companion, not a replacement, for --env_probe_follow_owner's own narrower rule (a probe that RIDES a mover keeps the movers out because from a capture point ON the owner they are slabs) — that one holds even with the overlay off. BYTE-NULL where it cannot bite: ANDed with the store's own EnvDynamic retention flag AND with --env_dynamic, which is compile-default OFF and only greets turns on, so city/fountain/chase bakes are untouched. Verify with [ENVDYN-CENSUS], which counts the mover meshes each static capture kept and names them.")

// ─── --greets_displace_plane_normal: ride the PATCH PLANE, not the average ────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (greets_displace_plane_normal, "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_PLANE_NORMAL", 0, "greets", "PLANE-NORMAL RIDE for the stone displacement bake — the round-3 doorway-jamb fix, default OFF. The bake moves each vertex by amp*(h-mean) along its SMOOTHED VERTEX NORMAL, and on greets 'rooms' that normal is nothing like the wall it belongs to: MEASURED on the flat jamb plane x=17.898, the ride direction sits a MEAN 11.25 deg (max 78 deg) out of the wall's own plane, because the authored normals are averaged across the room's corners. Splitting that tilt against the border line shows what round 2's de-slide actually did: it removed the ALONG-border component (|Ry| 0.043 mean, residual) and left the ACROSS-border one (|Rz| 0.144 mean, max 0.888) — 3.3x larger, and the ONLY one that can move a silhouette sideways. So freed border verts still travel up to 0.065 world u along the wall, in the plane, perpendicular to their own border: the jamb line stops being a line and the edge reads as a swollen, wandering silhouette. This flag rides the vertex's OWN PATCH PLANE normal instead (area-agnostic mean of its incident target faces' base winding normals, oriented to the vertex normal), which zeroes BOTH in-plane components at once by construction — a vertex can then only move perpendicular to its wall, never along it, so an authored straight border stays straight and only its depth profile changes. Applied ONLY where it is provably safe: the vertex's incident target faces must be COPLANAR within 2 deg, and the vertex must have NO POSITION TWIN (no second index at the same base position, and not coincident with non-displaced geometry). That second guard is the round-2 tear case, kept as a measurement: giving a position-sharing border its own plane normal pulls it off its twin and opened 1408 background px at t=5967 against 46. Corner verts and twins keep the smoothed normal they had. Composes with --greets_displace_free_edge (it supersedes that arm's de-slide, which becomes a no-op on every vert this flag claims) and with the recess-only clamp. Pinned borders are untouched — they do not displace at all.")

// ─── --greets_displace_block_level: mitre level per stone BLOCK, not per line ──
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (greets_displace_block_level, "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_BLOCK_LEVEL", 0, "greets", "PER-BLOCK MITRE LEVEL for the stone displacement bake (2026-08-17 jamb-cushion fix), default OFF and byte-null without --greets_displace_mitre; --greets_displace defaults it ON. The matched mitre weld's corner level was ONE median stone-top per corner line; each block's own top differs from that median by the stone-band wobble (~ +/-0.05 h), so every block either overhung the corner or showed a raised lip and the faces rolled into it — the t=5970/5975 jamb cushions ([STONE-BOW]/xsec, 2026-08-16y; minimal reproducing pair free_edge+mitre). This makes the level PIECEWISE CONSTANT: one median of the local upper envelope per notch-bounded run (a stone block), steps landing inside the groove cuts where masonry has joints, notch samples cutting relative to their nearest block's course. Straight per block (no grazing S-bow — the constant-per-line fix's own acceptance is kept within each run), flush per block (no per-block overhang/lip). Turn OFF to A/B the 2026-08-15 one-level-per-line weld.")

// ─── --greets_displace_front_orient: majority-front mitre bisector ───────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (greets_displace_front_orient, "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_FRONT_ORIENT", 1, "greets", "MAJORITY-FRONT MITRE BISECTOR (2026-08-28, the month's wall-seam root cause). The mitre used to orient BOTH wall normals by the vert's SMOOTHED normal, and PREPROC's Compute_Vertex_Normals is an UNGATED average — at corners it is polluted (pier seam measured: smN=(0.909,+0.391,-0.145), lid-averaged), the orientation dot goes negative (-0.343), the partner wall FLIPS and the bisector lands 90.0 deg from the true outward mitre: the seam column displaces IN-PLANE, sideways along the wall (applied vectors measured 1.7 deg from the predicted WRONG bisector; interiors a perfect 0.0). His works/broken angle split is that test's safety margin: the 207-deg jamb dots are +0.93/+0.97 (never flips), sharp reflex + pollution crosses zero. THIS FLAG rebuilds each mitre group's bisector eye- and smN-independently: wall AXES from the incident faces' winding crosses (sign-canonicalised), each wall's FRONT sign from the area-weighted MAJORITY of its faces' stored authored Face::N (per-face ~17%% winding-flipped, measured 220:46/414:83 at the pier walls — the area majority is unambiguous). Side-population clustering also goes |dot| (sign-insensitive). --no- restores the smN-oriented path byte-exactly.")
// ─── --greets_displace_geom_bisector: mitre bisector from FACE planes ─────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (greets_displace_geom_bisector, "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_GEOM_BISECTOR", 0, "greets", "GEOMETRIC MITRE BISECTOR for the stone displacement bake (2026-08-17 jamb-cushion fix, part 2), default OFF and byte-null without --greets_displace_mitre; --greets_displace defaults it ON. The mitre weld's ride direction was the average of per-index FAN normals, and at a corner whose indices are shared between the walls the fan STRADDLES both sheets: measured at the t=5970 jamb, one population averaged (+0.447,0,+0.894) — one +x face plus two +z faces — giving a bisector whose projections onto the two GEOMETRIC wall planes are 0.85 vs 0.53, so one wall received 62%% of every cut and level: its faces overhung their own arris and the reveal smeared (the cushions). This recovers the two wall planes from the incident FACE geometry (cluster by plane normal, >30 deg = distinct wall), bisects those, and takes cosHalf against them — both sheets then see full-depth cuts by construction. Turn OFF to A/B the fan-average bisector.")

// ─── --greets_displace_profile_agree: ONE value per s on a mitre line ─────────
FDS_FLAG_INT  (greets_displace_profile_agree, "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_PROFILE_AGREE", 0, "greets", "PAIRED-SAMPLE AGREEMENT COLLAPSE for the mitre weld's shared profile (2026-08-17, the t=5968 pier arris). A/B INSTRUMENT, default 0 = OFF and byte-null — deliberately NOT defaulted on by the --greets_displace umbrella, because neither mode survived its own render (below). THE DEFECT IT ADDRESSES, measured: on a SPLIT-VERTEX corner (position-coincident, index-distinct border columns — the [STONE-JUNC] 91-deg seam population; the t=5968 pier arris is the 'rooms' local (2.469,y,-4.937) edge at world (17.898,y,-58.014)) BOTH sheets' columns join one mitre group and the union profile becomes DOUBLE-VALUED: the columns interleave 3-5 milli-u apart in s and each side's sample carries its OWN u-column of the height field, so at one s the two sides disagree about stone-vs-joint. Welded dsp -0.1104 at s=3.200 (wall u=-3.2545) against -0.0532 at s=3.204 (wall u=+0.5000) — a sawtooth the weld itself wrote; each sheet's border chords its OWN subsequence of it and the two welded polylines diverge up to ~0.06 u mid-chord — the mid-height shear at the t=5968 joint rows and 105 px of z==0 punch-through (4 029 px with block_level off: that flag was partially papering over this, not fixing it). MODE 1 = collapse each s-cluster (eps 0.02 u — 10x the pair gap, under half the 0.065 vert pitch) to its mean s holding the MAX h: a joint cuts only where BOTH sheets carry it, the 2026-08-14 phase round's stated rule, enforced single-valuedly before the envelope/notch/block-level stages. VERIFIED single-valued on the census (paired welds agree to <=4 milli-u) and the joint-row shear closes — BUT z==0 punch-through grows 105 -> 3 346 px at t=5968: with the corner held at stone-top level, the 0.02 band's FAN-SLIVER tessellation (one authored apex serving up to 1.4 u of 0.043-pitch corner line, [STONE-CORNERF]) twists between the proud corner and its own carving apex and backface-culls. Mode 1's failure is therefore the TESSELLATION's, not the rule's — the structural fix is a band LADDER (inner polyline densified to the border pitch), not this knob. MODE 2 = MIN (a joint on EITHER sheet cuts through the arris): 105 px punch-through, look near the double-valued arm's. Both modes move >=11 %% of the t=5968 frame (SSAO ripple included) and are UNJUDGED by the user's eye. Size-1 clusters pass through untouched in both modes, so the ten paired-column-free 63-deg mitre lines are bit-identical by construction.")

// ─── --greets_displace_band_ladder: the border band as QUADS, not fans ────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (greets_displace_band_ladder, "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_BAND_LADDER", 0, "greets", "BAND LADDER re-tessellation for the stone displacement bake (2026-08-18, the t=5968 pier arris, part 2) — EXPERIMENT, default OFF and byte-null; needs --greets_displace_free_edge. **ADJUDICATED AGAINST at t=5968: kept as the A/B instrument, do not default it on.** THE DEFECT IT ATTACKS is real and measured: the 0.02 border band is triangulated as MEGA-SLIVER FANS (one authored apex serving up to 1.4 u of 0.043-pitch border, [STONE-CORNERF] ~30x density mismatch), which smear texels across their span — the stretched-texel channel — and twist into backface culls. WHAT THIS DOES: frees authored BREAK verts between freed same-line edges (so the mitre can cover them), then rebuilds each banded strip as a ladder — one inner node under every border vert along the strip's inner polyline, quads between, ladder bounded to the region the inner polyline spans. WHAT WAS MEASURED (corner rig FDS_DISPLACETEST_CORNER=1, then greets t=5968 full umbrella + profile_agree=1): the rig improves modestly (front punch-through 156 -> 112 px, gap mean 0.020 u) — but the REAL scene REGRESSES: z==0 punch-through 3 346 -> 5 086 px, thin cull-stripes down the whole channel; the near-vertical cells trip the fold-relax, which halves welded border verts PER SHEET and re-splits the weld. Four variants adjudicated: chord-pinned (retired: the interior side is a CELL polyline, not one chord), unpinned, one-freed-endpoint end densification (mega-fans, green x20), post-hoc re-band (per-face micro-bands, green x30). THE REAL FIX is a stage REORDER — densify borders full-length FIRST, band once at authored scale, tessellate strips against the cell-tessellated interior — a border-pipeline redesign, not an add-on pass; see docs/SESSION_STATE.md 2026-08-18.")

// ─── --greets_displace_border_v2: the border and its band densify in LOCKSTEP ──
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (greets_displace_border_v2, "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_BORDER_V2", 0, "greets", "BORDER-PIPELINE REORDER v2 for the stone displacement bake (2026-08-18, the t=5968 pier arris part 3) -- default OFF and byte-null by construction; needs --greets_displace_free_edge. Implements docs/STONE_BORDER_REORDER.md: the band strip and the border densify TOGETHER, instead of the band running once at authored scale and the border then being cut ~30x finer underneath it. WHAT IT DOES, four pieces, all inside the freed-border block of DisplaceStoneSubdiv. (1) The band pre-split RECORDS the pairing it already implies: for every border SEGMENT it bands, the two inner verts it planted and the three faces it left behind (band tri, return tri, interior tri across the band's inner edge). Per SEGMENT, not per (vert, authored edge) as the design sketch says -- two adjacent border faces each plant their OWN inner vert at a shared border station (same perpendicular offset, different position ALONG the border, because each rides toward its own face's opposite corner), so (vert, edge) does not name one partner. (1b) The border faces the pre-split CANNOT band -- it skips a face already narrower than 1.6x the band -- are paired with the cell quad they already sit in: the face plus its neighbour across one side edge, whose far edge is the strip's inner edge. This is a DEVIATION from the design's 'stage 1 unchanged' and it is load-bearing: measured on the corner rig, every corner face runs hC 0.0146 (far column) to 0.0293 (corner column) against a 0.032 threshold -- the edge-aligned tessellation lands a groove shoulder 0.027 u off the border -- so without it only 94 of ~700 segments carried a pairing and 2 178 of 2 513 splits fell back to fans. Sub-banding those faces instead was BUILT AND MEASURED WORSE (3 443/3 458 in lockstep but 0.007-deep strips: flips 492 -> 9 700, green graze/front 667/156 -> 667/662) -- a strip that thin inverts under any relief at all. Both quad diagonals are handled; the second is re-cut onto the first so one restructure serves. (2) The authored interior BREAK verts are freed (the abut-veto-rechecked pass --greets_displace_band_ladder introduced), with one v2-only correction: a BAND-INNER node is not a break vert. It sits kBandWidth off the border, so the pass's 0.98 along-the-line test passes for any neighbour more than ~0.1 u away -- measured on the rig, 6 of 6 'break' verts freed were inner nodes on sheet B's far column, after which the recursion densified the inner POLYLINE as if it were a border (+2 513 verts against ~700 of actual border). (3) LOCKSTEP: when the recursion splits a paired border segment at m it inserts m2 on the strip's inner edge at the same parameter and rebuilds the pair's quad as two quads (4 tris) plus one edge split of the interior tri across m2 -- and PINS m2 to the midpoint of its parent inner edge for the rest of the bake (re-imposed after the displacement loop and again after the fold relax). The pin is what makes the interior split safe: the two halves stay exactly COPLANAR with the triangle they came from, so a thin interior sliver can never twist, invert or crack. Without it the interior split IS the mega-sliver fan this campaign is trying to kill, one band inward -- rig green graze/front 667/156 -> 1 930/1 899. (These are v2's own per-segment pins, not the ladder's retired ones: the ladder chord-pinned a whole strip to ONE chord, each of these has exactly one parent segment, so the midpoint rule is exact.) (4) The END COURSES densify: a border segment with ONE freed endpoint splits, but ONLY where it carries a band pairing -- which is the whole difference from the standalone one-freed variant this campaign already adjudicated against at green x20, because a paired segment has no interior apex to fan to. The far end must be a real line END: not a band-inner node (that is a cascade, not a course) and NOT a vert the abut veto PINNED -- a pinned end means there really IS a far side there, and densifying into it frees midpoints that recess away from it. Measured at t=5968: allowing pinned ends opens a 182 px black slit along the wall/floor junction and takes the arm from 148 px to 364. WHAT IT IS WORTH, t=5968, his umbrella arm (--env_live_water --deferred --env_sphere_parallax --city-env-pixel --texture-filter=2 --ssao --ssao-gtao --env_dynamic --ssao_downscale=2 --greets-displace --greets_displace_block_level, FDS_GREETS_CAM=18.9410915,3.36436033,-60.2976227,...), pure-black px: double-valued default 105 / --greets_displace_profile_agree=1 alone 3 346 / +--greets_displace_band_ladder 5 086 / **+this flag 148**. The arris CHANNEL contributes ZERO of that 148 -- the black slit down the whole channel is gone, not smeared, and the residual sits in the same left-of-frame buckets the 105 px default arm already had (dark mech silhouette, 89 px there in the default arm against 130 here). v2 WITHOUT profile_agree is 901 px: this flag is the TESSELLATION half and mode 1 is the PROFILE half; neither is worth having alone. NOT DEFAULTED ON: --greets_displace_profile_agree is still an unjudged look call. WHAT IT DOES NOT DO, measured, so nobody re-derives it: the fold relax is still load-bearing -- v2 + mode 1 with --no-greets_displace_fold_relax is 3 878 px against 148 -- so the design's stage 5 ('the weld/relax conflict dissolves') is NOT what happened; the relax simply has far less to fix. On the CORNER RIG (mode 1, approved arm, amp 0.3) v2 does not reach the design's PASS line and two thirds of that line are unreachable: off -> on gives border-gap max 0.0942 -> 0.2474, mean 0.0199 -> 0.0337, twisted faces 492 -> 939, green graze/front 667/156 -> 667/63. The gap and the flips are the WELD/RELAX conflict, not the tessellation -- the same 0.2474 outlier appears with the lockstep restructure disabled and only stage 2+4 live. And the graze '667 punch-through floor' that docs/STONE_BORDER_REORDER.md's gate 1 requires to reach 0 is a RIG ARTEFACT, not the unwelded end courses: DisplaceTest.cpp's backA quad spans z in [-5.8,+2.8] while sheet A only covers z in [-6,0], so 2.8 u of backdrop protrude past sheet A's own open edge and are directly visible from the graze camera at (2.6,3.6,0.2). Proof: the green mask in the count window is BIT-IDENTICAL (667 px, the same 18x44 block at x 1134-1151, y 596-639) across arms whose vertex counts differ threefold. Fix the rig before quoting that number again.")

// ─── --greets_displace_reflex_weld: sharp-reflex corners JOIN the mitre weld ──
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (greets_displace_reflex_weld, "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_REFLEX_WELD", 0, "greets", "SHARP-REFLEX CORNER WELD (2026-08-27, the >240-deg wall-connection defect — HIS bulge class). DEFAULT OFF since 2026-08-28: his fly-through verdict — it did not fix the real issue and added tears; kept for A/B while the root-cause hunt runs on the normal chain. Needs --greets_displace_free_edge + --greets_displace_mitre; --no- restores the shared-vertex corner byte-exactly. THE DEFECT, measured with --greets_displace_junction_census: same-material corner lines whose walls meet SHARP-REFLEX from the visible side (the 270-deg colonnade arrises at z=-37, the 250.5-deg braces; solid wedge <= ~120 deg) are index-INTERIOR in the mesh the bake sees — the authored data shares their verts at/below 90-deg solid, and the seam-weld pass merges the rest — so the border machinery never classifies them: not freed, never welded ([STONE-MITRE-CAND] 'not-freed' down the whole (9.875,y,-37.03) column). Both sheets then displace the FULL height field along smoothed vertex normals that straddle the corner (measured dir (-0.918,+0.153,+0.367) on a (-1,0,0) wall) — the two sheets travel divergently and the connection tears and leans. The GENTLE lines (200-230 deg: the 207-deg jamb at (9.875,y,-49.374)) are authored split, get freed by the convex abut veto and take the matched-mitre weld with the signed per-block level — 83 welded verts measured — which is exactly why they work. THIS FLAG routes the sharp class into that same proven pipeline: a pre-pass detects same-material target edges whose two faces meet outside-convex with visible angle > greets_displace_reflex_min_vis, SPLITS the shared verts per wall sheet (coincident duplicates, house write-back pattern), exempts those lines from the seam-weld merge and from the split-vertex-seam pin, and lets the standard path take over: freed border -> matched mitre -> castellated signed course level. The weld groups formed on these lines also force the paired-sample MAX collapse (profile_agree mode 1 semantics, scoped to these lines only) because their two sheets' columns are position-paired by construction and a double-valued profile is exactly the divergence this flag exists to close.")
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(greets_displace_reflex_min_vis, "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_REFLEX_MIN_VIS", 240.0f, "greets", "--greets_displace_reflex_weld: minimum VISIBLE-side wall-connection angle (degrees, his convention: measured from the back-cull direction through where the viewer can stand) for a same-material corner line to be split+welded. Default 240 sits in the scene's empty 230-250 census gap, so the gentle-reflex class (200-230, works today) is untouched and the sharp class (250-280) is exactly the population treated. Visible = 180 + angle-between-rendered-normals, so 240 <=> normals > 60 deg apart, with the outside-corner side test deciding reflex-vs-interior.")

// ─── --greets_displace_border_mean: pin the freed border to the PATCH MEAN ────
FDS_FLAG_INT  (greets_displace_border_mean, "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_BORDER_MEAN", 0, "greets", "MEAN-LEVEL BORDER for the stone displacement bake, default 0 = OFF and byte-null; needs --greets_displace_free_edge (it moves exactly the verts that flag frees). THE DEFECT IT FIXES: the zero-pin holds an authored border at displacement EXACTLY 0, but the displacement convention is zero-mean against the WHOLE height map (d = amp*(h-mipMean), mipMean 0.5491) and a given wall's own visible window is not that mean. MEASURED at the greets doorway jamb (t=5998, --greets_displace_junction_census): the wall's realized surface sits 0.0248 world u BEHIND its authored plane while its border line is held at 0, so the border stands PROUD of the surface it bounds and the last band before it ramps outward -- which is the swollen, rounding-outward silhouette the user reported. As one number, near-edge minus far-field out-of-plane offset: bow -0.01912 u on the shipping displaced arm. Freeing the border instead (--greets_displace_free_edge alone) overshoots the other way, +0.00671, because the recess-only clamp lets a freed vert carve but never stand proud, and it leaves the border wandering per-vertex (silhouette std 3.21 px against the authored geometry's 1.73). This flag instead holds every freed border vert at ONE CONSTANT displacement, so the border stays a perfectly straight line by construction and only its depth is chosen. MODE 1 = one constant per MATERIAL. Straightest silhouette measured anywhere in this campaign -- std 1.37 px, span 4, better than the authored geometry itself -- but MEASURED AND REJECTED for depth: 'rooms' is an entire room, so its mean (-0.07148 u) is nothing like the jamb wall's own level (-0.0248 u), the border lands 0.022 u too deep and the bow simply changes sign, -0.01912 -> +0.02226. MODE 2 = one constant per authored PLANE, which is the surface that actually bounds the border: the vertex's incident target faces are hashed to a canonical quantised plane (normal 1/1024, offset 1/128 u, sign-canonicalised on the dominant component so the FLD's disagreeing windings hash together), and the mean is taken over that plane's own interior displaced verts. A border line lies in exactly one plane, so the constant is still constant ALONG the border -- mode 1's straightness is kept by construction -- while the depth becomes locally correct. Verts with no coplanar fan (corners, >2 deg between incident target faces) and planes with fewer than 8 interior verts fall back to the material mean; the counts are printed as [STONE-BMEAN]. Only FREED borders are eligible: a border with a neighbour on the far side still pins to zero, because moving it would open the T-junction the pin exists to close. Independent of --greets_displace_plane_normal, which changes the ride DIRECTION and is a separate (and separately measured) question. SEMANTICS 2026-08-13: the level applies to PINNED borders only; FREED borders (--greets_displace_free_edge) carry the full height field - see that flag's (4).")

// ─── --greets_displace_border_mean_scale: how much of that mean to actually use ─
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(greets_displace_border_mean_scale, "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_BORDER_MEAN_SCALE", 0.40f, "greets", "Fraction of the chosen mean level that --greets_displace_border_mean actually pins a freed border to. DEFAULT 0.40 IS A MEASURED CALIBRATION CONSTANT, NOT A TASTE SETTING -- see the sweep below. 0.0 reproduces the plain zero-pin exactly (verified: scale 0 is byte-for-byte the shipping displaced arm at t=5998, bow -0.01912 and silhouette std 3.23 both reproduced); 1.0 uses the full arithmetic mean. WHY THE ARITHMETIC MEAN IS THE WRONG TARGET: the level a border must match is the level of the surface AS RENDERED, and at the grazing angles where this defect is visible the rendered surface is not the mean of the relief but its UPPER ENVELOPE -- the peaks occlude the valleys, so the visible wall sits well proud of the mean of its own vertices. MEASURED at the greets jamb (t=5998): the vertex-mean displacement of the jamb plane is -0.0765 world u, but the rendered far-field surface sits only 0.0248 u behind the authored plane, a factor of 3.1. Pinning to the full vertex mean therefore overshoots -- the border lands BELOW the surface it should be flush with and the bow just changes sign. THE SWEEP (mode 2, --greets_displace_free_edge, bow = near-edge minus far-field out-of-plane offset, silhouette std over rows 150-950; t=5998 / t=5987): scale 0.00 bow -0.01912/-0.01858 std 3.23/6.63; 0.20 -0.00921/-0.00741 std 2.04/4.65; 0.30 -0.00425/-0.00241 std 1.66/3.70; 0.40 +0.00047/+0.00228 std 1.61/2.82; 0.50 +0.00499/+0.00673 std 1.55/2.40; 0.60 +0.00933/+0.01096 std 1.54/2.35; 0.80 +0.01754/+0.01886 std 1.45/2.27; 1.00 +0.02521/+0.02615 std 1.35/2.16. The bow is linear in the scale to three figures (bow = 0.0443*scale - 0.0179 at t=5998, 0.0442*scale - 0.0164 at t=5987), so the ZERO CROSSING IS A MEASUREMENT: 0.405 and 0.372, mean 0.39, and 0.40 is the swept point next to it. At 0.40 the silhouette std is 1.61 at t=5998 -- STRAIGHTER THAN THE AUTHORED GEOMETRY ITSELF (1.73) -- while the bow is 40x smaller than the shipping arm. Note the two objectives do NOT peak together: straightness keeps improving past 0.40 (1.35 at scale 1.0) while the bow keeps growing, which is exactly why this is a knob and why 0.40 is the point where the FLUSHNESS is right rather than the point where the line is straightest. Ignored when --greets_displace_border_mean=0.")
// ─── --env_probe_follow_owner: fading the budgeted capture-point SNAP ───────
FDS_FLAG_INT  (env_dyn_fade,           "FDS_ENV_DYN_FADE",             16,                             "parallax", "Frames over which a FOLLOWED probe's re-bake SNAP is faded out. 0 = the legacy snap. A followed capture point is BUDGETED (--env_probe_follow_budget, 1/frame) and THRESHOLDED (--env_probe_follow_eps, 1 u): it sits still while its owner walks away, then jumps the whole accumulated drift in ONE frame, and the WHOLE CUBE is re-captured from the new point. MEASURED on the greets canopy probe, his line, the re-bake at t=7029: mean |dRGB| between consecutive overlays of the live cube goes 1.80 on the frame before to 18.54 across the snap, and the mech's own coverage of its own cube steps 36864 -> 98222 texels. The user: 'the mech pop - let's fade the snap.' The ramp is a SMOOTHSTEP over dynFrame (a FRAME index, never wall-clock: the fade must be a pure function of the frame or the 24-run determinism gate goes red by construction). Smoothstep, not linear, so neither END of the ramp is a velocity step and the fade cannot introduce a second, smaller pop where it finishes. Fades CHAIN: the anchor is whatever the probe is showing at the instant of the next re-bake, so back-to-back snaps do not restart from a stale one. DEFAULT 16 BY MEASUREMENT, not by taste. Peak consecutive-overlay change inside the fade window: N=4 13.58 (a SPIKE - a fade this short is worse than none at that frame), N=8 10.03, N=16 10.09, N=32 5.03; and over the five overlays after the snap N=16 gives 0.50/1.41/2.90/4.22/5.60 against the ~6.2 the scene moves by itself in those same frames, i.e. the ramp stays UNDER the motion already there. N=32 is smoother still (0.48/0.23/0.79/1.25/1.92) but the re-bake interval on this walk is ~29 frames, so it blends almost every frame; use it if the ramp ever reads. NOTE the probe is scheduled, not updated every frame (~1 overlay per 2 frames here), so N counts FRAMES and the ramp gets about N/2 steps. [ENVFADE] traces each snap.")
FDS_FLAG_INT  (env_dyn_fade_mode,      "FDS_ENV_DYN_FADE_MODE",        1,                              "parallax", "WHICH SHAPE --env_dyn_fade uses. 1 (default, MEASURED WINNER) = CROSS-DISSOLVE THE CUBE: keep the pre-snap cube and blend it into the post-snap one, leaving the capture point alone, so every frame is ONE coherent cube. 0 = GLIDE THE POINT: leave the content alone and render the movers from a point that smoothsteps from the pre-snap point to the newly baked one. Glide is the cheaper and more obvious shape and it LOST, for a structural reason worth keeping written down: THE SNAP IS NOT ONLY THE MOVERS. A re-bake re-captures the whole cube - room included - from a point 1 u away, so every one of the six faces changes, and moving the overlay camera cannot touch any of that. Measured at the snap frame, mean |dRGB summed over channels| PER FACE: legacy snap 34.5 to 87.6 across the six; glide 29.9 to 76.5 (it removes the mover half of the +X face, 76.2 -> 43.5, and leaves every other face essentially untouched); cross-dissolve 0.9 to 1.6 on all six. On the whole-cube pop metric that is 18.54 (snap) / 14.83 (glide) / 0.48 (dissolve). Glide DOES do its own job - it cuts the mover coverage step from +61358 texels to +1272 - and its price is a bounded misregistration between where the movers are drawn and where the base was captured: max 0.99 u at N=8 (mean 0.069 u, 3 frames in 30) rising to max 1.03 u at N=32 (mean 0.275 u, 14 frames in 30), decaying to 0 at the fade's end. The dissolve's costs are a 6-face 256^2 blend plus a 6-FACE mip refilter on each fading frame (the overlay normally refilters only the ~2.7 faces it touched) and 1.57 MB held per fading probe. Its retained cube IS stale, but as a decaying WEIGHT, not as the hard depth-tested duplicate --env_dyn_static_exclude removed: it reads as a short temporal smear, not as a second mech. Keep mode 0 for the case where the blend's cost or its smear is the thing that bites.")
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (env_dyn_dump_seq,       "FDS_ENV_DYN_DUMP_SEQ",         0,                              "parallax", "DIAGNOSTIC, default OFF, byte-null. Companion to --env_dyn_dump=N: also write a FRAME-INDEXED copy of the selected store's live cube, /tmp/envdynseq_<mat>_f<dynFrame>.ppm, one per overlay. --env_dyn_dump alone overwrites one stable path every overlay, so building a strip across a TRANSITION — a follow snap, an --env_dyn_fade ramp, a scheduler change — otherwise costs one process per frame, each replaying the walk from its start (MEASURED at ~60 s per frame on the greets mech walk at t=7040, i.e. ~40 minutes for one 13-frame strip). With this it is a single run. Writes nothing unless --env_dyn_dump has already selected a store, so it cannot surprise a run that did not ask for dumps.")

// ─── --vol_cone_solve_vec: the cone quadratic, 8 lanes at a time ──────────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(vol_cone_solve_vec,       "FDS_VOL_CONE_SOLVE_VEC",       1,                              "atmos",    "Solve the per-pixel cone-interval quadratic 8 lanes wide instead of one scalar lane at a time (Render_VolumetricCones_Tile, FDS/RENDER/DeferredVolumetric.cpp). WHAT IT REPLACES: the cone pass is 37.5% of all running CPU on city t=1961, and an ablation put 2.681 of its 4.217 Ginstr/f -- 63.6% -- in this one scalar loop (commit a16567b): 46 authored headlights x ~12.4 spots/tile x 21600 px/tile = 25.6 M scalar solves per frame at ~105 instructions each, sitting INSIDE the per-spot loop and feeding a body that was already 8-wide. IPC was 4.0-4.2, so it was pure instruction COUNT, not stalls and not memory. MEASURED (matched 4-arm A/B, one binary set, one asset tree, city t=1961, iters=6, 6 rounds with the arm order rotating): cones 4.167 -> 2.886 Ginstr/f (-30.7%), 1.024 -> 0.722 Gcyc/f, wall_min 34.7 -> 25.4 ms; renderFrame 8.367 -> 7.090 Ginstr/f (-15.3%), 2.183 -> 1.915 Gcyc/f. IPC stays 3.9, which is the point -- instructions were removed from an instruction-bound loop. Across the city t-sweep the pass drops 24-31% at every pose and its share of frame instructions goes 32-50% -> 27-41%. Against ceiling: the solve itself goes 2.65 -> 1.37 Ginstr/f, a 1.94x rather than the 8x the lane count suggests -- 55% of a perfect 8-wide port, or 64% of the realistic floor, because on arm64 simde emulates every __m256 op with TWO 128-bit NEON ops so a _mm256_-spelled port cannot beat 1/4 of scalar, and because the wide arm computes both a-sign branches and the whole tail for all eight lanes where the scalar arm's dead lanes bail early. IT IS BIT-EXACT, which is why no pin moved: city 3cbe42b166847e40f7071eedb48d613c 2/2 with this ON and 2/2 with it off, greets 778fa6acd85a69cf241babefcdaf598e 2/2, fountain 8db68ccb59416e9a44037e9f387b7bd9 2/2, render_gate 3/3. That took reading the compiler's FMA CONTRACTION MAP off the arm64 disassembly rather than off the source -- under the tree-wide -ffp-contract=fast it chooses which product of each multiply-add pair to fuse and which to round, and its choices do not follow source order (it computes DP as fma(Pz,Dz,fma(Px,Dx,fl(Py*Dy))) from a source that reads Dx*Px + Dy*Py + Dz*Pz). Reproduced per lane: VP = fl(Pz + fma(Px,X,fl(Y*Py))), DV = fl(Dz + fma(Dx,X,fl(Y*Dy))), sphereDisc = fma(VP,VP,-fl(sphereC*uV)), a = fma(DV,DV,-fl(c2*uV)), b = t+t with t = fma(c2,VP,-fl(DP*DV)), disc = fma(b,b,fl(cq*fl(a*-4))), cq = fma(DP,DP,-fl(c2*PP)) -- in every pair the SECOND product is the rounded one. Only _mm256_fmadd_ps is used (simde lowers it to vfmaq_f32; _mm256_fmsub_ps and _mm256_fnmadd_ps lower to sub(mul(..)) and would hand the fusion choice back to the compiler), a*b-c is spelled fma(a,b,NEG(c)) with NEG an explicit sign-bit xor, std::min/std::max are cmp+blend rather than _mm256_min_ps (NEON FMIN resolves NaN and -0 the opposite way from the scalar FCSEL), and the `continue if <` tests use the unordered predicates that are their exact negation. The three reciprocals stay true IEEE divides and both square roots stay true sqrts -- NOT for the vec_ggx reason (a volumetric integrand really does tolerate approximation better than a reflect() direction: the raw-estimate build moved city by 200 px of 2 073 600, all at 1 LSB) but because approximation does not PAY here: raw vrecpe/vrsqrte measured +1.6% instructions, -1.7% cycles, -1.0% wall, i.e. nothing, since the loop is instruction-bound and NEON's estimates are 8-bit -- half what the x86 intrinsic names imply -- so any usable accuracy needs NR steps costing more than the divide they replace. WHAT STAYS SCALAR: mirror-clone (mirrorId != 0) and bounce-clamped spots, which need per-lane gathers and are rare. SEGMENTED cones (narrow < ~10 deg disco beams, and any turbulent cone) were excluded too until ROUND 6, on round 1's greets measurement -- ungated at the greets pin pose the wide arm then read instructions -3.8% but cycles +3.6% and wall +1.4% (7.63 -> 7.73 ms). THAT GATE IS GONE. Chase, which was never in the campaign, runs 32 NARROW cones of 34 spots, so 91% of its (batch x spot) pairs sat on the scalar arm the gate forced, and the ablation ladder re-run on chase put 53.2% of the whole pass (1.487 of 2.797 Ginstr/f) in exactly that loop. Re-measured against a parent binary, interleaved min-of-6, WITH the FDS_CONE_QUADEARLYOUT cut that landed beside it: chase t=800 cones 18.30 -> 14.93 ms / 2.796 -> 2.192 Ginstr/f / 0.622 -> 0.500 Gcyc/f; greets t=1588 7.53 -> 7.41 ms (its own beams are segmented and they IMPROVE); city t=1961 0.540 -> 0.537 Gcyc/f (all wide -- the term's removal only perturbs codegen). What changed since round 1 is the KERNEL, not the argument: NEONMINMAX, the round-4b/5 algebra folds and the lane_vec loops all landed after the gate was set. It is NOT byte-null on the segmented cones, unlike round 1's version: the wide arm's VP/DV fold (round 5) rounds Y*Py + Pz once where the scalar arm rounds Y*Py first, so segmented cones inherit that judge call -- chase's five pose pins and the greets pin move, quantified in the round-6 commit. OFF restores the scalar loop verbatim. Two further variants were built, measured and rejected, and are kept compiled out at their sites: a range-sphere early-out (FDS_CONE_SOLVE_EARLYOUT, +2.0% instructions -- it only fires when all EIGHT lanes miss the sphere, which is rarer than the 39.9%-dead-pairs figure suggests) and the approximate reciprocals above (FDS_CONE_SOLVE_APPROX). Full write-up, disassembly recipe and t-sweep in docs/HW_PROFILING.md section 9.")

// ─── mirror-shard per-face cone cull: the widening margin (tan², 1.3 = as measured) ──
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(shard_cone_cull_margin, "FDS_SHARD_CONE_CULL_MARGIN",   1.3f,                           "greets",   "LAB BUILD ONLY (-DFDS_SHARD_BAKE_LAB=ON; inert otherwise). Multiplier on tan^2(half-angle) of the cone --shard_cone_cull=2 tests each face's world bounding sphere against: tan2 = window_tan2 * margin + 1e-3, at both MirrorShatter call sites. 1.0 = the exact cone circumscribing the four corner rays of the shard's RENDERED SCREEN RECT, which is already a superset of the frustum by convexity; 1.3 (default) keeps the slack the legacy per-vertex path carried and is what the byte-identity was measured at. RAISE IT to widen the cone, i.e. cull LESS -- the dial to reach for if a shard cell ever differs from --shard_cone_cull=0, because the cull can only ever be wrong by being too NARROW. Below 1.0 is unsupported: the cone stops containing the window and the cull stops being conservative. IT IS ALSO THE INSTRUMENT THAT FOUND TWO ERRORS IN THE CONE ddb1d15 DESCRIBED. First, that cone is NOT '~1 degree wide': it is built about the shard NORMAL, and the reflected eye does not look at its own shard, so on greets it has to open to 17-19 degrees just to reach the window -- at which width it culls almost nothing. Second, the shard camera's basis is NOT orthonormal (its rows are wc1-wc0, wc3-wc0 and the normal, and a shard quad is not a rectangle), so reconstructing window points as Er + D*N + du*axisU + dv*axisV is wrong by the skew -- measured 6.8 degrees of axis error, four window widths, which culled faces sitting in the middle of the viewport. The shipping construction (shardFaceCone) instead INVERTS THE ACTUAL PROJECTION at the four screen corners, one 3x3 Cramer solve per shard, and assumes nothing. A margin sweep that never converges to the no-cull image is the signature of a cone that is wrong in SHAPE rather than merely narrow -- that is how both errors were caught.")

// ─── the offscreen bake's tile dispatch: stop round-tripping the SHARED semaphore ──
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(deferred_inline_tile_sem, "FDS_DEFERRED_INLINE_TILE_SEM", 0,                              "deferred", "REVERT LEVER, default 0 = the fix. 1 restores the legacy behaviour: an INLINE (offscreen-bake) deferred-lighting tile dispatch posts a renderns::tileDone permit at the end of every tile kernel and the dispatch loop immediately acquires it back, keeping the shared semaphore net-zero. WHY THAT WAS THE MIRROR-SHARD BAKE'S LARGEST SINGLE COST. Render_DeferredLighting runs 238 times per shatter frame (once per 64x64 shard cell) and each invocation walks the SAME 12x8=96 tile grid the 1920x1080 main frame uses -- 96 tiles for 4 096 pixels, 32 of them entirely off the right edge and shading nothing -- so the bake paid 96 x 238 = 22 848 release+acquire round trips per frame on ONE std::counting_semaphore that all 12 pool workers were hammering at once. MEASURED, standalone microbenchmark on this box (M2 Max): 34.5 ns per uncontended release+acquire pair, 3.4-4.0 us per pair in CORE time with 12 threads on one semaphore -- a 100x contention penalty. In the inline path the permit is pure ceremony: the thread that posts it is the thread that takes it back. IT IS BYTE-NULL BY CONSTRUCTION -- no pixel, depth or G-buffer value is a function of the semaphore; the kernels' pixel loops are untouched and the main frame (ov == nullptr, ctx.inlineDispatch == false) still posts and drains exactly as before, which the three scene pins certify. The kernels learn which mode they are in from DeferredLightingCtx::inlineDispatch, set once per Render_DeferredLighting call from DeferredOverride::inlineDispatch; the per-tile cost is one predicted branch on an already-hot ctx field, replacing an atomic RMW plus a futex wake. Use 1 only to reproduce the old timings.")

// ─── the offscreen bake's tile grid: sized to the TARGET, not to the main frame ──
FDS_FLAG_INT  (deferred_offscreen_tile_px, "FDS_DEFERRED_OFFSCREEN_TILE_PX", 32,                        "deferred", "MINIMUM TILE EDGE, IN PIXELS, for an OFFSCREEN deferred-lighting target (a DeferredOverride pass: the mirror-shard reflection bake, the mirror RTT). The grid becomes clamp(xres/N,1,12) x clamp(yres/N,1,8); 0 = LEGACY, i.e. the main frame's 12x8 grid whatever the target size. The main frame itself is never affected -- it is the ov == nullptr path and keeps 12x8 unconditionally. WHY: Render_DeferredLighting's tile grid was engine-global, so a 64x64 mirror-shard cell walked the same 96 tiles a 1920x1080 frame does -- 96 tile-kernel invocations for 4 096 pixels, 8x8 px each, and 32 of the 96 entirely off the right edge (12 tile columns of 8 px cover 96 px of a 64 px image), shading nothing but paying the kernel's per-tile prologue and, before the sibling fix, a contended semaphore round trip. At 238 shards that is 22 848 tile invocations per shatter frame. MEASURED, greets shatter bracket (--repro=greets@t=3122 --repro_from=3112 --repro_settle=0, FDS_GREETS_SHATTER=1, FDS_GREETS_CAM=\"28.8,10.8,-62.85,1,0,0\"), [SHARD-PHASE] Render_DeferredLighting core-ms summed over 12 workers, min-of-6 interleaved, run 1 discarded, with --deferred_inline_tile_sem already at its default 0: 12x8 grid 113.7 / 8x8 115.2 / 4x4 97.9 / 2x2 94.0 / 1x1 96.6, wall 13.7 / 13.9 / 11.6 / 11.6 / 11.5 ms. The optimum is BROAD and flat from 2x2 to 1x1: the win is fewer per-tile prologues, not better light culling -- 1x1 hands every pixel the union of all lights and still ties, which says the per-tile light cull buys almost nothing at this narrow off-axis FOV. 32 px lands a 64^2 cell on 2x2 and leaves every target >= 384 px wide on the full 12x8, so nothing but genuinely small offscreen passes moves. BYTE-NULL WHERE IT ACTS: the whole 1024x1024 shard reflection atlas is IDENTICAL (md5 f262e9497ca4ce122bed3f5e1a401f0c) at 12x8, 4x4, 2x2 and 1x1 -- a coarser tile's light list is a SUPERSET of a finer one's in the same global light order, and the extra lights fail the per-pixel `len2 <= range2` mask, contributing exact 0.0f. The three scene pins and render_gate's mirrortest (which covers the RTT) certify the main frame and the RTT.")

// ─── the mirror-shard bake's HDR round trip: BUILT, MEASURED, AND WRONG — default OFF ──
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (shard_hdr,              "FDS_SHARD_HDR",                0,                              "greets",   "MEASURED ARM, DEFAULT 0 = OFF, AND IT SHOULD STAY OFF. Gives the MIRROR-SHARD reflection bake its own per-worker HDR radiance buffer so each 64x64 cell accumulates linear radiance and runs exposure -> ACES -> sqrt before it lands in the atlas. This is the fix docs/OPTIMIZATION_BACKLOG.md designed for ddb1d15's residual (\"the shard bake never runs the HDR round-trip ... Hdr_WritableFor gates on the current dims, so at 64 sq the kernel silently takes the LDR combine\"). THE DIAGNOSIS IS RIGHT AND THE REMEDY IS WRONG, and this flag is kept only because the measurement that overturns it is worth being able to reproduce. WHAT IT MEASURES, greets shatter bracket (--repro=greets@t=3122 --repro_from=3112 --repro_settle=0, FDS_GREETS_SHATTER=1, FDS_GREETS_CAM=\"28.8,10.8,-62.85,1,0,0\"), mean luma over the panel window, against the MAIN deferred pass rendered from the shard's own reflected eye (FDS_GREETS_CAM=\"68.79,10.8,-62.85,-1,0,0\") as ground truth: reference 74.78; shard_hdr OFF (shipping) 87.31, i.e. +12.53 -- reproducing ddb1d15's recorded +12.5 to two decimals; shard_hdr ON 129.79, i.e. +55.01. IT MAKES THE RESIDUAL FOUR TIMES WORSE, and the cause is structural, not a tuning miss: THE SHARD ATLAS IS AN ALBEDO TEXTURE, NOT A FINISHED IMAGE. The shards are ordinary opaque scene geometry, so the MAIN frame's deferred kernel samples the atlas as a texel, lights it, writes linear radiance to g_hdrBuf and tonemaps it with everything else. Proof, one A/B: with this flag OFF, sweeping the FRAME's --hdr_exposure 1.0 -> 2.0 moves the mosaic 87.31 -> 130.78. The frame's tonemap already owns those pixels. Tonemapping the cell as well applies the transfer function TWICE -- and note ON-at-exposure-1.0 (129.79) lands essentially on OFF-at-exposure-2.0 (130.78), which is exactly the signature of a doubled tonemap. THE MIRROR RTT IS NOT A COUNTEREXAMPLE, it is the clue: it keeps the FLOAT radiance in the material's hdrRefl and hands THAT to the frame (GreetsMirror.cpp), and its Render_TonemapToVPage onto the 8-bit RTT surface is only the LDR fallback. So the real remedy for the +12.5 is an HDR ATLAS -- shard cells carrying linear radiance into the frame through an hdrRefl-shaped path -- not a tonemapped 8-bit cell. That is a bigger piece of work and is NOT done. What IS done and IS shipping unconditionally is the plumbing underneath: DeferredLightingCtx::hdrBuf now carries each pass's own HDR target instead of the kernels asking Hdr_WritableFor(ctx.xres, ctx.yres) -- \"the global happens to be sized like me\" standing in for \"am I the main pass?\" -- which is byte-null for the main frame (all three scene pins) and is the prerequisite any real per-target HDR needs. Turning this ON costs one clear plus two extra sweeps of each 64 sq cell per shard and changes ONLY the shard look; the main frame, the mirror RTT and every non-shatter frame are untouched either way.")

// ─── --vol_cone_lane_vec: the two leftover per-lane loops, 8 at a time ────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL(vol_cone_lane_vec,        "FDS_VOL_CONE_LANE_VEC",        1,                              "atmos",    "Run the cone pass's two remaining SCALAR per-lane loops 8 lanes wide (Render_VolumetricCones_Tile, FDS/RENDER/DeferredVolumetric.cpp): the dz / surface-fade-window setup, and the per-lane colour accumulate. WHY THESE TWO: 7e34645 widened the cone-interval SOLVE and left a note that the untouched integration body was now the majority of the pass at ~1.52 of 2.87 Ginstr/f -- INFERRED by holding a16567b's old split, never re-measured. Re-measuring it with a committed ablation ladder (-DFDS_CONE_ABLATE=n, staged `continue`s in the per-spot loop, reproduced to 0.3% across two independent sessions) OVERTURNED that inference: at city t=1961 the body is 1.089 G (38.0%), the 8-wide solve is 0.992 G (34.6%), the per-spot loop + scalar prologue 0.167 G (5.8%), the per-batch floor 0.047 G (1.6%) -- and 0.571 G (19.9%) sits in TWO SCALAR PER-LANE LOOPS the old split could not see, because they were pooled into `SIMD body + shadow taps + accumulate`: the dz/fade window setup at 0.270 G (9.4%) and the colour accumulate at 0.301 G (10.5%). The mechanism is not arithmetic, it is MEMORY TRAFFIC: the shipping kernel disassembles to 894 ldr + 583 str out of 4475 instructions (33%), and both loops are pure stack round-trips -- the solve writes zLoArr/zHiArr as vectors, the scalar loop reads them back one lane at a time to write three more stack arrays, which the 8-wide body immediately reloads as __m256; and accV, already an __m256, is spilled to accArr purely so eight scalar iterations can load-modify-store three more arrays that the composite reloads. Holding the colour accumulators in registers across the whole per-spot loop deletes that second set outright -- they drain to accB/accG/accR once per BATCH, so the composite loop is untouched. IT IS BIT-EXACT, and unlike the solve it cost nothing to make so: neither loop contains an a*b+c that -ffp-contract=fast could fuse ambiguously, so there is no contraction map to read off the disassembly this time. Only the three spellings 7e34645 already catalogued mattered -- std::max(d, fwMin) transcribed as cmp+blend rather than _mm256_max_ps (NEON FMAX resolves NaN and -0 the opposite way from the scalar FCSEL), the UNORDERED predicates _CMP_NEQ_UQ and _CMP_NLE_UQ as the exact negations of `if (alive == 0) continue` and `if (acc <= 0) continue` (an ordered predicate silently drops a NaN lane the scalar arm propagates), and the multiply order preserved as ((acc*density)*nNorm)*coneGain rather than folded into one factor, which would be a re-association. Default on; OFF restores both scalar loops verbatim and is byte-identical. Full ladder, the levers that died, and the disassembly evidence in docs/HW_PROFILING.md section 9.")

// ─── grazing anisotropy: the census that measures it ─────────────────────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (mip_aniso_stats,         "FDS_MIP_ANISO_STATS",          0,                              "render",   "DIAGNOSTIC PER-FACE ANISOTROPY CENSUS for MiplevelClipper, default OFF, changes no pixel. Prints one stderr table at process exit: for the 40 highest-covered-area faces, the area-weighted texels-per-screen-pixel on the LONG and SHORT UV->screen axes, their ratio (the anisotropy), the legacy GEOMETRIC-MEAN LOD, the MAX-AXIS LOD --mip_aniso would use, their difference dLOD, the integer level each metric lands on, the fraction of visits that took the poly-split branch, and the texture. A face is clipped once PER TILE, so every quantity is accumulated area-weighted and keyed on the Face* -- the row is the whole face as the eye sees it, not one tile's shard. This is the instrument that replaces reading tint chromaticity off --pom_mip_viz: it says WHICH faces are anisotropic and by HOW MUCH, and its dLOD column is exactly the blur --mip_aniso would add there. It opens the same UV->screen Jacobian accumulation --mip_aniso needs, so a census run costs the aniso path's 5 extra MACs per fan triangle plus a mutex take per face-visit -- measurement-only, never leave it on for a perf run.")

// ─── --env_live_water's water mask: how much coverage counts as water ────────
FDS_FLAG_FLOAT(env_live_water_mask_bias,"FDS_ENV_LIVE_WATER_MASK_BIAS", 0.5f,                           "parallax", "BAKE-TIME coverage threshold that turns --env_live_water's per-source-pixel water test into the per-texel WATER VERDICT BIT the tilt is gated on, 0..1. A baked texel is water iff at least one of its own source pixels was an unoccluded hit on the water plane AND their coverage >= this. The bake renders 1024^2 and stores 512^2, so a texel owns exactly 4 source pixels and the ladder is 1/4, 2/4, 3/4, 4/4: 0.5 (default) = MAJORITY, which is the exact classification and the same rule --env_water_region_viz paints; 0 = any water pixel (dilates the water region by up to a texel, i.e. the tilt creeps one texel up the reflected shore); 1 = all four (erodes it, the most facade-conservative). Values between two rungs behave as the rung above them -- 0.3 and 0.5 are the same mask. RAISE IT if a reflected shoreline still shimmers, LOWER IT if the reflected water goes stiff right at the waterline. RE-SITED, AND THE OLD SEMANTICS ARE GONE ON PURPOSE (5f1ffa92/b2e6c915 read this per FRAME as w' = clamp((w - bias)/(1 - bias)) over a BILINEAR 128^2-per-512^2-face COVERAGE plane): the coverage plane was a 4x4-texel box blur of the bake's exact answer, read bilinearly and re-quantized into the sheets' alpha byte, and that blur is what the user saw -- 'I think a simple bit map on the reflection bake can really help here to decide what pixels should be displaced and which won't - since currently it's not exact at all and bleeds.' With the verdict stored at full res there is no fractional coverage left to threshold at sample time, so the knob moves to the one place a fraction still exists: the texel's own source pixels, at bake time. CONSEQUENCE: changing it needs a re-bake -- which --env_live_water always pays anyway, since it bypasses the cube cache. The old 0.999 'mask-lookup-only' perf arm is retired with the ramp; the tilt is now a hard 0/1 gate and its per-pixel cost is one integer compare.")
// ─── matched mitre weld: same-material oblique corners cut by the height field ──
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (greets_displace_mitre,   "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_MITRE",    1,                              "greets",   "MATCHED MITRE WELD (edge-notch stage 3; inert without --greets_displace_free_edge, so flag-off arms and non-displaced scenes are untouched by construction). 1d1c2ce9 diagnosed the user's t=5975 straight edge: the corner there is two 'rooms' walls meeting OBLIQUE (>90 deg) along one line, both borders freed by the convex-abuttal rule, each side riding its OWN de-slid direction with its OWN UV phase of the height field — two disagreeing profiles, no cut, straight silhouette. This pass groups every freed border vert whose abuttal probe finds a CONVEX SAME-MATERIAL partner by the corner LINE it lies on (quantised direction + foot point), requires BOTH walls present on the line (one-sided lines stay plain-freed — a lone mitre would pull off its wall against a straight partner), then welds: ONE shared height profile h(s) built from the owner side's border field (the larger population; same line-height override as the main loop), every vert on the line displaced along the group BISECTOR of the two wall normals by amp*(h(s)-mean)/cos(theta/2) — the 1/cos so each wall's own plane still sees a full-depth cut, clamped at cos(theta/2) >= 0.35 against the theta->180 blowup. Both walls' border polylines become re-samplings of the SAME piecewise-linear curve (linear interpolation of a piecewise-linear function stays on it), so the corner is watertight by construction and the silhouette follows the stone rows from every azimuth — the acceptance criterion of the round that added this. Default ON inside the free-edge arm (the umbrella rule: the user runs --greets_displace_free_edge and expects edges cut; --no-greets_displace_mitre is the A/B escape). [STONE-MITRE] prints the weld census; per-line detail under --greets_displace_junction_census.")

// ─── --env_live_water's water mask, seen in SCREEN space ────────────────────
FDS_FLAG_INT  (env_water_region_viz,   "FDS_ENV_WATER_REGION_VIZ",     0,                              "parallax", "DIAGNOSTIC, default 0 = off, byte-null. The SCREEN-SPACE half of FDS_ENVBAKE_DUMP's water-mask PGM. The PGM answers 'where does the mask think the water is' in CUBE space; this answers 'which SCREEN pixels read those texels', which is the region a --env_live_water phase A/B has to be scored over and which nothing else in the tree can produce for the FORWARD paraboloid-sheet path (the deferred kernel can be probed per pixel; the forward filler interpolates per-vertex UVs, so the only way to ask a screen pixel what class of content it is reading is to CHANGE that content and see who moves). 1 = INVERT every baked texel the mask calls water (its verdict bit set); 2 = invert every texel it calls NOT water. Since the verdict is stored per texel, the class the viz paints is EXACTLY the class the tilt gates on -- no resampling between the instrument and the thing it measures. Diff the resulting frame against the same run with this at 0: the pixels that differ are exactly that class's screen region. INVERT rather than paint a flag colour, because a flag colour collides with the content -- a night skyline is mostly near-black, so 'paint the non-water black' classifies almost nothing -- while the complement differs from the original for every texel except an exact 0x808080 and survives bloom, grade, CA and tonemap unharmed. Requires --env_live_water (no flag, no masks, and the sheets are untouched). Affects ONLY the forward city paraboloid sheets; it is a bake-time recolour, so it costs nothing per frame and cannot perturb the geometry, the coverage or the post stack -- the two runs differ in sheet TEXELS alone.")

// ─── carved-groove shading: grooves are crevices, not ceiling mirrors ────────
FDS_FLAG_BOOL (greets_displace_groove_shade, "FDS_GREETS_DISPLACE_GROOVE_SHADE", 1, "greets", "Carved-vert SHADING restore for the displaced stone (2026-08-14, the t=6039/t=5975 grazing complaints). The bake displaces verts below the surface to carve mortar courses; the recomputed smoothed normals on those carved bands then TILT toward the camera/ceiling, so the groove's cut faces catch the env lobe (a course can mirror the sea ceiling) and present as a LIT LEDGE / herringbone at extreme grazing — the exact opposite of real masonry, where a groove is an occluded crevice that shades like the wall around it and reads dark from its albedo. Mechanism: the displacement loop records, for every vert carved BELOW its reference (dsp<0), the AUTHORED pre-displacement vertex normal it rode (on a flat wall that IS the wall plane) and a depth weight w=min(1,-dsp/(0.35*amp)); DisplaceStoneSmoothNormals then blends the geometric normal back toward the authored one by w (position-keyed, exact bits, so per-cell chunk copies inherit). GEOMETRY IS UNTOUCHED — silhouette notches, the mitre corner cut, parallax all keep the carve; only the shading normal (and its Gram-Schmidt'd tangent) stops advertising the cut faces to the lighting. Default ON (the carved look is wrong without it); --no-greets_displace_groove_shade restores the ledge/mirror behaviour for A/B. Inert without --greets_displace (no bake, no records); flag-off-displaced and flat arms byte-identical.")
